// Generated by tools/build-problem-catalog.cjs. Edit the source files and regenerate.
Object.assign(window.MATH_PROBLEM_SOLUTIONS ||= {}, {
"2000-aime-i-1": ["If $ab = 10^n$ and neither factor ends in a zero, neither can contain both $2$ and $5$, so the only possible split is $\\{2^n, 5^n\\}$. So the question becomes the smallest $n$ for which $2^n$ or $5^n$ contains a digit zero, and checking in order, the powers $5^1,\\ldots,5^7$ ($5$ to $78125$) and $2^1,\\ldots,2^7$ ($2$ to $128$) have no zero, while $5^8=390625$ does. So $n = 8$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-i-2": ["The reflections give $B=(v,u)$, $C=(-v,u)$, $D=(-v,-u)$ and $E=(v,-u)$. $BCDE$ is a $2v\\times2u$ rectangle of area $4uv$, and $ABE$ is a triangle with base $BE=2u$ and height $u-v$, of area $u(u-v)$. The pentagon has area $u^2+3uv=u(u+3v)=451=11\\cdot41$. Since $u\\lt u+3v$, the factor pairs are $1\\cdot451$ (then $v=150\\gt u$) and $11\\cdot41$ (then $v=10$), so $u=11$, $v=10$ and $u+v=21$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-i-3": ["The binomial theorem gives the $x^2$ and $x^3$ coefficients as $\\binom{2000}{2}a^2b^{1998}$ and $\\binom{2000}{3}a^3b^{1997}$. Setting them equal and cancelling leaves $b = 666a$, and coprimality forces $a = 1$, $b = 666$, so $a+b = 667$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-i-4": ["Name the two smallest squares $a$ and $b$ (the one between the left squares and the one under the top right) and read the other sides off the figure, each the sum of the squares along one of its edges: $a+b$, then $2a+b$, $3a+2b$, $5a+3b$, the top right square $(3a+2b)+(a+b)+b=4a+4b$, the bottom right $4a+5b$, and the bottom left $(5a+3b)+(2a+b)+a=8a+4b$. The two widths agree automatically, $12a+9b$, but the two heights give one condition: the left column is $(5a+3b)+(8a+4b)=13a+7b$ and the right is $(4a+4b)+(4a+5b)=8a+9b$, so $5a=2b$. Then $a=2k$, $b=5k$, and the rectangle is $69k\\times61k$; coprime sides need $k=1$, so the perimeter is $2(69+61)=260$ (the squares are $2,5,7,9,16,25,28,33,36$).","",[]],
"2000-aime-i-5": ["Let the boxes hold $a$ and $b=25-a$ marbles with $x$ and $y$ black. Then $\\tfrac{xy}{ab}=\\tfrac{27}{50}$, so $50\\mid ab$, which among the splits of $25$ allows only $\\{5,20\\}$ and $\\{10,15\\}$. With $ab=100$, $xy=54$ with $x\\lt5$ and $y\\lt20$ forces $(3,18)$, and the white probability is $\\tfrac{2\\cdot2}{100}$; with $ab=150$, $xy=81$ forces $(9,9)$, and it is $\\tfrac{1\\cdot6}{150}$. Both give $\\tfrac1{25}$, so $m+n=26$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-i-6": ["The condition $\\frac{x+y}2 = \\sqrt{xy} + 2$ rearranges to $x - 2\\sqrt{xy} + y = 4$, which completes the square as $\\left(\\sqrt y - \\sqrt x\\right)^2 = 4$, so $\\sqrt y - \\sqrt x = 2$ given $y \\gt x$. Both roots must be integers, and $\\sqrt y \\lt 1000$ bounds $\\sqrt x$ to $1$ through $997$ (so that $\\sqrt y\\le999$), giving $997$ ordered pairs.","",[]],
"2000-aime-i-7": ["Multiply the three cyclic expressions together. Expanding $\\left(x+\\frac1z\\right)\\left(y+\\frac1x\\right)\\left(z+\\frac1y\\right)$ and using $xyz = 1$ collapses the eight terms into $2$ plus the sum of the three expressions themselves. So $5 \\cdot 29 \\cdot r = 2 + 5 + 29 + r$, that is $144r=36$, and $r=\\tfrac14$ without ever finding $x$, $y$ or $z$; so $m+n=5$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-i-8": ["Point down, the liquid is a cone similar to the container with linear ratio $\\tfrac9{12}=\\tfrac34$, so it fills $\\left(\\tfrac34\\right)^3=\\tfrac{27}{64}$ of the volume, since volumes scale as the cube. Point up, the empty space at the top is a similar cone filling $\\tfrac{37}{64}$, so its height is $12\\sqrt[3]{\\tfrac{37}{64}}=3\\sqrt[3]{37}$. The liquid is $12-3\\sqrt[3]{37}$ deep, and $m+n+p=52$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-i-9": ["Let $a,b,c$ be $\\log x,\\log y,\\log z$; the product rule turns the equations into $a+b-ab=1-\\log2$, $b+c-bc=1-\\log2$ and $a+c-ac=1$. Simon's trick regroups them as $(1-a)(1-b)=\\log2$, $(1-b)(1-c)=\\log2$ and $(1-a)(1-c)=1$. Multiplying the first two and dividing by the third gives $(1-b)^2=(\\log2)^2$, so $b=1\\mp\\log2$ and $y=\\tfrac{10}2=5$ or $y=20$. So $y_1+y_2=25$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-i-10": ["The condition says $x_k=S-x_k-k$, so $S-2x_k=k$ for every $k$. Summing over $k=1$ to $100$ gives $100S-2S=\\tfrac{100\\cdot101}2=5050$ by the arithmetic series formula, so $S=\\tfrac{5050}{98}=\\tfrac{2525}{49}$. Then $x_{50}=\\tfrac{S-50}2=\\tfrac{2525-2450}{98}=\\tfrac{75}{98}$, and $m+n=173$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-i-11": ["Every divisor of $1000=2^35^3$ is $2^m5^n$, so each coprime ratio $\\tfrac ab$ is $2^x5^y$ with $-3\\le x,y\\le3$, and each such number arises exactly once. The sum therefore factors as the product of two geometric series, $\\left(2^{-3}+\\cdots+2^3\\right)\\left(5^{-3}+\\cdots+5^3\\right)=\\tfrac{127}8\\cdot\\tfrac{19531}{125}=2480.437$. So $\\tfrac S{10}=248.04\\ldots$ and the answer is $248$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-i-12": ["Composing the symmetries turns them into translations: $f(398-x)=f(2158-x)$ gives $f(u)=f(u+1760)$, and $f(2158-x)=f(3214-x)$ gives $f(u)=f(u+1056)$. The Euclidean algorithm gives $\\gcd(1056,1760)=352$, so $f$ has period $352$, and the reflection becomes $f(x)=f(46-x)$ since $398\\equiv46\\pmod{352}$. So the values are constant on the pairs $\\{x,46-x\\}$ of residues mod $352$; the pair is a single residue only when $2x\\equiv46$, at $x\\equiv23$ and $x\\equiv199$. That leaves $\\frac{352-2}2+2=176+1=177$ classes, all reached by $0,\\ldots,999$, and a function taking a different value on each class attains them, so the answer is $177$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-i-13": ["Six minutes is $\\tfrac1{10}$ hour: $5$ miles along a highway, or $\\tfrac75$ miles across the prairie. Put the highways on the axes. Leaving the highway at $(x,0)$, the truck has $\\tfrac1{10}-\\tfrac x{50}$ hours left and reaches the disc of radius $\\tfrac75-\\tfrac{7x}{25}$ about $(x,0)$. The line through $(5,0)$ with slope $-\\tfrac7{24}$ is $\\tfrac7{25}(5-x)=\\tfrac75-\\tfrac{7x}{25}$ from $(x,0)$, so it is tangent to every one of these discs and bounds them all. In the first quadrant the region is bounded by this line and its mirror image in $y=x$, which meet on $y=x$ at $x=\\tfrac{35}{31}$, so it is two triangles of area $\\tfrac12\\cdot5\\cdot\\tfrac{35}{31}=\\tfrac{175}{62}$. A quadrant holds $\\tfrac{175}{31}$, the whole region $\\tfrac{700}{31}$, and $m+n=731$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-i-14": ["Let $x=\\angle A$. In the isosceles triangle $APQ$, $\\angle AQP=x$ and $\\angle APQ=180^\\circ-2x$; in the isosceles triangle $PQB$, the exterior angle at $Q$ is $x$, so $\\angle QPB=\\angle QBP=\\tfrac x2$ and $PB=2\\,PQ\\cos\\tfrac x2=2\\,BC\\cos\\tfrac x2$. The base angles are $90^\\circ-\\tfrac x2$, so in triangle $PBC$ the angles are $90^\\circ-x$ at $B$, $90^\\circ-\\tfrac x2$ at $C$ and $\\tfrac{3x}2$ at $P$, and the Law of Sines gives $PB=\\tfrac{BC\\cos\\frac x2}{\\sin\\frac{3x}2}$. Comparing, $\\sin\\tfrac{3x}2=\\tfrac12$, so $x=20^\\circ$. Then $\\angle ACB=80^\\circ$ and $\\angle APQ=140^\\circ$, so $r=\\tfrac47$ and $\\lfloor1000r\\rfloor=571$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-i-15": ["Run the dealing backwards: move the bottom card of the stack to the top, then put the next lower label on top. Card $1999$ starts on top of the two-card stack $1999,2000$. Each backward step moves it down two places, except when it is the bottom card, which comes to the top and then sits second. From second place in a stack of $m+1$ it is at place $1+2t$ after $t$ steps in a stack of $m+1+t$, so it reaches the bottom again exactly when the stack has $2m$ cards: the wraps happen at $3,6,12,\\ldots,1536$ cards. After the last one it sits second in a stack of $1537$, and the remaining $463$ steps move it down $926$ more places, so $1+926=927$ cards were above it.","",[]],
"2000-aime-ii-1": ["Rewrite each numerator as a logarithm in the same base: $2 = \\log_4 16$ and $3 = \\log_5 125$. Change of base turns both fractions into common-base ratios with the same denominator $\\log 2000^6$, so they add to $\\frac{\\log(16 \\cdot 125)}{\\log 2000^6} = \\frac{\\log 2000}{6\\log 2000} = \\frac16$. In lowest terms that is $\\tfrac mn$ with $m+n=7$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-ii-2": ["Factor $x^2-y^2=(x-y)(x+y)=2000^2=2^8\\cdot5^6$. The two factors have the same parity, since they differ by $2y$, and they cannot both be odd, so both are even: $x-y=2u$, $x+y=2v$ with $uv=2^6\\cdot5^6=10^6$. Each ordered pair of divisors $(u,v)$ of $10^6$, of either common sign, gives one lattice point, and $10^6$ has $7\\cdot7=49$ divisors, so there are $2\\cdot49=98$ points.","",[]],
"2000-aime-ii-3": ["After removing a pair, $38$ cards remain and the combination count gives $\\binom{38}2 = 703$ draws. A pair comes either from one of the nine untouched numbers, $9\\binom42 = 54$ ways, or from the two survivors of the removed number, $1$ way. So the probability is $\\frac{55}{703}$. That is already in lowest terms, so $m+n=758$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-ii-4": ["Six odd divisors and twelve even ones means $18$ in total, and every even divisor is an odd one times a power of two, so the ratio $12 : 6$ forces exactly $2^2$ in the factorization. The divisor-count formula then needs the odd part to have six divisors, smallest at $3^2 \\cdot 5$, so $n = 4 \\cdot 45 = 180$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-ii-5": ["Choose which five of the eight rings are worn, $\\binom85=56$ ways, and order them $5!=120$ ways reading finger by finger. Splitting that ordered row among four fingers is placing three dividers among the five rings, $\\binom83=56$ ways. So $n=56\\cdot120\\cdot56=376320$, whose leftmost three nonzero digits are $376$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-ii-6": ["Let the bases be $b$ and $b+100$. The midline has length $b+50$ and splits the trapezoid into two trapezoids of height $\\tfrac h2$, with areas in the ratio $(2b+50):(2b+150)=2:3$, so $b=75$ and the bases are $75$ and $175$. The equal-area segment is the root mean square of the bases, $x^2=\\tfrac{75^2+175^2}2=18125$. So $\\left\\lfloor\\tfrac{x^2}{100}\\right\\rfloor=181$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-ii-7": ["Multiplying through by $19!$ turns each term into a binomial coefficient: $\\binom{19}2+\\binom{19}3+\\cdots+\\binom{19}9=19N$. By symmetry the terms $\\binom{19}0$ to $\\binom{19}9$ make half of the row sum $2^{19}$, so the left side is $2^{18}-1-19=262124$ and $N=\\tfrac{262124}{19}=13796$. The greatest integer below $\\tfrac N{100}=137.96$ is $137$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-ii-8": ["With $x=BC$ and $y=CD$, the perpendicular diagonals make $\\angle BAC=\\angle CBD$ (each is complementary to $\\angle ABD$), so the right triangles $ABC$ and $BCD$ are similar and $\\tfrac{x}{\\sqrt{11}}=\\tfrac{y}{x}$, that is $x^2=y\\sqrt{11}$. Dropping the altitude from $A$ to $CD$ makes a right triangle with legs $x$ and $y-\\sqrt{11}$ and hypotenuse $\\sqrt{1001}$, and the Pythagorean theorem gives $x^2+(y-\\sqrt{11})^2=1001$. Substituting the first relation leaves $y^2-\\sqrt{11}\\,y-990=0$, so $y=10\\sqrt{11}$ and $BC^2=110$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-ii-9": ["Clearing the fraction gives $z^2 - 2z\\cos 3^\\circ + 1 = 0$, and the quadratic formula yields $z = \\cos 3^\\circ \\pm i\\sin 3^\\circ = \\operatorname{cis}3^\\circ$. De Moivre then gives $z^{2000} = \\operatorname{cis}6000^\\circ$, so $z^{2000} + z^{-2000} = 2\\cos 6000^\\circ = 2\\cos 240^\\circ = -1$, and the least greater integer is $0$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-ii-10": ["Join the center $O$ to the vertices and to the four points of tangency. Equal tangents make the tangent lengths $19$ and $26$ at $A$ and $B$, $37$ and $23$ at $C$ and $D$, and each vertex sees the radius $r$ in a right triangle, so the angles at $O$ come in pairs $\\alpha$, $\\beta$, $\\gamma$, $\\delta$ with $\\tan\\alpha=\\tfrac{19}r$, $\\tan\\beta=\\tfrac{26}r$, $\\tan\\gamma=\\tfrac{37}r$, $\\tan\\delta=\\tfrac{23}r$ and $2(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma+\\delta)=360^\\circ$. So $\\tan(\\alpha+\\beta)=-\\tan(\\gamma+\\delta)$, and the tangent addition formula gives $\\tfrac{45r}{r^2-494}=-\\tfrac{60r}{r^2-851}$. Then $45(r^2-851)=-60(r^2-494)$, $105r^2=67935$, and $r^2=647$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-ii-11": ["Translate $A$ to the origin, so $D=(1,7)$. The trapezoid's axis of symmetry is perpendicular to $AB$ and swaps $A$ with $B$ and $D$ with $C$, so $C-B$ is $D$ reflected across that axis, which is $-D'$ where $D'$ is the reflection of $D$ across line $AB$. So $C=B-D'$, and with $B$ a lattice point, $C$ is one exactly when $D'$ is. Now $D'$ is a lattice point at distance $\\sqrt{50}$ from $A$, one of $(\\pm1,\\pm7)$, $(\\pm7,\\pm1)$, $(\\pm5,\\pm5)$, other than $D$ itself and $-D$ (which would make a rectangle), and line $AB$ bisects angle $DAD'$, so it points along $D+D'$. Discarding the two choices that make $AB$ horizontal or vertical leaves eight slopes, $-3,-\\tfrac43,-1,-\\tfrac12,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac34,1,2$, whose absolute values add to $\\tfrac{119}{12}$, so $m+n=131$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-ii-12": ["Let $D$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $O$ to the plane of $ABC$. The right triangles $ODA$, $ODB$, $ODC$ share the leg $OD$ and have hypotenuse $20$, so the Pythagorean theorem gives $DA=DB=DC$: $D$ is the circumcenter of triangle $ABC$. The $13$-$14$-$15$ triangle has area $84$, so its circumradius is $R=\\tfrac{abc}{4K}=\\tfrac{2730}{336}=\\tfrac{65}8$. Then $OD=\\sqrt{20^2-\\left(\\tfrac{65}8\\right)^2}=\\tfrac{\\sqrt{21375}}8=\\tfrac{15\\sqrt{95}}8$, and the answer is $15+95+8=118$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-ii-13": ["Group the terms as $2\\left[(10x^2)^3-1\\right]+x\\left[(10x^2)^2+10x^2+1\\right]$. The difference of cubes factors the first bracket and shares the second as a factor, so the polynomial is $\\left(20x^2+x-2\\right)\\left(100x^4+10x^2+1\\right)$. The quartic is always positive, so the real roots come from $20x^2+x-2=0$: $x=\\tfrac{-1\\pm\\sqrt{161}}{40}$. The one in the stated form has $m=-1$, $n=161$, $r=40$, and $m+n+r=200$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-ii-14": ["Since $k\\cdot k!=(k+1)!-k!$, a difference of factorials telescopes: $n!-m!=\\sum_{k=m}^{n-1}k\\cdot k!$. Group the sum as $16!+(48!-32!)+(80!-64!)+\\cdots+(2000!-1984!)$, where the $62$ brackets are $(32j+16)!-(32j)!=\\sum_{k=32j}^{32j+15}k\\cdot k!$. That is already a factorial-base expansion: $f_{16}=1$, $f_k=k$ for $32j\\le k\\le32j+15$ ($j=1,\\ldots,62$), and $0$ elsewhere. In the alternating sum, $f_{16}$ gives $-1$, and each block starts at an even $k$, so it pairs as $(-k)+(k+1)$ eight times, giving $8$. The total is $62\\cdot8-1=495$.","",[]],
"2000-aime-ii-15": ["The sine subtraction formula gives $\\sin(n+1)\\cos n - \\sin n\\cos(n+1) = \\sin 1^\\circ$, so each term equals $\\frac{\\cot n - \\cot(n+1)}{\\sin 1^\\circ}$. The sum takes only odd $n$ from $45$ to $133$, so it does not telescope outright: it is $\\tfrac1{\\sin1^\\circ}$ times $(\\cot45^\\circ+\\cot47^\\circ+\\cdots+\\cot133^\\circ)-(\\cot46^\\circ+\\cot48^\\circ+\\cdots+\\cot134^\\circ)$. Since $\\cot(180^\\circ-k)=-\\cot k$, the odd angles $47^\\circ,\\ldots,133^\\circ$ cancel in pairs adding to $180^\\circ$, leaving $\\cot45^\\circ=1$, and the even angles cancel the same way around $\\cot90^\\circ=0$. So the sum is $\\frac1{\\sin 1^\\circ}$, giving $n = 1$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-i-1": ["Place value writes the number as $10a+b$. Divisibility by $b$ forces $b \\mid 10a$ and divisibility by $a$ forces $a \\mid b$, so $b=ka$ with $ka\\mid10a$, that is $k\\mid10$; as $b$ is a digit, $k$ is $1$, $2$ or $5$. Then $b=a$ gives $11,22,\\ldots,99$ (sum $495$), $b=2a$ gives $12,24,36,48$ (sum $120$) and $b=5a$ gives $15$, so the total is $630$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-i-2": ["Let $\\mathcal S$ have $n$ elements and mean $\\mu$. Adjoining $1$ gives $\\frac{n\\mu+1}{n+1}=\\mu-13$, that is $\\mu-1=13(n+1)$, and adjoining $2001$ gives $2001-\\mu=27(n+1)$: each new element pulls the mean toward it by its distance divided by $n+1$. Adding the two, $2000=40(n+1)$, so $n=49$ and $\\mu=1+13\\cdot50=651$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-i-3": ["The binomial theorem gives $\\left(\\tfrac12-x\\right)^{2001}=-x^{2001}+\\tfrac{2001}2x^{2000}-\\binom{2001}2\\tfrac14x^{1999}+\\cdots$, so the $x^{2001}$ terms cancel and the equation has degree $2000$, with leading coefficient $\\tfrac{2001}2$ and next coefficient $-\\tfrac{2001\\cdot2000}8$. Vieta's makes the sum of the roots $\\tfrac{2001\\cdot2000/8}{2001/2}=\\tfrac{2000}4=500$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-i-4": ["The angle at $C$ is $75^\\circ$, and in triangle $ATC$ the angle at $A$ is $30^\\circ$, so $\\angle ATC=75^\\circ$ too: the triangle is isosceles and $AC=AT=24$. The Law of Sines in $\\triangle ABC$ gives $BC=\\tfrac{24\\sin60^\\circ}{\\sin45^\\circ}=12\\sqrt6$. The sine area formula on $AC$, $BC$ and the angle $C$ gives $\\tfrac12\\cdot24\\cdot12\\sqrt6\\cdot\\tfrac{\\sqrt6+\\sqrt2}4=216+72\\sqrt3$, so $a+b+c=291$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-i-5": ["The vertex $(0,1)$ is at the top of the altitude on the $y$-axis, and the apex angle is $60^\\circ$, so each side through it makes $30^\\circ$ with the axis: slope $\\pm\\sqrt3$. The right side is $y=1-\\sqrt3x$; substituting into $x^2+4y^2=4$ gives $13x^2-8\\sqrt3x=0$, so the lower vertex has $x=\\tfrac{8\\sqrt3}{13}$. The base, mirrored across the axis, has length $2x=\\tfrac{16\\sqrt3}{13}$, its square is $\\tfrac{768}{169}$, and $m+n=937$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-i-6": ["A nondecreasing sequence of four rolls is determined by how many of each face value appear, so these sequences match the multisets of size four from six values. Stars and bars counts those as $\\binom{6+4-1}4=\\binom94=126$, over the $6^4=1296$ equally likely outcomes, so the probability is $\\tfrac{126}{1296}=\\tfrac7{72}$ and $m+n=79$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-i-7": ["Since $DE \\parallel BC$, chasing alternate angles gives $\\angle BID = \\angle CBI = \\angle DBI$, so $\\triangle BDI$ is isosceles and $DB = DI$; likewise $EC = EI$. Hence $DE = DB + EC$, and the perimeter of $\\triangle ADE$ is exactly $AB + AC = 43$. Since $\\triangle ADE \\sim \\triangle ABC$, the perimeter ratio $\\frac{43}{63}$ is the similarity ratio, so $DE = \\frac{43}{63} \\cdot 20=\\frac{860}{63}$ and $m+n=860+63=923$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-i-8": ["With base-seven digits $a_i$, the condition $\\sum a_i10^i=2\\sum a_i7^i$ is $\\sum a_i(10^i-2\\cdot7^i)=0$, with coefficients $-1,-4,2,314,\\ldots$ for $i=0,1,2,3$. A fourth digit would contribute at least $314$, while the negative terms total at most $6\\cdot5=30$, so $N$ has three digits $\\overline{abc}_7$ with $2a=4b+c$. To make $N$ largest take $a=6$: then $4b+c=12$ with $c\\le6$, and the largest $b$ is $3$ with $c=0$. So $N=630_7=6\\cdot49+3\\cdot7=315$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-i-9": ["Triangles sharing an angle at a vertex have areas in the ratio of the products of the two adjacent sides, so $\\tfrac{[ADF]}{[ABC]}=p(1-r)$, $\\tfrac{[BED]}{[ABC]}=q(1-p)$ and $\\tfrac{[CFE]}{[ABC]}=r(1-q)$. Subtracting the three corners leaves $\\tfrac{[DEF]}{[ABC]}=1-(p+q+r)+(pq+qr+rp)$. The square of the sum gives $pq+qr+rp=\\tfrac12\\left(\\tfrac49-\\tfrac25\\right)=\\tfrac1{45}$, so the ratio is $1-\\tfrac23+\\tfrac1{45}=\\tfrac{16}{45}$ and $m+n=61$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-i-10": ["A midpoint has integer coordinates exactly when the two endpoints agree in parity in each coordinate separately. Counting ordered pairs of residues that match modulo $2$ gives $5$ choices for the $x$ pair, $8$ for $y$ and $13$ for $z$, so $5 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 13$ pairs total; removing the $60$ identical pairs and halving for order leaves $\\frac{520-60}2=230$, and dividing by $\\binom{60}2=1770$ gives $\\frac{23}{177}$, so $m+n=200$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-i-11": ["With $P_i$ in column $c_i$, the two numberings are $x_i = (i-1)N + c_i$ and $y_i = 5(c_i-1) + i$. The first two equalities give $c_1=5c_2-3$ and $N+c_2=5c_1-4$, so $N=24c_2-19$. Eliminating through the other three gives $c_3=\\tfrac{702c_2-543}{31}$, $c_4=\\tfrac{438c_2-338}{31}$ and $c_5=\\tfrac{534c_2-421}{31}$. These are integers exactly when $20c_2\\equiv16\\pmod{31}$, that is, $5c_2\\equiv4$, and multiplying by the inverse $5^{-1}\\equiv25$ gives $c_2\\equiv7\\pmod{31}$. The least choice $c_2=7$ gives $N=149$ with columns $c=(32,7,141,88,107)$, all between $1$ and $N$, so the smallest $N$ is $149$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-i-12": ["The inradius of a tetrahedron is $r=\\tfrac{3V}S$, its volume over its total face area. Here $V=\\tfrac16\\cdot6\\cdot4\\cdot2=8$. The three faces at $D$ are right triangles of areas $12$, $6$ and $4$, and the face $ABC$ has area $\\tfrac12\\left|(-6,4,0)\\times(-6,0,2)\\right|=\\tfrac12|(8,12,24)|=14$. So $S=36$, $r=\\tfrac{24}{36}=\\tfrac23$, and $m+n=5$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-i-13": ["Let $a=22$, $b$ and $c$ be the chords of $d$, $2d$ and $3d$ degrees. Three consecutive $d$-arcs and the chord across all three form an isosceles trapezoid in the circle with sides $a$, $a$, $a$, $c$ and diagonals $b$ (each spans two $d$-arcs). Ptolemy's Theorem gives $b^2=a^2+ac$, and with $b=c+20$ this is $(c+20)^2=484+22c$, that is $c^2+18c-84=0$. The positive root is $c=-9+\\sqrt{165}$, so $m+n=174$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-i-14": ["Write a delivery as a string of $1$s and $0$s with no two consecutive $1$s and no three consecutive $0$s. Classify by how the string ends: it can close with $00$, $01$ or $10$, and each of those extends in a determined way, giving a recursion on the count of $n$-digit strings by ending type. With $x_n$, $y_n$, $z_n$ counting the strings that end in $00$, $01$, $10$: $x_n=z_{n-1}$ (a $0$ after $10$), $y_n=x_{n-1}+z_{n-1}$ (a $1$ after a $0$) and $z_n=y_{n-1}$ (a $0$ after $01$), starting from $(1,1,1)$ at $n=2$. At $n=19$ this gives $(86,151,114)$, so there are $351$ patterns.","",[]],
"2001-aime-i-15": ["Think of the faces as the vertices of a cube, adjacent faces being cube edges. Fix $1$ on one vertex; the other $7$ labels are placed in $7!=5040$ equally likely ways. Its three neighbours avoid $2$ and $8$, so casework on the label opposite $1$: placing the remaining labels so that no two consecutive ones touch, $2$ (or $8$) opposite gives $18$ arrangements each; $5$ opposite gives $12$; $4$ or $6$ opposite gives $6$ each; and $3$ or $7$ opposite gives none. That is $18+18+12+6+6=60$ arrangements, so the probability is $\\tfrac{60}{5040}=\\tfrac1{84}$ and $m+n=85$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-ii-1": ["The two-digit squares are $16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81$, and each digit of $N$ after the first is forced by the pair it completes. Casework on the starting square and follow each chain until no square begins with the current last digit: the chains are $1649$, $25$, $3649$, $49$, $649$ and $81649$, so the longest is $81649$ and its leading three digits are $816$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-ii-2": ["Inclusion-exclusion gives $|S| + |F| - |S \\cap F| = 2001$, so the overlap is $|S|+|F|-2001$. The percentage bands, rounded inward with ceilings and floors, give $1601 \\le |S| \\le 1700$ and $601 \\le |F| \\le 800$. So $m=1601+601-2001=201$ and $M=1700+800-2001=499$, and $M-m=298$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-ii-3": ["Adding the recurrence at $n$ and at $n-1$ telescopes almost everything away and leaves $x_n = -x_{n-5}$, so applying it twice gives $x_n = x_{n-10}$ and the sequence has period $10$. The fifth term is $x_5=523-420+375-211=267$. Since $531$, $753$ and $975$ are $1$, $3$ and $5$ modulo $10$, the sum is $x_1+x_3+x_5=211+420+267=898$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-ii-4": ["Bash it in coordinates: parameterize the two lines as $P = (8a, 15a)$ and $Q = (10b, 3b)$. The midpoint conditions give $8a+10b = 16$ and $15a+3b = 12$, which solve to $a = \\tfrac47$ and $b = \\tfrac87$. So $P=\\left(\\tfrac{32}7,\\tfrac{60}7\\right)$ and $Q=\\left(\\tfrac{80}7,\\tfrac{24}7\\right)$, and the distance formula gives $PQ=\\tfrac17\\sqrt{48^2+36^2}=\\tfrac{60}7$, so $m+n=67$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-ii-5": ["A ten-element set fails the triangle property exactly when no three of its elements satisfy the triangle inequality, and for elements in increasing order that means each one is at least the sum of the two before it. Starting from $4$ and $5$, the smallest such set is the Fibonacci-like sequence $4,5,9,14,23,37,60,97,157,254$, and any bad ten-element set has a largest element at least $254$. So every ten-element subset of $\\{4,\\dots,n\\}$ has the property exactly when $n\\le253$, and the largest $n$ is $253$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-ii-6": ["Give $ABCD$ side $1$ and let $s$ be the side of $EFGH$, which sits outside $ABCD$ on the far side of $CD$. The chord through $F$ perpendicular to $CD$ is $FG$ extended; that chord is symmetric about the line through the center parallel to $CD$, so its far end is $s$ beyond $AB$, and from $F$ it runs $s$ one way and $1+s$ the other. The chord $CD$ is cut by $F$ into $\\tfrac{1-s}2$ and $\\tfrac{1+s}2$. The power of $F$ gives $s(1+s)=\\tfrac{(1-s)(1+s)}4$, so $4s=1-s$ and $s=\\tfrac15$. The ratio of areas is $\\tfrac1{25}$, and $10n+m=251$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-ii-7": ["Put the right angle at $P=(0,0)$ with $R=(120,0)$ and $Q=(0,90)$. The inradius of $PQR$ is $\\tfrac{90+120-150}2=30$, so $C_1$ is centered at $(30,30)$ and the two tangents are the lines $x=60$ and $y=60$. Triangle $RST$ is the original scaled by $\\tfrac{RS}{RP}=\\tfrac{60}{120}=\\tfrac12$, so its inradius is $15$ and, with its right angle at $S=(60,0)$, its incenter is $(75,15)$. Triangle $QUV$ is scaled by $\\tfrac{QU}{QP}=\\tfrac{30}{90}=\\tfrac13$, so its inradius is $10$ and its incenter, from the right angle at $U=(0,60)$, is $(10,70)$. The distance is $\\sqrt{65^2+55^2}=\\sqrt{7250}=\\sqrt{10\\cdot725}$, so $n=725$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-ii-8": ["Iterating the substitution $f(3x) = 3f(x)$ gives $f(x) = 3^kf\\left(\\frac x{3^k}\\right)$, and $k = 6$ brings $\\frac{2001}{3^6}$ into the interval where $f$ is given explicitly, yielding $f(2001) = 186$. Indeed $f(2001)=729\\left(1-\\left|\\tfrac{2001}{729}-2\\right|\\right)=729-543=186$. On $[3^k,3^{k+1}]$ the same scaling gives $f(x)=3^k-|x-2\\cdot3^k|$, a tent of height $3^k$, so the first interval whose tent reaches $186$ is $[243,729]$, of height $243$. There $243-|x-486|=186$ has the smaller root $x=486-57=429$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-ii-9": ["Count the complement: colorings containing at least one red $2 \\times 2$ block. There are four such blocks, each leaving $2^5$ free squares ($128$ in all). Two adjacent blocks cover $6$ squares ($4$ pairs, $2^3$ each) and two opposite corners cover $7$ ($2$ pairs, $2^2$ each), so the pairs give $40$; any three cover $8$ squares ($4\\cdot2=8$), and all four cover the grid ($1$). So $128-40+8-1=95$ colorings are bad, the good probability is $\\tfrac{512-95}{512}=\\tfrac{417}{512}$, and $m+n=929$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-ii-10": ["Since $10^j - 10^i = 10^i(10^{j-i}-1)$ and $1001 = 7 \\cdot 11 \\cdot 13$ is coprime to $10$, the condition is $1001 \\mid 10^{j-i}-1$. Powers of ten cycle mod $1001$ with period $6$, so $6 \\mid j-i$. A difference $j-i=6k$ occurs for $100-6k$ pairs, $k=1,\\ldots,16$, so the count is the arithmetic series $94+88+\\cdots+4=784$.","",["Rewrite $10^j - 10^i$ so that the factor $1001$ is isolated, and factor $1001$.","Find how often the powers of $10$ repeat modulo $1001$; that period decides which pairs $(i, j)$ work."]],
"2001-aime-ii-11": ["Wins and losses are interchangeable, so by symmetry the probability of more wins than losses equals that of more losses than wins. The complement of those two cases is an equal split, so the answer is half of one minus that probability. Counting equal splits by the number of ties $t$ with multinomial coefficients gives $\\tfrac{6!}{3!3!}+\\tfrac{6!}{2!2!2!}+\\tfrac{6!}{4!1!1!}+1=20+90+30+1=141$ of $3^6=729$ outcomes, so the answer is $\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac{588}{729}=\\tfrac{98}{243}$ and $m+n=341$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-ii-12": ["Each new tetrahedron has half the edge of the face it sits on, so by volume scaling it has $\\tfrac18$ the volume of a tetrahedron on that face's full edge. At the first step the four faces of $\\mathcal P_0$ get tetrahedra of volume $\\tfrac18$ each, adding $\\tfrac12$. Each step replaces every face by six faces of half its size (three corner triangles and three faces of the new tetrahedron), so the number of faces is multiplied by $6$ while each new tetrahedron's volume is divided by $8$: the added volume is multiplied by $\\tfrac68=\\tfrac34$ each time. So $\\mathcal P_3$ has volume $1+\\tfrac12+\\tfrac38+\\tfrac9{32}=\\tfrac{69}{32}$, and $m+n=101$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-ii-13": ["Extend $CB$ and $DA$ to meet at $E$. Then $\\angle DCE=\\angle BCD=\\angle ABD$ and $\\angle CDE=\\angle ADC=\\angle BAD$, so $\\triangle ABD\\sim\\triangle DCE$, matching $A$ with $D$, $B$ with $C$ and $D$ with $E$. That similarity also gives $\\angle DEC=\\angle ADB=\\angle BDE$, so $\\triangle BDE$ is isosceles with $BE=BD=10$, and $CE=6+10=16$. The ratio $\\tfrac{AB}{DC}=\\tfrac{BD}{CE}$ then gives $\\tfrac8{CD}=\\tfrac{10}{16}$, so $CD=\\tfrac{64}5$ and $m+n=69$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-ii-14": ["On the unit circle $\\bar z=\\tfrac1z$, so conjugating $z^{28}-z^8-1=0$ gives $z^{-28}-z^{-8}-1=0$, that is, $z^{28}=1-z^{20}$. With $z^{28}=z^8+1$ this gives $z^{20}=-z^8$, so $z^{12}=-1$, $z^{24}=1$, and $z^{28}=z^4$. The equation becomes $z^4=z^8+1$, so $w=z^4$ solves $w^2-w+1=0$ and $w=\\operatorname{cis}(\\pm60^\\circ)$, which also satisfies $z^{12}=w^3=-1$. By De Moivre, $z=\\operatorname{cis}\\theta$ with $4\\theta\\equiv\\pm60^\\circ\\pmod{360^\\circ}$: $\\theta=15^\\circ,75^\\circ,105^\\circ,165^\\circ,195^\\circ,255^\\circ,285^\\circ,345^\\circ$, so $2n=8$, and the even-indexed angles add to $75+165+255+345=840$.","",[]],
"2001-aime-ii-15": ["Put $E$ at the origin with $F$, $H$, $C$ along the axes, so $A=(8,8,8)$ and $I$, $J$, $K$ are $(2,0,0)$, $(0,2,0)$, $(0,0,2)$. The tunnel is the triangular prism of lines through triangle $IJK$ in the direction $(1,1,1)$. It removes a right triangle of legs $2$ and area $2$ from each of the three faces at $E$, and leaves the cube through the three faces at $A$; on the face $x=8$, for example, the lines through $I$, $J$, $K$ arrive at $(8,6,6)$, $(8,10,8)$, $(8,8,10)$, and the part of that triangle inside the face is a quadrilateral of area $2$. So the outer surface is $384-12=372$. Each wall is perpendicular to its edge, which has length $2\\sqrt2$; along the wall the lines run between $6\\sqrt3$ and $7\\sqrt3$ long, so the wall is two trapezoids of width $\\sqrt2$ with parallel sides $6\\sqrt3$ and $7\\sqrt3$, of total area $13\\sqrt6$. The surface area is $372+39\\sqrt6$, so $m+n+p=417$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-i-1": ["Each half is a palindrome when its last symbol copies its first: the letters with probability $\\tfrac1{26}$, the digits with probability $\\tfrac1{10}$, independently. Inclusion-exclusion over at least one gives $\\tfrac1{26}+\\tfrac1{10}-\\tfrac1{260}=\\tfrac{35}{260}=\\tfrac7{52}$, so $m+n=59$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-i-2": ["Let the radius be $r$. The rows hold $7$, $6$ and $7$ circles, so the long side is seven diameters, $14r$. The centers of touching circles in neighboring rows form equilateral triangles of side $2r$, whose height $\\sqrt3\\,r$ is the vertical step between rows, so the short side is $2r+2\\sqrt3\\,r=2r(\\sqrt3+1)$. The ratio is $\\tfrac{7}{\\sqrt3+1}$, and rationalizing the denominator gives $\\tfrac{7(\\sqrt3-1)}2=\\tfrac12\\left(\\sqrt{147}-7\\right)$, so $p+q=154$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-i-3": ["Place value makes the reversed pair: in $n$ years Jane is $10a+b$ and Dick is $10b+a$, with digits $a, b$ from $1$ to $9$ (both ages have two digits), and Dick being older forces $b \\gt a$. Each such pair fixes $n=10a+b-25$ and $d=10b+a-n$, so count pairs: $\\binom92 = 36$. Casework removes those where Jane's future age is not above $25$, since $n$ is positive: with $a=1$ all $8$ pairs ($12$ to $19$) fail, and with $a=2$ the pairs $23$, $24$, $25$ fail, $3$ more. That leaves $36-8-3=25$ ordered pairs $(d,n)$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-i-4": ["Partial fractions give $\\frac{1}{k^2+k} = \\frac1k - \\frac1{k+1}$, so the block sum telescopes to $\\frac1m - \\frac1n = \\frac{n-m}{mn} = \\frac{1}{29}$. Clearing gives $29n - 29m - mn = 0$, and adding $29^2$ factors it as $(29-m)(29+n) = 29^2$ by Simon's trick. Since $m \\lt n$, the only split is $29-m = 1$ and $29+n = 841$, so $m+n = 840$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-i-5": ["Each of the $\\binom{12}2 = 66$ vertex pairs, a combination count, determines three squares, two using the pair as a side and one as a diagonal, giving $198$ before correction. But the three squares whose four vertices all lie on the dodecagon get counted once for each of their four sides and two diagonals, six times each, so subtract the overcount to get $198 - 3 \\cdot 5 = 183$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-i-6": ["Set $A=\\log_{225}x$ and $B=\\log_{64}y$; change of base turns the second equation into $\\tfrac1A-\\tfrac1B=1$ while the first is $A+B=4$. Solving gives $(A,B)=(3\\mp\\sqrt5,1\\pm\\sqrt5)$, so the two $A$ values sum to $6$ and the two $B$ values to $2$. Hence $x_1x_2=225^6$ and $y_1y_2=64^2=2^{12}$, and $x_1y_1x_2y_2=15^{12}\\cdot2^{12}=30^{12}$, whose logarithm is $12$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-i-7": ["The generalized binomial expansion of $\\left(10^{2002}+1\\right)^{10/7}$ starts $10^{2860}+\\tfrac{10}7\\cdot10^{858}+\\tfrac{15}{49}\\cdot10^{-1144}+\\cdots$, since $2002\\cdot\\tfrac37=858$, and everything after the second term is far too small to reach the first three decimal places. So the digits are those of the fractional part of $\\tfrac{10^{859}}7$. Fermat's little theorem gives $10^6\\equiv1\\pmod7$, so $10^{859}\\equiv10\\equiv3$, the fractional part is $\\tfrac37=0.428\\ldots$, and the digits are $428$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-i-8": ["Unrolling the Fibonacci-type recurrence expresses $a_9=13a_1+21a_2=k$, with $1\\le a_1\\le a_2$. Since $\\gcd(13,21)=1$, the solutions of $13a_1+21a_2=k$ differ by shifts $a_1\\to a_1+21$, $a_2\\to a_2-13$, so two valid sequences need some solution with $a_1+21\\le a_2-13$, that is $a_2\\ge a_1+34$. The smallest such $k$ takes $a_1=1$, $a_2=35$, giving $k=13+735=748$ (its other sequence starts $22,22$).","",[]],
"2002-aime-i-9": ["The three painters cover the pickets $\\equiv1 \\pmod h$, $\\equiv2\\pmod t$ and $\\equiv3\\pmod u$, which must partition the pickets. No step is $1$, and $h\\ne2$, since Harold would paint picket $3$. Picket $4$ then belongs to Harold ($h=3$) or Tanya ($t=2$). If $h=3$, Harold has $1,4,7,\\ldots$; picket $5$ cannot be Ulysses's ($u=2$ would give him $7$ too), so $t=3$ and Ulysses takes the rest, $u=3$. If $t=2$, Tanya has every even picket and picket $5$ must be Harold's ($u=2$ would leave Harold only picket $1$), so $h=4$ and Ulysses takes $3,7,11,\\ldots$, $u=4$. So the triples are $(h,t,u)=(3,3,3)$ and $(4,2,4)$, and the sum is $333+424=757$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-i-10": ["Here $AB=12$ and $AC=37$, so the right angle at $B$ gives $BC=\\sqrt{37^2-12^2}=35$ and $[ABC]=210$. The Angle Bisector Theorem gives $BD:DC=12:37$, so $BD=\\tfrac{60}7$, $[ABD]=\\tfrac{360}7$ and $[ADC]=210-\\tfrac{360}7=\\tfrac{1110}7$. In the diagram $G$ is where $AD$ meets $EF$. Triangle $AEF$ shares the angle at $A$ with $ABC$, so $[AEF]=\\tfrac{3\\cdot10}{12\\cdot37}\\cdot210=\\tfrac{525}{37}$. In it $AG$ bisects the angle at $A$, so $EG:GF=3:10$ and the cevian splits the area the same way: $[AGF]=\\tfrac{10}{13}\\cdot\\tfrac{525}{37}=\\tfrac{5250}{481}$. The quadrilateral is $[ADC]-[AGF]=\\tfrac{1110}7-\\tfrac{5250}{481}\\approx147.66$, so the answer is $148$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-i-11": ["Reflect the cube across each face the beam hits instead of reflecting the beam. In the tiling of space by copies of the cube the path becomes one straight line from $A=(0,0,0)$ through $P=(12,7,5)$, and the beam is at a vertex exactly when it reaches a point whose three coordinates are all multiples of $12$. The point $t(12,7,5)$ first has that property at $t=12$, since $7$ and $5$ are prime to $12$. The distance formula gives the length $12\\sqrt{12^2+7^2+5^2}=12\\sqrt{218}$, so $m+n=230$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-i-12": ["Compose the map with itself: $F(F(z)) = \\frac{z+1}{z-1}i$, and one more step returns $F(F(F(z))) = z$, so the iteration cycles with period $3$. Since $2002 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$, $z_{2002} = F(z_0)$, and one application gives $z_{2002}=\\frac{\\frac1{137}+2i}{\\frac1{137}}=1+274i$, so $a+b=275$.","",["Apply the map twice, then three times, and see what happens.","Use the pattern to reduce the number of applications to a small remainder."]],
"2002-aime-i-13": ["With $a=BC$, $b=CA$ and $c=AB=24$, the median-length formula gives $4\\cdot27^2=2a^2+2b^2-24^2$ and $4\\cdot18^2=2b^2+2c^2-a^2$, so $a^2+b^2=1746$ and $2b^2-a^2=144$: $b^2=630$ and $a^2=1116$. The power of the point $E$, the midpoint of $AB$, gives $CE\\cdot EF=AE\\cdot EB=144$, so $EF=\\tfrac{16}3$. In triangle $CEB$ the Law of Cosines gives $1116=27^2+12^2-2\\cdot27\\cdot12\\cos\\angle CEB$, so $\\cos\\angle CEB=-\\tfrac38$ and $\\sin\\angle CEB=\\tfrac{\\sqrt{55}}8$. The height from $F$ to $AB$ is $EF\\sin\\angle CEB$, so $[AFB]=\\tfrac12\\cdot24\\cdot\\tfrac{16}3\\cdot\\tfrac{\\sqrt{55}}8=8\\sqrt{55}$, and $m+n=63$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-i-14": ["If the set has $n+1$ elements summing to $N$, deleting $x$ leaves a mean requiring $n\\mid N-x$, so by the congruence rules every element is congruent to $N$ modulo $n$. Since $1$ and $2002$ are in the set, all elements are $\\equiv1\\pmod n$ and $n\\mid2001=3\\cdot23\\cdot29$. The $n+1$ elements are then at least $1,n+1,\\ldots,n\\cdot n+1$, so $n^2+1\\le2002$ and $n\\le44$. The largest divisor of $2001$ allowed is $n=29$, and $2001=29\\cdot69$ leaves room for $30$ elements $\\equiv1\\pmod{29}$ from $1$ to $2002$, so the greatest size is $30$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-i-15": ["Put the square at height $0$ with $A=(-6,-6,0)$, $B=(6,-6,0)$, $C=(6,6,0)$, $D=(-6,6,0)$. The solid is symmetric across the plane $x=0$, so $E=(0,e,12)$, and $CE=14$ gives $36+(e-6)^2+144=196$, so $e=2$ or $10$. The distance to the face, not just its plane, is $12$, so $E$ stands over the square and $e=2$. Face $ADEG$ is flat and contains the edge $AD$, so its plane is $z=2(x+6)$, and with $GF=6$ centered that puts $G=(-3,g,6)$. Then $AG=8$ gives $9+(g+6)^2+36=64$, so $g=-6+\\sqrt{19}$ (the other root puts $G$ in front of the square), and the distance formula gives $EG^2=9+(8-\\sqrt{19})^2+36=128-16\\sqrt{19}$. So $p+q+r=163$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-ii-1": ["Place value gives $x = 100a+10b+c$ and $y = 100c+10b+a$, so the middle digit cancels and $z = |99a - 99c| = 99|a-c|$. Both $a$ and $c$ are nonzero digits, so $|a-c|$ ranges over $0$ through $8$, giving $9$ distinct values of $z$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-ii-2": ["The distance formula gives $PQ^2=1+16+81=98$, $PR^2=16+81+1=98$ and $QR^2=9+25+64=98$, so the three vertices form an equilateral triangle. Two vertices of a cube are an edge, a face diagonal or a space diagonal apart, and three vertices cannot be pairwise an edge apart or pairwise a space diagonal apart, so these three are pairwise a face diagonal apart. The face diagonal is $s\\sqrt2=\\sqrt{98}$, so the edge is $s=7$ and the surface area is $6s^2=294$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-ii-3": ["The product rule collapses the three logarithms to $abc = 6^6$. In a geometric sequence the middle term is the geometric mean, so $b = \\sqrt[3]{abc} = 36$. Then $c=\\tfrac{1296}a$ must be a whole number above $36$ and $36-a$ a perfect square, so $a$ is $35$, $32$, $27$, $20$ or $11$; of these only $27$ divides $1296$, giving $c = 48$ and the sum $111$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-ii-4": ["The blocks inside the path form a hexagonal patch: one center block and rings of $6$, $12$, $\\dots$, $6(n-2)$ blocks. For $n=202$ that is $1+6(1+2+\\cdots+200)=1+6\\cdot\\tfrac{200\\cdot201}2=120601$ blocks. Each unit regular hexagon has area $\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}2=3\\cdot\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$, so $m=3\\cdot120601=361803$, and the remainder is $803$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-ii-5": ["Compare prime exponents on both sides. With $a = 2^n3^m$, the condition $a^6 \\mid 6^a$ means $6n \\le a$ and $6m \\le a$, so $a$ fails when $6n \\gt a$ or $6m \\gt a$. If $n,m\\ge1$ it never fails, since $6n\\le3\\cdot2^n$ and $6m\\le2\\cdot3^m$ for every $n,m\\ge1$. If $m=0$, $a=2^n$ fails exactly when $6n\\gt2^n$, that is, $n=1,2,3,4$ ($a=2,4,8,16$; at $n=5$, $30\\lt32$). If $n=0$, $a=3^m$ fails exactly when $6m\\gt3^m$, that is, $m=1,2$ ($a=3,9$). The sum is $2+4+8+16+3+9=42$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-ii-6": ["Partial fractions split each term as $\\frac1{n^2-4} = \\frac14\\left(\\frac1{n-2} - \\frac1{n+2}\\right)$, so the sum telescopes with an offset of four. The positive parts run over denominators $1$ through $9998$ and the negative over $5$ through $10002$, leaving only $\\frac14\\left(1+\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac14\\right)$ minus four vanishing tail terms. The main part is $250\\cdot\\tfrac{25}{12}=520.83\\ldots$, and the tail $250\\left(\\tfrac1{9999}+\\tfrac1{10000}+\\tfrac1{10001}+\\tfrac1{10002}\\right)\\approx0.1$ brings it to $520.73\\ldots$, so the closest integer is $521$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-ii-7": ["The sum-of-squares formula makes the condition $1200=2^4\\cdot3\\cdot5^2$ dividing $k(k+1)(2k+1)$. The factor $3$ always divides it. Since $2k+1$ is odd and the three factors are pairwise coprime, $16$ must divide $k$ or $k+1$, so $k\\equiv0$ or $15\\pmod{16}$, and $25$ must divide one factor, so $k\\equiv0$, $24$ or $12\\pmod{25}$. Combining these by the Chinese remainder theorem, the smallest positive solution is $k=112$ ($112=7\\cdot16$ and $2\\cdot112+1=225$).","",[]],
"2002-aime-ii-8": ["Consecutive values $\\frac{2002}n$ and $\\frac{2002}{n+1}$ differ by $\\frac{2002}{n(n+1)}$, which is less than $1$ once $n(n+1)\\gt2002$, that is from $n=45$ on ($44\\cdot45=1980$, $45\\cdot46=2070$). So the floors for $n\\ge45$ take every value from $\\lfloor\\frac{2002}{45}\\rfloor=44$ down to $0$, and a missing $k$ must be at least $45$. For $n=44,43,42,41,40$ the floors are $45,46,47,48,50$, so $49$ is skipped, and it is the least $k$ with no solution.","",[]],
"2002-aime-ii-9": ["Build each assignment element by element: each lands in $A$, in $B$, or in neither, so there are $3^{10}$ assignments. Inclusion-exclusion removes those leaving $A$ or $B$ empty, subtracting $2^{10}$ twice and adding back the single all-neither case. That leaves $3^{10}-2\\cdot2^{10}+1=57002$ ordered pairs, and since the pair is unordered, $n=28501$, whose remainder is $501$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-ii-10": ["The angle $x$ degrees is $\\tfrac{\\pi x}{180}$ radians, and two sines are equal exactly when the angles differ by a multiple of $2\\pi$ or are supplementary up to a multiple of $2\\pi$, since $\\sin(\\pi-\\theta)=\\sin\\theta$. The first family, $x=\\tfrac{\\pi x}{180}+2\\pi k$, gives $x=\\tfrac{360\\pi k}{180-\\pi}$, smallest at $k=1$, about $6.39$. The second, $x=\\pi-\\tfrac{\\pi x}{180}+2\\pi k$, gives $x=\\tfrac{180(2k+1)\\pi}{180+\\pi}$, about $3.08$ at $k=0$ and $9.26$ at $k=1$. So the two least values are $\\tfrac{180\\pi}{180+\\pi}$ and $\\tfrac{360\\pi}{180-\\pi}$, and $m+n+p+q=360+180+180+180=900$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-ii-11": ["Each infinite geometric series sums to $\\tfrac a{1-r}=1$, so $a=1-r$, its second term is $r(1-r)$ and its third is $r^2(1-r)$. For the series with third term $\\tfrac18$, $8r^3-8r^2+1=0$; scanning the candidates $\\pm\\tfrac pq$ with $p\\mid1$, $q\\mid8$ finds $r=\\tfrac12$, and dividing it out leaves $4r^2-2r-1=0$, so $r=\\tfrac{1\\pm\\sqrt5}4$. Since $r(1-r)$ is unchanged under $r\\mapsto1-r$, the other series has ratio $1-r$, and the two are distinct only if $r\\ne\\tfrac12$. Using $4r^2=2r+1$, the second term is $r-r^2=\\tfrac{2r-1}4$, which for $r=\\tfrac{1+\\sqrt5}4$ is $\\tfrac{\\sqrt5-1}8$ (the negative root makes the other ratio $1-r$ greater than $1$, so that series would not converge). So $100m+10n+p=500+10+8=518$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-ii-12": ["Encode the ten shots as a lattice path, a make being a step up and a miss a step right, so the sequences are in bijection with paths from $(0,0)$ to $(6,4)$. The condition $a_n \\le 0.4$ throughout says the path never rises above the line $y = \\frac{2x}3$. Counting those constrained paths by a Pascal-style recursion gives $23$, so the probability is $23\\cdot0.4^4\\cdot0.6^6=\\tfrac{2^4\\cdot3^6\\cdot23}{5^{10}}$, and $(2+3+23+5)(4+6+10)=33\\cdot20=660$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-ii-13": ["Extend $CP$ to meet $AB$ at $X$. Since $PQ\\parallel CA$ and $PR\\parallel CB$, the scaling about $X$ that takes $C$ to $P$ takes $A$ to $Q$ and $B$ to $R$, so $\\triangle PQR$ is $\\triangle CAB$ scaled by $\\tfrac{XP}{XC}$ and the area ratio is the square of that. Mass points from $CD:DB=2:5$ and $CE:EA=1:3$ put masses $2$ at $B$, $5$ at $C$ and $\\tfrac53$ at $A$, so $\\tfrac{CP}{PX}=\\tfrac{5/3+2}5=\\tfrac{11}{15}$ and $\\tfrac{XP}{XC}=\\tfrac{15}{26}$. The area ratio is $\\tfrac{225}{676}$, and $m+n=901$.","",["Extend $CP$ until it meets $AB$, and ask what the two parallel lines say about $PQR$ and $ABC$.","The area ratio is the square of a length ratio along one line through $C$, and mass points find that ratio from the division ratios on $BC$ and $AC$."]],
"2002-aime-ii-14": ["Let the circle touch $PM$ at $B$. Equal tangents from $M$ give $MB=MA$, and the right triangles $OPB$ and $MPA$ share the angle at $P$, so they are similar with ratio $\\tfrac{OB}{MA}=\\tfrac{19}{AM}$. The perimeter of $OPB$ is $OP+19+(PM-AM)=152-2AM$, and perimeters scale by the same ratio, so $\\tfrac{19}{AM}=\\tfrac{152-2AM}{152}$, which is $(AM-38)^2=0$. With $AM=38$ the ratio is $\\tfrac12$, so $PB=\\tfrac12PA$ and $OP=\\tfrac12PM$: then $2\\,OP-38=\\tfrac{OP+19}2$, so $OP=\\tfrac{95}3$ and $m+n=98$.","",[]],
"2002-aime-ii-15": ["A circle tangent to the $x$-axis and to $y=mx$ has its center on the bisector of the angle $\\theta$ between them, so the center is $(kr,r)$ with $k=\\cot\\tfrac\\theta2$. The point $(9,6)$ is on both circles: $(9-kr)^2+(6-r)^2=r^2$, that is $k^2r^2-(18k+12)r+117=0$, whose two roots are the two radii. Vieta's product gives $\\tfrac{117}{k^2}=68$, so $\\tan^2\\tfrac\\theta2=\\tfrac{68}{117}$. The double angle formula gives $m=\\tan\\theta=\\tfrac{2\\tan\\frac\\theta2}{1-\\tan^2\\frac\\theta2}=\\tfrac{234}{49}\\sqrt{\\tfrac{68}{117}}=\\tfrac{12\\sqrt{221}}{49}$, and $a+b+c=12+221+49=282$.","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-11": ["The distance is fixed and the two trips miss the target time by the same three minutes in opposite directions, so the required speed is the harmonic mean of the two: $\\frac2s = \\frac1{40} + \\frac1{60}$ gives $s = 48$. Setting up $d = 40(t + \\tfrac1{20}) = 60(t - \\tfrac1{20})$ and solving for $d/t$ reaches the same place with more algebra. The speed is $48$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-aime-i-1": ["Unwrap the nested factorials: $\\frac{((3!)!)!}{3!} = \\frac{720!}6 = \\frac{720 \\cdot 719!}6 = 120 \\cdot 719!$. Since $120 \\cdot 719! \\lt 720!$, the factorial cannot be pushed any higher, so the bound gives $n = 719$ and $k = 120$, making $k+n = 839$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-i-2": ["The green regions are the rings between radii $2k-1$ and $2k$ for $k=1,\\ldots,50$, the ring from $99$ to $100$ among them. Each has area $\\pi\\left((2k)^2-(2k-1)^2\\right)=\\pi(4k-1)$, so the green area is $\\pi\\sum_{k=1}^{50}(4k-1)=\\pi(4\\cdot1275-50)=5050\\pi$, an arithmetic series. The ratio to $\\pi\\cdot100^2$ is $\\tfrac{5050}{10000}=\\tfrac{101}{200}$, and $m+n=301$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-i-3": ["Sort the set and count how often each element is the larger one. The $k$th largest element beats exactly the $8-k$ elements below it, so it appears that many times on the list. In increasing order $1,2,3,5,8,13,21,34$ appear $0,1,\\ldots,7$ times, so the total is $2+6+15+32+65+126+238=484$, without listing any subset.","",[]],
"2003-aime-i-4": ["The product rule turns the first equation into $\\sin x\\cos x = \\frac1{10}$. The second rearranges to $(\\sin x + \\cos x)^2 = \\frac n{10}$, and expanding the left side with the Pythagorean identity gives $1 + 2\\sin x\\cos x = 1 + \\frac15 = \\frac65$. So $\\frac n{10} = \\frac65$ and $n = 12$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-i-5": ["This is Steiner's formula for a box. Split the set into the box, $3\\cdot4\\cdot5=60$; six slabs of thickness $1$ on the faces, $2(12+15+20)=94$; twelve quarter-cylinders of radius $1$ along the edges, $\\tfrac\\pi4$ per unit of the total edge length $4(3+4+5)=48$, so $12\\pi$; and eight eighth-spheres at the corners, which make one unit sphere, $\\tfrac43\\pi$. The total is $154+\\tfrac{40\\pi}3=\\tfrac{462+40\\pi}3$, and $m+n+p=462+40+3=505$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-i-6": ["The $\\binom83=56$ triangles fall into three classes by their sides. Three vertices of one face give a right triangle with legs $1,1$ and area $\\tfrac12$: $4$ per face, $24$ in all, total $12$. An edge, a face diagonal and a space diagonal give a right triangle with legs $1,\\sqrt2$ and area $\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2$; each of the $6$ diagonal rectangles holds $4$, so $24$ of them, total $12\\sqrt2=\\sqrt{288}$. The remaining $8$ are equilateral with side $\\sqrt2$ and area $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$, total $4\\sqrt3=\\sqrt{48}$. So the sum is $12+\\sqrt{288}+\\sqrt{48}$ and $m+n+p=348$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-i-7": ["Let $m=AD=CD$ and $d=BD$. Stewart's Theorem on the cevian $BD$ of triangle $ACD$ gives $21m^2+9m^2=30\\left(d^2+9\\cdot21\\right)$, so $m^2-d^2=189$, that is $(m-d)(m+d)=189$. The factor pairs $1\\cdot189$, $3\\cdot63$, $7\\cdot27$ and $9\\cdot21$ give $(m,d)=(95,94)$, $(33,30)$, $(17,10)$ and $(15,6)$. The last puts $D$ at the midpoint of $AC$, on the line, so it is excluded. The perimeters $2m+30$ are $220$, $96$ and $64$, and $s=380$.","Alternatively, drop the perpendicular from $D$; since $AD=CD$ its foot is the midpoint of $AC$, which is $15$ from $A$ and $6$ from $B$. Two right triangles with the same height $h$ give $m^2=h^2+15^2$ and $d^2=h^2+6^2$, so $m^2-d^2=225-36=189$ at once.",[]],
"2003-aime-i-8": ["Write the terms as $a,\\, a+d,\\, a+2d,\\, \\frac{(a+2d)^2}{a+d}$, an arithmetic sequence of common difference $d$, with the last term forced by the geometric relation on the final three. The gap of $30$ between the first and fourth clears to $2d(2d-15) = 3a(10-d)$. Since $a$ and $d$ are positive integers, bounding the two sides forces $2d-15$ and $10-d$ to share a sign, and only both-positive is possible, leaving $d = 8$ or $9$; testing gives $18, 27, 36, 48$ and a sum of $129$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-i-9": ["Casework on the common digit sum $n$. For $1\\le n\\le9$ the leading pair has $n$ options, since the first digit cannot be zero, and the trailing pair has $n+1$, giving $\\sum_{n=1}^9n(n+1)=285+45=330$ by the sum-of-squares and sum formulas. For $10\\le n\\le18$ both pairs have $19-n$ options, giving $\\sum_{m=1}^9m^2=285$. The total is $330+285=615$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-i-10": ["Construct the point $N$ inside the triangle with $\\angle CBN=7^\\circ$ and $\\angle BCN=23^\\circ$, mirroring $M$. Then $\\triangle AMC\\cong\\triangle BNC$ by ASA, so $CM=CN$, and $\\angle MCN=106^\\circ-23^\\circ-23^\\circ=60^\\circ$ makes $\\triangle CMN$ equilateral, so $MN=CN$. At $N$, $\\angle CNB=180^\\circ-7^\\circ-23^\\circ=150^\\circ$ and $\\angle CNM=60^\\circ$, so $\\angle MNB=360^\\circ-150^\\circ-60^\\circ=150^\\circ$. Then triangles $CNB$ and $MNB$ are congruent by SAS, so $\\angle NMB=\\angle NCB=23^\\circ$, and $\\angle CMB=60^\\circ+23^\\circ=83^\\circ$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-i-11": ["By symmetry about $45^\\circ$ take $x\\lt45^\\circ$, so $\\cos^2x$ is the largest of the three. The triangle inequality fails exactly when $\\cos^2x\\ge\\sin^2x+\\sin x\\cos x$, and the double angle formulas turn that into $\\cos2x\\ge\\tfrac12\\sin2x$, that is, $\\tan2x\\le2$, or $x\\le\\tfrac12\\arctan2$. As a fraction of the $45^\\circ$ range, $p=\\tfrac{\\arctan2}{90}$, so $m=2$, $n=90$ and $m+n=92$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-i-12": ["Apply the Law of Cosines to the diagonal $BD$ from both angles, which are equal: $180^2+AD^2-360\\cdot AD\\cos A=180^2+BC^2-360\\cdot BC\\cos A$. The squares combine as a difference of squares, so $(AD-BC)(AD+BC)=360(AD-BC)\\cos A$, and since $AD\\ne BC$ that factor cancels to give $AD+BC=360\\cos A$. The perimeter fixes $AD+BC=640-360=280$, so $\\cos A=\\tfrac79$, and $\\lfloor1000\\cos A\\rfloor=\\lfloor777.7\\ldots\\rfloor=777$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-i-13": ["An $n$-digit binary number starts with $1$, and its other $n-1$ digits are free. For even $n=2m$ the free digits need at least $m$ ones, which by the symmetry $\\binom{2m-1}k=\\binom{2m-1}{2m-1-k}$ is exactly half of the $2^{2m-1}$ strings; for odd $n=2m+1$ they need at least $m$ ones, which is half of $2^{2m}+\\binom{2m}m$. So lengths $1$ to $11$ contribute $1,1,3,4,11,16,42,64,163,256,638$, totalling $1199$. The $44$ numbers from $2004$ to $2047$ all begin $11111$ and have at least $7$ ones, so they all qualify and must be removed: $N=1155$, with remainder $155$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-i-14": ["Shifting the decimal point, some $\\frac{m'}n$ (the fractional part of $10^k\\frac mn$) satisfies $0.251\\le\\frac{m'}n\\lt0.252$. Then $\\frac{4m'}n$ lies in $[1.004,1.008)$, so $4m'-n$ is a positive integer, and $\\frac{4m'-n}n\\lt0.008$ forces $n\\gt125(4m'-n)\\ge125$. With $4m'-n=1$, $n\\equiv3\\pmod4$, so $n\\ge127$, and $\\frac{32}{127}=0.25196\\ldots$ shows $n=127$ works.","",[]],
"2003-aime-i-15": ["Work in vectors from $B$, with $u$ and $v$ the unit vectors along $BA$ and $BC$, so $A=360u$, $C=507v$ and $M=180u+\\tfrac{507}2v$. Step 1, the bisector. The Angle Bisector Theorem gives $AD:DC=360:507=120:169$, so $D=\\tfrac{169}{289}A+\\tfrac{120}{289}C=k(u+v)$ with $k=\\tfrac{60840}{289}$; the two coefficients agree, as they must on the bisector. Step 2, the perpendicular. A line perpendicular to the bisector cuts off an isosceles triangle: reflecting $F$ in $BD$ gives $F'$ on $BA$ with $BF'=BF$ and $D$ the midpoint of $FF'$, so $F=2kv$. Step 3, the crossing. The point $D+t(F-D)=k(1-t)u+k(1+t)v$ lies on $BM$ when $\\tfrac{1-t}{1+t}=\\tfrac{180}{507/2}=\\tfrac{120}{169}$, that is $t=\\tfrac{49}{289}$. So $DE:EF=t:(1-t)=49:240$, and $m+n=289$.","",["Reflect $F$ across the bisector $BD$. Where does the image land, and what does that do to $D$?","With the new point drawn, the angle bisector theorem gives ratios along $CA$, and those ratios are enough to balance masses at the vertices."]],
"2003-aime-ii-1": ["With $c = a+b$, the condition $abc = 6(a+b+c)$ becomes $ab(a+b) = 12(a+b)$, so $ab = 12$. Running over the factor pairs $\\{1,12\\}$, $\\{2,6\\}$, $\\{3,4\\}$ gives $a+b$ equal to $13$, $8$, $7$, and since $N = 12(a+b)$ the possible values sum to $12 \\cdot 28 = 336$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-ii-2": ["Distinct digits means using each of $0$ through $9$ at most once, so the greatest candidate starts $9876543$ and ends with some arrangement of $0$, $1$, $2$. The divisibility rule for $8$ looks only at the last three digits, so try the arrangements of $\\{0,1,2\\}$ from the largest down: $210$ and $201$ are not multiples of $8$, but $120=8\\cdot15$ is. So $N=9876543120$ and the remainder is $120$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-ii-3": ["Build the word letter by letter. The first letter is free, three ways, and after any letter exactly one of the three is forbidden as its successor, leaving two ways at each of the remaining six positions. So the count is $3 \\cdot 2^6 = 192$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-ii-4": ["Each face center is the average of that face's three vertices, so placing the four vertices at $(1,0,0,0)$ through $(0,0,0,1)$ in $4$-space makes every center a point with three coordinates $\\tfrac13$. The distance formula gives edges $\\sqrt2$ and $\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}{3}$, a linear ratio of $1:3$, so the volume ratio is $\\tfrac1{27}$ and $m+n = 28$. No tetrahedron volume formula is needed, since the answer is a ratio.","",[]],
"2003-aime-ii-5": ["Two copies of the wedge fit together into a full cylinder of radius $6$ and height $12$, since the $45^\\circ$ cut is symmetric about the tangent line. So the wedge is half of $Bh = \\pi \\cdot 6^2 \\cdot 12$, giving $216\\pi$ and $n = 216$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-ii-6": ["The $13$-$14$-$15$ triangle has area $84$. The half-turn about $G$ sends line $BC$ to the parallel line on the other side of $G$; since $G$ is a third of the way up the altitude from $BC$ to $A$, that line is a third of the altitude from $A$, so it cuts off a corner triangle at $A$ similar to $ABC$ with ratio $\\tfrac13$ and area $\\tfrac{84}9$. The same happens at $B$ and $C$, so the two triangles overlap in a hexagon of area $84-3\\cdot\\tfrac{84}9=56$. The union has area $84+84-56=112$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-ii-7": ["Let the side be $x$ and the half-diagonals $a=\\tfrac{AC}2$ and $b=\\tfrac{BD}2$, so $x^2=a^2+b^2$. Triangle $ABD$ has sides $x$, $x$, $2b$ and area $ab$, so $R=\\tfrac{x\\cdot x\\cdot2b}{4ab}=\\tfrac{x^2}{2a}=12.5$; likewise triangle $ACD$ gives $\\tfrac{x^2}{2b}=25$. So $a=2b$, then $x^2=5b^2$ and $\\tfrac{5b^2}{2b}=25$ gives $b=10$, $a=20$. The area is $2ab=400$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-ii-8": ["Multiplying two arithmetic sequences term by term gives a quadratic in the index, so the second differences are constant. From $1440$, $1716$, $1848$ the first differences are $276$ and $132$, so the second difference is $-144$ and the quadratic is $-72n^2+492n+1020$. Evaluating at $n=8$ gives $-4608+3936+1020=348$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-ii-9": ["Dividing $P$ by $Q$ gives $P(x)=(x^2+1)Q(x)+x^2-x+1$, so at each root of $Q$ only the remainder survives: $\\sum P(z_k)=\\sum z_k^2-\\sum z_k+4$. Vieta's on $Q=x^4-x^3-x^2-1$ gives $\\sum z_k=1$ and $\\sum_{j\\lt k}z_jz_k=-1$, so Newton's first step gives $\\sum z_k^2=1^2-2(-1)=3$. The total is $3-1+4=6$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-ii-10": ["With the integers $b$ and $b+60$, squaring $\\sqrt b+\\sqrt{b+60}=\\sqrt c$ gives $c=2b+60+2\\sqrt{b^2+60b}$, so $b^2+60b$ must be a perfect square $n^2$. Completing it to $(b+30)^2-n^2=900$ and factoring the difference of squares gives $(b+30+n)(b+30-n)=900$, with both factors even. The even factor pairs $(450,2)$, $(150,6)$, $(90,10)$, $(50,18)$ give $b=196$, $48$, $20$, $4$, with $c=900$, $300$, $180$, $100$. The largest $b$ makes $c=900=30^2$, which is excluded, so the answer comes from $b=48$: the sum is $48+108=156$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-ii-11": ["The Pythagorean theorem gives $AB=25$. $D$ is equidistant from $A$ and $B$, so it lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, which passes through $M$, and $DM=\\sqrt{15^2-12.5^2}=\\tfrac{5\\sqrt{11}}2$. Take $DM$ as the base of triangle $CDM$: its height is the distance from $C$ to the line $DM$, which is $MN$, where $N$ is the foot of the altitude from $C$. The altitude-to-the-hypotenuse relation $BC^2=BN\\cdot AB$ gives $BN=\\tfrac{576}{25}$, so $MN=\\tfrac{576}{25}-\\tfrac{25}2=\\tfrac{527}{50}$. The area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac{5\\sqrt{11}}2\\cdot\\tfrac{527}{50}=\\tfrac{527\\sqrt{11}}{40}$, and $m+n+p=578$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-ii-12": ["With $s$ members and $v$ votes for a candidate, the condition $\\frac{100v}s+1\\le v$ reads $v(s-100)\\ge s$, so $s\\gt100$ and each of the $27$ candidates needs $v\\ge\\left\\lceil\\frac s{s-100}\\right\\rceil$; extra votes only help, so $s$ works exactly when $27\\left\\lceil\\frac s{s-100}\\right\\rceil\\le s$. For $101\\le s\\le133$, $\\frac s{s-100}\\ge\\frac{133}{33}\\gt4$, so each candidate needs $5$ votes and $27\\cdot5=135\\gt s$. At $s=134$, $\\frac{134}{34}\\lt4$, so $4$ votes each, $108$ in all, with the other $26$ votes added anywhere. The smallest committee has $134$ members.","",[]],
"2003-aime-ii-13": ["Let $P_n$ be the probability of being at the start after $n$ moves. To be there at step $n$ the bug must have been elsewhere at step $n-1$ and then chosen the right one of two edges, so $P_n=\\tfrac12(1-P_{n-1})$ with $P_0=1$. This first-order linear recurrence has fixed point $\\tfrac13$, so $P_n=\\tfrac13+\\tfrac23\\left(-\\tfrac12\\right)^n$, and $P_{10}=\\tfrac13+\\tfrac2{3\\cdot1024}=\\tfrac{171}{512}$. So $m+n=683$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-ii-14": ["In an equilateral hexagon with opposite sides parallel, opposite sides are equal vectors pointing opposite ways. With $u=\\vec{AB}$, $v=\\vec{BC}$ and $w=\\vec{CD}$, that makes $\\vec{DE}=-u$, $\\vec{EF}=-v$, $\\vec{FA}=-w$, so $F=w$. If $F$ is at height $f$ and $v$ rises $g$, the heights are $0$, $2$, $2+g$, $2+g+f$, $g+f$, $f$, which must be $0,2,\\ldots,10$; they add to $6+3(f+g)=30$, so $f+g=8$, $D$ is at $10$, $E$ at $8$, and $\\{2+g,f\\}=\\{4,6\\}$. With $f=6$ the sides force $v=u$, a straight angle at $B$, so $f=4$ and $g=4$. As complex numbers, $\\angle FAB=120^\\circ$ means $F=B\\,e^{2\\pi i/3}$, and its height $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2b-1=4$ gives $b=\\tfrac{10}{\\sqrt3}$, $F=\\left(-\\tfrac8{\\sqrt3},4\\right)$, with side length squared $\\tfrac{112}3$. Then $v=\\left(\\tfrac8{\\sqrt3},4\\right)$ (the other sign makes a dent at $C$), so the vertices are $(0,0)$, $\\left(\\tfrac{10}{\\sqrt3},2\\right)$, $\\left(\\tfrac{18}{\\sqrt3},6\\right)$, $\\left(\\tfrac{10}{\\sqrt3},10\\right)$, $(0,8)$, $\\left(-\\tfrac8{\\sqrt3},4\\right)$, and the shoelace formula gives the area $\\tfrac{144}{\\sqrt3}=48\\sqrt3$, so $m+n=51$.","",[]],
"2003-aime-ii-15": ["The coefficients $1,2,\\dots,24,\\dots,2,1$ are the convolution of a run of ones with itself, so $P(x)=x\\left(1+x+\\cdots+x^{23}\\right)^2$. Summing that geometric series gives $\\tfrac{x^{24}-1}{x-1}$, so the distinct zeros are $0$ and the twenty-fourth roots of unity $e^{2\\pi ik/24}$, $k=1,\\dots,23$. Squaring gives $e^{\\pi ik/6}$, so $|b_k|=\\left|\\sin\\tfrac{\\pi k}6\\right|$: eight values $\\tfrac12$, eight $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$, four $1$ and three $0$ (and $0$ for the root $0$). Adding them gives $4+4\\sqrt3+4=8+4\\sqrt3$, and $m+n+p=8+4+3=15$.","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-4": ["Average speed is total distance over total time, not the mean of the two speeds. The round trip is $2$ km in $30+10=40$ minutes, that is $\\tfrac23$ of an hour, so the average speed is $2\\div\\tfrac23=3$ km/hr, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-aime-i-1": ["Place value turns the four decreasing consecutive digits into $1000(n+3)+100(n+2)+10(n+1)+n = 3210 + 1111n$ for $n = 0$ through $6$. Since $1111 = 30 \\cdot 37 + 1$, each step up in $n$ raises the residue modulo $37$ by exactly one, so the seven remainders are consecutive starting from that of $3210$. Since $3210\\equiv28\\pmod{37}$ the remainders are $28$ through $34$, which add to $217$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-i-2": ["For consecutive integers the mean is the median, so set $\\mathcal A$ has median $\\tfrac{2m}m=2$ and set $\\mathcal B$ has median $\\tfrac m{2m}=\\tfrac12$. The greatest elements are therefore $2+\\tfrac{m-1}2$ and $\\tfrac12+\\tfrac{2m-1}2=m$, whose difference is $\\left|\\tfrac{m-3}2\\right|=99$. So $m=201$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-i-3": ["Every pair of vertices gives an edge, a face diagonal, or a space diagonal, and the pair count is $\\binom{26}2 = 325$. Subtract the $60$ edges and the face diagonals: triangles have none and each quadrilateral has $2$, so $24$ of them. The complement of those, $325 - 60 - 24 = 241$, is the space diagonals.","",[]],
"2004-aime-i-4": ["Each segment is the hypotenuse of a right triangle in a corner, and the midpoint of a hypotenuse is half its length from the right angle, so every midpoint sits exactly $1$ from that corner. The midpoints therefore trace four quarter circles of radius $1$, and the enclosed region is the square minus four such quarter circles, whose combined area is that of one circle, of area $4 - \\pi\\approx0.858$, so $k=0.86$ and $100k=86$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-i-5": ["With $q$ points attempted on day one, Beta's daily rates must beat neither of Alpha's, giving $\\frac aq \\lt \\frac8{15}$ and $\\frac b{500-q} \\lt \\frac7{10}$. Adding the resulting bounds gives $a+b \\lt 350 - \\frac q6$, so $a+b\\le349$ for every $q\\ge1$. It is reached: $q=2$ allows $a=1$ ($\\tfrac12\\lt\\tfrac8{15}$) and $b=348$ ($\\tfrac{348}{498}\\lt\\tfrac7{10}$). So the best two-day ratio is $\\tfrac{349}{500}$ and $m+n=849$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-i-6": ["Choose four distinct digits; a snakelike order goes up, down, up, and exactly $5$ orders of four numbers do that ($1324,1423,2314,2413,3412$ for $1,2,3,4$). Without $0$ there are $\\binom94=126$ sets, giving $630$ numbers. With $0$ the smallest digit cannot lead, and it leads in $2$ of the $5$ orders, so each of the $\\binom93=84$ sets gives $3$, that is $252$. The total is $882$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-i-7": ["The $x^2$ coefficient is the second elementary symmetric sum $e_2$ of the linear coefficients $-1,2,-3,\\ldots,-15$, and squaring their sum gives $e_2=\\tfrac{(\\sum a_i)^2-\\sum a_i^2}2$. Pairing consecutive terms makes $\\sum a_i=-8$, and $\\sum a_i^2=1+4+\\cdots+225=\\tfrac{15\\cdot16\\cdot31}6=1240$ by the sum-of-squares formula, so $C=\\tfrac{64-1240}2=-588$ and $|C|=588$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-i-8": ["Joining every $k$th vertex of a regular $n$-gon traces a single closed path exactly when the step is coprime to $n$, since a step of size $k$ splits the vertices into $\\gcd(n,k)$ orbits. So the admissible steps number $\\phi(1000) = 400$; steps $k$ and $n-k$ give the same star, and $k = 1$ gives the polygon itself, so the count is $\\frac{400}2 - 1 = 199$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-i-9": ["A segment splits the rectangle into a triangle and a trapezoid only by running from a corner to a point of a far side, so $U_2$ is a right triangle and $V_2$ a right trapezoid. Then $U_1$ is a right triangle and $V_1$ a right trapezoid, so the cut in $ABC$ is parallel to a leg and $U_1$ is a $3$-$4$-$5$ triangle scaled by some $k$. In the rectangle the $3$-$4$-$5$ triangle must take a full side as its longer leg: legs $6$ and $\\tfrac92$, leaving $V_2$ with parallel sides $7$ and $\\tfrac52$ and height $6$; or legs $7$ and $\\tfrac{21}4$, leaving parallel sides $6$ and $\\tfrac34$ and height $7$. Cutting $ABC$ parallel to the leg $3$ leaves a trapezoid with parallel sides $3$ and $3k$ and height $4(1-k)$, and matching shapes gives $k=\\tfrac5{14}$ in the first case and $k=\\tfrac18$ in the second (the cut parallel to the leg $4$ matches neither). The smaller area is $6\\cdot\\tfrac1{64}=\\tfrac3{32}$, so $m+n=35$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-i-10": ["The center may lie anywhere in the inner $34\\times13$ rectangle, of area $442$. Missing the diagonal means being at least $1$ from it, so in each half of the rectangle the center lies at least $1$ from all three sides of the right triangle with legs $15$ and $36$. Those points form a smaller triangle with the same incenter and inradius one less. The inradius is $\\tfrac{15+36-39}2=6$, so the scaling is $\\tfrac56$ and each small triangle has area $270\\cdot\\tfrac{25}{36}=187.5$. The probability is $\\tfrac{375}{442}$, and $m+n=817$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-i-11": ["The whole cone has slant height $5$, lateral area $15\\pi$, base $9\\pi$ and volume $12\\pi$. The cut is parallel to the base, so $\\mathcal C$ is the cone scaled by some $x$, with painted lateral area $15\\pi x^2$ and volume $12\\pi x^3$; the frustum gets the rest, $24\\pi-15\\pi x^2$ of paint and $12\\pi(1-x^3)$ of volume. Setting $\\tfrac{15x^2}{24-15x^2}=\\tfrac{x^3}{1-x^3}$ and cross-multiplying, the $x^5$ terms cancel and $15=24x$, so $x=\\tfrac58$. Then $k=\\tfrac{125}{512-125}=\\tfrac{125}{387}$, and $m+n=512$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-i-12": ["The floor of $\\log_2\\frac1x$ is even exactly on the intervals $\\left(\\tfrac12,1\\right]$, $\\left(\\tfrac18,\\tfrac14\\right]$, $\\left(\\tfrac1{32},\\tfrac1{16}\\right]$ and so on, whose lengths $\\tfrac12,\\tfrac18,\\tfrac1{32},\\dots$ form a geometric series with ratio $\\tfrac14$ and sum $\\tfrac{1/2}{3/4}=\\tfrac23$. For $y$ with base $5$ the intervals are $\\left(\\tfrac15,1\\right]$, $\\left(\\tfrac1{125},\\tfrac1{25}\\right]$, ..., with lengths $\\tfrac45,\\tfrac4{125},\\dots$ summing to $\\tfrac{4/5}{24/25}=\\tfrac56$. The region is the product of those two sets, so its area is $\\tfrac23\\cdot\\tfrac56=\\tfrac59$ and $m+n=14$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-i-13": ["Sum the geometric series first: $1+x+\\cdots+x^{17}=\\tfrac{x^{18}-1}{x-1}$, so $P(x)=\\tfrac{(x^{18}-1)^2-x^{17}(x-1)^2}{(x-1)^2}=\\tfrac{(x^{19}-1)(x^{17}-1)}{(x-1)^2}$. The roots are the nineteenth and seventeenth roots of unity other than $1$, with $\\alpha$ values $\\tfrac k{19}$ and $\\tfrac k{17}$. The smallest five are $\\tfrac1{19},\\tfrac1{17},\\tfrac2{19},\\tfrac2{17},\\tfrac3{19}$, which add to $\\tfrac6{19}+\\tfrac3{17}=\\tfrac{159}{323}$, so $m+n=482$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-i-14": ["Unroll the tower's surface: the taut rope then becomes one straight segment, so it climbs at a constant slope along its whole length, the wrapped part and the free part alike. Seen from above, the free part leaves the tower along a tangent, which is perpendicular to the radius, and the unicorn is $8+4=12$ from the center, so the Pythagorean theorem gives the free part's horizontal run $\\sqrt{12^2-8^2}=4\\sqrt5$. The whole rope rises $4$ over its length of $20$, so its total horizontal run is $\\sqrt{20^2-4^2}=8\\sqrt6$. Lengths along a straight segment are proportional to their horizontal runs, so the wrapped length is $20\\cdot\\tfrac{8\\sqrt6-4\\sqrt5}{8\\sqrt6}=\\tfrac{60-\\sqrt{750}}3$, and $a+b+c=813$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-i-15": ["Split the run from $x$ to $1$ into stages: each stage is some number $k$ of steps adding $1$, followed by one division by $10$, so it uses $k+1$ steps with $0\\le k\\le9$. Reading backwards from $1$, a stage multiplies by $10$ and subtracts $k$, and any choices give a valid start, except that the stage ending at $1$ cannot start at $10-9=1$, so its $k\\le8$. So the starting values with $d(x)=20$ (that is, $19$ steps) are in bijection with compositions of $19$ into parts of size at most $10$ whose last part is at most $9$. All compositions of $19$ number $2^{18}$; those with a part of $11$ or more number $1280$ (such a part is unique, and the rest split around it in $2^r+(r-1)2^{r-2}$ ways for a remainder $r\\ge1$, $1$ way for $r=0$), and those ending in a part of exactly $10$ number $2^8=256$. So $m=2^{18}-1280-256=260608=2^9\\cdot509$, and the sum of its distinct prime factors is $2+509=511$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-ii-1": ["Half the chord, half the radius and a full radius form a right triangle with leg $\\tfrac r2$ and hypotenuse $r$, so it is a $30$-$60$-$90$ and the central angle over the chord is $120^\\circ$. The smaller region is the circular segment, the $120^\\circ$ sector minus the triangle: $\\tfrac{\\pi r^2}3-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3r^2}4$. The larger is the rest of the circle, $\\tfrac{2\\pi r^2}3+\\tfrac{\\sqrt3r^2}4$. Multiplying both by $\\tfrac{12}{r^2}$, the ratio is $\\tfrac{8\\pi+3\\sqrt3}{4\\pi-3\\sqrt3}$, and $8\\cdot3\\cdot3\\cdot4\\cdot3\\cdot3=2592$ leaves remainder $592$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-ii-2": ["Do casework on Terry's draw. Two reds happens with probability $\\tfrac{10\\cdot9}{20\\cdot19}=\\tfrac9{38}$, after which Mary takes two reds with probability $\\tfrac{8\\cdot7}{18\\cdot17}=\\tfrac{28}{153}$; two blues is the same. One of each has probability $\\tfrac{2\\cdot10\\cdot10}{20\\cdot19}=\\tfrac{10}{19}$, and Mary then matches with $\\tfrac{2\\cdot9\\cdot9}{18\\cdot17}=\\tfrac9{17}$. The total is $2\\cdot\\tfrac9{38}\\cdot\\tfrac{28}{153}+\\tfrac{10}{19}\\cdot\\tfrac9{17}=\\tfrac{118}{323}$, so $m+n=441$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-ii-3": ["The hidden cubes form the block one shorter in each direction, so $(l-1)(m-1)(n-1) = 231 = 3 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 11$. The factorizations of $231$ into three factors are $1\\cdot1\\cdot231$, $1\\cdot3\\cdot77$, $1\\cdot7\\cdot33$, $1\\cdot11\\cdot21$ and $3\\cdot7\\cdot11$, giving $N=2\\cdot2\\cdot232=928$, $624$, $544$, $528$ and $4\\cdot8\\cdot12=384$. The smallest is $N=384$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-ii-4": ["Split by how many distinct digits appear. Single-digit repdigits give $9$ per length, so $36$ in all. For exactly two distinct digits, choose the pair and then build the string: with no zero there are $\\binom92$ pairs and $2^n - 2$ arrangements of length $n$, giving $36(2+6+14)=792$, while a pair $\\{0,d\\}$ must lead with $d$, leaving $2^{n-1}-1$ strings of length $n$, so $9(1+3+7)=99$. The total is $36+792+99=927$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-ii-5": ["Work rates add, so scale the units to make $1000$ workers finish a quarter of the job in one day. The next quarter then takes $\\tfrac{10}{9}$ days at $900$ workers and the third $\\tfrac{10}{8}$ at $800$, so $\\tfrac{121}{36}$ days are gone and only $\\tfrac{23}{36}$ remain for the last quarter. That needs $\\tfrac{36}{23}$ of $1000$ workers, about $1565.2$, so $1566$ must be present and $766$ more are hired.","",[]],
"2004-aime-ii-6": ["Let the monkeys take $x$, $y$, $z$ bananas. Whole splits need $8\\mid x$ (each other monkey gets $\\tfrac x8$), $8\\mid y$ (each gets $\\tfrac{3y}8$) and $24\\mid z$ (each gets $\\tfrac{11z}{24}$). The final holdings are $\\tfrac34x+\\tfrac38y+\\tfrac{11}{24}z$, $\\tfrac18x+\\tfrac14y+\\tfrac{11}{24}z$ and $\\tfrac18x+\\tfrac38y+\\tfrac1{12}z$, and setting them to $3t$, $2t$, $t$ and eliminating gives $x:y:z=11:13:27$. So $x=11k$, $y=13k$, $z=27k$, and the divisibility conditions need $8\\mid k$ (for $z$, $24\\mid27k$ means $8\\mid k$). The least is $k=8$: $x=88$, $y=104$, $z=216$, with holdings $204$, $136$, $68$, so the least total is $408$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-ii-7": ["A fold is a reflection across the crease, so the crease $EF$ is the perpendicular bisector of $BB'$, and each of its points is equally far from $B$ and $B'$. For $E$ that gives $B'E=BE=17$, and the Pythagorean theorem in triangle $AEB'$ gives $AB'=\\sqrt{17^2-8^2}=15$. The side is $AB=25$, so with $BC=y$ the point $F$ has $BF^2=y^2+3^2$ and $B'F^2=(y-15)^2+22^2$. Setting $BF=B'F$ gives $30y=700$, so $y=\\tfrac{70}3$, the perimeter is $2\\left(25+\\tfrac{70}3\\right)=\\tfrac{290}3$, and $m+n=293$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-ii-8": ["Since $2004^{2004} = 2^{4008}3^{2004}167^{2004}$, a divisor is $2^a3^b167^c$ and its own divisor count is $(a+1)(b+1)(c+1)$. Setting that equal to $2004 = 2^2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 167$ means running over the ways to write $2004$ as an ordered product of three factors, subject to $a \\le 4008$ and $b, c \\le 2004$. Every factor is at most $2004$, so the bounds never bind, and the ordered factorizations number $\\binom42\\cdot3\\cdot3=54$: the two $2$s go into three slots in $6$ ways, and the $3$ and the $167$ in $3$ ways each.","",[]],
"2004-aime-ii-9": ["Let $x=a_2$ and alternate the two conditions: the geometric step gives $a_3=x^2$ and the arithmetic step gives $a_4=x(2x-1)$, and the pattern continues as $a_{2n}=f(n-1)f(n)$, $a_{2n+1}=f(n)^2$ with $f(n)=nx-(n-1)$. Then $a_9+a_{10}=(4x-3)^2+(4x-3)(5x-4)=(4x-3)(9x-7)=646=17\\cdot38$, so $x=5$ and $f(n)=4n+1$. The terms near $1000$ are $a_{15}=29^2=841$, $a_{16}=29\\cdot33=957$ and $a_{17}=33^2=1089$, so the greatest below $1000$ is $a_{16}=957$ and $n+a_n=973$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-ii-10": ["Such a number is $2^j+2^k$ with $0\\le j\\lt k\\le39$, so there are $\\binom{40}2=780$ of them. Powers of two cycle mod $9$ with period $6$ through $1,2,4,8,7,5$, and since $2^3\\equiv-1$, $2^j+2^k\\equiv0$ exactly when $k-j\\equiv3\\pmod6$. A difference $d$ occurs for $40-d$ pairs, so the count is $37+31+25+19+13+7+1=133$ over $d=3,9,\\ldots,39$. The probability is $\\tfrac{133}{780}$, already in lowest terms, so $p+q=913$.","",["Write each such number as a sum of two powers of $2$, and count how many there are.","Powers of $2$ repeat modulo $9$. List the cycle, and see which pairs of residues sum to a multiple of $9$."]],
"2004-aime-ii-11": ["Unroll the cone into a circular sector. The slant height is $\\sqrt{600^2+(200\\sqrt7)^2}=800$, and the base circumference $1200\\pi$ makes the sector's angle $\\tfrac{1200\\pi}{800}=\\tfrac{3\\pi}2$. Points on exactly opposite sides of the cone are half of that, $135^\\circ$, apart in the unrolled picture, and the shortest path is the straight segment between them. The Law of Cosines with $\\cos135^\\circ=-\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2$ gives $d^2=125^2+(375\\sqrt2)^2+2\\cdot125\\cdot375\\sqrt2\\cdot\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2=15625+281250+93750=390625$, so $d=625$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-ii-12": ["Join the centers. Each leg leans in by $\\tfrac{6-4}2=1$, so the trapezoid has height $\\sqrt{5^2-1^2}=2\\sqrt6$. By symmetry the small circle's center $O$ lies on the trapezoid's axis, and external tangency makes $OA=r+3$ and $OD=r+2$. The Pythagorean theorem with the horizontal offsets $3$ and $2$ puts $O$ at height $\\sqrt{(r+3)^2-9}=\\sqrt{r^2+6r}$ above $AB$ and $\\sqrt{(r+2)^2-4}=\\sqrt{r^2+4r}$ below $CD$. So $\\sqrt{r^2+6r}+\\sqrt{r^2+4r}=2\\sqrt6$; isolating one root and squaring twice gives $23r^2+120r-144=0$, whose positive root is $r=\\tfrac{-60+48\\sqrt3}{23}$, so $k+m+n+p=60+48+3+23=134$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-ii-13": ["Let $F$ be where $AD$ meets $CE$. The parallels make $ABCF$ a parallelogram, so $[FAC]=[FAB]=[FBC]=[ABC]$, call it $S$. The Law of Cosines gives $AC^2=9+25-30\\cos120^\\circ=49$, so $AC=7$. Since $DE\\parallel AC$, triangle $FDE$ is triangle $FAC$ scaled through $F$ by $k=\\tfrac{15}7$, so $[FDE]=k^2S$, and triangles $FBD$ and $FBE$ have bases $k$ times those of $FBA$ and $FBC$, so each has area $kS$. Then $[EBD]=(k^2+2k)S=\\tfrac{435}{49}S$, the ratio is $\\tfrac{49}{435}$, and $m+n=484$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-ii-14": ["With $a$ single $7$s, $b$ copies of $77$ and $c$ of $777$, dividing by $7$ gives $a + 11b + 111c = 1000$, and the count of digits is $n = a+2b+3c$. Substituting, $n = 1000 - 9(b+12c)$, so $n \\equiv 1 \\pmod 9$ and $0 \\le 9(b+12c) \\lt 1000$ bounds it. Writing $k=b+12c$, the constraint $11b+111c\\le1000$ allows every $k$ from $0$ to $108$ except $k=107$, so there are $108$ values of $n$.","",[]],
"2004-aime-ii-15": ["Track one square through the folds. Before a fold the strip has length $L$ and thickness $t$. A square at position $p\\le\\tfrac L2$ stays put; one at $p\\gt\\tfrac L2$ moves to $L+1-p$ and lands upside down on top of that column, so if $b$ squares were below it, now $t+(t-1-b)$ are. Starting from $p=942$, $b=0$, the folds give $(p,b)=(83,1)$, which stays put for two folds, then $(46,14)$, $(19,17)$, $(14,46)$, $(3,81)$, which stays put once, then $(2,430)$ and finally $(1,593)$. So $593$ squares lie below it.","",[]],
"2005-aime-i-1": ["Join the centers. External tangency puts adjacent small centers $2r$ apart, so the six centers form a regular hexagon, which splits into equilateral triangles of side $2r$ whose circumradius is also $2r$. Internal tangency then gives $30 = 2r + r$, so $r = 10$. The wanted area uses the circle area formula: the big disc minus six small ones, $K=\\pi(30^2 - 6 \\cdot 10^2) = 300\\pi\\approx942.48$, so $\\lfloor K\\rfloor=942$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-i-2": ["The general term of the arithmetic sequence is $1 + (n-1)k$, so containing $2005$ means $k(n-1) = 2004 = 2^2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 167$. Every factorization gives a valid $k$, so the count is the number of divisors of $2004$, namely $3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 12$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-i-3": ["Three proper divisors means four divisors in all, so the divisor-count formula forces $n = pq$ with distinct primes or $n = p^3$. Casework on the two shapes: for $pq$ both primes must be under $50$, giving $\\binom{15}2$ choices from the fifteen such primes; for $p^3$ the proper divisors are $p$ and $p^2$, needing $p^2 \\lt 50$. There are $\\binom{15}2=105$ of the first kind and $4$ of the second ($p=2,3,5,7$), so $109$ in all.","",[]],
"2005-aime-i-4": ["With $n$ columns the band has $n(n+7)$ members, which must be five more than a perfect square. For $n \\gt 14$ the quantity $n(n+7)$ is squeezed strictly between $(n+3)^2+5$ and $(n+4)^2+5$, so no such square exists and the search is bounded to $n \\le 14$. At $n=14$ it works, $14\\cdot21=294=17^2+5$, so the maximum is $294$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-i-5": ["Color and orientation are independent. The gold positions are any of the $\\binom84 = 70$ combinations, which fixes the silver ones. For orientation, write the stack as a string of faces: no two adjacent faces may touch, so once the string switches from down to up it can never switch back, leaving $9$ strings. Multiplying gives $630$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-i-6": ["The left side is the binomial expansion of $(x-1)^4$ short of its constant term, so adding $1$ to both sides gives $(x-1)^4=2006$. With $r=\\sqrt[4]{2006}$ the four solutions are $x=1+i^nr$, the fourth roots of unity scaled by $r$ and shifted. The two nonreal ones are $1\\pm ir$, whose product is $1+r^2=1+\\sqrt{2006}\\approx45.79$, since $44^2=1936\\lt2006\\lt2025=45^2$. So $\\lfloor P\\rfloor=45$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-i-7": ["Extend $AD$ and $BC$ to meet at $P$. The $60^\\circ$ angles at $A$ and $B$ make $ABP$ equilateral, with side $s=AB$, so $PD=s-10$, $PC=s-8$, and the angle at $P$ is $60^\\circ$. The Law of Cosines in triangle $PDC$ gives $144=(s-10)^2+(s-8)^2-(s-10)(s-8)=s^2-18s+84$, so $s^2-18s-60=0$ and $s=9+\\sqrt{141}$. So $p+q=9+141=150$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-i-8": ["Substituting $y = 2^{111x}$ turns the equation into the cubic $y^3 - 8y^2 + 16y - 4 = 0$. Vieta's says its three roots multiply to $4$, so $2^{111(x_1+x_2+x_3)} = 4$, and taking logarithms gives $111\\sum x_i = 2$. Hence the sum is $\\frac2{111}$. In lowest terms that gives $m+n=113$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-i-9": ["Each unit cube's orientation is independent, so multiply position by position over its $24$ orientations. The center shows nothing. A face-center cube shows one face, orange unless it is one of the two unpainted faces: $\\tfrac46=\\tfrac23$. An edge cube shows two adjacent faces, which must avoid both unpainted faces: $\\tfrac{10}{24}=\\tfrac5{12}$. A corner shows three mutually adjacent faces, which must be the three faces away from the unpainted edge: $\\tfrac6{24}=\\tfrac14$. So the probability is $\\left(\\tfrac23\\right)^6\\left(\\tfrac5{12}\\right)^{12}\\left(\\tfrac14\\right)^8=\\tfrac{5^{12}}{2^{34}3^{18}}$, and $a+b+c+p+q+r=12+34+18+5+2+3=74$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-i-10": ["The midpoint of $BC$ is $\\left(\\tfrac{35}2,\\tfrac{39}2\\right)$, so the median of slope $-5$ through it forces $q=-5p+107$. The shoelace determinant gives $2\\cdot70=\\left|(12-p)(20-q)-(23-p)(19-q)\\right|=|11q-p-197|$. With $q=107-5p$ this is $|980-56p|=140$, so $p=15$ or $p=20$, and $p+q=107-4p$ is $47$ or $27$. The largest is $47$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-i-11": ["The largest semicircle is symmetric about a diagonal of the square: its arc touches the two far sides and its diameter, perpendicular to the diagonal, ends on the two near sides. With radius $r$, tangency to the far sides puts the center at $(8-r,8-r)$. Each half of the diameter has length $r$ and runs at $45^\\circ$ to the sides, so as the hypotenuse of a $45$-$45$-$90$ triangle it moves $\\tfrac r{\\sqrt2}$ horizontally, and the end on the left side gives $8-r-\\tfrac r{\\sqrt2}=0$. Rationalizing, $r=\\tfrac{8\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt2+1}=8\\sqrt2(\\sqrt2-1)=16-8\\sqrt2$, so $d=32-16\\sqrt2=32-\\sqrt{512}$ and $m+n=544$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-i-12": ["Divisors pair off as $d$ with $\\tfrac nd$, so $\\tau(n)$ is odd exactly for perfect squares, and $S(n)$ has the parity of the number of squares up to $n$, that is of $\\lfloor\\sqrt n\\rfloor$. So $S(n)$ is odd on the blocks $k^2\\le n\\lt(k+1)^2$ with $k$ odd, and consecutive squares differ by $(k+1)^2-k^2=2k+1$. Since $44^2=1936\\le2005\\lt45^2$, the odd blocks are $k=1,3,\\dots,43$, of total length $\\sum(2k+1)=2\\cdot484+22=990$. So $a=990$, $b=2005-990=1015$, and $|a-b|=25$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-i-13": ["With $d$ diagonal steps the path has $10-d$ moves in total, since each diagonal replaces one right and one up. Do casework on $d$ from $1$ to $5$: within each case the moves form a string of $R$s, $U$s and $D$s, and the no-right-angle-turn rule forbids an $R$ directly beside a $U$, so the diagonals act as separators: each of the $d+1$ gaps between them holds only $R$s or only $U$s. Counting the ways to spread $5-d$ of each letter over those gaps gives $2$, $18$, $42$, $20$ and $1$ paths for $d=1,\\ldots,5$ (with $d=0$ an $R$ must touch a $U$), so the total is $83$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-i-14": ["In a square, two perpendicular segments that each join a pair of opposite sides are equal. $B$ and $D$ lie on one pair of opposite sides, $A$ and $C$ on the other, so the segment from $A$ perpendicular to $BD$ and as long as $BD$ ends on the side through $C$. Turning $\\overrightarrow{BD}=(-14,-2)$ a quarter turn gives $(2,-14)$, so $E=A+(2,-14)=(2,-2)$ is on that side, which therefore runs along $CE$, of slope $\\tfrac13$: $x-3y-8=0$. The opposite side through $A$ is $x-3y+36=0$, at distance $\\tfrac{44}{\\sqrt{10}}$, so $K=\\tfrac{1936}{10}$ and $10K=1936$, remainder $936$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-i-15": ["Let the median $CM$ meet the incircle at $X$ and $Y$, with $CX=XY=YM=t$, and let $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB=20$. The power of $C$ is $CX\\cdot CY=2t^2$, which is the square of the tangent length $s-c$; the power of $M$ is also $2t^2$, which is the square of the distance from $M$ to the incircle's touch point on $AB$, namely $\\tfrac{|a-b|}2$. So $\\tfrac{|a-b|}2=s-20=\\tfrac{a+b-20}2$, which forces the smaller of $a$, $b$ to be $10$; take $b=10$. The median formula gives $9t^2=CM^2=\\tfrac{2a^2+2b^2-400}4$, and $2t^2=\\left(\\tfrac{a-10}2\\right)^2$, so $9(a-10)^2=4(a^2-100)$, that is, $9(a-10)=4(a+10)$ and $a=26$. Heron's formula on $20$, $10$, $26$ gives $\\sqrt{28\\cdot2\\cdot18\\cdot8}=24\\sqrt{14}$, so $m+n=38$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-ii-1": ["Write both counts as combinations: $\\binom n6 = 6\\binom n3$. Dividing, $\\tfrac{\\binom n6}{\\binom n3}=\\tfrac{(n-3)(n-4)(n-5)}{120}=6$, so $(n-3)(n-4)(n-5)=720=10\\cdot9\\cdot8$, three consecutive integers, giving $n=13$. Check: $\\binom{13}6=1716=6\\cdot286=6\\binom{13}3$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-ii-2": ["Hand out the bags one at a time, multiplying conditional probabilities. The first guest gets one of each with probability $\\tfrac{9\\cdot6\\cdot3}{9\\cdot8\\cdot7}=\\tfrac9{28}$, the second then with probability $\\tfrac{6\\cdot4\\cdot2}{6\\cdot5\\cdot4}=\\tfrac25$, and the third is forced. The product is $\\tfrac9{70}$, so $m+n=79$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-ii-3": ["The original sum is $\\tfrac a{1-r}=2005$ and the squared series sums to $\\tfrac{a^2}{1-r^2}=20050$. Dividing the second by the first gives $\\tfrac a{1+r}=10$, so $2005(1-r)=10(1+r)$ and $r=\\tfrac{1995}{2015}=\\tfrac{399}{403}$. So $m+n=802$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-ii-4": ["The divisor-count formula gives $121$, $64$ and $276$ divisors for $2^{10}5^{10}$, $3^75^7$ and $2^{11}3^{22}$. Inclusion-exclusion then removes the overlaps, which are the divisors of the pairwise gcds, and adds back the divisors of the three-way gcd, which is just $1$. The pairwise gcds $5^7$, $2^{10}$ and $3^7$ have $8$, $11$ and $8$ divisors, so the count is $121+64+276-8-11-8+1=435$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-ii-5": ["Change of base makes $k = \\log_a b$ the single unknown, turning the equation into $k + \\frac6k = 5$. The quadratic formula gives $k = 2$ or $k = 3$, so $b = a^2$ or $b = a^3$. Counting the $a$ with $a^2 \\le 2005$ gives $43$ values and $a^3 \\le 2005$ gives $11$, for $54$ ordered pairs.","",[]],
"2005-aime-ii-6": ["Count positions from the bottom of the new stack: it reads $n+1,1,n+2,2,\\ldots$, so card $k$ of pile $A$ sits at bottom position $2k$ and card $n+j$ of pile $B$ at $2j-1$. From the top these are positions $2n+1-2k$ and $2n+2-2j$. Card $131$ keeps its place only as a pile $A$ card, since a fixed $B$ card would need $2n+2-2j=n+j$: so $2n+1-262=131$, giving $n=196$. A $B$ card is fixed when $3j=n+2$, and $3\\mid198$ gives $j=66$, card $262$, so the stack is magical. It has $2n=392$ cards.","",[]],
"2005-aime-ii-7": ["Each factor is one half of a difference of squares: $(\\sqrt[2^n]5+1)(\\sqrt[2^n]5-1) = \\sqrt[2^{n-1}]5 - 1$. So rationalizing by multiplying numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt[16]5 - 1$ telescopes the whole denominator down to $5-1 = 4$, leaving $x = \\sqrt[16]5 - 1$. Then $x+1 = 5^{1/16}$ and $(x+1)^{48} = 5^3 = 125$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-ii-8": ["Join the centers. $\\mathcal C_1$ and $\\mathcal C_2$ touch each other and $\\mathcal C_3$ with all centers in line, so together they span a diameter of $\\mathcal C_3$: its radius is $4+10=14$, and its center $O_3$ is $14-4=10$ from $O_1$ and $14-10=4$ from $O_2$, on opposite sides. The chord touches $\\mathcal C_1$ and $\\mathcal C_2$, so $O_1$ and $O_2$ are $4$ and $10$ from it. Distance to a line changes in proportion along a segment, and $O_3$ is $\\tfrac{10}{14}$ of the way from $O_1$ to $O_2$, so $O_3$ is $4+\\tfrac{10}{14}\\cdot6=\\tfrac{58}7$ from the chord. The chord-length formula then gives $2\\sqrt{14^2-\\left(\\tfrac{58}7\\right)^2}=\\tfrac{8\\sqrt{390}}7$, and $m+n+p=405$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-ii-9": ["Rewrite $\\sin t + i\\cos t$ as $\\cos\\left(\\tfrac\\pi2 - t\\right) + i\\sin\\left(\\tfrac\\pi2 - t\\right)$, so De Moivre gives the $n$th power as $\\cos\\left(\\tfrac{n\\pi}2 - nt\\right) + i\\sin\\left(\\tfrac{n\\pi}2 - nt\\right)$. Matching that against $\\sin nt + i\\cos nt$ for all $t$ forces the congruence $\\tfrac{n\\pi}2 \\equiv \\tfrac\\pi2$ modulo $2\\pi$, that is $n \\equiv 1 \\pmod 4$, and there are $250$ such $n$ up to $1000$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-ii-10": ["Scale the octahedron so its vertices are $(\\pm1,0,0)$, $(0,\\pm1,0)$ and $(0,0,\\pm1)$. Each of its eight octants is a tetrahedron of volume $1/6$, so its volume is $8/6=4/3$. Every triangular face has centroid $(\\pm1/3,\\pm1/3,\\pm1/3)$, with one independent sign for each coordinate. These eight points form a cube with side length $2/3$ and volume $8/27$. Thus the ratio is $(4/3)/(8/27)=9/2$, so $m+n=11$. The two solids are different shapes; their volume ratio does not require a similar-solids label.","",[]],
"2005-aime-ii-11": ["Multiplying the recurrence by $a_k$ gives $a_ka_{k+1} = a_{k-1}a_k - 3$, so the product of consecutive terms is a monovariant dropping by exactly $3$ each step. Telescoping from $a_0a_1 = 37 \\cdot 72 = 2664$ gives $a_ka_{k+1} = 2664 - 3k$, which first vanishes at $k = 888$. Since $a_m = 0$, that makes $m = 889$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-ii-12": ["Drop the perpendicular from $O$ to $AB$, meeting it at the midpoint $G$ with $OG=450$, and let $x=EG$, $y=FG$, so $x+y=400$. The angles $EOG$ and $GOF$ have tangents $\\tfrac x{450}$ and $\\tfrac y{450}$ and add to $45^\\circ$, so the tangent addition formula gives $\\tfrac{400/450}{1-xy/450^2}=1$, that is $xy=450^2-400\\cdot450=22500$. So $x$ and $y$ are the roots of $t^2-400t+22500=0$, $t=200\\pm50\\sqrt7$. $AE\\lt BF$ means $F$ is closer to $G$, so $y=200-50\\sqrt7$ and $BF=450-y=250+50\\sqrt7$; $p+q+r=307$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-ii-13": ["Build a shifted polynomial: $P(x)-x+7$ has integer coefficients and vanishes at $17$ and $24$, so by the factor theorem $P(x)-x+7=(x-17)(x-24)R(x)$ with $R$ an integer polynomial. Then $P(n)=n+3$ becomes $(n-17)(n-24)R(n)=10$. With $d=n-17$, the integers $d$ and $d-7$ must both divide $10$ and their product must too, and the only choices are $d=2$ ($2\\cdot(-5)$) and $d=5$ ($5\\cdot(-2)$), each with $R(n)=-1$. So $n_1=19$, $n_2=22$, and the product is $418$.","",["Look for an expression in $P(x)$ that vanishes at both $17$ and $24$.","Once $P(x)$ is written with factors $(x - 17)(x - 24)$ and one unknown constant, the condition on $P(n)$ becomes a product of integers equal to a small number."]],
"2005-aime-ii-14": ["The cevians $AD$ and $AE$ are isogonal, since $\\angle BAE=\\angle CAD$ forces $\\angle BAD=\\angle CAE$. The Ratio Lemma gives $\\tfrac{BD}{DC}=\\tfrac{AB\\sin\\angle BAD}{AC\\sin\\angle CAD}$ and $\\tfrac{BE}{EC}=\\tfrac{AB\\sin\\angle BAE}{AC\\sin\\angle CAE}$; multiplying, the sines cancel and $\\tfrac{BD\\cdot BE}{DC\\cdot EC}=\\tfrac{AB^2}{AC^2}$, which is Steiner's Ratio Theorem. With $BD=9$ and $DC=6$, $\\tfrac{BE}{15-BE}=\\tfrac{169\\cdot6}{196\\cdot9}=\\tfrac{169}{294}$, so $BE=\\tfrac{15\\cdot169}{463}=\\tfrac{2535}{463}$, already in lowest terms ($463$ is prime and $2535=3\\cdot5\\cdot13^2$), and $q=463$.","",[]],
"2005-aime-ii-15": ["Completing the squares, $\\omega_1$ has center $F_1=(-5,12)$ and radius $16$, and $\\omega_2$ has center $F_2=(5,12)$ and radius $4$. A circle of radius $r$ and center $C$ inside $\\omega_1$ and outside $\\omega_2$ has $CF_1=16-r$ and $CF_2=4+r$, so $CF_1+CF_2=20$: the centers lie on the ellipse with foci $F_1$, $F_2$, $a=10$, $c=5$ and $b^2=75$, that is $\\tfrac{x^2}{100}+\\tfrac{(y-12)^2}{75}=1$. The line $y=ax$ meets it when $(3+4a^2)x^2-96ax+276=0$ has a real root; the smallest positive $a$ makes the discriminant zero, $96^2a^2=4\\cdot276(3+4a^2)$, so $4800a^2=3312$ and $a^2=\\tfrac{69}{100}$. Then $p+q=169$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-i-1": ["Two right angles chain two applications of the Pythagorean theorem: $AC^2 = AB^2 + BC^2$ and $AD^2 = AC^2 + CD^2$, so $AD^2 = 18^2 + 21^2 + 14^2 = 961$ and $AD = 31$. The perimeter is $18+21+14+31 = 84$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-i-2": ["The extreme sums come from the smallest and largest ninety elements, and the arithmetic series gives $1+2+\\cdots+90 = 4095$ and $11+12+\\cdots+100 = 4995$. Starting with $1,ldots,90$, raise $90$ to $100$ one unit at a time, then $89$ to $99$, and so on until $1$ reaches $11$. Every move keeps ninety distinct valid integers and raises the sum by one, so every value in between occurs, giving $4995-4095+1 = 901$. Equivalently, count the complementary ten-element subset, whose sum ranges over the same number of values.","",[]],
"2006-aime-i-3": ["Write $N=a\\cdot10^n+N_0$, where $a$ is the leading digit and $N_0\\lt10^n$ is what remains. The condition $N=29N_0$ gives $a\\cdot10^n=28N_0$, so $7\\mid a\\cdot10^n$, and since $7\\nmid10^n$ the digit is $a=7$. Then $N_0=\\tfrac{7\\cdot10^n}{28}=25\\cdot10^{n-2}$, which needs $n\\ge2$, and the least case $n=2$ gives $N_0=25$ and $N=725$ (indeed $725=29\\cdot25$).","",[]],
"2006-aime-i-4": ["Trailing zeros count factors of $5$, which are scarcer than factors of $2$. Legendre's formula gives the exponent of $5$ in each $k!$ as $\\lfloor\\tfrac k5\\rfloor+\\lfloor\\tfrac k{25}\\rfloor$. Over $k=1$ to $100$, the multiple $5m$ is reached by $101-5m$ values of $k$, so the first parts sum to the arithmetic series $96+91+\\cdots+1=970$, and the second parts to $76+51+26+1=154$. So $N=1124$, and the remainder is $124$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-i-5": ["Denest by assuming the radicand is the square of $a\\sqrt2+b\\sqrt3+c\\sqrt5$. Expanding that square gives $2a^2+3b^2+5c^2+2ab\\sqrt6+2ac\\sqrt{10}+2bc\\sqrt{15}$, so matching the surd coefficients gives $ab=52$, $ac=234$, $bc=72$. Multiplying all three gives $(abc)^2=876096$, so $abc=936$; indeed $a=13$, $b=4$, $c=18$ give $2\\cdot169+3\\cdot16+5\\cdot324=2006$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-i-6": ["A three-digit repeat is $\\frac{\\overline{abc}}{999}$, and there are $10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8 = 720$ choices with distinct digits. Pair each with the number whose digits are its nines-complement: the two sum to $0.\\overline{999} = 1$, and distinctness is preserved. So the $720$ numbers form $360$ pairs each summing to $1$, giving a total of $360$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-i-7": ["Number the parallel lines $0,1,2,\\dots$ at unit spacing and put the vertex of the angle at distance $a$ past line $0$, with $0\\lt a\\lt1$. The angle cuts off similar triangles from the vertex to each line, with sizes proportional to their distances $1-a$, $2-a$, $3-a$, and so on, so their areas are proportional to the squares. Region $\\mathcal A$ is the first triangle, area $\\propto(1-a)^2$, and each other region is the difference of two consecutive triangles: $\\mathcal B\\propto(3-a)^2-(2-a)^2=5-2a$, $\\mathcal C\\propto9-2a$ and $\\mathcal D\\propto13-2a$. Then $\\tfrac{9-2a}{5-2a}=\\tfrac{11}5$ gives $a=\\tfrac56$, and $\\tfrac{\\mathcal D}{\\mathcal A}=\\tfrac{13-5/3}{1/36}=408$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-i-8": ["With common side $x$ and acute angle $y$, the sine area formula gives each congruent rhombus area $x^2\\sin y=\\sqrt{2006}$, and the figure gives rhombus $\\mathcal T$ the same side and angle $2y$, so $K=x^2\\sin2y$. The double angle formula turns that into $K=2x^2\\sin y\\cos y=2\\sqrt{2006}\\cos y$, which takes every value in $\\left(0,2\\sqrt{2006}\\right)$ as $y$ varies. Since $2\\sqrt{2006}=\\sqrt{8024}$ lies between $89$ and $90$, $K$ can be any of $1,2,\\dots,89$: there are $89$ values.","",[]],
"2006-aime-i-9": ["The product rule collapses the twelve logarithms into $\\log_8\\left(a^{12}r^{0+1+\\cdots+11}\\right)$, and the arithmetic series in the exponent sums to $66$. So $a^{12}r^{66}=8^{2006}=2^{6018}$, which reduces to $a^2r^{11}=2^{1003}$. Writing $a=2^x$ and $r=2^y$ turns it into $2x+11y=1003$; $y$ must be odd and at most $91$, so $y=1,3,\\dots,91$ give $46$ pairs.","",[]],
"2006-aime-i-10": ["A line through the point where two congruent circles touch splits that pair into congruent halves, by the half-turn symmetry about the tangency point. The line of slope $3$ through the tangency points $\\left(1,\\tfrac12\\right)$ (bottom circles of the first two columns) and $\\left(\\tfrac32,2\\right)$ (upper two circles of the second column) halves those four circles, and it leaves two whole circles on each side, so it halves the region. Its equation is $y-\\tfrac12=3(x-1)$, that is, $6x=2y+5$, and $a^2+b^2+c^2=36+4+25=65$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-i-11": ["Build up by inserting the largest cube last. Given a valid tower of cubes $1$ through $m$, the cube of edge $m+1$ can go at the bottom, where nothing is beneath it, or directly above the cube of edge $m$ or of edge $m-1$; on top of any smaller cube it would break the $k+2$ rule. Each of the three is legal, and every tower arises once: removing the largest cube leaves a valid tower, because if a cube was above it, that cube (edge at most $m$) now sits on the cube that was below it, of edge $m$ or $m-1$. So $T_{m+1}=3T_m$ for $m\\ge2$, and from $T_2=2$ this gives $T_8=2\\cdot3^6=1458$, whose remainder is $458$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-i-12": ["Sum-to-product gives $2\\cos4x\\cos x=\\cos5x+\\cos3x$, so with $a=\\cos3x$ and $b=\\cos5x$ the equation reads $a^3+b^3=(a+b)^3$. Since $a^3+b^3=(a+b)(a^2-ab+b^2)$, the difference is $(a+b)^3-(a^3+b^3)=3ab(a+b)$, so the equation holds exactly when $\\cos3x=0$, $\\cos5x=0$, or $\\cos4x\\cos x=0$. In $(100,200)$ these give $x=150$; $126$, $162$, $198$; $112.5$, $157.5$; and none from $\\cos x=0$. The sum is $906$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-i-13": ["Splitting the sum by odd and even arguments gives $S_n = 2^{n-1} + 2S_{n-1}$, and solving that linear recurrence yields $S_n = (n+1)2^{n-1}$. Then casework on the parity of $n-1$: when it is even, $S_n$ is a square exactly when $n+1$ is; when odd, exactly when $2(n+1)$ is. The largest $n \\lt 1000$ meeting either condition is $899$, since $900 = 30^2$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-i-14": ["Each foot is $\\sqrt{5^2-4^2}=3$ from the point below the top, so put the feet at $3$ from the origin, $120^\\circ$ apart, with the top at $T=(0,0,4)$. Breaking $1$ foot off one leg leaves its end $\\tfrac45$ of the way from $T$, at $(2.4,0,0.8)$ when that foot was $(3,0,0)$. The new ground plane passes through this point and the two other feet, and the distance from $T$ to it is $h=\\tfrac{144}{\\sqrt{1585}}$. Since $39\\lt\\sqrt{1585}\\lt40$, $\\lfloor m+\\sqrt n\\rfloor=183$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-i-15": ["Scale by $b_i = \\frac{x_i}3$, so $b_k^2 = (b_{k-1}+1)^2$. Summing that over $k$ telescopes the squares away and leaves $\\sum_{i=1}^{2006} b_i = \\frac{(b_{2006}+1)^2 - 2007}2$. Since $b_k=\\pm(b_{k-1}+1)$, each step flips the parity, so $b_k\\equiv k\\pmod2$ and $b_{2006}$ is even, making $(b_{2006}+1)^2$ an odd square. The odd squares nearest $2007$ are $45^2=2025$ and $43^2=1849$, and $2025$ is closer: $\\tfrac{2025-2007}2=9$. It is reachable: climb $b_k=k$ up to $b_{44}=44$, then alternate $44,-45,44,\\ldots$, which returns to $44$ at every even step. So the minimum of $\\left|\\sum x_i\\right|=3\\left|\\sum b_i\\right|$ is $3\\cdot9=27$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-ii-1": ["The angles of a hexagon sum to $720^\\circ$, so the four equal angles are each $\\tfrac{720-180}4=135^\\circ$. With side $x$, triangles $FAB$ and $CDE$ are right isosceles, so $BF$ and $CE$ are $45$-$45$-$90$ hypotenuses of length $x\\sqrt2$, and what is left of the angles at $B$, $C$, $E$, $F$ is $135^\\circ-45^\\circ=90^\\circ$, which makes $BCEF$ a rectangle with sides $x$ and $x\\sqrt2$. The area is $x^2\\sqrt2+2\\cdot\\tfrac12x^2=x^2(\\sqrt2+1)$, so $x^2=2116$ and $AB=46$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-ii-2": ["Apply the triangle inequality to the three logarithms and use the product rule to combine each pair: $\\log12+\\log n\\gt\\log75$ becomes $12n\\gt75$, so $n\\gt6.25$, and $\\log12+\\log75\\gt\\log n$ becomes $n\\lt900$; the third, $\\log75+\\log n\\gt\\log12$, always holds. The integers from $7$ to $899$ number $893$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-ii-3": ["The product of the first $100$ odd numbers is $P=\\tfrac{200!}{2^{100}\\cdot100!}$, since $2\\cdot4\\cdots200=2^{100}\\cdot100!$. Powers of $2$ do not affect the count of $3$s, so $k=v_3(200!)-v_3(100!)$. Legendre's formula gives $v_3(200!)=66+22+7+2=97$ and $v_3(100!)=33+11+3+1=48$, so $k=49$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-ii-4": ["The valley forces $a_6 = 1$. Choosing which five of the remaining eleven values go to the left determines the whole permutation, since each side must then be sorted, so the map from five-element subsets to valid permutations is a bijection. Hence the count is $\\binom{11}5 = 462$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-ii-5": ["Let the two altered faces have probabilities $\\tfrac16\\pm x$. A sum of $7$ pairs each face with its opposite, so its probability is $4\\cdot\\tfrac1{36}+2\\left(\\tfrac16+x\\right)\\left(\\tfrac16-x\\right)=\\tfrac16-2x^2$. Setting this to $\\tfrac{47}{288}$ gives $2x^2=\\tfrac1{288}$, $x=\\tfrac1{24}$, so face $F$ has probability $\\tfrac16+\\tfrac1{24}=\\tfrac5{24}$ and $m+n=29$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-ii-6": ["Put $A$ at the origin with $B=(1,0)$ and $D=(0,1)$. The triangle is symmetric about the diagonal $AC$, so $CE=CF=x$ and $EF=x\\sqrt2$, and $AF=EF$ with the Pythagorean theorem in triangle $ADF$ gives $1+(1-x)^2=2x^2$, so $x=\\sqrt3-1$ and $BE=2-\\sqrt3$. The small square of side $s$ has its far corner at $(1-s,s)$ on $AE$, and the right triangle that corner cuts off with $AB$ is similar to triangle $ABE$, so $\\tfrac{s}{1-s}=\\tfrac{BE}{AB}=2-\\sqrt3$. Then $s=\\tfrac{2-\\sqrt3}{3-\\sqrt3}=\\tfrac{3-\\sqrt3}6$, and $a+b+c=12$.","Alternatively, chase the angle at $A$: $\\angle EAF=60^\\circ$, and the symmetry about $AC$ splits the remaining $30^\\circ$ of the right angle equally, so $\\angle BAE=15^\\circ$. Then $\\tfrac{s}{1-s}=\\tan15^\\circ=2-\\sqrt3$ at once, with no equation for $E$.",[]],
"2006-aime-ii-7": ["Count directly by the digits of $a$, since those of $b=1000-a$ follow from them. A units digit $u\\ne0$ is needed (otherwise $10\\mid a$), and then $b$'s units digit is $10-u\\ne0$, its tens digit is $9-t$ and its hundreds digit $9-h$ for $a=100h+10t+u$. If $a$ has three digits with $h\\le8$, $b$ has three digits too, so $h,t\\in\\{1,\\ldots,8\\}$ and $u\\in\\{1,\\ldots,9\\}$: $8\\cdot8\\cdot9=576$. If $h=9$, $b\\lt100$, so its leading digit may drop: $t\\le8$ gives $72$ and $t=9$ gives $9$, $81$ in all. A two-digit $a$ makes $b\\gt900$ with tens digit $9-t$, so $t\\le8$: $72$. A one-digit $a$ gives $b=1000-u$, always fine: $9$. The total is $576+81+72+9=738$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-ii-8": ["Count colorings of the center and three corner triangles up to the six symmetries of the large triangle with Burnside's lemma. The identity fixes all $6^4=1296$. Each of the two rotations cycles the three corners, so the corners match and $6^2=36$ colorings are fixed. Each of the three reflections swaps two corners and fixes one, fixing $6^3=216$. The count is $\\tfrac{1296+2\\cdot36+3\\cdot216}6=\\tfrac{2016}6=336$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-ii-9": ["An internal common tangent passes through the internal center of similitude of the two circles, the point dividing the segment of centers in the ratio of the radii. For $\\mathcal C_1$ and $\\mathcal C_2$ that is $(4,0)$, and for $\\mathcal C_2$ and $\\mathcal C_3$ it is $(16,0)$. From $(4,0)$ the tangent to $\\mathcal C_1$ makes a right triangle with hypotenuse $4$ and radius $1$, so by the Pythagorean theorem its slope is $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt{15}}$; from $(16,0)$ the tangent to $\\mathcal C_2$ has hypotenuse $4$ and radius $2$, so its slope is $-\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}$. Solving $\\tfrac{x-4}{\\sqrt{15}}=\\tfrac{16-x}{\\sqrt3}$ gives $x=\\tfrac{4+16\\sqrt5}{1+\\sqrt5}=19-3\\sqrt5$, so $p+q+r=27$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-ii-10": ["Apart from their game with each other, $A$ and $B$ each play five independent games. Having already won, $A$ finishes ahead unless $B$ wins more of those five. A tie over the five has probability $\\sum_k\\binom5k^2/2^{10}=\\tfrac{252}{1024}=\\tfrac{63}{256}$, and by symmetry each of the other two outcomes has probability $\\tfrac12\\left(1-\\tfrac{63}{256}\\right)=\\tfrac{193}{512}$. So $A$ finishes ahead with probability $\\tfrac{193}{512}+\\tfrac{126}{512}=\\tfrac{319}{512}$, and $m+n=831$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-ii-11": ["Rearranging the recurrence gives $a_n=a_{n+3}-a_{n+2}-a_{n+1}$, so summing that from $n=1$ to $27$ telescopes the three shifted sums against each other. What survives is a relation between the wanted sum $s$ and the known terms, $2s=a_{28}+a_{30}-a_3+a_1=26693668$, so $s=13346834$ without computing any middle term, and the remainder is $834$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-ii-12": ["$ADFE$ is a parallelogram, so $\\angle AEF=180^\\circ-60^\\circ=120^\\circ$ and $EF=AD=13$. $G$ is on arc $BC$, so $\\angle BGC=180^\\circ-\\angle A=120^\\circ$, and $\\angle GBC=\\angle GAC=\\angle FAE$ on the same arc; so $\\triangle GBC\\sim\\triangle AEF$, with $BC$ matching $AF$. The Law of Cosines gives $AF^2=11^2+13^2+11\\cdot13=433$, and the side of the equilateral triangle in a circle of radius $2$ is $BC=2\\sqrt3$. The sine area formula gives $[AEF]=\\tfrac12\\cdot11\\cdot13\\sin120^\\circ=\\tfrac{143\\sqrt3}4$, and scaling by $\\tfrac{BC^2}{AF^2}=\\tfrac{12}{433}$ gives $[CBG]=\\tfrac{429\\sqrt3}{433}$. So $p+q+r=865$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-ii-13": ["Starting at $2n+1$ and taking $j$ consecutive odd numbers, the arithmetic series sums to $N=j(2n+j)$. So each valid $j$ is a divisor with $j\\le\\tfrac Nj$ and $\\tfrac Nj$ of the same parity as $j$. For odd $N$ every factor pair works, so exactly five values means $\\tau(N)=9$ or $10$: $225$, $441$ and $405$, $567$, $891$. Even $N$ must be $4M$ with both factors even, so the count is $\\lceil\\tfrac{\\tau(M)}2\\rceil$ and $\\tau(M)=9$ or $10$ with $M\\lt250$: $M=36,100,196,225$ and $48,80,112,176,208,162$. That is $5+10=15$ values of $N$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-ii-14": ["Count how often each nonzero digit appears. Among the numbers below $10^n$ each digit occupies each of the $n$ places $10^{n-1}$ times, so with $K = \\sum_{i=1}^9 \\frac1i$ we get $S_n = n10^{n-1}K + 1$. In lowest terms $K=\\frac{7129}{2520}$, whose denominator is the lcm of $1$ through $9$, $2^3\\cdot3^2\\cdot5\\cdot7$, and $7129$ shares no factor with it. So $S_n$ is an integer exactly when $2520\\mid n10^{n-1}$. The power of $10$ supplies the $2^3$ and the $5$ once $n\\ge4$, so the condition is $63\\mid n$, and the smallest $n$ is $63$.","",[]],
"2006-aime-ii-15": ["Read each equation as a triangle split by an altitude. If $x$, $y$, $z$ are the sides of an acute triangle and the altitude to side $x$ is $\\tfrac14$, the Pythagorean theorem gives the two pieces of $x$ as $\\sqrt{y^2-\\tfrac1{16}}$ and $\\sqrt{z^2-\\tfrac1{16}}$, which is the first equation. So the altitudes to $x$, $y$, $z$ are $\\tfrac14$, $\\tfrac15$, $\\tfrac16$, and since $\\tfrac12\\cdot\\text{side}\\cdot\\text{altitude}$ is the same area $K$ each time, the sides are $4t$, $5t$, $6t$ with $K=\\tfrac t2$. Heron's formula gives this triangle the area $\\tfrac{15\\sqrt7}4t^2$, so $t=\\tfrac{2}{15\\sqrt7}$ and $x+y+z=15t=\\tfrac2{\\sqrt7}$. So $m+n=9$.","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-24": ["By the multinomial theorem the term $x^ay^bz^c$ appears in the second expansion with sign $(-1)^{b+c}$, so adding the two doubles the terms with $b+c$ even and cancels the rest; since $a+b+c=2006$, that means $a$ is even. For each even $a$ the pair $(b,c)$ has $2007-a$ choices, so the count is $2007+2005+\\cdots+1=1004^2=1008016$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-aime-i-1": ["Since $24 = 2^3 \\cdot 3$, a square divisible by $24$ needs at least $2^4 \\cdot 3^2$, so its root must be a multiple of $12$. The squares below $10^6$ have roots below $1000$, so the count is the number of multiples of $12$ up to $999$, namely $\\left\\lfloor\\frac{999}{12}\\right\\rfloor = 83$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-i-2": ["On the ground the three move at $6$, $4+6=10$ and $8$ feet per second, starting at times $0$, $2$ and $4$, so at time $t\\ge4$ they are at $6t$, $10(t-2)$ and $8(t-4)$ (distance equals rate times time). Case on who is in the middle. Al: $6t=\\tfrac{10(t-2)+8(t-4)}2=9t-26$ gives $t=\\tfrac{26}3$, with Bob at $\\tfrac{200}3$, Al at $52$ and Cy at $\\tfrac{112}3$, all still on the walkway, so this works. Bob in the middle needs $10(t-2)=7t-16$, $t=\\tfrac43$, before Cy starts, and Cy in the middle needs $8(t-4)=8t-10$, which has no solution. So the middle person is $52$ feet from the start.","",[]],
"2007-aime-i-3": ["Expand with the binomial theorem: $(9+bi)^2 = 81 - b^2 + 18bi$, and cubing gives an imaginary part of $243b - b^3$. Setting the two imaginary parts equal gives $18b = 243b - b^3$, so $b(b^2-225) = 0$ and the positive solution is $b = 15$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-i-4": ["Collinearity with the star depends only on the differences of the planets' angles, so work in relative terms: two planets are collinear with the star whenever their angle difference is a multiple of $\\pi$, that is at multiples of half their synodic period $\\frac1{1/T_1-1/T_2}$. For the pairs $(60,84)$, $(60,140)$ and $(84,140)$ the synodic periods are $210$, $105$ and $210$ years, so the half-periods are $105$, $52.5$ and $105$. Two of the pairs being collinear already forces the third, and the first time the half-periods $105$ and $52.5$ share a multiple is $n=105$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-i-5": ["Write $F-32=9x+r$ with $0\\le r\\le8$. Then $C=5x+\\tfrac{5r}9$ rounds to $5x+c$ with $c$ the nearest integer to $\\tfrac{5r}9$, and converting back gives $32+9x+\\tfrac{9c}5$, which rounds to $32+9x$ plus the nearest integer to $\\tfrac{9c}5$ (no value lands on a half). For $r=0,\\ldots,8$, $c=0,1,1,2,2,3,3,4,4$, and these return $0,2,2,4,4,5,5,7,7$, so exactly $r\\in\\{0,2,4,5,7\\}$ come back unchanged. The $969$ values of $T-32$ from $0$ to $968$ are $107$ full cycles of $9$ and then $r=0,\\ldots,5$, giving $107\\cdot5+4=539$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-i-6": ["Let $f(p)$ count the move sequences reaching each reachable point $p$. From any point the frog jumps to the next multiple of $3$ or the next multiple of $13$, so $f$ at a point is the sum of $f$ over its predecessors, a recursion built up along the line. Each of $0,3,6,9,12$ is reached one way and jumps to $13$, so $f(13)=5$; then $f(15)=f(12)+f(13)=6$ and $f(18)=f(21)=f(24)=6$; $26$ is reached from $13,15,18,21,24$, so $f(26)=5+4\\cdot6=29$; then $f(27)=29+6=35$ and $f(30)=\\cdots=f(36)=35$; and $39$ is reached from $26,27,30,33,36$, so $f(39)=29+4\\cdot35=169$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-i-7": ["The ceiling minus the floor is $0$ when the argument is an integer and $1$ otherwise. Change of base gives $\\log_{\\sqrt2}k=\\tfrac{2\\log k}{\\log2}=2\\log_2k$, an integer exactly when $k$ is a power of $2$ (for integer $k$). So $N$ is the sum of all $k$ from $1$ to $1000$ minus the geometric series of powers of $2$ up to $512$: $500500-1023=499477$, whose remainder is $477$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-i-8": ["Two quadratics can both divide a cubic only if they share a root $a$ (otherwise their product, of degree $4$, would divide it). Then $a$ is also a root of $Q_2-2Q_1=15x+3k$, so $a=-\\tfrac k5$. Putting it into $Q_1$: $\\tfrac{k^2}{25}-\\tfrac{(k-29)k}5-k=0$, that is $-4k^2+120k=0$, so $k=0$ or $k=30$. The largest is $30$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-i-9": ["The hypotenuse is $34$, and the triangle is $8$-$15$-$17$ doubled, so $\\sin A=\\tfrac8{17}$, $\\cos A=\\tfrac{15}{17}$ and $\\sin B=\\tfrac{15}{17}$, $\\cos B=\\tfrac8{17}$. The circle at $A$ lies in the exterior angle there, so its center is on that angle's bisector and the tangent length from $A$ to its point on $AB$ is $r\\tan\\tfrac A2=r\\cdot\\tfrac{\\sin A}{1+\\cos A}=\\tfrac r4$; likewise the tangent length from $B$ is $r\\tan\\tfrac B2=\\tfrac{3r}5$. The two equal circles touch each other and both touch $AB$, so their points on $AB$ are $2r$ apart. So $\\tfrac r4+2r+\\tfrac{3r}5=\\tfrac{57r}{20}=34$, $r=\\tfrac{680}{57}$, and $p+q=737$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-i-10": ["Fix the first column: $\\binom63=20$ choices of which rows it shades, and by relabeling rows assume it is rows $1$ to $3$. Then rows $1$ to $3$ need one more shaded square among the other three columns and rows $4$ to $6$ need two, so each of rows $4$ to $6$ misses exactly one column. If $x_j$ of rows $4$ to $6$ miss column $j$, that column gets $3-x_j$ from them, so exactly $x_j$ of rows $1$ to $3$ must choose it. For a given $(x_1,x_2,x_3)$ both groups can be arranged in $\\tfrac{3!}{x_1!x_2!x_3!}$ ways, so the count is $3\\cdot1^2+6\\cdot3^2+1\\cdot6^2=93$ (types $(3,0,0)$, $(2,1,0)$, $(1,1,1)$). So $N=20\\cdot93=1860$ and the remainder is $860$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-i-11": ["Rounding $\\sqrt p$ to $k$ means $\\left(k-\\tfrac12\\right)^2\\lt p\\lt\\left(k+\\tfrac12\\right)^2$, that is $k^2-k\\lt p\\le k^2+k$, a block of exactly $2k$ integers each contributing $k$. Since $44\\cdot45=1980\\le2007$, the full blocks $k=1,\\dots,44$ add $2\\sum k^2=2\\cdot\\tfrac{44\\cdot45\\cdot89}6=58740$ by the sum-of-squares formula. The leftover $p=1981,\\dots,2007$ are $27$ terms with $b(p)=45$, adding $1215$. So $S=59955$ and the remainder is $955$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-i-12": ["The base angles are $75^\\circ$, so $AC=\\tfrac{10}{\\cos75^\\circ}$, and $C$ is at $75^\\circ$ from the $x$-axis: the rotation is by $15^\\circ$, taking $B$ to $B'$ at $15^\\circ$. The common region is triangle $AB'P_3$, where $P_3$ is where $B'C'$ crosses $AC$, minus the small triangle $P_1B'P_2$ that $BC$ cuts off near $B'$. Angle chasing gives what is needed: $AB'$ makes $15^\\circ$ with $AB$ and $\\angle B=75^\\circ$, so $AB'$ meets $BC$ at a right angle at $P_1$, with $AP_1=20\\cos15^\\circ$; triangle $AB'P_3$ has a $60^\\circ$ angle at $A$ and the $75^\\circ$ base angle at $B'$, so $AP_3=10+10\\sqrt3$. The sine area formula gives $[AB'P_3]=\\tfrac12\\cdot20\\cdot(10+10\\sqrt3)\\sin60^\\circ=150+50\\sqrt3$, and the right triangle $P_1B'P_2$ with angle $75^\\circ$ at $B'$ has area $\\tfrac12(20-20\\cos15^\\circ)^2\\tan75^\\circ$. Expanding, the overlap is $500\\sqrt2-350\\sqrt3+300\\sqrt6-600$, so $\\tfrac{p-q+r-s}2=\\tfrac{500+350+300+600}2=875$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-i-13": ["Put the base at $(\\pm2,\\pm2,0)$ and the apex at $(0,0,2\\sqrt2)$, so the midpoints are $(1,1,\\sqrt2)$ on $AE$, $(-2,0,0)$ on $BC$ and $(0,-2,0)$ on $CD$. The plane through them is $x+y-2\\sqrt2z=-2$, and it also cuts $BE$ and $DE$ at $\\left(-\\tfrac32,\\tfrac32,\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2\\right)$ and $\\left(\\tfrac32,-\\tfrac32,\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2\\right)$, making a pentagon. Its shadow on the base has shoelace area $8$, and the normal $(1,1,-2\\sqrt2)$ makes $\\cos\\theta=\\tfrac{2\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt{10}}$ with the vertical. The area is $\\tfrac8{\\cos\\theta}=\\sqrt{80}$, so $p=80$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-i-14": ["Add the recurrence at $n$ and at $n-1$ to get $a_{n-1}(a_{n-1}+a_{n+1}) = a_n(a_n + a_{n-2})$, so $\\frac{a_{n+1}+a_{n-1}}{a_n}$ never changes: the nonlinear recurrence is secretly the linear one $a_{n+1} = 225a_n - a_{n-1}$, since $a_3 = 672$ gives the constant $225$. With $b_n = a_n/a_{n-1}$ the target is $b_{2007} + \\frac1{b_{2007}}$, which sits just under $b_{2007} + \\frac1{b_{2006}} = 225$, so the floor is $224$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-i-15": ["Let the side be $x$ and $AE=y$, so $CE=x-y$. Since $\\angle DEF=60^\\circ=\\angle A=\\angle C$, the angles $AEF$ and $CED$ add to $120^\\circ$, as do $AEF$ and $AFE$, so $\\angle AFE=\\angle CED$ and $\\triangle AFE\\sim\\triangle CED$: $\\tfrac{AF}{CE}=\\tfrac{AE}{CD}$, that is, $y(x-y)=10$. The big triangle is $\\triangle DEF$ plus three corner triangles with $60^\\circ$ angles, so by the sine area formula $x^2=56+5y+(x-5)(x-2)+2(x-y)$, which gives $y=\\tfrac{5x-66}3$. Substituting, $(5x-66)(66-2x)=90$, that is, $5x^2-231x+2223=0$. Its roots are $\\tfrac{231\\pm\\sqrt{8901}}{10}$, and $8901=9\\cdot989$, so $r=989$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-ii-1": ["Seven distinct characters are available ($A,I,M,E,2,0,7$) and only $0$ may appear twice, so split on the number of $0$s. With at most one $0$ every character is used at most once: $7\\cdot6\\cdot5\\cdot4\\cdot3=2520$ sequences. With two $0$s, choose their positions in $\\binom52=10$ ways and fill the other three places from the remaining six characters in $6\\cdot5\\cdot4=120$ ways: $1200$. So $N=3720$ and $\\tfrac N{10}=372$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-ii-2": ["Write $b = ax$ and $c = ay$, so the condition becomes $a(1+x+y) = 100$ and $1+x+y$ must be one of the nine divisors of $100 = 2^25^2$. For each, $x+y$ is fixed and stars and bars counts the positive solutions as one fewer than that sum. The divisors $4,5,10,20,25,50,100$ give $2+3+8+18+23+48+98$ (and $1$, $2$ give none), so there are $200$ triples.","",[]],
"2007-aime-ii-3": ["The symmetry makes $BEDF$ a parallelogram with sides $5$ and $\\sqrt{433}$, the latter from the Law of Cosines on $\\triangle FCB$ using the $13$ and $12$ legs and the angle at $C$. Apollonius's theorem in its parallelogram form says the sum of the squares of the four sides equals the sum of the squares of the diagonals, so with $DB^2 = 338$ we get $EF^2 = 2(25+433) - 338 = 578$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-ii-4": ["Let $x$ and $y$ be the worker-hours to make one widget and one whoosit. Worker-hours multiply, so the three scenarios read $100 = 300x+200y$, $120 = 240x+300y$ and $150 = 150x+my$. The first two are $3x+2y=1$ and $4x+5y=2$, which give $x=\\tfrac17$ and $y=\\tfrac27$. The third then reads $150=\\tfrac{150}7+\\tfrac{2m}7$, so $m=450$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-ii-5": ["The intercepts $(223,0)$ and $(0,9)$ bound a rectangle of $223 \\cdot 9 = 2007$ unit squares. The diagonal passes through $223 + 9 - \\gcd(223,9) = 231$ of them, since that greatest common divisor is $1$, and those are exactly the squares the line cuts rather than lies above. Counting the complement, removing them leaves $2007-231$ squares split evenly by symmetry, so half of that, $888$, lie strictly below.","",[]],
"2007-aime-ii-6": ["Build the number by prepending a digit. Whatever digit currently leads, exactly four choices of new leading digit satisfy the parity rule, since an odd digit needs a larger successor and an even digit a smaller one and the counts work out to four either way. The new leading digit is never $0$, since an even $0$ would need a smaller digit after it. So the recursion is $S_n = 4S_{n-1}$, and from the $10$ one-digit endings it gives $10\\cdot4^3=640$ four-digit numbers.","",[]],
"2007-aime-ii-7": ["The floor of the cube root equals $k$ exactly on the block $k^3\\le n\\lt(k+1)^3$. Its multiples of $k$ are $km$ with $k^2\\le m\\le\\frac{(k+1)^3-1}k=k^2+3k+3$, which is $3k+4$ of them. Setting $3k+4=70$ gives $k=22$, and the largest $\\frac{n_i}k$ is $k^2+3k+3=484+66+3=553$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-ii-8": ["With $x$ horizontal and $y$ vertical lines the basic rectangles number $(x-1)(y-1)$, and the total length condition is $5x + 4y = 2007$. Substituting $y = \\frac{2007-5x}4$ makes the count a downward quadratic in $x$, whose vertex from completing the square locates the real maximum; the count is $\\tfrac{(x-1)(2003-5x)}4$, largest at $x=200.8$. Then $y$ must be an integer, which needs $x\\equiv3\\pmod4$, so the nearest candidates are $x=199$ (count $198\\cdot252=49896$) and $x=203$ ($202\\cdot247=49894$). (With the lines the other way round, $4x+5y=2007$, the same maximum $49896$ comes back.) So $N=49896$ and the remainder is $896$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-ii-9": ["The lengths hide Pythagorean triples: $BE=DF=\\sqrt{63^2+84^2}=105$ ($3$-$4$-$5$ times $21$), and $EF=\\sqrt{63^2+280^2}=287$ ($9$-$40$-$41$ times $7$), with $BF=DE=448-84=364$. Both triangles have semiperimeter $\\tfrac{105+364+287}2=378$. The incircle's touch point on a side sits $s$ minus the opposite side from a vertex, so $EP=378-BF=14$ in triangle $BEF$ and $EQ=378-DF=273$ in triangle $DEF$. Then $PQ=273-14=259$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-ii-10": ["Build each pair element by element: classify each of the six by its membership in $A$ and in $B$, four categories. $B \\subseteq A$ forbids the in-$B$-not-in-$A$ category, leaving $3^6$ pairs, and $B \\subseteq S-A$ likewise gives $3^6$. Inclusion-exclusion subtracts the overlap, where $B$ is empty, $2^6$ pairs, so the probability is $\\frac{2 \\cdot 3^6 - 2^6}{4^6}=\\tfrac{1394}{4096}=\\tfrac{697}{2^{11}}$, and $m+n+r=697+2+11=710$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-ii-11": ["Count the big tube's turning, since one full revolution is the stopping rule; on the ground each radian of turning moves it $72$. Join the centers: while the big tube is on the small one they stay $72+24=96$ apart, and when it touches both the small tube and the ground the centers differ in height by $72-24=48$, half of $96$. So the line of centers forms a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle with legs $48$ and $48\\sqrt3$: it starts and ends $30^\\circ$ above the horizontal, swinging through $120^\\circ$, while the big center moves $2\\cdot48\\sqrt3=96\\sqrt3$ to the right. With no slipping the two tubes trade equal arcs, $24\\cdot\\tfrac{2\\pi}3=16\\pi$, so the big tube turns $\\tfrac{16\\pi}{72}=\\tfrac{2\\pi}9$ against the line of centers, which itself turns $\\tfrac{2\\pi}3$, a total of $\\tfrac{8\\pi}9$. The other $\\tfrac{10\\pi}9$ is spent on the ground, which moves it $72\\cdot\\tfrac{10\\pi}9=80\\pi$. So $x=80\\pi+96\\sqrt3$ and $a+b+c=179$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-ii-12": ["Set $y_n = \\log_3 x_n$; since the $x_n$ are powers of $3$ in geometric progression, the $y_n$ form an arithmetic sequence. The sum $\\sum_{n=0}^7 y_n = 308$ is an arithmetic series equal to $4(y_0+y_7)$, and the bound forces $y_7 = 56$: with common difference $d\\ge1$ the sum is $3^{y_7}\\left(1+3^{-d}+3^{-2d}+\\cdots\\right)$, between $3^{y_7}$ and $\\tfrac32\\cdot3^{y_7}$, so its $\\log_3$ lies in $[y_7,y_7+1)$, and $56\\le\\log_3\\le57$ leaves only $y_7=56$. So $y_0 = 21$ and $y_{14} = 2y_7 - y_0 = 91$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-ii-13": ["Each bottom entry reaches the top along Pascal paths, so the top square equals $\\sum_{k=0}^{10}\\binom{10}kx_k$. Modulo $3$, Lucas' theorem with $10=101_3$ says $\\binom{10}k\\not\\equiv0$ only for $k=0,1,9,10$ (base-three digits under $1,0,1$), and those four coefficients are all $\\equiv1$. So the condition is $x_0+x_1+x_9+x_{10}\\equiv0\\pmod3$: the four are all $0$ ($1$ way) or three of them are $1$ ($4$ ways). The other seven entries are free, so there are $5\\cdot2^7=640$ distributions.","",[]],
"2007-aime-ii-14": ["Substituting a root $r$ into $f(x)f(2x^2)=f(2x^3+x)$ shows that $r(2r^2+1)$ is also a root. Comparing leading coefficients ($a\\cdot a2^n=a2^n$) makes $f$ monic, so by the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra $f$ is the product of $x-r$ over its roots, and $|f(0)|=1$ is the product of their moduli. A root with $|r|\\gt1$ would give a root of larger modulus, $|r|(2|r|^2-1)\\gt|r|$, and so infinitely many; hence every root has $|r|\\le1$, and since the moduli multiply to $1$, every $|r|=1$. Then $|2r^2+1|=1$ with $|r^2|=1$ forces $r^2=-1$, so the roots are $\\pm i$ and, with real coefficients, $f(x)=(x^2+1)^n$, which does satisfy the equation since $(x^2+1)(4x^4+1)=(2x^3+x)^2+1$. Now $5^n+10^n=125$ gives $n=2$, and $f(5)=26^2=676$.","",[]],
"2007-aime-ii-15": ["The $13$-$14$-$15$ triangle has area $84$, inradius $\\rho=4$ and circumradius $R=\\tfrac{abc}{4K}=\\tfrac{65}8$. Each corner circle of radius $r$ has its center on an angle bisector at distance $r$ from two sides, so the three centers form the triangle $ABC$ scaled about the incenter by $\\tfrac{\\rho-r}\\rho$, whose circumradius is $R\\cdot\\tfrac{4-r}4$. The circle $\\omega$ is tangent to all three, so its center is $2r$ from each of them: it is the circumcenter of that small triangle, and $2r=\\tfrac{65}8\\cdot\\tfrac{4-r}4$. So $64r=65(4-r)$, $r=\\tfrac{260}{129}$ and $m+n=389$.","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-14": ["By Viviani's Theorem the distances from any interior point of an equilateral triangle to its three sides add up to its height, so the height is $1+2+3=6$. The height of an equilateral triangle is $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$ times its side, so $AB=\\tfrac{2\\cdot6}{\\sqrt3}=4\\sqrt3$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-aime-i-1": ["Write the original counts as $3k$ girls and $2k$ boys, so $2k$ students like to dance. Adding $20$ dancing boys leaves the girls at $3k$ out of $5k+20$, and setting up the proportion $\\frac{3k}{5k+20} = \\frac{29}{50}$ cross-multiplies to $k = 116$. The dancers now number $2k+20 = 252$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-i-2": ["If the altitude were at most $10$, the triangle would lie inside the square with area at most $50$, so the apex $G$ lies beyond the side $AI$ and the common region is a trapezoid with parallel sides $EM=10$ and $XY$ on $AI$, and height $10$. Its area $\\tfrac{10+XY}2\\cdot10=80$ gives $XY=6$. Triangle $GXY$ is triangle $GEM$ scaled by $\\tfrac6{10}$, so with altitude $h$, $\\tfrac{h-10}h=\\tfrac35$ and $h=25$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-i-3": ["The two trips give $2b+3j+4s = 74$ and $4b+2j+3s = 91$. Eliminating $b$ leaves $4j+5s = 57$, and that congruence modulo $4$ forces $s \\equiv 1$, bounding $s$ to just $1$, $5$ and $9$. Testing those, only $s = 5$ makes $b$ a whole number, giving $(b,j,s) = (15,8,5)$ and the sum of squares $225+64+25=314$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-i-4": ["Complete the square to $y^2=(x+42)^2+244$, so $244=(y-x-42)(y+x+42)$ by difference of squares. Both factors share a parity, and $244=2^2\\cdot61$ forces the factor pair $2$ and $122$; solving the pair gives $x+y=80$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-i-5": ["Rolling on its side, the cone turns about its vertex, and the point where the base meets the table traces a circle whose radius is the slant height $\\sqrt{r^2+h^2}$, of length $2\\pi\\sqrt{r^2+h^2}$. Seventeen rotations roll out $17$ base circumferences, $34\\pi r$, so $\\sqrt{r^2+h^2}=17r$. Then $h^2=288r^2$ and $\\tfrac hr=12\\sqrt2$, so $m+n=14$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-i-6": ["Row $n$ is built from row $n-1$ by adding neighbours, and induction shows its $k$th entry is $2^{n-1}(n+2k-2)$ (row $2$ reads $4,8,12,\\ldots$). Since $67$ is odd it shares no factor with $2^{n-1}$, so an entry is a multiple of $67$ exactly when $67\\mid n+2k-2$. Row $n$ has $51-n$ entries, so $n\\le n+2k-2\\le100-n\\lt134$, and the only multiple of $67$ in reach is $67$ itself. That needs $n$ odd (so that $n+2k-2$ is odd) and $67\\le100-n$, that is $n\\le33$. Each odd row $1,3,\\ldots,33$ holds exactly one such entry, at $k=\\tfrac{69-n}2$, so there are $17$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-i-7": ["Consecutive squares differ by $(x+1)^2-x^2=2x+1$, which is below $100$ for $x\\le49$. So every one of $S_0$ through $S_{25}$ contains a square (they reach $50^2=2500$), while from $50^2$ on the squares are more than $100$ apart and each lands in its own set. The squares $51^2$ through $316^2$ (since $316^2=99856$ and $317^2\\gt99999$) fill $266$ more sets, so $26+266=292$ sets contain a square and $1000-292=708$ do not.","",[]],
"2008-aime-i-8": ["Add the inverse tangents two at a time with $\\arctan a+\\arctan b=\\arctan\\frac{a+b}{1-ab}$, valid while $ab\\lt1$: $\\arctan\\tfrac13+\\arctan\\tfrac14=\\arctan\\tfrac{7/12}{11/12}=\\arctan\\tfrac7{11}$, and adding $\\arctan\\tfrac15$ gives $\\arctan\\tfrac{46/55}{48/55}=\\arctan\\tfrac{23}{24}$. The last term must make up $\\tfrac\\pi4$, so $\\tfrac1n=\\tan\\left(\\tfrac\\pi4-\\arctan\\tfrac{23}{24}\\right)=\\frac{1-23/24}{1+23/24}=\\tfrac1{47}$, and $n=47$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-i-9": ["Each crate stands $3$, $4$ or $6$ feet tall with probability $\\tfrac13$ each. With $a$, $b$, $c$ crates of those heights, $3a+4b+6c=41$ and $a+b+c=10$, and eliminating $a$ gives $b+3c=11$, so $(a,b,c)=(5,2,3)$, $(3,5,2)$ or $(1,8,1)$. Their arrangements number $\\tfrac{10!}{5!2!3!}=2520$, $2520$ and $\\tfrac{10!}{8!}=90$, a total of $5130$, so the probability is $\\tfrac{5130}{3^{10}}=\\tfrac{190}{2187}$ and $m=190$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-i-10": ["Two bounds squeeze $AD$. In triangle $AED$ the triangle inequality gives $AD\\ge DE-AE=20\\sqrt7$. In triangle $ACD$ the angle at $D$ is $60^\\circ$, so the Law of Sines gives $AD=\\tfrac{AC\\sin\\angle ACD}{\\sin60^\\circ}\\le\\tfrac{10\\sqrt{21}}{\\sqrt3/2}=20\\sqrt7$. So $AD=20\\sqrt7$, both bounds are equalities: $\\angle ACD=90^\\circ$, and $E$ lies on line $AD$ beyond $A$, $10\\sqrt7$ from it. The right triangle $ACD$ is $30$-$60$-$90$ with $CD=10\\sqrt7$, and $CDF$ is another with $DF=5\\sqrt7$, so $F$ is $15\\sqrt7$ from $A$ and $EF=10\\sqrt7+15\\sqrt7=25\\sqrt7$, giving $m+n=32$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-i-11": ["Classify by the final letter. A sequence ending in $A$ must close with a pair of $A$s, and one ending in $B$ either appends a single $B$ to a string ending in $A$ or two more $B$s to a string already ending in $B$. That gives the pair of recursions $a_n = a_{n-2}+b_{n-2}$ and $b_n = a_{n-1}+b_{n-2}$, iterated from $a_1=0$, $b_1=1$, $a_2=1$, $b_2=0$ up to $n = 14$, which gives $a_{14}+b_{14}=172$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-i-12": ["At a speed just under $15n$ km/h the rule needs $n$ car lengths of gap, so the fastest flow with $n$ lengths is at $15n$ km/h exactly. Each car with its gap then occupies $4(n+1)$ metres, and by distance over time $\\frac{15000n}{4(n+1)}=3750\\cdot\\frac n{n+1}$ car-units pass per hour. That increases toward $3750$ without reaching it, so for large $n$ it is between $3749$ and $3750$. The eye counts whole cars whose fronts pass in the hour, and with more than $3749$ spacings per hour the hour can hold $3750$ fronts but never $3751$. So $M=3750$ and the quotient is $375$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-i-13": ["Substitute the eight given points one at a time. From $(0,0)$, $a_0 = 0$; adding and subtracting the equations at $(\\pm1, 0)$ gives $a_3 = 0$ and $a_6 = -a_1$, and the same at $(0,\\pm1)$ gives $a_5 = 0$ and $a_9 = -a_2$. Then $(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ give $a_4+a_7+a_8=0$ and $-a_4-a_7+a_8=0$, so $a_8=0$ and $a_4=-a_7$, and $(2,2)$ gives $-6a_1-6a_2+4a_7=0$. What is left is a two-parameter family, $p=-\\tfrac{a_7}3\\,x(x-1)(2x-3y+2)-a_9\\,(x-y)(x^2+xy+y^2-1)$. A point where every member vanishes is a common zero of the two factored polynomials; away from the given points that needs $2x-3y+2=0$ and $x^2+xy+y^2=1$, so $y=\\tfrac{2x+2}3$ and $19x^2+14x-5=(19x-5)(x+1)=0$. The new point is $\\left(\\tfrac5{19},\\tfrac{16}{19}\\right)$, so $a+b+c=5+16+19=40$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-i-14": ["Let $O$ be the center and $x=OC$. The radius $OT$ is perpendicular to the tangent, as is $AP$, so the right triangles $CAP$ and $COT$ are similar and $AP=9\\cdot\\tfrac{CA}{CO}=\\tfrac{9(x-9)}x$; also $\\angle CAP=\\angle COT$, whose cosine is $\\tfrac9x$, so $\\cos\\angle BAP=-\\tfrac9x$. The Law of Cosines in $\\triangle BAP$ then gives, with $s=\\tfrac9x$, $BP^2=18^2+81(1-s)^2+2\\cdot18\\cdot9(1-s)s=405+162s-243s^2$. This quadratic in $s$ is largest at $s=\\tfrac13$, where $BP^2=405+54-27=432$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-i-15": ["At a corner, the two cut endpoints $X$ and $Y$ are $\\sqrt{17}$ from the corner along the sides, so they are $\\sqrt{34}$ apart, as the hypotenuse of a $45$-$45$-$90$ triangle. The cuts are equal and meet at $60^\\circ$, so the cut triangle is equilateral with side $\\sqrt{34}$, and its apex $Q$ lies $\\sqrt{\\tfrac{17}2}+\\sqrt{\\tfrac{51}2}$ along the diagonal, at $(s,s)$ with $s=\\tfrac{\\sqrt{17}+\\sqrt{51}}2$. The base of the tray is the square with the four apexes as vertices, and each side flap turns up about a line $s$ from the paper's edge. When a flap turns by an angle, $X$ rises to height $h$ and its distance $s$ from the hinge shrinks horizontally. With the corner at the origin, $X=(\\sqrt{17},0)$ moves in the plane $x=\\sqrt{17}$ and $Y=(0,\\sqrt{17})$ in the plane $y=\\sqrt{17}$, so where they meet lies above $(\\sqrt{17},\\sqrt{17})$, a horizontal distance $s-\\sqrt{17}$ from each hinge. So $h^2=s^2-(s-\\sqrt{17})^2=2s\\sqrt{17}-17=\\sqrt{867}$, giving $h=\\sqrt[4]{867}$ and $m+n=871$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-ii-1": ["Pair each added square with the subtracted square two below it: $(100^2-98^2)+(99^2-97^2)+(96^2-94^2)+\\cdots+(3^2-1^2)$. Each difference of squares is $2(k+(k-2))$, and the pairs use every number from $1$ to $100$ exactly once, so $N=2(1+2+\\cdots+100)=2\\cdot5050=10100$, whose remainder is $100$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-ii-2": ["Separate riding time from break time. Rudolph stops $49$ times for $245$ minutes and Jennifer stops $24$ times for $120$ minutes, so if the shared total is $t$ then their riding times are $t-245$ and $t-120$. Distance over rate makes those $\\frac{50}r$ and $\\frac{50}{\\frac34 r} = \\frac{200}{3r}$, whose ratio is $\\frac34$; setting $\\frac{t-245}{t-120} = \\frac34$ gives $4t-980=3t-360$, so $t=620$ minutes.","",[]],
"2008-aime-ii-3": ["Each cut shortens one dimension by $1$, so after ten cuts the three sides satisfy $a+b+c = 10+13+14-10 = 27$ no matter which faces were cut. AM-GM then bounds the volume: $\\sqrt[3]{abc} \\le \\frac{a+b+c}3 = 9$, so $abc \\le 729$, attained at $a=b=c=9$ which the cuts can reach.","",[]],
"2008-aime-ii-4": ["Write $2008$ in base three as $2202101_3$. A digit $2$ is not allowed, but $2 \\cdot 3^k = 3^{k+1} - 3^k$ converts each one into a difference, carrying upward until every digit is $1$, $0$ or $-1$ — balanced ternary. The resulting representation is $3^7 - 3^5 + 3^4 - 3^3 + 3^2 + 3^0$, so the exponents sum to $21$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-ii-5": ["Extend the legs to meet at $E$. Since $\\angle A+\\angle D=90^\\circ$, the angle at $E$ is right, so $EN$ and $EM$ are medians to the hypotenuses of the right triangles $AED$ and $BEC$, each half its hypotenuse: $EN=\\tfrac{AD}2=1004$ and $EM=\\tfrac{BC}2=500$. Since $BC\\parallel AD$, the triangle $BEC$ is a scaled copy of $AED$ from $E$, and that scaling sends the midpoint $N$ of $AD$ to the midpoint $M$ of $BC$, so $E$, $M$, $N$ are collinear. Hence $MN=1004-500=504$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-ii-6": ["Divide the recurrence by $a_{n-1}$ to get $\\frac{a_n}{a_{n-1}} = \\frac{a_{n-1}}{a_{n-2}} + 1$, so the ratios increase by one each step and telescope to $\\frac{a_n}{a_{n-1}} = n$; the same gives $\\frac{b_n}{b_{n-1}} = n+2$. Hence $a_n = n!$ and $b_n = \\frac{(n+2)!}2$, so $\\frac{b_{32}}{a_{32}} = \\frac{34 \\cdot 33}2 = 561$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-ii-7": ["There is no $x^2$ term, so Vieta's gives $r+s+t = 0$ and each of $r+s$, $s+t$, $t+r$ is the negative of the remaining root. The target is therefore $-(r^3+s^3+t^3)$, and a vanishing sum collapses that to $-3rst$. Vieta's again gives $rst = -2008/8 = -251$, so the answer is $753$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-ii-8": ["The product-to-sum identity $2\\cos a \\sin b = \\sin(a+b) - \\sin(a-b)$ turns each term into a difference, here $2\\cos(k^2a)\\sin(ka)=\\sin(k(k+1)a)-\\sin(k(k-1)a)$, so the whole sum telescopes to $\\sin(n(n+1)a)$. That is an integer only when it is $-1$, $0$ or $1$, that is when $\\frac{n(n+1)\\pi}{2008}$ is a multiple of $\\frac\\pi2$, so $1004 = 2^2\\cdot 251$ divides $n(n+1)$. Of two consecutive numbers one must carry the prime $251$ and one the factor $4$: $n=250$ fails ($4$ divides neither $250$ nor $251$), and $n=251$ works since $252=4\\cdot63$. So $n=251$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-ii-9": ["Each move is $z\\mapsto\\omega z+10$ with $\\omega=e^{i\\pi/4}$, so after $n$ moves $z_n=\\omega^nz_0+10\\left(\\omega^{n-1}+\\cdots+1\\right)=\\omega^nz_0+10\\cdot\\tfrac{\\omega^n-1}{\\omega-1}$, a geometric series. Since $\\omega^8=1$ and $150=8\\cdot18+6$, $\\omega^{150}=\\omega^6=-i$, and evaluating gives $z_{150}=-5\\sqrt2+(5+5\\sqrt2)i$. So $|p|+|q|=5+10\\sqrt2\\approx19.14$, and the answer is $19$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-ii-10": ["The distance formula allows only nine distances between points of the grid, $\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$ with $0\\le a,b\\le3$: $\\sqrt1$, $\\sqrt2$, $\\sqrt4$, $\\sqrt5$, $\\sqrt8$, $\\sqrt9$, $\\sqrt{10}$, $\\sqrt{13}$, $\\sqrt{18}$. A growing path uses them in strictly increasing order, so it has at most $10$ points; a $10$-point path exists, so $m=10$ and such a path uses all nine distances in order. Work backwards from its end: the last step, $\\sqrt{18}$, joins opposite corners, and the $\\sqrt{13}$ step before it must reach a corner from a point $3$ and $2$ away, which leaves each earlier step only a few choices. Casework on those choices finds $6$ paths into each corner, so $r=24$ and $mr=240$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-ii-11": ["Drop the altitude from $A$: it bisects $BC$, so the Pythagorean theorem gives its length $\\sqrt{100^2-28^2}=96$, and $\\sin C=\\tfrac{24}{25}$, $\\cos C=\\tfrac7{25}$. A circle tangent to both sides of an angle has its center on the bisector, so it touches $BC$ at $r\\cot\\tfrac C2$ from the vertex, and the half-angle formula gives $\\tan\\tfrac C2=\\tfrac{\\sin C}{1+\\cos C}=\\tfrac34$. So $P$ touches $BC$ at $\\tfrac43\\cdot16=\\tfrac{64}3$ from $C$, and $Q$, of radius $q$, at $\\tfrac43q$ from $B$. Two externally tangent circles on the same line touch it $2\\sqrt{16q}$ apart, so $\\tfrac{64}3+\\tfrac43q+8\\sqrt q=56$, that is $q+6\\sqrt q-26=0$. Then $\\sqrt q=\\sqrt{35}-3$, $q=44-6\\sqrt{35}$, and $m+nk=44+6\\cdot35=254$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-ii-12": ["Add an eleventh blue flag to serve as the divider between the poles. Line up the $11$ blue flags and put the $9$ green ones into the $12$ gaps, at most one per gap so no two greens touch: $\\binom{12}9=220$ ways. Any of the $11$ blues can be the divider, giving $2420$; greens on either side of the divider are on different poles, so they may stand next to it. A pole is empty exactly when the divider is the first or last flag of the row; with the divider first, the greens fill $9$ of the remaining $11$ gaps, $\\binom{11}9=55$ ways, and likewise last. So $N=2420-110=2310$, with remainder $310$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-ii-13": ["The hexagon's sides lie on six lines at distance $\\tfrac12$ from $0$, such as $\\operatorname{Re}z=\\tfrac12$, and a point outside the hexagon lies beyond at least one of them. The map $z\\mapsto\\tfrac1z$ inverts the modulus and negates the argument: with $\\tfrac1z=\\rho e^{i\\varphi}$, the condition $\\operatorname{Re}z\\ge\\tfrac12$ reads $\\rho\\le2\\cos\\varphi$, the unit disc centered at $1$. This is inversion sending a line that misses the center to a circle through it, so $S$ is the union of six unit discs centered at the sixth roots of unity. By symmetry take the wedge $|\\varphi|\\le30^\\circ$, where the disc centered at $1$ reaches farthest. There $S$ is the equilateral triangle with vertices $0$ and $\\sqrt3e^{\\pm i\\pi/6}$, of area $\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}4$, plus the circular segment beyond its far side, a chord subtending $120^\\circ$ at the center $1$, of area $\\tfrac\\pi3-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}4$. Six wedges give $2\\pi+3\\sqrt3=2\\pi+\\sqrt{27}$, so $a+b=29$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-ii-14": ["Expanding the third expression against the first two collapses the system to $a^2+y^2=b^2+x^2=2(ax+by)$. Then $a^2-2ax=2by-y^2\\ge0$ because $0\\le y\\lt b$ (the trivial inequality step), so $x\\le\\tfrac a2$. Hence $b^2=a^2+y^2-x^2\\ge a^2-\\tfrac{a^2}4=\\tfrac34a^2$, so $\\rho^2\\le\\tfrac43$, and $(a,b,x,y)=\\left(1,\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2,\\tfrac12,0\\right)$ attains it. So $m+n=7$.","",[]],
"2008-aime-ii-15": ["Condition (i) says $n^2=(m+1)^3-m^3=3m^2+3m+1$, which rearranges by difference of squares to $(2n-1)(2n+1)=3(2m+1)^2$. The two odd factors differ by $2$, so they are coprime, and since their product is three times a square, one is a square and the other three times a square. $2n+1=a^2$, $2n-1=3b^2$ would give $a^2=3b^2+2\\equiv2\\pmod3$, which no square is, so $2n-1=a^2$. Condition (ii) then says $2n+79=a^2+80=k^2$, so $(k-a)(k+a)=80$ with $a$ odd, leaving $(2,40)$ and $(8,10)$, that is $a=19$ or $a=1$. The larger gives $n=\\tfrac{19^2+1}2=181$, and indeed $2n+1=363=3\\cdot11^2$, $181^2=105^3-104^3$, $2\\cdot181+79=21^2$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-i-1": ["Digits in geometric progression mean the middle one is the geometric mean, $b^2 = ac$. For the largest, start at $9$: a ratio above $1$ overflows, and of the ratios below $1$ that keep the digits whole, $\\tfrac23$ gives $964$ and $\\tfrac13$ only $931$, so $964$. For the smallest, start at $1$: the ratio must be a whole number at least $2$ with $r^2\\le9$, and $r=2$ gives $124$, below $139$. The difference is $840$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-i-2": ["Rewrite $\\frac z{z+n} = 4i$ as $1 - \\frac n{z+n} = 4i$, so $\\frac n{z+n} = 1-4i$ and, inverting and multiplying by the conjugate, $\\frac{z}{n} + 1 = \\frac{1+4i}{17}$. Matching imaginary parts gives $\\frac{4}{17} = \\frac{164}{n}$, so $n = 697$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-i-3": ["The two binomial probabilities share the coefficient $\\binom83$, so the condition $25\\binom83p^3(1-p)^5 = \\binom83p^5(1-p)^3$ cancels down to $25(1-p)^2 = p^2$. Both $p$ and $1-p$ are positive, so taking square roots gives $5(1-p) = p$ and $p = \\tfrac56$, making $m+n = 11$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-i-4": ["Use vectors from $A$, with $\\vec b=\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\vec d=\\overrightarrow{AD}$, so $\\overrightarrow{AC}=\\vec b+\\vec d$. Then $\\overrightarrow{AP}=t(\\vec b+\\vec d)$, and since $P$ is on $MN$, also $\\overrightarrow{AP}=s\\cdot\\tfrac{17}{1000}\\vec b+(1-s)\\tfrac{17}{2009}\\vec d$. Matching coefficients, $s=\\tfrac{1000t}{17}$ and $1-s=\\tfrac{2009t}{17}$, and adding gives $1=\\tfrac{3009t}{17}$. So $\\frac{AC}{AP}=\\frac1t=\\frac{3009}{17}=177$.","",["Write every point as a combination of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AD}$: $P$ is a multiple of $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ and also a weighted average of $M$ and $N$.","Match the coefficients of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AD}$; the two weights add to $1$."]],
"2009-aime-i-5": ["The Angle Bisector Theorem gives $AL:LB=AC:BC=450:300=3:2$. Put masses $2$ at $A$ and $3$ at $B$, so $L$ weighs $5$; $K$ is the midpoint of $AC$, so $C$ weighs $2$ as well. Balancing $CL$ at $P$ gives $CP:PL=5:2$. The diagonals $AC$ and $PM$ of $AMCP$ bisect each other at $K$, so $AMCP$ is a parallelogram and $PC=AM=180$. Then $LP=\\tfrac25\\cdot180=72$.","",["The angle bisector theorem turns the side lengths into the ratio in which $L$ divides $AB$.","Use that ratio, and the midpoint $K$, to give each vertex a mass, then balance along $BK$ and $CL$ to locate $P$."]],
"2009-aime-i-6": ["Since $5^5\\gt1000$, the floor $\\lfloor x\\rfloor$ can only be $0$ through $4$, so run the casework on $k=\\lfloor x\\rfloor$. For $k=0$ and $k=1$ the only integer value is $N=1$. For $k\\ge2$, $x^k$ increases continuously over $\\left[k^k,(k+1)^k\\right)$, so it takes the $(k+1)^k-k^k$ integers $k^k,\\ldots,(k+1)^k-1$: that is $9-4=5$ for $k=2$, $64-27=37$ for $k=3$ and $625-256=369$ for $k=4$, all below $1000$. The total is $1+5+37+369=412$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-i-7": ["Clear the exponent: $5^{a_{n+1}-a_n} = \\frac{3n+5}{3n+2}$, so taking logarithms gives $a_{n+1}-a_n = \\log_5(3n+5) - \\log_5(3n+2)$. Summing from $1$ telescopes to $5^{a_n} = 3n+2$, so $a_n$ is an integer exactly when $3n+2$ is a power of $5$; the first after $n=1$ is $125$, giving $k = 41$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-i-8": ["In every positive difference, $2^x$ is added once for each smaller element and subtracted once for each larger, so it appears with coefficient $x-(10-x)=2x-10$. Hence $N=\\sum_{x=0}^{10}(2x-10)2^x=2\\sum x2^x-10\\sum2^x$. The arithmetico-geometric sum is $\\sum_{x=0}^{10}x2^x=9\\cdot2^{11}+2=18434$ and the geometric one is $2^{11}-1=2047$, so $N=36868-20470=16398$, with remainder $398$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-i-9": ["Concatenating the three prices gives a string of seven digits with three $3$s, and there are $\\binom73=35$ such strings. Each string is cut into three nonempty prices of at most four digits: of the $\\binom62=15$ ways to place two cuts, the $3$ that leave a $5$-digit part with two single digits are illegal, leaving $12$. Every guess is one string with one legal cut, so there are $35\\cdot12=420$ guesses.","",[]],
"2009-aime-i-10": ["The seating must read as repeated MVE blocks around the table, since no Earthling may precede a Martian and so on. Casework on the number $k$ of blocks: distributing five Martians into $k$ nonempty runs is stars and bars, $\\binom4{k-1}$ ways, and the same for each other species. So $N = \\sum_{k=1}^5 \\binom4{k-1}^3 = 1+64+216+64+1 = 346$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-i-11": ["The points on $41x+y=2009$ have $x=0,1,\\ldots,49$ and $y=2009-41x$. The shoelace formula makes $[OPQ]=\\tfrac12|x_1y_2-x_2y_1|$, and substituting, $x_1y_2-x_2y_1=2009(x_1-x_2)$. As $2009$ is odd, the area is an integer exactly when $x_1$ and $x_2$ have the same parity, and it is positive when $x_1\\ne x_2$. There are $25$ even and $25$ odd values of $x$, so the count is $2\\binom{25}2=600$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-i-12": ["Here $AB=37$, and the altitude-to-the-hypotenuse relations give $AD=\\tfrac{12^2}{37}=\\tfrac{144}{37}$, $BD=\\tfrac{35^2}{37}=\\tfrac{1225}{37}$ and $CD=\\tfrac{12\\cdot35}{37}=\\tfrac{420}{37}$. The circle on $CD$ touches $AB$ at $D$, since $CD\\perp AB$, and it touches $AI$ and $BI$, so it is the incircle of $\\triangle ABI$, with radius $r=\\tfrac{210}{37}$ and tangent lengths $x=AD$, $y=BD$ and an unknown $z$ from $I$. Writing the area as $rs$ and by Heron's formula as $\\sqrt{sxyz}$, with $s=x+y+z$, gives $r^2(x+y+z)=xyz$, so $z=\\tfrac{37r^2}{xy-r^2}=\\tfrac{37}3$. The perimeter is $2s=2\\left(37+\\tfrac{37}3\\right)$, its ratio to $AB$ is $\\tfrac83$, and $m+n=11$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-i-13": ["Clear the fraction to $a_{n+2}a_{n+1} + a_{n+2} = a_n + 2009$ and write the same relation one step later; subtracting the two telescopes into $(a_{n+2}+1)(a_{n+3}-a_{n+1}) = a_{n+2}-a_n$. If the odd-indexed or even-indexed terms ever differed, these differences would satisfy $|a_{n+3}-a_{n+1}|=\\tfrac{|a_{n+2}-a_n|}{a_{n+2}+1}\\lt|a_{n+2}-a_n|$, an endless strictly decreasing sequence of positive integers; so the differences are $0$ and $a_{n+3} = a_{n+1}$, so the sequence alternates and $a_1a_2 = 2009 = 7^2 \\cdot 41$. Minimizing the sum over the factor pairs gives $49+41 = 90$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-i-14": ["Only the counts matter, so let $w,x,y,z$ be how many $a_i$ equal $1,2,3,4$. The three given conditions are linear: $w+x+y+z = 350$, $w+2x+3y+4z = 513$, and $w+16x+81y+256z = 4745$. Subtracting the first from the others gives $x+2y+3z=163$ and $3x+16y+51z=879$, and eliminating $x$ leaves $5y+21z=195$, so $5\\mid z$: $(y,z)=(39,0)$ or $(18,5)$. Then $S_2=350+3x+8y+15z=839+2y+6z$ is $917$ or $905$, and the minimum is $905$ (with $w=215$, $x=112$, $y=18$, $z=5$).","",[]],
"2009-aime-i-15": ["The Law of Cosines gives $\\cos\\angle BAC=\\tfrac{100+256-196}{2\\cdot10\\cdot16}=\\tfrac12$, so $\\angle BAC=60^\\circ$. In triangle $ABD$ the incenter gives $\\angle BI_BD=90^\\circ+\\tfrac12\\angle BAD$, and likewise $\\angle CI_CD=90^\\circ+\\tfrac12\\angle CAD$, so the two add to $180^\\circ+30^\\circ=210^\\circ$. In the cyclic quadrilaterals $BI_BDP$ and $CI_CDP$ the angles at $P$ are the supplements, so $\\angle BPC=360^\\circ-210^\\circ=150^\\circ$ whatever $D$ is. So $P$ lies on the arc over $BC=14$ that subtends $150^\\circ$, and the area is largest at the arc's midpoint, where $PB=PC$ and the height is $7\\tan15^\\circ=7(2-\\sqrt3)$. The largest area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot14\\cdot7(2-\\sqrt3)=98-49\\sqrt3$, so $a+b+c=98+49+3=150$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-ii-1": ["Let each stripe use $x$ ounces and let $L$ be the amount of each color left. Two linear conditions fix them: the total paint gives $482-4x=3L$, since $130+164+188=482$, and the blue gives $L=130-x$. Substituting the second into the first, $482-4x=390-3x$, so $x=92$, $L=38$, and the paint left totals $3\\cdot38=114$ ounces.","",[]],
"2009-aime-ii-2": ["Note that $x^{(\\log_y z)^2} = \\left(x^{\\log_y z}\\right)^{\\log_y z}$ by the exponent laws, so each given equation can be raised to its own logarithm. Since $27 = 3^3$, $49 = 7^2$ and $\\sqrt{11} = 11^{1/2}$, the identity $x^{\\log_x y} = y$ turns the three terms into $7^3$, $11^2$ and $25^{1/2}$, which sum to $343 + 121 + 5 = 469$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-ii-3": ["Since $AC\\perp BE$, the right triangles $BAE$ and $ABC$ have $\\angle AEB=\\angle BAC$ (both complementary to $\\angle ABE$), so they are similar. That gives $\\tfrac{AE}{AB}=\\tfrac{AB}{BC}$, and with $AE=\\tfrac{AD}2$ and $BC=AD$ it becomes $AD^2=2\\cdot100^2$. So $AD=100\\sqrt2\\approx141.42$ and the answer is $141$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-ii-4": ["The grape counts form an arithmetic progression starting at $n$ with difference $-2$, so with $c$ children the arithmetic series sums to $c(n-c+1)=2009$. Every solution comes from a factor pair of $2009=7^2\\cdot41$, with $n=c+\\tfrac{2009}c-1$, and the last child eats $n+2-2c\\ge0$. The divisor $c=49$ gives $n=88$ but a last count of $-8$, while $c=41$ gives $n=89$ with last count $9$; the other divisors give larger $n$. So the smallest is $89$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-ii-5": ["Internal tangency puts each small center on a radius of circle $A$, so $AB=10-3=7$ and $AC=AD=10-2=8$, with $C$ and $D$ at $120^\\circ$ from $B$ about $A$. By symmetry $E$ is on line $AB$, on the far side of $A$ from $B$; let $AE=p$, so external tangency gives $BE=7+p=3+r$, that is, $r=p+4$. The angle $CAE$ is $180^\\circ-120^\\circ=60^\\circ$, so the Law of Cosines in triangle $ACE$ gives $(2+r)^2=64+p^2-8p$. Substituting $r=p+4$, $(p+6)^2=p^2-8p+64$, so $20p=28$, $p=\\tfrac75$, and $r=\\tfrac{27}5$, so $m+n=27+5=32$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-ii-6": ["Count the complement: five-element subsets of $\\{1,\\ldots,14\\}$ with no two consecutive. For such a subset $a_1\\lt\\cdots\\lt a_5$, the shift $a_i\\mapsto a_i-(i-1)$ closes the forced gap between each pair of neighbours and gives five distinct numbers in $\\{1,\\ldots,10\\}$; it is reversible, so there are $\\binom{10}5=252$ of them. So $m = \\binom{14}5 - \\binom{10}5 = 2002 - 252 = 1750$, and the remainder is $750$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-ii-7": ["Since $(2i)!!=2^ii!$ and $(2i)!!(2i-1)!!=(2i)!$, each term is $\\tfrac{(2i)!}{4^i(i!)^2}=\\tfrac{\\binom{2i}i}{4^i}$. The sum telescopes to a closed form, checked by induction from $\\tfrac12=\\tfrac{3\\cdot2}4-1$: $\\sum_{i=1}^n\\tfrac{\\binom{2i}i}{4^i}=\\tfrac{(2n+1)\\binom{2n}n}{4^n}-1$. With $n=2009$, the numerator $4019\\binom{4018}{2009}$ is odd except for the $2$s in the binomial, and Legendre's formula $v_2(m!)=m-s_2(m)$ (with $s_2$ the binary digit sum) gives $v_2\\binom{4018}{2009}=s_2(2009)=8$, since $2009=11111011001_2$. So the sum is $\\tfrac N{2^{4010}}-1=\\tfrac{N-2^{4010}}{2^{4010}}$ with $N$ odd, the numerator stays odd, and the denominator is $2^{4010}$: $a=4010$, $b=1$, and $\\tfrac{ab}{10}=401$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-ii-8": ["Each roll count is geometric, $P(k)=\\left(\\tfrac56\\right)^{k-1}\\tfrac16$. Equal counts have probability $\\sum_k\\left(\\tfrac{25}{36}\\right)^{k-1}\\tfrac1{36}=\\tfrac{1/36}{11/36}=\\tfrac1{11}$, a geometric series. Dave rolling exactly one more time than Linda has probability $\\tfrac56\\cdot\\tfrac1{11}=\\tfrac5{66}$, and the reverse the same. The total is $\\tfrac6{66}+\\tfrac{10}{66}=\\tfrac8{33}$, so $m+n=41$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-ii-9": ["Reducing modulo $2$ forces $y$ odd in the first equation and even in the second: $y=2y'-1$ turns it into $2x+3y'+z=1006$, and $y=2y'$ turns the second into $2x+3y'+z=1000$. Every pair $(x,y')$ of positive integers with $2x+3y'\\le1005$ (respectively $\\le999$) gives exactly one positive $z$, so $m-n$ counts the pairs with $1000\\le2x+3y'\\le1005$. For those six values the number of positive solutions is $166,167,166,167,167,167$, so $m-n=1000$ and the remainder is $0$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-ii-10": ["The triangle is the $5$-$12$-$13$ right triangle with the right angle at $B$, so put $B=(0,0)$, $A=(0,5)$, $C=(12,0)$. The bisector from $A$ meets $BC$ at $O$ with $BO:OC=AB:AC=5:13$, so $BO=\\tfrac{10}3$. The equal angles at $C$ make line $CD$ the mirror image of $CA$ in $BC$, the line through $C$ and $A'=(0,-5)$. Points of $AO$ are $\\left(\\tfrac{10s}3,\\,5-5s\\right)$ and points of $CA'$ are $(12-12u,\\,-5u)$; equal coordinates give $u=s-1$ and $\\tfrac{10s}3=24-12s$, so $s=\\tfrac{36}{23}$ and $D=\\left(\\tfrac{120}{23},-\\tfrac{65}{23}\\right)$. Then $AD=s\\cdot AO=\\tfrac{36}{23}\\cdot\\tfrac{5\\sqrt{13}}3=\\tfrac{60\\sqrt{13}}{23}$, and $p+q+r=60+23+13=96$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-ii-11": ["Combine the logarithms: $\\left|\\log\\tfrac mk\\right|\\lt\\log n$ exponentiates to $\\tfrac1n\\lt\\tfrac mk\\lt n$, that is $\\tfrac mn\\lt k\\lt mn$. The integers strictly between those bounds number $mn-1-\\lfloor\\tfrac mn\\rfloor$, so $mn-\\lfloor\\tfrac mn\\rfloor=51$. For $n=2$ this gives $m=34$, for $n=3$ it gives $m=19$, and for $n\\ge4$ there is no solution with $m\\ge n$. The sum of the products is $68+57=125$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-ii-12": ["Double-count the total of all $2k$ chosen numbers. Being distinct positive integers, their sum is at least $1+2+\\cdots+2k=k(2k+1)$; being $k$ distinct pair-sums each at most $2009$, it is at most $2009+2008+\\cdots+(2010-k)=\\tfrac{k(4019-k)}2$. So $2(2k+1)\\le4019-k$, that is $k\\le\\tfrac{4017}5$, and $k\\le803$. It is attained: pair $a$ with $1205+a$ for $a=1,\\ldots,402$ (sums $1207,1209,\\ldots,2009$) and $a$ with $402+a$ for $a=403,\\ldots,803$ (sums $1208,1210,\\ldots,2008$). The partners run over $1206$ to $1607$ and $805$ to $1205$, all distinct and above $803$, and the sums are the $803$ values $1207$ to $2009$. (The larger numbers cannot simply be $804$ to $1606$: then all $1606$ numbers would total $1290421$, short of the $1291224$ the sums need.) So the maximum is $803$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-ii-13": ["Place the semicircle on the circle of radius $2$ with $A=2$ and $B=-2$, so $C_i=2\\omega^i$ with $\\omega=\\operatorname{cis}\\frac\\pi7$, a primitive $14$th root of unity (De Moivre). Then $AC_i=2|1-\\omega^i|$ and $BC_i=2|1+\\omega^i|=2|1-\\omega^{i+7}|$, so the twelve chords are $2|1-\\omega^j|$ for $j=1,\\ldots,13$ except $j=7$. The product of the distances from $1$ to the other $14$th roots of unity is $14$, and the missing one is $|1-\\omega^7|=2$, so $n=2^{12}\\cdot\\frac{14}2=28672$, whose remainder is $672$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-ii-14": ["Normalize with $b_n=\\frac{a_n}{2^n}$, turning the recurrence into $b_{n+1}=\\tfrac45b_n+\\tfrac35\\sqrt{1-b_n^2}$. Substitute $b_n=\\sin\\theta_n$ with $0\\le\\theta_n\\le90^\\circ$, so the root is $\\cos\\theta_n$ and the step is the sine addition formula: $b_{n+1}=\\sin(\\theta_n+\\varphi)$ with $\\cos\\varphi=\\tfrac45$, $\\varphi\\approx36.9^\\circ$. So $b_1=\\sin\\varphi=\\tfrac35$ and $b_2=\\sin2\\varphi=\\tfrac{24}{25}$. But $3\\varphi\\gt90^\\circ$, so $b_3=\\sin3\\varphi=\\sin(180^\\circ-3\\varphi)$ and the angle folds back to $180^\\circ-3\\varphi$; adding $\\varphi$ gives $180^\\circ-2\\varphi$, whose sine is again $\\tfrac{24}{25}$. From then on $b_n$ alternates, $\\tfrac{24}{25}$ for even $n$ and $\\sin3\\varphi=\\tfrac{117}{125}$ for odd $n$. So $a_{10}=2^{10}\\cdot\\tfrac{24}{25}=983.04$, and the answer is $983$.","",[]],
"2009-aime-ii-15": ["Let $V$ and $W$ be where $CA$ and $CB$ meet $MN$, and put $x=CM$, $y=CN$, so $x^2+y^2=1$ by Thales. Step 1, $V$. $A$ is the midpoint of the arc $MN$ away from $C$, so $CA$ bisects $\\angle MCN$ and the Angle Bisector Theorem gives $MV=\\tfrac x{x+y}$. Step 2, $W$. Thales also makes $\\angle MBN$ right, so $NB=\\tfrac45$. The inscribed angles $\\angle MCB=\\angle MNB$ and $\\angle NCB=\\angle NMB$ have sines $\\tfrac35$ and $\\tfrac45$, so the Ratio Lemma gives $MW:WN=3x:4y$ and $MW=\\tfrac{3x}{3x+4y}$. Step 3, the maximum. $d=MV-MW=\\tfrac{xy}{(x+y)(3x+4y)}$, and with $k=\\tfrac yx$, $\\tfrac1d=4k+7+\\tfrac3k\\ge7+2\\sqrt{12}=7+4\\sqrt3$ by AM-GM, with equality at $k=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$. So the largest $d$ is $\\tfrac1{7+4\\sqrt3}=7-4\\sqrt3$, and $r+s+t=7+4+3=14$.","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-19": ["In any regular polygon of side $2$, the circumradius $R$, the inradius (apothem) $\\rho$ and half a side form a right triangle, so the Pythagorean theorem gives $R^2-\\rho^2=1$ whatever the number of sides. The ring between the two circles has area $\\pi R^2-\\pi\\rho^2=\\pi$ for the pentagon and the heptagon alike, so $A=B$, answer (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-1": ["Brianna is $21$, so Caitlin is $16$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-2": ["$-2$ has reciprocal $-\\tfrac12$, which is larger. For $-1$ and $1$ the number equals its reciprocal, $0$ has none, and $2\\gt\\tfrac12$. So the answer is $-2$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-3": ["$\\tfrac53\\approx1.67$ and $2\\pi\\approx6.28$, so the whole numbers between are $2,3,4,5,6$, which is $5$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-4": ["The graph must pass through $5\\%$, $8\\%$, $15\\%$ and $30\\%$ at the four decades, rising faster each decade. Only graph (E) does, choice (E).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-5": ["The period can catch the last year of one term, two full terms ($6$ years), and the first year of another: $1+3+3+1=8$. Five principals would require three complete middle terms, already $9$ years, as well as parts of two more, so the maximum is $4$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-6": ["The big square has side $1+3+1=5$. The shaded L runs $1$ wide up the left side for $4$ and along the bottom for $3$ more: $4+3=7$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-7": ["A negative product needs one or three negative factors. With one, the smallest is $-8\\cdot7\\cdot5=-280$, the negative factor of greatest size times the two largest positives; with three, $-8\\cdot(-6)\\cdot(-4)=-192$. The minimum is $-280$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-8": ["Three dice carry $3\\cdot21=63$ dots. The visible faces show $1,3,2,4,6,5,1$, a total of $22$, so $41$ are hidden, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-9": ["The three-digit powers of $5$ are $125$ and $625$, and of $2$ are $128$, $256$, $512$. The shared square is the middle digit of $5^n$, which is $2$, and the first digit of $2^m$, so $2^m=256$ and the outlined square holds $6$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-10": ["Shea grew from $50$ to $60$ inches, $10$ inches, so Ara grew $5$ from $50$ and is now $55$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-11": ["Go through the units digits from $11$ to $49$: every number ending in $1$, $2$ or $5$ works ($4+4+4=12$), and of the rest only $33$, $24$, $44$, $36$ and $48$ do (those ending in $3$, $4$, $6$, $7$, $8$, $9$ are tested one by one; $12$ is already counted). The total is $17$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-12": ["A row of only $2$-foot blocks takes $50$ blocks; a staggered row must start and end with a $1$-foot block, $1+49+1=51$ blocks. The rows alternate, so the best is $4$ rows of $50$ and $3$ rows of $51$: $353$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-13": ["The base angles are $\\tfrac{180-36}2=72^\\circ$, so the bisector makes $\\angle CTR=36^\\circ$. In triangle $CRT$, $\\angle CRT=180-72-36=72^\\circ$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-14": ["Odd powers of a number ending in $9$ end in $9$, so both terms end in $9$ and the sum ends in $8$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-15": ["The sides are $4$, $2$ and $1$ for the three triangles. The perimeter runs $AB+BC+CD+DE+EF+FG+GA=4+4+2+2+1+1+1=15$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-16": ["The length is $\\tfrac{1000}{25}=40$ and the perimeter $\\tfrac{1000}{10}=100$, so the width is $10$ and the area $400$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-17": ["Apply the newly defined operation as written: $1\\otimes2=\\tfrac12$ and $\\tfrac12\\otimes3=\\tfrac1{12}$, while $2\\otimes3=\\tfrac43$ and $1\\otimes\\tfrac43=\\tfrac34$. The difference is $\\tfrac1{12}-\\tfrac34=-\\tfrac23$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-18": ["The Shoelace Formula applied to the grid vertices gives area $1$ for each quadrilateral. By the Pythagorean Theorem, the perimeter of I is $2+2\\sqrt2\\approx4.83$, and that of II is $1+2\\sqrt2+\\sqrt5\\approx6.06$. So the areas match and I has the smaller perimeter, choice (E).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-19": ["Cut along $\\overline{BD}$. Above it is a semicircle of radius $5$, area $\\tfrac{25\\pi}2$. Below it is a $10\\times5$ rectangle with two quarter-circles of radius $5$ removed, $50-\\tfrac{25\\pi}2$. The total is $50$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-20": ["One coin of each type makes $41$ cents, leaving $61$ cents in $5$ more coins. The pennies must make the total end correctly, so exactly $1$ more penny (six would be too many coins), and the other $60$ cents in $4$ coins can only be $25+25+5+5$. So the coins are $2$ pennies, $3$ nickels, $1$ dime and $3$ quarters: $1$ dime, choice (A).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-21": ["Keiko gets $0$ or $1$ head, each with probability $\\tfrac12$, and Ephraim's two coins are independent of hers: he gets $0$ heads with probability $\\tfrac14$ and $1$ head with probability $\\tfrac12$. The chance they match is $\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac14+\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12=\\tfrac38$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-22": ["The big cube has surface area $6\\cdot2^2=24$. The small cube covers $1$ square unit of the top but its own top replaces it, so the only new area is its four sides, $4$. The increase is $\\tfrac4{24}\\approx16.7\\%$, closest to $17$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-23": ["Turn each average into a total: the first four sum to $4\\cdot5=20$, the last four to $4\\cdot8=32$, and all seven to $7\\cdot6\\tfrac47=46$. The two groups of four count the shared number twice, so it is $20+32-46=6$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-24": ["Triangle $AFG$ is isosceles with apex angle $20^\\circ$, so $\\angle AFG=\\tfrac{180-20}2=80^\\circ$. Since $A$, $F$, $D$ are collinear, $\\angle AFG$ is an exterior angle of triangle $BFD$ at $F$, so by the Exterior Angle Theorem $\\angle B+\\angle D=80^\\circ$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-8-25": ["Let the rectangle be $w$ by $h$ with $wh=72$, and cut off the three corner right triangles. The two at $B$ and $D$ each have legs $w$ and $\\tfrac h2$ (or $h$ and $\\tfrac w2$), area $\\tfrac{wh}4=18$; the one at $C$ has legs $\\tfrac w2$ and $\\tfrac h2$, area $\\tfrac{wh}8=9$. The triangle is $72-18-18-9=27$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-1": ["$300$ dimples at $2$ seconds each take $600$ seconds, $10$ minutes, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-2": ["The pair with product $24$ and sum $11$ is $3$ and $8$, so the larger is $8$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-3": ["Anjou has $\\tfrac{63}3=21$ dollars, so Elberta has $23$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-4": ["The number must end in $2$ or $4$; ending in $4$ and using $1,2,3,9$ in increasing order gives $12394$, smaller than any ending in $2$ ($13492$ and up). Its tens digit is $9$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-5": ["Sound travels $10880$ feet in $10$ seconds, about $\\tfrac{10880}{5280}\\approx2.06$ miles, nearest to $2$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-6": ["From the first tree to the fourth there are $3$ gaps of $20$ feet. Six trees have $5$ gaps, $100$ feet, choice (B).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-7": ["On the grid the kite's diagonals are $6$ and $7$ inches, and they are perpendicular, so its area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot6\\cdot7=21$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-8": ["Tripling the grid makes the diagonals $18$ and $21$ inches, so the cross takes $39$ inches, choice (E).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-9": ["The small kite has perpendicular diagonals $6$ and $7$, hence area $\\tfrac12\\cdot6\\cdot7=21$. Tripling lengths makes the large grid $18\\times21=378$ square inches and the kite area $9\\cdot21=189$. The waste is $378-189=189$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-10": ["Four quarters are worth $\\$1$, and $2000\\%$ of that is $\\$20$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-11": ["$AB$ and $CD$ are vertical sides of lengths $4$ and $2$, $6$ apart, so the trapezoid has area $\\tfrac{4+2}2\\cdot6=18$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-12": ["Apply the newly defined operation from the inside: $6\\otimes4=\\tfrac{10}2=5$, then $5\\otimes3=\\tfrac82=4$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-13": ["$36-26=10$ students remain, so $5$ prefer cherry, which is $\\tfrac5{36}$ of $360^\\circ$, that is $50^\\circ$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-14": ["There are $3$ meats, $\\binom42=6$ pairs of vegetables and $4$ desserts: $3\\cdot6\\cdot4=72$ meals, choice (C).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-15": ["Homer peels $12$ in the first $4$ minutes, leaving $32$. Together they peel $8$ per minute, so $4$ more minutes, during which Christen peels $20$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-16": ["Folding gives a $2\\times4$ strip of two layers; cutting it in half parallel to the fold leaves two $1\\times4$ rectangles from the outer halves and one $2\\times4$ rectangle from the middle. Their perimeters are $10$ and $12$, a ratio of $\\tfrac56$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-17": ["From $200$ to $300$ is a $50\\%$ increase; every other step is larger ($100\\%$ for most doublings, $\\tfrac23$ from $300$ to $500$, and $\\tfrac{61}{64}\\approx95.3\\%$ from $64$K to $125$K). So the smallest is from question $2$ to $3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-18": ["The product is a multiple of $5$ when at least one die shows $5$: $36-25=11$ of the $36$ outcomes, $\\tfrac{11}{36}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-19": ["At twice the speed, car $N$ covers the same distance in half the time. So its line is twice as high and half as long as $M$'s, which is graph (D), choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-20": ["Each knows only her own score and Kaleana's. Quay can be sure two scores match only if hers equals Kaleana's. Marty knows she is not lowest only if she beat Kaleana, and Shana knows she is not highest only if she scored below Kaleana. So from lowest: Shana, Quay, Marty, choice (A).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-21": ["The five numbers total $75$ with $18$ in the middle. To make the largest as big as possible, make the others small: $1,2,18,19$, leaving $75-40=35$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-22": ["With $c$ correct and $u$ unanswered, the score is $5c+u$ with $c+u\\le20$. A score of $97$ needs $c=19$ and $u=2$ (or fewer correct and too many blanks), which is $21$ questions, so it is impossible; the others occur, for example $90=5\\cdot18$, $91=5\\cdot18+1$, $92=5\\cdot18+2$, $95=5\\cdot19$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-23": ["With side $2$, the triangles that can be formed have side lengths $(2,2,2)$, $(1,1,1)$, $(1,1,\\sqrt3)$ and $(1,\\sqrt3,2)$, which a short check of the $20$ triples confirms. That is $4$ shapes, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-24": ["In each half, $2$ reds and $3$ blues pair with their own color. The leftover red in each half lands on a white (the $2$ red-white pairs), and the $2$ leftover blues in each half also land on whites. That uses $1+2=3$ whites in each half, so the other $5$ whites in each half pair with each other: $5$ white pairs, choice (B).","",[]],
"2001-amc-8-25": ["The largest arrangement, $7542$, is just over $3$ times the smallest, $2457$, so the multiplier is $2$ or $3$. Checking the choices, $7425=3\\cdot2475$, and $2475$ uses the same four digits, so the answer is $7425$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-1": ["Each line meets the circle at most twice and the lines meet each other at most once: $2+2+1=5$, and this is reached when the lines cross inside the circle at a point not on it, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-2": ["With $a$ fives and $b$ twos, $5a+2b=17$ needs $a$ odd: $a=1$, $b=6$ or $a=3$, $b=1$. That is $2$ ways, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-3": ["The smallest four are $2,4,6,8$, with average $5$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-4": ["The next palindrome year is $2112$, whose digits multiply to $4$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-5": ["$706=7\\cdot100+6$, so the day is $6$ weekdays after Saturday: Friday, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-6": ["While filling, the volume grows at $20-18=2$ milliliters per minute; once it overflows, the extra runs off and the volume stays constant. That is graph (A), a rising line and then a flat one, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-7": ["The bars show $6+8+4+2+5=25$ students, of whom $5$ chose E: $20\\%$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-8": ["In the '80s France has $15$ and Spain $9$ stamps, so $24$ European ones, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-9": ["Before the '70s he has $4+7=11$ Brazilian stamps at $6$ cents and $6+4=10$ Peruvian at $4$ cents: $66+40=106$ cents, or $\\$1.06$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-10": ["The '70s stamps cost $12\\cdot6+12\\cdot6+6\\cdot4+13\\cdot5=233$ cents for $43$ stamps, about $5.4$ cents each, closest to $5.5$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-11": ["The squares use $n^2$ tiles, so the seventh needs $49-36=13$ more than the sixth, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-12": ["The probabilities add to $1$, so region $C$ has $1-\\tfrac13-\\tfrac12=\\tfrac16$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-13": ["Doubling every dimension multiplies the volume by $8$, so Carrie's box holds about $8\\cdot125=1000$ jellybeans, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-14": ["The final price is $0.7\\cdot0.8=0.56$ of the original, a $44\\%$ discount, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-15": ["Counting unit squares and half squares on the grid, the areas are $5$, $5$, $5$, $4.5$, and $5.5$. The largest is E, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-16": ["A right isosceles triangle with legs $s$ has area $\\tfrac{s^2}2$, so $X=\\tfrac92$, $Y=8$, $Z=\\tfrac{25}2$, and the $3$-$4$-$5$ triangle $W=6$. Since $9+16=25$, $X+Y=Z$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-17": ["With $c$ correct, $5c-2(10-c)=29$, so $7c=49$ and $c=7$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-18": ["Nine days at $85$ minutes is $765$ minutes. He has $5\\cdot75+3\\cdot90=645$, so the ninth day needs $120$ minutes, $2$ hours, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-19": ["The $0$ is the tens or the units digit, and the other two digits are nonzero: $2\\cdot9\\cdot9=162$ numbers, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-20": ["The altitude splits the triangle into two halves of area $4$. In the left half, $\\overline{AB}$ cuts off at the top a triangle similar to it with ratio $\\tfrac12$, area $1$. The shaded region is the rest of that half, $4-1=3$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-21": ["Of $16$ outcomes, $\\binom42+\\binom43+\\binom44=6+4+1=11$ have at least two heads, so the probability is $\\tfrac{11}{16}$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-22": ["The six cubes have $36$ faces, and each of the $5$ joins between neighbouring cubes hides $2$. That leaves $36-10=26$ square inches, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-23": ["The pattern repeats in $6\\times6$ blocks. Row by row, a block holds $1,2,5,5,2,1$ squares' worth of dark tile (counting the cut corners as halves), $16$ of $36$. So $\\tfrac49$ of the floor is dark, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-24": ["A pear gives $\\tfrac83$ ounces and an orange $4$, so equal numbers give pear and orange juice in the ratio $\\tfrac83:4=2:3$. Pear juice is $40\\%$ of the blend, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-8-25": ["If each gave $x$, Moe had $5x$, Loki $4x$ and Nick $3x$, so the group has $12x$ and Ott now has $3x$, one fourth, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-1": ["A cube has $12$ edges, $8$ corners and $6$ faces: $26$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-2": ["$58$ is even, so its smallest prime factor is $2$, the smallest possible, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-3": ["$90$ of the $120$ grams are not filler, that is $75\\%$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-4": ["If all $7$ rode bicycles there would be $14$ wheels; each tricycle adds $1$, so there are $5$ tricycles, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-5": ["The number is $\\tfrac{12}{0.2}=60$, and $30\\%$ of it is $18$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-6": ["The squares give sides $5$, $12$, $13$, a right triangle with legs $5$ and $12$ ($25+144=169$). Its area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot5\\cdot12=30$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-7": ["Jenny's total is Blake's plus $10-10+20+20=40$, so her average is $10$ higher, choice (A).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-8": ["With equal thickness, the number of cookies is the dough area divided by one cookie's area. Art's trapezoid has area $\\tfrac{3+5}2\\cdot3=12$, Roger's rectangle $8$, Paul's parallelogram $6$ and Trisha's triangle $6$. Art's cookies are largest, so he gets the fewest, choice (A).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-9": ["A batch is $12\\cdot12=144$ square inches, so Roger makes $\\tfrac{144}8=18$ cookies. Art earns $12\\cdot60=720$ cents, so Roger's cookies should cost $\\tfrac{720}{18}=40$ cents, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-10": ["A batch is $144$ square inches and each of Trisha's triangles is $6$, so she makes $24$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-11": ["$40\\cdot1.1\\cdot0.9=39.60$ dollars, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-12": ["The visible product is $\\tfrac{720}k$ with $k$ the hidden number. Even with $6$ hidden it is $120$, a multiple of $6$, and every other case still shows a $6$. So the probability is $1$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-13": ["The figure is a ring of $10$ cubes around a $4\\times3$ rectangle with one cube on top of each corner. The $6$ ring cubes away from the corners show their top, bottom, outer and inner faces, exactly $4$. Corner ring cubes show $3$ and the top cubes $5$, so the answer is $6$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-14": ["The hundreds column gives $2T$ plus any carry, $14$ or $15$, so $F=1$ and the even $O$ is $4$ with no carry from the tens. Then $R=8$, and the tens column needs $2W\\lt10$ with $U=2W$ a new digit: $W=0$ repeats $0$, $W=2$ gives $U=4=O$, $W=4$ is taken, so $W=3$ ($734+734=1468$), choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-15": ["Both views need a column $2$ high and a bottom row $2$ wide. A tower of $2$ gives the tall column; one cube beside it fills the front view and one cube behind or in front fills the side view. A single extra cube on the diagonal would serve both views but would share no face, so the minimum is $4$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-16": ["There are $2$ choices of driver, then $3!=6$ ways to seat the others, so $12$ arrangements, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-17": ["Each family shares a characteristic. Jim is brown-eyed and blond, so his family is either all brown-eyed (only Nadeen besides him, too few) or all blond: Jim, Austin and Sue. The other three, Benjamin, Nadeen and Tevyn, all have black hair, which works. So Jim's siblings are Austin and Sue, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-18": ["Reading the graph, Sarah has $9$ friends, and they and their friends make $14$ invited classmates. The other $6$ are a classmate with no friends, a separate group of three, and two classmates three steps from Sarah along the long chain on the right, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-19": ["The number must be a multiple of $\\operatorname{lcm}(15,20,25)=300$. Between $1000$ and $2000$ these are $1200$, $1500$, $1800$, which is $3$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-20": ["The minute hand is at $120^\\circ$. The hour hand has gone a third of the way from $4$ to $5$, so it is at $120^\\circ+10^\\circ$. The angle is $10^\\circ$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-21": ["The altitude cuts off right triangles with bases $\\sqrt{10^2-8^2}=6$ and $\\sqrt{17^2-8^2}=15$, so $AD=BC+21$. The area gives $\\tfrac{BC+AD}2\\cdot8=164$, so $BC+AD=41$ and $BC=10$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-22": ["In A the shaded area is $4-\\pi$, and in B the four circles of radius $\\tfrac12$ also cover $\\pi$, leaving $4-\\pi\\approx0.86$. In C the circle of radius $1$ minus the square with diagonal $2$ leaves $\\pi-2\\approx1.14$, the largest, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-23": ["The cat returns to its start every $4$ moves and the mouse every $8$. Since $247=4\\cdot61+3=8\\cdot30+7$, the cat is where it is after move $3$ (bottom right) and the mouse where it is after move $7$ (lower left side), as in figure (A), choice (A).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-24": ["On the semicircle the ship stays one radius from $X$, a flat graph. On the straight path from $B$ to $C$ its distance first shrinks toward the closest point and then grows. Only graph (B) is flat and then dips and rises, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-8-25": ["The big square has side $5$. From the figure, the small squares form steps of $1$ that put $\\overline{BC}$ $2$ to the left of $\\overline{WZ}$, with $B$ and $C$ $1$ in from the top and bottom levels of the steps, so $BC=5-2=3$. Folding $A$ onto $O$ makes the height from $A$ to $\\overline{BC}$ equal to the distance from $O$ to it, $\\tfrac52+2=\\tfrac92$. The area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot3\\cdot\\tfrac92=\\tfrac{27}4$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-1": ["Each centimeter is $6$ km, so $17$ cm is $102$ km, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-2": ["The leading digit is $2$ or $4$, and the other three digits (two $0$s and the remaining one) fill the rest in $3$ ways. So $2\\cdot3=6$ numbers, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-3": ["One meal feeds $\\tfrac{18}{12}=1.5$ people, so $12$ people need $8$ meals, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-4": ["Choosing $3$ starters from $4$ is the same as choosing the $1$ who sits out: $4$ ways, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-5": ["Each game eliminates one team, and $15$ teams must be eliminated, so $15$ games, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-6": ["She made $11$ of $20$, and $56\\%$ of $25$ is $14$, so she made $3$ of the last $5$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-7": ["The maximum is $220-26=194$, and $80\\%$ of that is $155.2$, about $155$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-8": ["The tens digit runs from $1$ to $7$, fixing the units digit each time: $16,25,34,43,52,61,70$, which is $7$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-9": ["The five numbers total $270$ and the first two $96$, so the last three total $174$, an average of $58$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-10": ["He worked $75+50+145+30=300$ minutes, $5$ hours, earning $\\$15$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-11": ["The rules force $12$ into place $2$ or $3$, $-2$ into place $3$ or $4$, and the median $6$ into places $2$ to $4$. So places $2$, $3$, $4$ hold $12$, $-2$, $6$ in some order, and the ends are $4$ and $9$, with average $6.5$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-12": ["Idle for $8$ hours used $\\tfrac8{24}=\\tfrac13$ of the battery, and $1$ hour of talking used another $\\tfrac13$. The last $\\tfrac13$ lasts $8$ idle hours, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-13": ["If I were true, II would be true too, so I is false. If II were true, Amy is not oldest and Bill is not oldest, so Celine is oldest, making III true as well. So II is false: Amy is oldest, and III must be the true one, so Celine is not youngest. The order is Amy, Celine, Bill, choice (E).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-14": ["With the lower-left peg at $(0,0)$, the corners are $(0,5)$, $(3,4)$, $(10,10)$, $(4,0)$. The Shoelace Formula gives $22\\tfrac12$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-15": ["The next ring of hexagons around the figure has $18$ tiles, all unshaded. Then there are $6+18=24$ unshaded and $13$ shaded tiles, a difference of $11$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-16": ["The pitchers hold $200$ and $240$ mL of juice in $1200$ mL of mixture, so the fraction is $\\tfrac{440}{1200}=\\tfrac{11}{30}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-17": ["Give each friend one pencil, then share the other $3$ freely: by stars and bars, $\\binom52=10$ ways, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-18": ["Ben's $4$ must be $1+3$, so Cindy's $7$ is $2+5$. From $4,6,7,8,9,10$, Dave's $11$ is $4+7$, Alice's $16$ is then $6+10$, and Ellen's $17$ is $8+9$. Alice hits the $6$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-19": ["The number is $2$ more than a common multiple of $3,4,5,6$, and the least common multiple is $60$, so it is $62$, between $60$ and $79$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-20": ["The $6$ empty chairs are a quarter of the chairs, so there are $24$ chairs and $18$ seated people. These are two-thirds of the room, which holds $27$ people, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-21": ["The product is odd only when both numbers are odd: $\\tfrac24\\cdot\\tfrac23=\\tfrac13$. So it is even with probability $\\tfrac23$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-22": ["Take $5$ women: $2$ single and $3$ married, so $3$ married men. Of the $8$ people, $\\tfrac38$ are married men, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-23": ["Along $JK$ her distance grows steadily; along $KL$ it keeps growing to its largest value at $L$, the far corner; along $LM$ it shrinks; and along $MJ$ it falls steadily to $0$. Only graph (D) rises to one peak and comes back to $0$ that way, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-24": ["The rectangle is $10\\times8=80$, and the four corner triangles have areas $6,15,6,15$, so the parallelogram has area $80-42=38$. Its side $HE=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5$, so the height to it is $d=\\tfrac{38}5=7.6$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-8-25": ["The squares overlap in a $2\\times2$ square, so together they cover $16+16-4=28$. The circle's diameter is that square's diagonal, $2\\sqrt2$, so its area is $2\\pi$, and the shaded area is $28-2\\pi$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-1": ["The number is $\\tfrac{60}2=30$, and half of it is $15$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-2": ["The folders cost $\\$12.50$, and $20\\%$ of that is $\\$2.50$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-3": ["Reflect each shaded square in $\\overline{BD}$. The top-right corner square lies on the diagonal, and the other four shaded squares reflect to four unshaded squares. So at least $4$ more must be shaded, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-4": ["The triangle's perimeter is $24$, so the square has side $6$ and area $36$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-5": ["Four packs can make $90$ only if they include at least three $24$-packs (otherwise the total is at most $2\\cdot24+2\\cdot12=72$), and then the fourth would need $18$ cans, which no pack holds. Five packs work: $24+24+24+12+6=90$. The minimum is $5$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-6": ["Compare $2.00d5$ with $2.0050$: it is larger exactly when $d\\ge5$, that is for $5,6,7,8,9$, which is $5$ values, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-7": ["He ends $1$ mile south and $\\tfrac34$ mile east of the start, so the distance is $\\sqrt{1+\\tfrac9{16}}=\\tfrac54$ miles, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-8": ["A product of odd numbers is odd, so $3mn$ is odd. The others are even: $m+3n$ and $3m-n$ add two odds, $3m^2+3n^2$ likewise, and $nm+3$ is even, choice (E).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-9": ["Triangle $ACD$ has two sides of $17$ with a $60^\\circ$ angle between them, so it is equilateral and $AC=17$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-10": ["Running $3$ times as fast, the second half took $\\tfrac63=2$ minutes, so the trip took $8$ minutes, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-11": ["Both compute $90\\cdot1.06\\cdot0.8$, just in a different order, so the difference is $\\$0$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-12": ["With $a$ bananas on May $1$, the five days total $5a+60=100$, so $a=8$ and May $5$ had $8+24=32$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-13": ["The $8\\times9$ rectangle has area $72$, so the missing corner rectangle has area $20$. Its height is $9-5=4$, so its width $EF$ is $5$, and its other side $DE$ is $4$. Then $DE+EF=9$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-14": ["Each division plays $2\\binom62=30$ games, $60$ for both, and the cross-division games number $6\\cdot6=36$. The total is $96$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-15": ["With sides $a,a,b$, $2a+b=23$, so $b$ is odd, and the Triangle Inequality needs $b\\lt2a=23-b$, so $b\\le11$. That gives $b=1,3,5,7,9,11$, which is $6$ triangles, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-16": ["With $12$ socks it is possible to have $4$ of each color. A $13$th sock forces $5$ of one color by the Pigeonhole Principle, so $13$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-17": ["Average speed is distance over time, the slope of the line from $O$ to each point. Evelyn's point, far up and near the left, gives the steepest slope, choice (E).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-18": ["The three-digit multiples of $13$ run from $13\\cdot8=104$ to $13\\cdot76=988$, which is $76-8+1=69$ numbers, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-19": ["The height $24$ cuts off right triangles with bases $\\sqrt{30^2-24^2}=18$ and $\\sqrt{25^2-24^2}=7$, so $AD=18+50+7=75$. The perimeter is $30+50+25+75=180$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-20": ["Moving $9$ counterclockwise is the same as $3$ clockwise. After $t$ turns Alice is $5t$ and Bob $3t$ points clockwise from $12$, which agree when $2t$ is a multiple of $12$. The first time is $t=6$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-21": ["Of the $\\binom63=20$ triples of dots, the $2$ rows are collinear, leaving $18$ triangles, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-22": ["Let the large box hold $10$ units, the medium $8$ and the small $5$, and let the small cost $1$; then the medium costs $1.5$ and the large $1.95$. The costs per unit are $0.1875$ (M), $0.195$ (L) and $0.2$ (S), so the ranking is $MLS$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-23": ["The semicircle has $\\tfrac12\\pi r^2=2\\pi$, so $r=2$. The radii to the tangent points are perpendicular to the legs, making a $2\\times2$ square at $C$, and $O$ is the midpoint of the hypotenuse, so each leg is $4$. The area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot4\\cdot4=8$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-24": ["Work backward from $200$ to $1$: the reverse moves are halving an even number and subtracting $1$. Let $d(n)$ be the fewest keystrokes from $1$ to $n$; then $d(1)=0$, $d(n)=1+d(n-1)$ for odd $n\\gt1$, and $d(n)=1+\\min\\big(d(n-1),d(n/2)\\big)$ for even $n$. These give $d(3)=2$, $d(6)=3$, $d(12)=4$, $d(25)=6$ (via $24$, $d(24)=5$), $d(50)=7$, $d(100)=8$ and $d(200)=9$, along the route $200,100,50,25,24,12,6,3,2,1$: $9$ keystrokes, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-8-25": ["Adding the shared region to both equal parts shows that the circle and the square have equal areas: $\\pi r^2=4$, so $r=\\tfrac2{\\sqrt\\pi}$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-1": ["Round each to the nearest dollar: $2+5+10=17$ (the exact total $16.91$ agrees), choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-2": ["On the AMC 8 each correct answer is worth $1$ point and nothing else counts, so his score is $13$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-3": ["She went from $2.5$ minutes per lap to $2$ minutes per lap, an improvement of $\\tfrac12$ minute, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-4": ["The net turn is $3\\tfrac34-2\\tfrac14=1\\tfrac12$ revolutions counterclockwise, which leaves the spinner pointing opposite to west: east, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-5": ["Joining the midpoints cuts off four corner triangles that together equal the smaller square, so the smaller square is half of $60$, that is $30$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-6": ["The two rectangles have perimeter $12$ each, and joining them hides $2$ inches of edge on each, so the T has perimeter $24-4=20$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-7": ["The radii are $\\pi\\approx3.14$ for $X$, $4$ for $Y$ (from $2\\pi r=8\\pi$) and $3$ for $Z$ (from $\\pi r^2=9\\pi$). In order: $Z,X,Y$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-8": ["From the totals, $136-58=78$ males listen and $200-96=104$ males were surveyed, so $\\tfrac{78}{104}=75\\%$ listen, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-9": ["The product telescopes to $\\tfrac{2006}2=1003$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-10": ["The pairs are $(1,12),(2,6),(3,4),(4,3),(6,2),(12,1)$: as $w$ grows $l$ falls, quickly at first and then slowly, with uneven gaps in $w$. That is graph (A), choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-11": ["The digit sum is $1$, $4$, $9$ or $16$. Sum $1$: $10$; sum $4$: $13,22,31,40$; sum $9$: $18,27,\\ldots,90$, nine numbers; sum $16$: $79,88,97$. That is $1+4+9+3=17$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-12": ["She got $7+16+27=50$ of $60$ right, about $83\\%$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-13": ["By $9{:}00$ Cassie has gone $6$ miles, leaving $56$. Together they close $28$ miles per hour, so they meet $2$ hours later, at $11{:}00$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-14": ["Bob takes $15$ more seconds per page, so $760\\cdot15=11400$ more seconds, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-15": ["If Chandra reads $x$ pages, $30x=45(760-x)$, so $75x=34200$ and $x=456$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-16": ["Together they read $\\tfrac1{20}+\\tfrac1{45}+\\tfrac1{30}=\\tfrac{19}{180}$ pages per second, so reading at the same time the $760$ pages take $760\\cdot\\tfrac{180}{19}=7200$ seconds each, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-17": ["Spinner $Q$ is always even and $R$ always odd, so the sum is odd exactly when $P$ is even, which happens with probability $\\tfrac13$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-18": ["The surface has $54$ unit squares. Each black corner cube shows $3$, so $24$ are black and $30$ white, $\\tfrac{30}{54}=\\tfrac59$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-19": ["The congruence gives $AB=CE=11$, so $BC=AB=11$, and $D$ is its midpoint: $BD=5.5$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-20": ["There are $\\binom62=15$ games and one win per game. The others won $4+3+2+2+2=13$, so Monica won $2$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-21": ["The rock displaces $1000$ cubic centimeters over a base of $100\\cdot40=4000$ square centimeters, raising the water $0.25$ cm, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-22": ["With bottom numbers $a,b,c$, the top is $(a+b)+(b+c)=a+2b+c$. The largest is $2\\cdot9+8+7=33$ and the smallest $2\\cdot1+2+3=7$, a difference of $26$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-23": ["Numbers leaving $4$ on division by $6$ are $4,10,16,22,28,\\ldots$; the first leaving $3$ on division by $5$ is $28$. Divided among $7$ people, $28$ leaves $0$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-24": ["$\\overline{CDCD}=101\\cdot\\overline{CD}$, so $\\overline{ABA}\\cdot\\overline{CD}=101\\cdot\\overline{CD}$ and $\\overline{ABA}=101$. Then $A=1$, $B=0$ and $A+B=1$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-8-25": ["All three cards have the same sum $S$. The hidden primes behind the even $44$ and $38$ have the same parity, opposite to the prime behind odd $59$. They cannot both be even, since that would repeat the prime $2$, contrary to the six distinct numbers. Thus the prime behind $59$ is $2$ and $S=61$. The hidden primes are $17$, $2$, and $23$, with average $14$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-1": ["She needs $60$ hours and has $48$, so she must work $12$ hours, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-2": ["The graph shows $250$ for spaghetti and $100$ for manicotti, a ratio of $\\tfrac52$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-3": ["$250=2\\cdot5^3$, so the two smallest prime factors are $2$ and $5$, with sum $7$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-4": ["There are $6$ windows to enter by and then $5$ to leave by: $30$ ways, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-5": ["His gifts give $\\$100$, so he must earn $\\$400$, which takes $\\tfrac{400}{16}=25$ weeks, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-6": ["The cost fell by $34$ cents out of $41$, about $83\\%$, closest to $80$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-7": ["The ages total $150$; without the $18$-year-old they total $132$ for $4$ people, an average of $33$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-8": ["$ABED$ is a $3\\times3$ square, so $BE=3$ and $EC=6-3=3$. The right triangle $BEC$ has area $\\tfrac12\\cdot3\\cdot3=4.5$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-9": ["In row $1$ the missing $3$ and $4$ go so that the $4$ avoids the last column (which already has a $4$): row $1$ ends in $3$. In row $2$ the missing $1$ and $4$ likewise put $1$ in the last column. The last column then has $3,1,4$, so its bottom entry is $2$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-10": ["$\\boxed{11}=1+11=12$, and $\\boxed{12}=1+2+3+4+6+12=28$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-11": ["Tile III has a $1$ on its left that only tile IV's right side matches, and a $7$ on top that only tile I's bottom matches. So I sits above III in the right column, with III at $D$ and I at $B$, and IV is to the left of III, at $C$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-12": ["The hexagon splits into $6$ equilateral triangles of side $1$, and each extension is one more such triangle. So the ratio is $6:6=1:1$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-13": ["With $|A|=|B|=x$, inclusion-exclusion gives $2x-1001=2007$, so $x=1504$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-14": ["The height is $\\tfrac{2\\cdot60}{24}=5$, and it bisects the base, so each congruent side is $\\sqrt{12^2+5^2}=13$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-15": ["Since $c\\gt b$ and $a\\gt0$, $a+c\\gt b$ always, so choice (A) is impossible. Choices (B), (C), and (E) all hold for $(a,b,c)=(\\tfrac12,1,2)$, while (D) holds for $(\\tfrac14,1,2)$.","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-16": ["$C=2\\pi r$ grows in equal steps while $A=\\pi r^2=\\tfrac{C^2}{4\\pi}$ grows by larger and larger steps. So the points rise, more steeply each time, as in graph (A), choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-17": ["The mixture had $9$ liters of yellow; now it has $14$ liters in $35$, that is $40\\%$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-18": ["The last four digits of the product depend only on the last four digits of the factors, $0303$ and $0505$: $303\\cdot505=153015$, ending in $3015$. So $A=3$, $B=5$, and $A+B=8$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-19": ["$(n+1)^2-n^2=2n+1$, which is the sum of the two integers, an odd number below $100$. Of the choices only $79$ qualifies, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-20": ["With $g$ games before district play, $0.45g+6=\\tfrac12(g+8)$, so $0.05g=2$ and $g=40$. They played $48$ games in all, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-21": ["Of the $\\binom82=28$ pairs, $2\\binom42=12$ share a color and $4$ share a letter, with no overlap. The probability is $\\tfrac{16}{28}=\\tfrac47$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-22": ["For any point inside, the distances to opposite sides add to $10$, so the four distances add to $20$ and average $5$, wherever the lemming is, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-23": ["Each blade has corners at three grid points and the center $(2.5,2.5)$; for the top-left blade, $(1,5),(1,4),(0,4),(2.5,2.5)$, the Shoelace Formula gives $1.5$. Four blades make $6$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-24": ["The number is a multiple of $3$ exactly when its digit sum is. The digit sets $\\{1,2,3\\}$, $\\{1,2,4\\}$, $\\{1,3,4\\}$, $\\{2,3,4\\}$ are equally likely, with sums $6,7,8,9$, so the probability is $\\tfrac24=\\tfrac12$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-8-25": ["Each inner region has area $3\\pi$ and each outer region $9\\pi$, out of $36\\pi$. The value $1$ covers $3\\pi+9\\pi+9\\pi$, probability $\\tfrac7{12}$, and $2$ has $\\tfrac5{12}$. An odd score needs one of each: $2\\cdot\\tfrac7{12}\\cdot\\tfrac5{12}=\\tfrac{35}{72}$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-1": ["She spent $12+24=36$ dollars, leaving $14$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-2": ["The letters stand for $B=0,E=1,S=2,T=3,O=4,F=5,L=6,U=7,C=8,K=9$, so CLUE is $8671$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-3": ["February $1$ is $12$ days before the $13$th, and $12\\equiv5\\pmod7$, so it is $5$ weekdays before Friday: Sunday, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-4": ["The three trapezoids share $16-1=15$, so each has area $5$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-5": ["He rode $1661-1441=220$ miles in $10$ hours, $22$ miles per hour, choice (E).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-6": ["In units of the small squares, the figure is $4\\times4=16$. The shaded parts are the central $2\\times2$ square and two small squares, $6$ in all, leaving $10$ unshaded: $3:5$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-7": ["$M=\\tfrac35\\cdot45=27$ and $N=\\tfrac53\\cdot60=100$, so $M+N=127$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-8": ["The bars show $100,60,40,120$ dollars, a total of $320$ over $4$ months, $80$ per month, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-9": ["The investment becomes $0.85\\cdot1.2=1.02$ times the original, a $2\\%$ gain, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-10": ["The ages total $6\\cdot40+4\\cdot25=340$ for $10$ people, an average of $34$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-11": ["By inclusion-exclusion $20+26-39=7$ students have both, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-12": ["The bounces reach $2,\\tfrac43,\\tfrac89,\\tfrac{16}{27}\\approx0.59,\\tfrac{32}{81}\\approx0.40$, so the $5$th is the first below $0.5$ meters, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-13": ["Each box is weighed twice, so the three readings add to twice the total: $\\tfrac{122+125+127}2=187$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-14": ["With A in the corner, the rest of the first row is BC or CB, and the first column is BC or CB. Each of these $2\\cdot2=4$ choices completes in exactly one way, so there are $4$ arrangements, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-15": ["The first eight games total $37$. The ninth score under $10$ making the total a multiple of $9$ is $8$, giving $45$; the tenth making it a multiple of $10$ is $5$. The product is $40$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-16": ["The volume is $7$. The center cube is hidden, and each of the six outer cubes shows $5$ faces, so the surface area is $30$. The ratio is $7:30$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-17": ["The sides add to $25$. The largest area is $12\\cdot13=156$ and the smallest $1\\cdot24=24$, a difference of $132$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-18": ["The path is a quarter of the big circle ($10\\pi$), a radius piece ($10$), a quarter of the small circle ($5\\pi$), a diameter of the small circle ($20$), another quarter of the small circle ($5\\pi$) and another radius piece ($10$). The total is $20\\pi+40$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-19": ["Of the $\\binom82=28$ pairs, the $8$ neighbouring pairs around the square are one unit apart, so the probability is $\\tfrac8{28}=\\tfrac27$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-20": ["With $b$ boys and $g$ girls, $\\tfrac23b=\\tfrac34g$, so $8b=9g$. The least is $b=9$, $g=8$, which is $17$ students, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-21": ["The wedge is half of the cylinder of radius $4$ and height $6$: $\\tfrac12\\pi\\cdot16\\cdot6=48\\pi\\approx151$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-22": ["$n$ is a multiple of $3$ with $\\tfrac n3\\ge100$, so $n\\ge300$, and $3n\\le999$, so $n\\le333$. The multiples of $3$ from $300$ to $333$ number $12$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-23": ["Take the square to have side $3$, so $EF=ED=1$ and $AF=CD=2$. Subtract the three corner triangles from its area: $[ABF]=3$, $[BCD]=3$, and $[EFD]=\\tfrac12$. Thus $[BFD]=9-3-3-\\tfrac12=\\tfrac52$, a fraction $\\tfrac5{18}$ of the square, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-24": ["Count the $60$ equally likely pairs whose product is a square: $(1,1),(1,4),(2,2),(3,3),(4,1),(4,4),(5,5),(6,6),(8,2),(9,1),(9,4)$, which is $11$. The probability is $\\tfrac{11}{60}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-8-25": ["The circles have radii $2,4,6,8,10,12$, and the shaded parts are the smallest circle and the rings from $4$ to $6$ and from $8$ to $10$: $\\pi(4+20+36)=60\\pi$. Out of $144\\pi$ that is about $42\\%$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-1": ["After giving Ann half, she had $3+4=7$ apples, so she bought $14$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-2": ["$28$ is $7$ times $4$, so the dealership expects $7\\cdot7=49$ sedans, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-3": ["The graph shows $1$ mile every $5$ minutes, so in $30$ minutes she rides $6$ miles, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-4": ["The pieces are straight bars of $1$ to $5$ squares, so every figure needs a straight row or column of $5$ squares for the longest bar. Figure (B) has no line of $5$ squares, so it cannot be formed, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-5": ["Continue: $1,2,3,6,11,20,37,68$. The eighth number is $68$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-6": ["The hoses supply $4\\cdot2.5=10$ gallons per minute, so the pool takes $2400$ minutes, which is $40$ hours, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-7": ["Take $CD=3$ on the railroad as the base. The height is the distance from $A$ to the railroad, $AB=3$, so the area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot3\\cdot3=4.5$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-8": ["The new area is $1.1\\cdot0.9=0.99$ of the old, that is $99\\%$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-9": ["The polygons have $3+4+5+6+7+8=33$ sides. Each of the $5$ joins hides one side of each of the two polygons it joins, removing $10$, so the figure has $23$ sides, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-10": ["The squares away from the edge form a $6\\times6$ block, $36$ of the $64$, a probability of $\\tfrac9{16}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-11": ["The price divides both $143=11\\cdot13$ and $195=3\\cdot5\\cdot13$, so it is $13$ cents ($1$ cent would mean $195$ sixth graders, more than $30$). Then $11$ seventh graders and $15$ sixth graders bought pencils, a difference of $4$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-12": ["An odd number plus an even one gives an odd sum from $3$ to $11$. Of the $9$ equally likely pairs, only $3+6$ and $5+4$ give the non-prime $9$, so the probability is $\\tfrac79$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-13": ["The number is divisible by $5$ exactly when it ends in $5$, which happens for $1$ of the $3$ equally likely last digits: $\\tfrac13$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-14": ["The trip takes $\\tfrac{50}{60}+\\tfrac{50}{40}=\\tfrac{25}{12}$ hours for $100$ miles, an average of $48$ miles per hour, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-15": ["Chocolate allows $\\tfrac52\\cdot5=12.5$ servings, sugar $8\\cdot5=40$, and milk $\\tfrac74\\cdot5=8.75$. The milk limits her to $8\\tfrac34$ servings, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-16": ["The digit sets with product $24$ are $\\{1,3,8\\}$, $\\{1,4,6\\}$, $\\{2,3,4\\}$, each in $6$ orders, and $\\{2,2,6\\}$ in $3$ orders. The total is $21$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-17": ["$360=2^3\\cdot3^2\\cdot5$. To make a square, multiply by $2\\cdot5$, so $x=10$; to make a cube, multiply by $3\\cdot5^2$, so $y=75$. The sum is $85$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-18": ["The unshaded tiles sit in the odd-numbered rows and columns: $4\\times4$ on the $7\\times7$ floor. On a $15\\times15$ floor that is $8\\times8=64$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-19": ["If $70^\\circ$ is a base angle, the apex is $40^\\circ$ (or $x$ is the other base angle, $70^\\circ$); if it is the apex, the base angles are $55^\\circ$. So $x$ can be $40$, $55$ or $70$, with sum $165$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-20": ["The points form a $2\\times4$ grid with unit spacing. Every triangle has two vertices in one row, and listing the triangles by their squared side lengths gives $8$ shapes: $(1,1,2)$, $(1,2,5)$, $(1,4,5)$, $(1,5,10)$, $(1,9,10)$, $(2,2,4)$, $(2,4,10)$, $(2,5,9)$. The answer is $8$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-21": ["Both add up the same grand total $S$: $A=\\tfrac S{40}$ and $B=\\tfrac S{75}$. So $\\tfrac AB=\\tfrac{75}{40}=\\tfrac{15}8$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-22": ["Writing numbers below $1000$ with three digits (leading zeros allowed), each digit has $9$ choices other than $1$, giving $9^3=729$ strings, one of which is $000$. So $728$ numbers, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-23": ["With $g$ girls and $g+2$ boys she gave out $g^2+(g+2)^2=394$ jelly beans. Dividing by $2$ gives $g^2+2g+2=197$, so completing the square gives $(g+1)^2=196$. Since $g$ is positive, $g=13$, there are $15$ boys, and $28$ students, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-24": ["In the sum the units digits give $B+A$ ending in $A$, so $B=0$. In the difference, $0-A$ needs a borrow, so $10-A=A$ and $A=5$; the tens give $5-1-C=0$, so $C=4$. Then $50+45=95$ and $D=9$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-8-25": ["The tops and bottoms of the four pieces are all exposed, $8$ square feet. Seen from the front or back the solid shows the four heights side by side, $1$ square foot each way. Seen from either end, the exposed vertical faces add up to the tallest piece, $A$, of height $\\tfrac12$, so $1$ more. The total is $11$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-1": ["Factor $10^{2000}$ out of the top: $\\tfrac{10^{2000}(1+100)}{2\\cdot10^{2001}}=\\tfrac{101}{20}=5.05$, closest to $5$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-2": ["$(2,12,9)=\\tfrac2{12}+\\tfrac{12}9+\\tfrac92=\\tfrac16+\\tfrac43+\\tfrac92=\\tfrac{1+8+27}6=6$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-3": ["There are five ways to group four $2$s. Besides $2^{(2^{(2^2)})}=2^{16}$, the grouping $2^{((2^2)^2)}=2^{16}$ gives the same value, and the other three, $(2^2)^{(2^2)}$, $(2^{(2^2)})^2$ and $((2^2)^2)^2$, all equal $2^8=256$. So there is $1$ other value, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-4": ["$mn\\le m+n$ is $(m-1)(n-1)\\le1$, and $n=1$ makes the left side $0$ for every $m$. So every positive integer $m$ works: infinitely many, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-5": ["The large circle passes through the outer edges of the ring circles, whose centers are $2$ from the middle, so its radius is $2+1=3$. The shaded region is the large circle minus seven unit circles: $9\\pi-7\\pi=2\\pi$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-6": ["Undo her steps: the number is $3\\cdot43+9=138$. Done correctly, $\\tfrac{138-3}9=15$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-7": ["Equal arc lengths give $\\tfrac{45}{360}\\cdot2\\pi r_A=\\tfrac{30}{360}\\cdot2\\pi r_B$, so $3r_A=2r_B$ and $\\tfrac{r_A}{r_B}=\\tfrac23$. Areas go as the square: $\\tfrac49$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-8": ["Take the red square as $2\\times2$ in a $4\\times4$ flag, so the border ring has area $16-4=12$. Each white square is a diamond inscribed in a $1\\times1$ cell, area $\\tfrac12$, and there are $12$ of them along the ring, so $W=6$ and the blue triangles fill the rest, $B=12-6=6$. So $B=W$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-9": ["Divide by $1001$: $C-2A=4$ and $B+3A=5$. Adding them gives $A+B+C=9$, so the average is $3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-10": ["Factor out $2x+3$: $(2x+3)(2x-10)=0$, with roots $-\\tfrac32$ and $5$. Their sum is $\\tfrac72$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-11": ["No disk holds a $0.8$ file with a $0.7$ one ($1.5\\gt1.44$), a $0.8$ file leaves room for one $0.4$ file, two $0.7$ files fill a disk, and three $0.4$ files fit together. So pair each $0.8$ file with a $0.4$ file ($3$ disks), put the $0.7$ files two to a disk ($6$ disks), and the remaining $12$ files of $0.4$ three to a disk ($4$ disks): $13$ disks. Using $p$ disks with a single $0.7$ file (each holding at most one $0.4$ file) only costs more, $3+p+\\tfrac{12-p}2+\\lceil\\tfrac{12-p}3\\rceil\\ge13$. The answer is $13$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-12": ["The distance is fixed and the two trips miss the target time by the same three minutes in opposite directions, so the required speed is the harmonic mean of the two: $\\frac2s = \\frac1{40} + \\frac1{60}$ gives $s = 48$. Setting up $d = 40(t + \\tfrac1{20}) = 60(t - \\tfrac1{20})$ and solving for $d/t$ reaches the same place with more algebra. The speed is $48$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-13": ["$15$-$20$-$25$ is a $3$-$4$-$5$ right triangle scaled by $5$. Its two legs are altitudes of length $15$ and $20$, and the altitude to the hypotenuse is $\\tfrac{15\\cdot20}{25}=12$, the shortest, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-14": ["The roots add to $63$, an odd number, so one root is even, that is $2$. The other is $61$, which is prime, so $k=2\\cdot61=122$ is the only value. That is $1$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-15": ["A two-digit prime ends in $1$, $3$, $7$ or $9$, so those four digits are the units digits and $2$, $4$, $5$, $6$ are the tens digits. The sum is $10(2+4+5+6)+(1+3+7+9)=190$, choice (E), and such primes exist: $29$, $41$, $53$, $67$.","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-16": ["Let $s=a+b+c+d$; then every expression equals $s+5$, so $a=s+4$, $b=s+3$, $c=s+2$, $d=s+1$. Adding gives $s=4s+10$, so $s=-\\tfrac{10}3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-17": ["After the first pour, cup $1$ holds $2$ ounces of coffee and cup $2$ holds $2$ of coffee and $4$ of cream. Half of cup $2$ is $1$ ounce of coffee and $2$ of cream. Cup $1$ then holds $3$ ounces of coffee and $2$ of cream, so $\\tfrac25$ of it is cream, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-18": ["On the surface, the $8$ corner dice show three faces, the $12$ edge dice two, and the $6$ face-center dice one. Three faces meeting at a corner are pairwise adjacent, so they can be $1,2,3$ (sum $6$); two adjacent faces can be $1,2$ (sum $3$); one face can be $1$. The smallest total is $8\\cdot6+12\\cdot3+6\\cdot1=90$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-19": ["The hexagon's interior angle is $120^\\circ$, so from the tethered vertex Spot sweeps a $240^\\circ$ sector of radius $2$, area $\\tfrac23\\cdot4\\pi=\\tfrac{8\\pi}3$. Around each neighboring vertex the last yard of rope sweeps a further $60^\\circ$ sector of radius $1$, area $\\tfrac\\pi6$ each. The total is $\\tfrac{8\\pi}3+\\tfrac\\pi3=3\\pi$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-20": ["$HC\\parallel AG$ makes triangle $DHC$ similar to $DGA$, so $HC=\\tfrac{DC}{DA}AG=\\tfrac13AG$. $JE\\parallel AG$ makes triangle $FJE$ similar to $FGA$, so $JE=\\tfrac{FE}{FA}AG=\\tfrac15AG$. The ratio is $\\tfrac{1/3}{1/5}=\\tfrac53$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-21": ["The eight numbers $a_1\\le\\cdots\\le a_8$ add to $64$, the median gives $a_4+a_5=16$ with $a_5\\ge8$, and the range gives $a_1=M-8$ for the largest $M$. Then $a_2,a_3\\ge M-8$ and $a_6,a_7\\ge8$, so $64\\ge3(M-8)+16+16+M=4M+8$ and $M\\le14$. The set $6,6,6,8,8,8,8,14$ meets every condition ($8$ appears most often), so the answer is $14$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-22": ["Each operation on $n$ tiles removes $\\lfloor\\sqrt n\\rfloor$ of them. From a perfect square $k^2$, one step leaves $k^2-k$, which still has floor root $k-1$, and the next leaves $(k-1)^2$. So it takes $2$ steps to go from each $k^2$ to $(k-1)^2$, and from $10^2$ to $1^2$ that is $2\\cdot9=18$ steps, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-23": ["The altitude from $E$ hits the midpoint of $BC$, $6$ from $B$, and has length $\\sqrt{10^2-6^2}=8$. Triangle $BEC$ has perimeter $32$, so $AED$ has perimeter $64$. With $t=AB+6$, $AE=DE=\\sqrt{64+t^2}$ and $2t+2\\sqrt{64+t^2}=64$, so $\\sqrt{64+t^2}=32-t$, giving $64=1024-64t$ and $t=15$. So $AB=9$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-24": ["Tina's $10$ pairs have sums $3,4,5,5,6,6,7,7,8,9$. For a sum $s$, Sergio wins with probability $\\tfrac{10-s}{10}$. Averaging over the pairs: $\\tfrac{7+6+5+5+4+4+3+3+2+1}{100}=\\tfrac{40}{100}=\\tfrac25$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10a-25": ["Slide leg $DA$ parallel to itself until $D$ lands on $C$. It cuts off a triangle with sides $5$, $12$ and $52-39=13$, a right triangle, and its altitude to the side of length $13$ is the trapezoid's height, $\\tfrac{5\\cdot12}{13}=\\tfrac{60}{13}$. The area is $\\tfrac{52+39}2\\cdot\\tfrac{60}{13}=210$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-1": ["Factor out $2x+3$: $(2x+3)(2x-10)=0$, with roots $-\\tfrac32$ and $5$. Their sum is $\\tfrac72$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-2": ["Undo her steps: the number is $3\\cdot43+9=138$. Done correctly, $\\tfrac{138-3}9=15$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-3": ["There are five ways to group four $2$s. Besides $2^{(2^{(2^2)})}=2^{16}$, the grouping $2^{((2^2)^2)}=2^{16}$ gives the same value, and the other three, $(2^2)^{(2^2)}$, $(2^{(2^2)})^2$ and $((2^2)^2)^2$, all equal $2^8=256$. So there is $1$ other value, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-4": ["With the angle $x$, $90-x=\\tfrac{180-x}4$, so $360-4x=180-x$ and $x=60$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-5": ["The large circle passes through the outer edges of the ring circles, whose centers are $2$ from the middle, so its radius is $2+1=3$. The shaded region is the large circle minus seven unit circles: $9\\pi-7\\pi=2\\pi$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-6": ["$mn\\le m+n$ is $(m-1)(n-1)\\le1$, and $n=1$ makes the left side $0$ for every $m$. So every positive integer $m$ works: infinitely many, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-7": ["Equal arc lengths give $\\tfrac{45}{360}\\cdot2\\pi r_A=\\tfrac{30}{360}\\cdot2\\pi r_B$, so $3r_A=2r_B$ and $\\tfrac{r_A}{r_B}=\\tfrac23$. Areas go as the square: $\\tfrac49$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-8": ["Take the red square as $2\\times2$ in a $4\\times4$ flag, so the border ring has area $16-4=12$. Each white square is a diamond inscribed in a $1\\times1$ cell, area $\\tfrac12$, and there are $12$ of them along the ring, so $W=6$ and the blue triangles fill the rest, $B=12-6=6$. So $B=W$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-9": ["No disk holds a $0.8$ file with a $0.7$ one ($1.5\\gt1.44$), a $0.8$ file leaves room for one $0.4$ file, two $0.7$ files fill a disk, and three $0.4$ files fit together. So pair each $0.8$ file with a $0.4$ file ($3$ disks), put the $0.7$ files two to a disk ($6$ disks), and the remaining $12$ files of $0.4$ three to a disk ($4$ disks): $13$ disks. Using $p$ disks with a single $0.7$ file (each holding at most one $0.4$ file) only costs more, $3+p+\\tfrac{12-p}2+\\lceil\\tfrac{12-p}3\\rceil\\ge13$. The answer is $13$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-10": ["After the first pour, cup $1$ holds $2$ ounces of coffee and cup $2$ holds $2$ of coffee and $4$ of cream. Half of cup $2$ is $1$ ounce of coffee and $2$ of cream. Cup $1$ then holds $3$ ounces of coffee and $2$ of cream, so $\\tfrac25$ of it is cream, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-12": ["The roots add to $63$, an odd number, so one root is even, that is $2$. The other is $61$, which is prime, so $k=2\\cdot61=122$ is the only value. That is $1$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-13": ["A number differing from its reciprocal by $1$ solves $a-\\tfrac1a=1$ or $a-\\tfrac1a=-1$, that is $a^2-a-1=0$ or $a^2+a-1=0$. The positive roots are $\\tfrac{1+\\sqrt5}2$ and $\\tfrac{\\sqrt5-1}2$, with sum $\\sqrt5$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-14": ["$N=\\log_{2002}(11\\cdot13\\cdot14)^2=\\log_{2002}2002^2=2$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-15": ["The eight numbers $a_1\\le\\cdots\\le a_8$ add to $64$, the median gives $a_4+a_5=16$ with $a_5\\ge8$, and the range gives $a_1=M-8$ for the largest $M$. Then $a_2,a_3\\ge M-8$ and $a_6,a_7\\ge8$, so $64\\ge3(M-8)+16+16+M=4M+8$ and $M\\le14$. The set $6,6,6,8,8,8,8,14$ meets every condition ($8$ appears most often), so the answer is $14$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-16": ["Tina's $10$ pairs have sums $3,4,5,5,6,6,7,7,8,9$. For a sum $s$, Sergio wins with probability $\\tfrac{10-s}{10}$. Averaging over the pairs: $\\tfrac{7+6+5+5+4+4+3+3+2+1}{100}=\\tfrac{40}{100}=\\tfrac25$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-17": ["A prime with two or more digits ends in $1$, $3$, $7$ or $9$, so the even digits $4$, $6$, $8$ cannot be units digits; at best they are tens digits. Then the sum is at least $(4+6+8)\\cdot10+(45-18)=207$, and $\\{2,3,5,41,67,89\\}$ reaches it, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-18": ["The centers are $25$ apart and the radii are $6$ and $9$. The shortest common tangent segment is an internal one, of length $\\sqrt{25^2-(6+9)^2}=20$ (an external one is $\\sqrt{25^2-3^2}$), choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-19": ["From the graph, $f(y)=6$ only at $y=-2$ and $y=1$. The line $y=-2$ crosses the graph $2$ times (on the outer two pieces), and the line $y=1$ crosses it $4$ times (once on each piece). So $f(f(x))=6$ has $6$ solutions, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-20": ["$0.\\overline{ab}=\\tfrac{\\overline{ab}}{99}$, and in lowest terms the denominator is a divisor of $99$ other than $1$. Each of $3,9,11,33,99$ occurs (from $\\tfrac{33}{99},\\tfrac{11}{99},\\tfrac9{99},\\tfrac3{99},\\tfrac1{99}$), so there are $5$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-21": ["The terms run $4,7,1,8,9,7,6,3,9,2,1,3$ and then $4,7$ again, so the sequence has period $12$ with sum $60$ per period. After $166$ periods ($1992$ terms) the sum is $9960$; the next terms add $4,11,12,20,29,36,42$, first passing $40$ at the $7$th. So $n=1999$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-22": ["Here $BC=5$ and $AC=5\\sqrt3$, and $BD^2=25+CD^2$, so $BD\\gt5\\sqrt2$ means $CD\\gt5$. Those $D$ fill the part of $\\overline{AC}$ beyond the point $D_0$ with $CD_0=5$, and the matching $P$ fill triangle $ABD_0$. Sharing the height from $B$, its area is the fraction $\\tfrac{AD_0}{AC}=\\tfrac{5\\sqrt3-5}{5\\sqrt3}=\\tfrac{3-\\sqrt3}3$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-23": ["$D$ is on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{BC}$, so $DB=DC=7$ and $\\angle DBC=\\angle C$; the bisector makes $\\angle ABD$ equal to it too. So triangles $ABD$ and $ACB$ share $\\angle A$ and have $\\angle ABD=\\angle ACB$, hence are similar: $AB^2=AD\\cdot AC=9\\cdot16$ and $AB=12$. Triangle $ABD$ has sides $12,9,7$, and Heron's Formula gives $\\sqrt{14\\cdot2\\cdot5\\cdot7}=14\\sqrt5$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-24": ["Taking absolute values, $|z|^{2002}=|z|$, so $|z|=0$ or $1$. The case $z=0$ gives one pair. If $|z|=1$, then $\\overline z=\\tfrac1z$ and the equation becomes $z^{2003}=1$, with $2003$ roots on the unit circle. The total is $2004$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12a-25": ["The coefficients of $Q$ are the same in number as the nonzero ones of $P$ and have the same sum, so $Q(1)=P(1)$: the two graphs must meet at $x=1$. Only graph (B) shows that, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-1": ["Split $6^{2002}=2^{2002}\\cdot3^{2002}$: the ratio is $2^{-1}\\cdot3^{1}=\\tfrac32$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-2": ["$(2,4,6)=\\tfrac{2\\cdot4\\cdot6}{2+4+6}=\\tfrac{48}{12}=4$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-3": ["Write each term as $10^k-1$: the sum is $(10+100+\\cdots+10^9)-9=1111111110-9=1111111101$. Dividing by $9$ gives $M=123456789$, which has no $0$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-4": ["Factor $3x-2$ out of the first two terms: $(3x-2)\\big((4x+1)-4x\\big)+1=3x-1$. At $x=4$ that is $11$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-5": ["The two circles lie along a diameter of the big one, so its diameter is $4+6=10$ and its radius $5$. The shaded area is $25\\pi-4\\pi-9\\pi=12\\pi$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-6": ["$n^2-3n+2=(n-1)(n-2)$ is a product of two consecutive integers, so it is even, and it is prime only when it equals $2$. That happens for $n=3$ alone among positive integers, so there is one, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-7": ["$\\tfrac12+\\tfrac13+\\tfrac17=\\tfrac{41}{42}$, and adding $\\tfrac1n\\le1$ reaches an integer only at $1$, so $n=42$. Then $2$, $3$, $6$ and $7$ divide $n$, but $n\\gt84$ is false, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-8": ["A $31$-day month is four weeks plus three days, and the days of the week that occur five times are those of its first three dates. July has five Mondays, so July $1$ is a Saturday, Sunday or Monday, and August $1$, $31\\equiv3\\pmod7$ days later, is a Tuesday, Wednesday or Thursday. The five-times days of August are then Tue to Thu, Wed to Fri or Thu to Sat, and only Thursday is in all three, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-9": ["In alphabetical order $A\\lt M\\lt O\\lt S\\lt U$. Words starting with $A$, $M$, $O$ or $S$ come first, $4\\cdot4!=96$ of them. Then the words starting $UA$, $UM$, $UO$, $3\\cdot3!=18$ more. $USAMO$ is the first word starting $US$, so it is number $96+18+1=115$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-10": ["By Vieta, $a+b=-a$ and $ab=b$. Since $b\\ne0$, $a=1$, and then $b=-2a=-2$. The pair is $(1,-2)$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-11": ["With the integers $n-1$, $n$, $n+1$, the condition is $(n-1)n(n+1)=8\\cdot3n$, so $n^2-1=24$ and $n=5$. The squares add to $16+25+36=77$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-12": ["Cross-multiplying gives $x^2-7x+6=x^2-(k+2)x+2k$, that is $(k-5)x=2k-6$. For $k=5$ this reads $0=4$, so there is no solution, choice (E). For the other choices $x=\\tfrac{2k-6}{k-5}$ is a solution, since it equals $2$ for no $k$ and $6$ only for $k=6$.","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-13": ["Group by $y$: $8xy-12y+2x-3=4y(2x-3)+(2x-3)=(2x-3)(4y+1)$. It vanishes for every $y$ only when $2x-3=0$, so $x=\\tfrac32$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-14": ["$25^{64}\\cdot64^{25}=5^{128}\\cdot2^{150}$, so $N=5^{64}\\cdot2^{75}=10^{64}\\cdot2^{11}=2048\\cdot10^{64}$. Its digits add to $2+0+4+8=14$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-15": ["$A+B$ is a prime greater than $2$, so it is odd, and one of $A$, $B$ is $2$; since $A-B\\gt0$, $B=2$. Then $A-2$, $A$, $A+2$ are all prime, and of three numbers spaced by $2$ one is a multiple of $3$, so $A-2=3$ and $A=5$. The primes $2,3,5,7$ add to $17$, which is prime, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-16": ["Set $\\tfrac n{20-n}=k^2$; then $n=\\tfrac{20k^2}{k^2+1}$. Since $k^2$ and $k^2+1$ are consecutive, they share no factor, so $k^2+1$ divides $20$: $k^2+1\\in\\{1,2,5,10\\}$, that is $k=0,1,2,3$. These give $n=0,10,16,18$, which is $4$ values, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-17": ["Draw the octagon with horizontal and vertical sides: its width is $2+2\\sqrt2$, and $AD$ is a full vertical width, of length $2+2\\sqrt2$. $G$ sits on the far side, at distance $\\sqrt2+2$ from line $AD$ (a corner offset $\\sqrt2$ plus the side $2$). The area is $\\tfrac12(2+2\\sqrt2)(2+\\sqrt2)=4+3\\sqrt2$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-18": ["Two distinct circles meet in at most $2$ points, and there are $\\binom42=6$ pairs, so there are at most $12$ points. Four circles of equal radius with centers close together, in general position, reach all $12$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-19": ["Each of $a_{101},\\ldots,a_{200}$ is $100d$ more than the matching term of $a_1,\\ldots,a_{100}$, so the second sum exceeds the first by $100\\cdot100d=100$. Then $d=a_2-a_1=0.01$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-20": ["Move the $b$ terms across: $a+8c=4+7b$ and $8a-c=7-4b$. Square both and add: the cross terms cancel on each side, leaving $65(a^2+c^2)=65+65b^2$. So $a^2-b^2+c^2=1$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-21": ["Give Andy's lawn area $6$, so Beth's is $3$ and Carlos's is $2$, and give Carlos's mower rate $1$, so Beth's is $2$ and Andy's is $3$. The times are $\\tfrac63=2$ for Andy, $\\tfrac32$ for Beth and $\\tfrac21=2$ for Carlos, so Beth finishes first, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-22": ["Let $OX=2a$ and $OY=2b$. Then $XN^2=4a^2+b^2=361$ and $YM^2=a^2+4b^2=484$. Adding gives $5(a^2+b^2)=845$, so $a^2+b^2=169$, and $XY=\\sqrt{4a^2+4b^2}=2\\cdot13=26$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-23": ["Take $n=1$: $a_{m+1}=a_m+1+m$. So each step adds $m+1$, and $a_{12}=1+2+\\cdots+12=\\tfrac{12\\cdot13}2=78$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-24": ["After turning through angle $\\theta$ from the bottom, the rider is $20-20\\cos\\theta$ above the bottom. Setting this to $10$ gives $\\cos\\theta=\\tfrac12$, so $\\theta=60^\\circ$, a sixth of a revolution. At one revolution a minute that takes $10$ seconds, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-10b-25": ["Say the list has $n$ numbers with mean $m$. Appending $15$: $nm+15=(n+1)(m+2)$, so $m+2n=13$. Appending $1$: $nm+16=(n+2)(m+1)$, so $2m+n=14$. Then $m=5$ and $n=4$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-1": ["Write each term as $10^k-1$: the sum is $(10+100+\\cdots+10^9)-9=1111111110-9=1111111101$. Dividing by $9$ gives $M=123456789$, which has no $0$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-2": ["Factor $3x-2$ out of the first two terms: $(3x-2)\\big((4x+1)-4x\\big)+1=3x-1$. At $x=4$ that is $11$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-3": ["$n^2-3n+2=(n-1)(n-2)$ is a product of two consecutive integers, so it is even, and it is prime only when it equals $2$. That happens for $n=3$ alone among positive integers, so there is one, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-4": ["$\\tfrac12+\\tfrac13+\\tfrac17=\\tfrac{41}{42}$, and adding $\\tfrac1n\\le1$ reaches an integer only at $1$, so $n=42$. Then $2$, $3$, $6$ and $7$ divide $n$, but $n\\gt84$ is false, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-5": ["The angles sum to $540^\\circ$, and the middle term of an arithmetic sequence is its mean, so $x=\\tfrac{540}5=108$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-6": ["By Vieta, $a+b=-a$ and $ab=b$. Since $b\\ne0$, $a=1$, and then $b=-2a=-2$. The pair is $(1,-2)$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-7": ["With the integers $n-1$, $n$, $n+1$, the condition is $(n-1)n(n+1)=8\\cdot3n$, so $n^2-1=24$ and $n=5$. The squares add to $16+25+36=77$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-8": ["A $31$-day month is four weeks plus three days, and the days of the week that occur five times are those of its first three dates. July has five Mondays, so July $1$ is a Saturday, Sunday or Monday, and August $1$, $31\\equiv3\\pmod7$ days later, is a Tuesday, Wednesday or Thursday. The five-times days of August are then Tue to Thu, Wed to Fri or Thu to Sat, and only Thursday is in all three, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-9": ["Write the terms as $a,a+k,a+2k,a+3k$. The geometric condition $(a+k)^2=a(a+3k)$ gives $k^2=ak$, so $k=a$ and $d=4a$. Then $\\tfrac ad=\\tfrac14$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-10": ["The members are $1+3k$ for $k=0,\\ldots,6$, so a sum of three is $3+3s$ where $s$ is a sum of three distinct numbers from $0$ to $6$. Such $s$ take every value from $0+1+2=3$ to $4+5+6=15$, which is $13$ sums, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-11": ["$A+B$ is a prime greater than $2$, so it is odd, and one of $A$, $B$ is $2$; since $A-B\\gt0$, $B=2$. Then $A-2$, $A$, $A+2$ are all prime, and of three numbers spaced by $2$ one is a multiple of $3$, so $A-2=3$ and $A=5$. The primes $2,3,5,7$ add to $17$, which is prime, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-12": ["Set $\\tfrac n{20-n}=k^2$; then $n=\\tfrac{20k^2}{k^2+1}$. Since $k^2$ and $k^2+1$ are consecutive, they share no factor, so $k^2+1$ divides $20$: $k^2+1\\in\\{1,2,5,10\\}$, that is $k=0,1,2,3$. These give $n=0,10,16,18$, which is $4$ values, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-13": ["Starting at $a$, the sum is $18a+153=9(2a+17)$. This is a square when $2a+17$ is an odd square, the least being $25$ at $a=4$, so the sum is $225$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-14": ["Two distinct circles meet in at most $2$ points, and there are $\\binom42=6$ pairs, so there are at most $12$ points. Four circles of equal radius with centers close together, in general position, reach all $12$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-15": ["Write $N=1000d+m$ with $m=\\tfrac N9$. Then $1000d=8m$, so $m=125d$, a three-digit number for $d=1,\\ldots,7$. That is $7$ numbers, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-16": ["The product misses a factor of $3$ only when both rolls do: $\\tfrac68\\cdot\\tfrac46=\\tfrac12$. So the probability is $1-\\tfrac12=\\tfrac12$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-17": ["Give Andy's lawn area $6$, so Beth's is $3$ and Carlos's is $2$, and give Carlos's mower rate $1$, so Beth's is $2$ and Andy's is $3$. The times are $\\tfrac63=2$ for Andy, $\\tfrac32$ for Beth and $\\tfrac21=2$ for Carlos, so Beth finishes first, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-18": ["The points equidistant from $(0,0)$ and $(3,1)$ form the perpendicular bisector $3x+y=5$. It cuts the rectangle from $(\\tfrac43,1)$ to $(\\tfrac53,0)$, leaving on the far side a trapezoid with parallel sides $\\tfrac23$ and $\\tfrac13$ and height $1$, area $\\tfrac12$. The near side has area $\\tfrac32$ of the $2$, a probability of $\\tfrac34$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-19": ["Adding the equations, $2(ab+bc+ca)=484$, so $ab+bc+ca=242$. Subtracting each equation from this gives $bc=90$, $ca=80$, $ab=72$. Then $(abc)^2=72\\cdot80\\cdot90=518400$ and $abc=720$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-20": ["Let $OX=2a$ and $OY=2b$. Then $XN^2=4a^2+b^2=361$ and $YM^2=a^2+4b^2=484$. Adding gives $5(a^2+b^2)=845$, so $a^2+b^2=169$, and $XY=\\sqrt{4a^2+4b^2}=2\\cdot13=26$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-21": ["No $n\\lt2002=11\\cdot13\\cdot14$ is divisible by all three. Below $2002$ there are $10$ multiples of $182=13\\cdot14$, $12$ of $154=11\\cdot14$, and $13$ of $143=11\\cdot13$. The sum is $10\\cdot11+12\\cdot13+13\\cdot14=110+156+182=448$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-22": ["By Change of Base, $a_n=\\log_{2002}n$. So $b-c=\\log_{2002}\\tfrac{2\\cdot3\\cdot4\\cdot5}{10\\cdot11\\cdot12\\cdot13\\cdot14}=\\log_{2002}\\tfrac{120}{240240}=\\log_{2002}\\tfrac1{2002}=-1$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-23": ["With $BC=a$, Apollonius's Theorem gives the median as $m^2=\\tfrac{2\\cdot1^2+2\\cdot2^2-a^2}4=\\tfrac{10-a^2}4$. Setting $m=a$: $10-a^2=4a^2$, so $a=\\sqrt2$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-24": ["Split $ABCD$ into the four triangles at $P$. Each has area at most half the product of its two sides at $P$, so the area is at most $\\tfrac12(24\\cdot32+32\\cdot28+28\\cdot45+45\\cdot24)=\\tfrac12(24+28)(32+45)=2002$. Equality forces all four angles at $P$ to be right, so the sides are $\\sqrt{24^2+32^2}=40$, $\\sqrt{32^2+28^2}=4\\sqrt{113}$, $\\sqrt{28^2+45^2}=53$, $\\sqrt{45^2+24^2}=51$, with total $144+4\\sqrt{113}=4(36+\\sqrt{113})$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2002-amc-12b-25": ["The first condition is $(x+3)^2+(y+3)^2\\le16$, a disk of radius $4$ about $(-3,-3)$. The second is $(x-y)(x+y+6)\\le0$, two opposite quarter-planes cut by perpendicular lines through that center. So $R$ is half the disk, area $8\\pi\\approx25.1$, closest to choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-1": ["Pair the $k$th even number $2k$ with the $k$th odd number $2k-1$: each pair differs by $1$, and there are $2003$ pairs. The difference is $2003$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-2": ["A T-shirt costs $4+5=9$ dollars, so each member spends $2(4+9)=26$ dollars. The league has $\\tfrac{2366}{26}=91$ members, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-3": ["The eight corner cubes remove $8\\cdot27=216$ of the box's $15\\cdot10\\cdot8=1200$ cubic centimeters, and $\\tfrac{216}{1200}=18\\%$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-4": ["Average speed is total distance over total time, not the mean of the two speeds. The round trip is $2$ km in $30+10=40$ minutes, that is $\\tfrac23$ of an hour, so the average speed is $2\\div\\tfrac23=3$ km/hr, choice (A).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-5": ["Expand: $(d-1)(e-1)=de-(d+e)+1$. By Vieta, $d+e=-\\tfrac32$ and $de=-\\tfrac52$, so the value is $-\\tfrac52+\\tfrac32+1=0$, choice (B).","Alternatively, the coefficients add to $2+3-5=0$, so $x=1$ is a root and the factor $d-1$ or $e-1$ is $0$.",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-6": ["Read each choice with $x\\heartsuit y=\\lvert x-y\\rvert$. (A), (B), (D) and (E) are true for every $x$, $y$. But $x\\heartsuit0=\\lvert x\\rvert$, which is not $x$ when $x$ is negative ($-1\\heartsuit0=1$), so the false statement is choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-7": ["List the sides $a\\le b\\le c$ with $a+b+c=7$; the triangle inequality needs $c\\lt\\tfrac72$, so $c=3$. Then $a+b=4$ with $a\\le b\\le3$: $(1,3,3)$ and $(2,2,3)$. That is $2$ triangles, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-8": ["$60=2^2\\cdot3\\cdot5$ has $3\\cdot2\\cdot2=12$ factors, and $1,2,3,4,5,6$ are the six below $7$. The probability is $\\tfrac6{12}=\\tfrac12$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-9": ["Work from the inside out with exponents: $x\\sqrt x=x^{3/2}$, whose cube root is $x^{1/2}$. Then the next layer is $x\\cdot x^{1/2}=x^{3/2}$ again, with cube root $x^{1/2}$, and the same happens at the outer layer. The value is $\\sqrt x$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-10": ["Fold the four squares with the middle one of the top row as the floor: its two neighbors in the row become the left and right walls, and the square hanging below the left one folds up into the front wall. The missing faces are the back wall and the lid, and the fifth square must become one of them. Positions $2$ and $3$ fold onto the front wall and position $1$ onto the right wall, so they fail; positions $4$ to $9$ each become the back wall or the lid. That is $6$ positions, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-11": ["$\\overline{AMC10}+\\overline{AMC12}=2\\cdot\\overline{AMC}\\cdot100+22=123422$, so $\\overline{AMC}=\\tfrac{123400}{200}=617$. Then $A+M+C=6+1+7=14$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-12": ["Inside the rectangle, $x\\lt y$ is the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(1,1)$, of area $\\tfrac12$. The rectangle has area $4$, so the probability is $\\tfrac{1/2}4=\\tfrac18$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-13": ["Call them $a$, $b$, $c$. From $a=4(b+c)$ and $a+b+c=20$, $5(b+c)=20$, so $b+c=4$ and $a=16$. With $b=7c$, $8c=4$ gives $c=\\tfrac12$ and $b=\\tfrac72$. The product is $16\\cdot\\tfrac72\\cdot\\tfrac12=28$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-14": ["The digits $d$ and $e$ are among the one-digit primes $2,3,5,7$, and $10d+e$ must be prime too: $23$, $37$, $53$ and $73$. The products are $2\\cdot3\\cdot23=138$, $3\\cdot7\\cdot37=777$, $5\\cdot3\\cdot53=795$ and $7\\cdot3\\cdot73=1533$. The largest is $1533$, with digit sum $12$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-15": ["There are $50$ even numbers up to $100$, and $\\lfloor\\tfrac{100}6\\rfloor=16$ of them are multiples of $3$. That leaves $34$, a probability of $\\tfrac{34}{100}=\\tfrac{17}{50}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-16": ["Only the units digit $3$ matters, and powers of $3$ end in $3,9,7,1$, repeating every $4$. Since $2003\\equiv3\\pmod4$, $13^{2003}$ ends like $3^3=27$, in $7$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-17": ["An equilateral triangle inscribed in a circle of radius $R$ has side $R\\sqrt3$, so its perimeter is $3\\sqrt3R$. Setting it equal to $\\pi R^2$ gives $R=\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}\\pi$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-18": ["Multiply by $x$: $\\tfrac{2003}{2004}x^2+x+1=0$. For roots $r$, $s$, $\\tfrac1r+\\tfrac1s=\\tfrac{r+s}{rs}$, and Vieta gives $r+s=-\\tfrac1a$ and $rs=\\tfrac1a$ with $a=\\tfrac{2003}{2004}$. The ratio is $-1$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-19": ["The small semicircle stands on a chord of length $1$ of the big circle, whose radius is $1$, so the chord subtends $60^\\circ$ at the big center. The lune is the small semicircle minus the big circle's segment cut off by that chord: $\\tfrac\\pi8-\\left(\\tfrac\\pi6-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}4\\right)=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}4-\\tfrac\\pi{24}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-20": ["A base-$9$ numeral has three digits for $81\\le n\\le728$, and a base-$11$ numeral for $121\\le n\\le1330$. Both hold for $121\\le n\\le728$, which is $608$ of the $900$ three-digit numbers. The probability is $\\tfrac{608}{900}\\approx0.68$, closest to $0.7$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-21": ["An assortment is a count of each kind adding to $6$, the solutions of $a+b+c=6$ in nonnegative integers. Stars and bars gives $\\binom{8}{2}=28$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-22": ["Put $A=(0,0)$, $B=(8,0)$, $C=(8,9)$, $D=(0,9)$. Then $H=(8,6)$ and $E=(0,5)$. Line $AH$ is $y=\\tfrac34x$ and line $EC$ is $y=5+\\tfrac x2$; they meet where $\\tfrac x4=5$, at $G=(20,15)$. The foot $F$ on line $AD$ is $(0,15)$, so $GF=20$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-23": ["A base row of $2003$ small triangles means $1002$ rows, the $k$th row having $k$ upward triangles. Every toothpick is an edge of exactly one upward triangle, so count those: $1+2+\\cdots+1002=\\tfrac{1002\\cdot1003}2=502503$, and $3\\cdot502503=1507509$ toothpicks, choice (C). (Check: the $3$-row figure has $3\\cdot6=18$.)","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-24": ["Red $5$ divides only blue $5$, and red $4$ only blue $4$, so each sits at an end of the stack beside that blue card. Blue $5$'s other neighbor must divide $5$, so it is red $1$. Red $3$ needs blue $3$ and blue $6$ on both sides, and red $2$ then sits between blue $6$ and blue $4$. The stack is $5,5,1,3,3,6,2,4,4$ (or reversed), and the middle three add to $3+3+6=12$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10a-25": ["Since $n=100q+r$ and $100\\equiv1\\pmod{11}$, $n\\equiv q+r\\pmod{11}$. So $q+r$ is divisible by $11$ exactly when $n$ is. The five-digit multiples of $11$ number $\\lfloor\\tfrac{99999}{11}\\rfloor-\\lfloor\\tfrac{9999}{11}\\rfloor=9090-909=8181$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-1": ["Pair the $k$th even number $2k$ with the $k$th odd number $2k-1$: each pair differs by $1$, and there are $2003$ pairs. The difference is $2003$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-2": ["A T-shirt costs $4+5=9$ dollars, so each member spends $2(4+9)=26$ dollars. The league has $\\tfrac{2366}{26}=91$ members, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-3": ["The eight corner cubes remove $8\\cdot27=216$ of the box's $15\\cdot10\\cdot8=1200$ cubic centimeters, and $\\tfrac{216}{1200}=18\\%$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-5": ["$\\overline{AMC10}+\\overline{AMC12}=2\\cdot\\overline{AMC}\\cdot100+22=123422$, so $\\overline{AMC}=\\tfrac{123400}{200}=617$. Then $A+M+C=6+1+7=14$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-6": ["Read each choice with $x\\heartsuit y=\\lvert x-y\\rvert$. (A), (B), (D) and (E) are true for every $x$, $y$. But $x\\heartsuit0=\\lvert x\\rvert$, which is not $x$ when $x$ is negative ($-1\\heartsuit0=1$), so the false statement is choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-7": ["List the sides $a\\le b\\le c$ with $a+b+c=7$; the triangle inequality needs $c\\lt\\tfrac72$, so $c=3$. Then $a+b=4$ with $a\\le b\\le3$: $(1,3,3)$ and $(2,2,3)$. That is $2$ triangles, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-8": ["$60=2^2\\cdot3\\cdot5$ has $3\\cdot2\\cdot2=12$ factors, and $1,2,3,4,5,6$ are the six below $7$. The probability is $\\tfrac6{12}=\\tfrac12$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-9": ["The reflections send $(2,3)$ to all of $(\\pm2,\\pm3)$ and $(\\pm3,\\pm2)$, and these $8$ points form a symmetric set, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-10": ["Each takes his fraction, $\\tfrac12$, $\\tfrac13$ or $\\tfrac16$, of what he finds, so in any order the part left is $\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac23\\cdot\\tfrac56=\\tfrac5{18}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-11": ["Take the perimeter $12$. The square has side $3$ and circumradius $\\tfrac3{\\sqrt2}$, so $A=\\tfrac{9\\pi}2$; the triangle has side $4$ and circumradius $\\tfrac4{\\sqrt3}$, so $B=\\tfrac{16\\pi}3$. Then $\\tfrac AB=\\tfrac{27}{32}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-12": ["Red $5$ divides only blue $5$, and red $4$ only blue $4$, so each sits at an end of the stack beside that blue card. Blue $5$'s other neighbor must divide $5$, so it is red $1$. Red $3$ needs blue $3$ and blue $6$ on both sides, and red $2$ then sits between blue $6$ and blue $4$. The stack is $5,5,1,3,3,6,2,4,4$ (or reversed), and the middle three add to $3+3+6=12$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-13": ["Fold the four squares with the middle one of the top row as the floor: its two neighbors in the row become the left and right walls, and the square hanging below the left one folds up into the front wall. The missing faces are the back wall and the lid, and the fifth square must become one of them. Positions $2$ and $3$ fold onto the front wall and position $1$ onto the right wall, so they fail; positions $4$ to $9$ each become the back wall or the lid. That is $6$ positions, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-14": ["The square has side $4$, and each apex lies on a line through the center perpendicular to a side, at distance $2+2\\sqrt3$ from the center (half a side plus the triangle's height). So $KLMN$ is a square with half-diagonal $2+2\\sqrt3$ and area $2(2+2\\sqrt3)^2=32+16\\sqrt3$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-15": ["The small semicircle stands on a chord of length $1$ of the big circle, whose radius is $1$, so the chord subtends $60^\\circ$ at the big center. The lune is the small semicircle minus the big circle's segment cut off by that chord: $\\tfrac\\pi8-\\left(\\tfrac\\pi6-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}4\\right)=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}4-\\tfrac\\pi{24}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-16": ["The three triangles $ABP$, $BCP$, $CAP$ have equal-length bases, the sides of the equilateral triangle, so their areas compare as $P$'s distances to the three sides. $\\triangle ABP$ is largest exactly when $P$ is farther from $\\overline{AB}$ than from the other two sides. The angle bisectors from $A$ and $B$ (here also medians) cut out that region, the quadrilateral formed by $C$, the midpoints of $\\overline{CA}$ and $\\overline{CB}$, and the center. By symmetry the three such regions are congruent, so the probability is $\\tfrac13$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-17": ["Put $D=(0,0)$, $A=(0,4)$, $C=(4,0)$, so $M=(2,0)$. The circles are $x^2+y^2-8y=0$ and $x^2+y^2-4x=0$; subtracting gives $x=2y$, and then $5y^2=8y$, so $P=(\\tfrac{16}5,\\tfrac85)$. Its distance to $\\overline{AD}$, the $y$-axis, is $\\tfrac{16}5$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-18": ["Since $n=100q+r$ and $100\\equiv1\\pmod{11}$, $n\\equiv q+r\\pmod{11}$. So $q+r$ is divisible by $11$ exactly when $n$ is. The five-digit multiples of $11$ number $\\lfloor\\tfrac{99999}{11}\\rfloor-\\lfloor\\tfrac{9999}{11}\\rfloor=9090-909=8181$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-19": ["Say $f(x)=a(x-5)^2+b(x-5)+c$ and $g(x)=-a(x+5)^2-b(x+5)-c$. Then $f+g=a[(x-5)^2-(x+5)^2]-10b=-20ax-10b$, a line with slope $-20a\\ne0$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-20": ["Say the first five letters hold $k$ B's and $5-k$ C's. The other $5-k$ B's cannot sit in the middle five, so they go in the last five, with $k$ A's; the middle five get the remaining $5-k$ A's and $k$ C's. Each block is then arranged in $\\binom5k$ ways, so the count is $\\sum_{k=0}^5\\binom5k^3$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-21": ["The root $0$ gives $e=0$, so $P(x)=x(x^4+ax^3+bx^2+cx+d)$. The quartic has the other four roots, all nonzero, so its constant term $d$, their product, is not $0$. The rest can vanish: $e$ always does, and $x(x^2-1)(x^2-4)=x^5-5x^3+4x$ has $a=c=0$, while the roots $0,\\pm1,2,\\tfrac12$ give $b=0$. So the answer is $d$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-22": ["The objects start $12$ steps apart, so they can meet only after $6$ steps each, at a point $(i,6-i)$ with $0\\le i\\le5$. Object $A$ gets there in $\\binom6i$ of its $2^6$ paths, and object $B$ (which needs $5-i$ left steps) in $\\binom6{5-i}$. By Vandermonde's Identity the total is $\\sum\\binom6i\\binom6{5-i}=\\binom{12}5=792$ out of $2^{12}=4096$, about $0.19$, closest to choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-23": ["Adding the exponents in $1!$ to $9!$ gives $2^{30}3^{13}5^57^3$. A square divisor takes an even exponent of each prime, so there are $16\\cdot7\\cdot3\\cdot2=672$ of them, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-24": ["With $t=\\log_ab\\in(0,1]$, the sum is $(1-t)+(1-\\tfrac1t)=2-\\left(t+\\tfrac1t\\right)$. By AM–GM $t+\\tfrac1t\\ge2$, so the sum is at most $0$, reached at $a=b$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12a-25": ["If $a=0$, $f(x)=\\sqrt{bx}$ has domain and range $[0,\\infty)$, so $a=0$ works. If $a\\gt0$, the domain is unbounded on both sides of $0$ but the range is $[0,\\infty)$, so it fails. If $a\\lt0$, the domain is $[0,-\\tfrac ba]$ and the range is $[0,\\sqrt{\\max(ax^2+bx)}]=[0,\\tfrac b{2\\sqrt{-a}}]$; these agree when $2\\sqrt{-a}=-a$, that is $a=-4$. So $2$ values, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-1": ["The numerator is $2(1-2+3-4+5-6+7)=2\\cdot4$ and the denominator is $3(1-2+3-4+5-6+7)=3\\cdot4$, so the fraction is $\\tfrac23$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-2": ["Over $14$ days the pair of pills costs $\\tfrac{546}{14}=39$ dollars a day. A green pill is $1$ more than a pink one, so green is $\\tfrac{39+1}2=20$ dollars, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-3": ["The first $8$ odd numbers add to $8^2=64$, so the five even integers add to $60$. Their middle one is the mean, $12$, and the smallest is $12-4=8$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-4": ["The regions have areas $4\\cdot5=20$, $7\\cdot3=21$, $5\\cdot3=15$, $6\\cdot1=6$ and $2\\cdot2=4$ (they fill the $11\\times6$ bed, $66$ square feet). The cheapest plan puts the cheapest flower in the largest region: $21\\cdot1+20\\cdot1.5+15\\cdot2+6\\cdot2.5+4\\cdot3=108$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-5": ["Each pass cuts a new strip $28-4=24$ inches, $2$ feet, wide. Mowing along the $150$-foot side takes $\\tfrac{90}2=45$ passes, $45\\cdot150=6750$ feet, which is $\\tfrac{6750}{5000}=1.35$ hours, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-6": ["A $4:3$ rectangle has a $3$-$4$-$5$ right triangle under its diagonal, so the sides are $\\tfrac45$ and $\\tfrac35$ of the diagonal. The horizontal length is $\\tfrac45\\cdot27=21.6$, closest to $21.5$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-7": ["Group the terms with the same floor: $\\lfloor\\sqrt k\\rfloor=1$ for $k=1$ to $3$, $2$ for $4$ to $8$, $3$ for $9$ to $15$, and $4$ for $16$. The sum is $3\\cdot1+5\\cdot2+7\\cdot3+1\\cdot4=38$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-8": ["Two steps take $2$ to $6$, so $r^2=3$ and $r=\\pm\\sqrt3$. The first term is $\\tfrac2r=\\pm\\tfrac2{\\sqrt3}=\\pm\\tfrac{2\\sqrt3}3$, and $-\\tfrac{2\\sqrt3}3$ is choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-9": ["Write everything in base $5$: the right side is $5^{(48-26-34)/x}=5^{-12/x}$ and the left side is $5^{-4}$. So $\\tfrac{12}x=4$ and $x=3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-10": ["There were $26\\cdot10^4$ old plates and are $26^3\\cdot10^3$ new ones. The ratio is $\\tfrac{26^3\\cdot10^3}{26\\cdot10^4}=\\tfrac{26^2}{10}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-11": ["From $(10,15)$, a line of slope $m$ reaches $y=0$ after moving $\\tfrac{15}m$ to the left. The $x$-intercepts are $10-5=5$ and $10-3=7$, which are $2$ apart, choice (A).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-12": ["With portions $a$, $b$, $c$: $a+b+c=1000$ and $(a-100)+2b+2c=1500$. Substitute $b+c=1000-a$: $a-100+2000-2a=1500$, so $a=400$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-13": ["A two-digit $x$ has digit sum between $1$ and $18$, and the ones whose own digit sum is $3$ are $3$ and $12$. Digit sum $3$: $12$, $21$, $30$ ($3$ numbers). Digit sum $12$: $39$, $48$, $57$, $66$, $75$, $84$, $93$ ($7$ numbers). That is $10$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-14": ["$a^b=3^8\\cdot5^2$ needs $b$ to divide both exponents, so $b=1$ or $b=2$. With $b=2$, $a=3^4\\cdot5=405$ and $a+b=407$; with $b=1$, $a$ is far larger. The smallest sum is $407$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-15": ["Each match eliminates exactly one player, and $99$ of the $100$ players are eliminated, so there are $99=9\\cdot11$ matches whatever the byes. That is divisible by $11$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-16": ["With $m$ main courses there are $2m$ appetizers, so $2m\\cdot m\\cdot3=6m^2$ dinners, and $6m^2\\ge365$ needs $m^2\\ge61$. Since $6\\cdot7^2=294$ falls short and $6\\cdot8^2=384$ does not, $m=8$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-17": ["The melted ice cream is $\\tfrac34\\cdot\\tfrac43\\pi r^3=\\pi r^3$, and it fills the cone of the same radius: $\\tfrac13\\pi r^2h=\\pi r^3$. So $h=3r$, a ratio of $3:1$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-18": ["For even $n$ the factors are five consecutive odd numbers. Five consecutive odd numbers run through every residue mod $5$, so one is a multiple of $5$, and any three of them run through every residue mod $3$, so one is a multiple of $3$. No larger number always divides: $n=10$ gives $11\\cdot13\\cdot15\\cdot17\\cdot19$, with one $3$ and one $5$, and $n=12$ gives $13\\cdot15\\cdot17\\cdot19\\cdot21$, with no $11$. The answer is $15$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-19": ["Put $A=(-2,0)$ and $B=(2,0)$, so the small semicircles have centers $-1$, $0$, $1$. The two outer ones touch only at the origin, and the middle one overlaps each of them in half a lens of two unit circles $1$ apart, of area $\\tfrac\\pi3-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}4$. The union of the three is $3\\cdot\\tfrac\\pi2-2\\left(\\tfrac\\pi3-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}4\\right)=\\tfrac{5\\pi}6+\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$. The shaded part is $2\\pi$ minus that, $\\tfrac{7\\pi}6-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-20": ["$FG=5-1-2=2$, and $FG\\parallel AB$, so triangles $EFG$ and $EAB$ are similar with ratio $\\tfrac25$. If $E$ is at height $h$ above $AB$, it is $h-3$ above $FG$, and $\\tfrac{h-3}h=\\tfrac25$ gives $h=5$. The area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot5\\cdot5=\\tfrac{25}2$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-21": ["Both green beads must be drawn within three draws. The second green can come on draw $2$ ($\\tfrac24\\cdot\\tfrac14=\\tfrac18$) or on draw $3$, after one green and one red in either order: $\\tfrac24\\cdot\\tfrac34\\cdot\\tfrac14+\\tfrac24\\cdot\\tfrac24\\cdot\\tfrac14=\\tfrac3{32}+\\tfrac1{16}$. The total is $\\tfrac{4+3+2}{32}=\\tfrac9{32}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-22": ["A full day has $2(1+2+\\cdots+12)=156$ hour chimes and $24$ half-hour chimes, $180$ in all. On February $26$ after $11{:}15$ there are $11{:}30$, the hours from noon to $11$ PM ($12+66=78$) and $12$ more half-hours: $91$ chimes. That leaves $2003-91=1912=10\\cdot180+112$. The ten full days are February $27$ to March $8$, so the $2003$rd chime comes on March $9$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-23": ["Cut the octagon from its center $O$ into $8$ congruent triangles of area $\\tfrac18$. $AE$ is a diameter through $O$, so $O$ is the midpoint of $AE$ and $[ABE]=2[ABO]=\\tfrac14$. The diagonal $BE$ halves the rectangle, so $[ABEF]=2\\cdot\\tfrac14=\\tfrac12$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-24": ["The common difference is $(x-y)-(x+y)=-2y$, so $xy=x-3y$ and $\\tfrac xy=x-5y$. The first gives $x=\\tfrac{3y}{1-y}$; substituting into the second gives $y=-\\tfrac35$ and $x=-\\tfrac98$. The fifth term is $\\tfrac xy-2y=\\tfrac{15}8+\\tfrac65=\\tfrac{123}{40}$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-10b-25": ["The numbers are $100k+23$ for $k=10,\\ldots,99$. Since $100\\equiv1$ and $23\\equiv2\\pmod3$, the number is divisible by $3$ when $k\\equiv1\\pmod3$: $k=10,13,\\ldots,97$, which is $\\tfrac{97-10}3+1=30$ values, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-1": ["The numerator is $2(1-2+3-4+5-6+7)=2\\cdot4$ and the denominator is $3(1-2+3-4+5-6+7)=3\\cdot4$, so the fraction is $\\tfrac23$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-2": ["Over $14$ days the pair of pills costs $\\tfrac{546}{14}=39$ dollars a day. A green pill is $1$ more than a pink one, so green is $\\tfrac{39+1}2=20$ dollars, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-3": ["The regions have areas $4\\cdot5=20$, $7\\cdot3=21$, $5\\cdot3=15$, $6\\cdot1=6$ and $2\\cdot2=4$ (they fill the $11\\times6$ bed, $66$ square feet). The cheapest plan puts the cheapest flower in the largest region: $21\\cdot1+20\\cdot1.5+15\\cdot2+6\\cdot2.5+4\\cdot3=108$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-4": ["Each pass cuts a new strip $28-4=24$ inches, $2$ feet, wide. Mowing along the $150$-foot side takes $\\tfrac{90}2=45$ passes, $45\\cdot150=6750$ feet, which is $\\tfrac{6750}{5000}=1.35$ hours, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-5": ["A $4:3$ rectangle has a $3$-$4$-$5$ right triangle under its diagonal, so the sides are $\\tfrac45$ and $\\tfrac35$ of the diagonal. The horizontal length is $\\tfrac45\\cdot27=21.6$, closest to $21.5$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-6": ["Two steps take $2$ to $6$, so $r^2=3$ and $r=\\pm\\sqrt3$. The first term is $\\tfrac2r=\\pm\\tfrac2{\\sqrt3}=\\pm\\tfrac{2\\sqrt3}3$, and $-\\tfrac{2\\sqrt3}3$ is choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-7": ["From $n+d+q=100$ and $5n+10d+25q=835$, subtracting gives $5d+20q=335$, so $d=67-4q$ with $n=33+3q\\ge0$. As $q$ runs from $0$ to $16$, $d$ runs from $67$ down to $3$, a difference of $64$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-8": ["A two-digit $x$ has digit sum between $1$ and $18$, and the ones whose own digit sum is $3$ are $3$ and $12$. Digit sum $3$: $12$, $21$, $30$ ($3$ numbers). Digit sum $12$: $39$, $48$, $57$, $66$, $75$, $84$, $93$ ($7$ numbers). That is $10$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-9": ["The slope is $\\tfrac{12}4=3$, so $f(12)-f(2)=3\\cdot10=30$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-10": ["Any two sides of a regular pentagon are either adjacent or separated by one side, and a rotation or reflection takes any pair of either kind to any other. So there are $2$ figures, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-11": ["The watch shows $57.6$ minutes for every $60$, that is $0.96$ of the real time. It shows $10$ hours gone after $\\tfrac{10}{0.96}=10\\tfrac5{12}$ real hours, which is $10$ hours $25$ minutes, so $10{:}25$ PM, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-12": ["For even $n$ the factors are five consecutive odd numbers. Five consecutive odd numbers run through every residue mod $5$, so one is a multiple of $5$, and any three of them run through every residue mod $3$, so one is a multiple of $3$. No larger number always divides: $n=10$ gives $11\\cdot13\\cdot15\\cdot17\\cdot19$, with one $3$ and one $5$, and $n=12$ gives $13\\cdot15\\cdot17\\cdot19\\cdot21$, with no $11$. The answer is $15$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-13": ["The melted ice cream is $\\tfrac34\\cdot\\tfrac43\\pi r^3=\\pi r^3$, and it fills the cone of the same radius: $\\tfrac13\\pi r^2h=\\pi r^3$. So $h=3r$, a ratio of $3:1$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-14": ["$FG=5-1-2=2$, and $FG\\parallel AB$, so triangles $EFG$ and $EAB$ are similar with ratio $\\tfrac25$. If $E$ is at height $h$ above $AB$, it is $h-3$ above $FG$, and $\\tfrac{h-3}h=\\tfrac25$ gives $h=5$. The area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot5\\cdot5=\\tfrac{25}2$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-15": ["Cut the octagon from its center $O$ into $8$ congruent triangles of area $\\tfrac18$. $AE$ is a diameter through $O$, so $O$ is the midpoint of $AE$ and $[ABE]=2[ABO]=\\tfrac14$. The diagonal $BE$ halves the rectangle, so $[ABEF]=2\\cdot\\tfrac14=\\tfrac12$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-16": ["Put $A=(-2,0)$ and $B=(2,0)$, so the small semicircles have centers $-1$, $0$, $1$. The two outer ones touch only at the origin, and the middle one overlaps each of them in half a lens of two unit circles $1$ apart, of area $\\tfrac\\pi3-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}4$. The union of the three is $3\\cdot\\tfrac\\pi2-2\\left(\\tfrac\\pi3-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}4\\right)=\\tfrac{5\\pi}6+\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$. The shaded part is $2\\pi$ minus that, $\\tfrac{7\\pi}6-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-17": ["Let $u=\\log x$ and $v=\\log y$: $u+3v=1$ and $2u+v=1$, so $u=\\tfrac25$, $v=\\tfrac15$, and $\\log(xy)=\\tfrac35$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-18": ["Only $7$ and $11$ can divide $x$; write $x=7^n11^m$. The exponents of $7x^5$ must be multiples of $13$: $5n+1\\equiv0$ and $5m\\equiv1\\pmod{13}$, so the least are $n=5$ and $m=8$. Then $x=7^511^8$ and $a+b+c+d=7+11+5+8=31$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-19": ["There are $5!-4!=96$ permutations in $S$. Those with second term $2$ have first term $3$, $4$ or $5$, and the rest in $3!$ orders: $18$. The probability is $\\tfrac{18}{96}=\\tfrac3{16}$, so $a+b=19$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-20": ["The graph passes through $(1,0)$ and $(-1,0)$, so $f(1)+f(-1)=2b+2d=0$, and through $(0,2)$, so $d=2$. Hence $b=-2$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-21": ["With the angle $\\angle ABC=\\pi-\\alpha$, the Law of Cosines gives $AC^2=89+80\\cos\\alpha$. This is below $49$ when $\\cos\\alpha\\lt-\\tfrac12$, that is $\\alpha\\gt\\tfrac{2\\pi}3$, a third of the interval, choice (D).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-22": ["The diagonals are perpendicular at the center $O$, so $NPOQ$ is a rectangle and $PQ=ON$. The least $ON$ is the distance from $O$ to $\\overline{AB}$; in the right triangle with legs $8$ and $15$ and hypotenuse $17$ that is $\\tfrac{8\\cdot15}{17}=\\tfrac{120}{17}\\approx7.06$, closest to choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-23": ["The zeros are $x=\\tfrac1{k\\pi}$. They lie in the interval when $\\tfrac{1000}\\pi\\lt k\\lt\\tfrac{10000}\\pi$, that is $319\\le k\\le3183$, which is $2865$ values, closest to choice (A).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-24": ["The graph of $|x-a|+|x-b|+|x-c|$ has slopes $-3,-1,1,3$ on the four pieces. The line has slope $-2$, so left of $a$ the graph falls faster and to the right of $a$ it falls slower; a single meeting must be at the corner $x=a$, where the graph's value is $b+c-2a$. That point is on the line when $b+c=2003$, and with $b\\lt c$ the least $c$ is $1002$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2003-amc-12b-25": ["A chord is shorter than the radius when its arc is under $60^\\circ$. Fix the first point and measure the others by angles $\\theta_1,\\theta_2$ in $(-\\pi,\\pi)$. We need $|\\theta_1|,|\\theta_2|\\lt\\tfrac\\pi3$ and $|\\theta_1-\\theta_2|\\lt\\tfrac\\pi3$: the square of side $\\tfrac{2\\pi}3$ less two corner triangles of legs $\\tfrac\\pi3$, area $\\tfrac{4\\pi^2}9-\\tfrac{\\pi^2}9=\\tfrac{\\pi^2}3$. Out of $4\\pi^2$ this is $\\tfrac1{12}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-1": ["Six people share $\\$1500$, so each raises $250$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-2": ["The inner values are $\\tfrac1{-1}=-1$, $\\tfrac22=1$ and $\\tfrac3{-1}=-3$. Then $\\diamond(-1,1,-3)=\\tfrac{-1}{4}$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-3": ["$1.45\\%$ of $2000$ cents is $29$ cents, choice (E).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-4": ["$x$ is equally far from $1$ and $2$, so it is the midpoint $\\tfrac32$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-5": ["The $3\\times3$ grid has $\\binom93=84$ triples. Collinear ones lie on the $3$ rows, $3$ columns or $2$ diagonals, $8$ in all. The probability is $\\tfrac8{84}=\\tfrac2{21}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-6": ["There are $24$ granddaughters, so $4$ daughters have $6$ each. Everyone else in the $30$ has no daughters: $30-4=26$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-7": ["Each level shrinks by one in both directions: $5\\cdot8+4\\cdot7+3\\cdot6+2\\cdot5+1\\cdot4=100$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-8": ["In each round the leader gives one token to each of the other two players and one to the discard pile, so the leader loses $3$ while the others gain $1$: $(15,14,13)\\to(12,15,14)\\to(13,12,15)\\to(14,13,12)$. Every three rounds each player has lost exactly $1$ and the order is restored. After $36$ rounds the counts are $3$, $2$, $1$, and round $37$ leaves $(0,3,2)$, so the game ends after $37$ rounds, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-9": ["Adding triangle $ABD$ to each, $[ADE]-[BDC]=[ABE]-[ABC]=\\tfrac12\\cdot4\\cdot8-\\tfrac12\\cdot4\\cdot6=4$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-10": ["Add over each common number of heads $k$: $\\sum\\binom3k\\binom4k=1+12+18+4=35$, which is $\\binom73$ by Vandermonde's identity, of the $2^7=128$ outcomes. The probability is $\\tfrac{35}{128}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-11": ["The volume $\\pi r^2h$ stays fixed while $r$ grows by $\\tfrac54$, so $h$ is multiplied by $\\tfrac{16}{25}$. That is a $36\\%$ decrease, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-12": ["Each of the $8$ condiments is on or off, $2^8=256$ ways, times $3$ patty choices: $768$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-13": ["Count the dancing pairs two ways: $12\\cdot3=36$ from the men, and $2$ per woman. So there are $18$ women, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-14": ["With $n$ coins, $\\tfrac{20n+25}{n+1}=21$ gives $n=4$, worth $80$ cents. Four coins making $80$ cents must be three quarters and a nickel, so there are $0$ dimes, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-15": ["$\\tfrac{x+y}x=1+\\tfrac yx$, and $\\tfrac yx$ is negative, closest to $0$ when $y=2$ and $x=-4$. The maximum is $1-\\tfrac12=\\tfrac12$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-16": ["Count by size how many positions cover the center: $1$ of size $1$, $4$ of size $2$, $9$ of size $3$, $4$ of size $4$ and $1$ of size $5$. That is $19$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-17": ["Up to the first meeting they cover half a lap together, and Brenda runs $100$. Between meetings they cover a full lap together, so Brenda runs $200$ while Sally runs $150$. The lap is $350$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-18": ["Take the progression $9$, $9+d$, $9+2d$. Then $9$, $11+d$, $29+2d$ is geometric: $(11+d)^2=9(29+2d)$, so $d^2+4d-140=0$ and $d=10$ or $-14$. The third terms are $49$ and $1$, so the smallest is $1$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-19": ["Unroll the silo into a rectangle. The stripe becomes a slanted band, which is a parallelogram with horizontal width $3$ and height $80$. Its area is $3\\cdot80=240$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-20": ["Take side $1$ and $DE=DF=t$ by symmetry. Then $EF^2=2t^2$ equals $BE^2=1+(1-t)^2$, so $t^2+2t-2=0$ and $t=\\sqrt3-1$. The ratio is $\\tfrac{t^2/2}{(1-t)/2}=\\tfrac{4-2\\sqrt3}{2-\\sqrt3}=2$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-21": ["With angle $\\theta$ between the lines, the shading covers the inner disk and outer ring in the two $\\theta$ sectors and the middle ring in the two others. That area is $\\theta\\cdot1+(\\pi-\\theta)\\cdot3+\\theta\\cdot5=3\\pi+3\\theta$, leaving $6\\pi-3\\theta$ unshaded. Then $13(3\\pi+3\\theta)=8(6\\pi-3\\theta)$ gives $\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi7$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-22": ["Sides $CB$ and $EA$ are tangent to the semicircle, so the equal-tangent rule gives $CE=CB+EA=2+e$, with $e=EA$. In right triangle $CDE$, $(2+e)^2=4+(2-e)^2$, so $e=\\tfrac12$ and $CE=\\tfrac52$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-23": ["Circle $D$ has radius $2$. Put its center at the origin and $A$'s at $(1,0)$, with $B$ at $(x,r)$. Tangencies give $x^2+r^2=(2-r)^2$ and $(x-1)^2+r^2=(1+r)^2$. Subtracting, $x=-\\tfrac23$, and then $r=\\tfrac89$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-24": ["With $n=2^{k-1}$, $a_{2^k}=2^{k-1}a_{2^{k-1}}$. So $a_{2^{100}}=2^{0+1+\\cdots+99}=2^{4950}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10a-25": ["The small centers are at height $1$ and form an equilateral triangle of side $2$, with circumradius $\\tfrac2{\\sqrt3}$. The big center is $3$ from each, so it is $\\sqrt{9-\\tfrac43}=\\tfrac{\\sqrt{69}}3$ above their plane. The top is at $1+\\tfrac{\\sqrt{69}}3+2=3+\\tfrac{\\sqrt{69}}3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-1": ["$1.45\\%$ of $2000$ cents is $29$ cents, choice (E).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-2": ["The blanks give $8\\cdot2.5=20$ points, so she needs $6c\\ge80$, that is $c\\ge14$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-3": ["Any $y$ from $1$ to $49$ leaves a positive $x=100-2y$, so there are $49$ pairs, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-4": ["There are $24$ granddaughters, so $4$ daughters have $6$ each. Everyone else in the $30$ has no daughters: $30-4=26$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-5": ["The line crosses the $y$-axis between $0$ and $1$ and falls by less than $1$ per unit (it meets the $x$-axis at $2$), so $0\\lt b\\lt1$ and $-1\\lt m\\lt0$. Then $-1\\lt mb\\lt0$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-6": ["$U-V=2004^{2005}$, while $V-W=2004^{2004}$, and $W-X$, $X-Y$, $Y-Z$ are all below $2004^{2004}$. So $U-V$ is largest, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-7": ["In each round the leader gives one token to each of the other two players and one to the discard pile, so the leader loses $3$ while the others gain $1$: $(15,14,13)\\to(12,15,14)\\to(13,12,15)\\to(14,13,12)$. Every three rounds each player has lost exactly $1$ and the order is restored. After $36$ rounds the counts are $3$, $2$, $1$, and round $37$ leaves $(0,3,2)$, so the game ends after $37$ rounds, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-8": ["Adding triangle $ABD$ to each, $[ADE]-[BDC]=[ABE]-[ABC]=\\tfrac12\\cdot4\\cdot8-\\tfrac12\\cdot4\\cdot6=4$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-9": ["The volume $\\pi r^2h$ stays fixed while $r$ grows by $\\tfrac54$, so $h$ is multiplied by $\\tfrac{16}{25}$. That is a $36\\%$ decrease, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-10": ["The median of consecutive integers is their mean, $\\tfrac{7^5}{49}=7^3$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-11": ["With $n$ coins, $\\tfrac{20n+25}{n+1}=21$ gives $n=4$, worth $80$ cents. Four coins making $80$ cents must be three quarters and a nickel, so there are $0$ dimes, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-12": ["Line $AC$ is $y=9-\\tfrac x2$, meeting $y=x$ at $A'=(6,6)$; line $BC$ is $y=12-2x$, meeting it at $B'=(4,4)$. So $A'B'=2\\sqrt2$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-13": ["The $\\binom92=36$ pairs each give a line, but the $8$ lines through three points (three rows, three columns, two diagonals) are each counted $3$ times. So there are $36-8\\cdot2=20$ lines, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-14": ["Take the progression $9$, $9+d$, $9+2d$. Then $9$, $11+d$, $29+2d$ is geometric: $(11+d)^2=9(29+2d)$, so $d^2+4d-140=0$ and $d=10$ or $-14$. The third terms are $49$ and $1$, so the smallest is $1$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-15": ["Up to the first meeting they cover half a lap together, and Brenda runs $100$. Between meetings they cover a full lap together, so Brenda runs $200$ while Sally runs $150$. The lap is $350$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-16": ["Work from the outside in: $\\log_{2004}$ needs $\\log_{2003}(\\cdots)\\gt0$, so $\\log_{2002}(\\log_{2001}x)\\gt1$, so $\\log_{2001}x\\gt2002$, so $x\\gt2001^{2002}$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-17": ["With $n=2^{k-1}$, $f(2^k)=2^{k-1}f(2^{k-1})$. So $f(2^{100})=2^{0+1+\\cdots+99}=2^{4950}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-18": ["Sides $CB$ and $EA$ are tangent to the semicircle, so the equal-tangent rule gives $CE=CB+EA=2+e$, with $e=EA$. In right triangle $CDE$, $(2+e)^2=4+(2-e)^2$, so $e=\\tfrac12$ and $CE=\\tfrac52$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-19": ["Circle $D$ has radius $2$. Put its center at the origin and $A$'s at $(1,0)$, with $B$ at $(x,r)$. Tangencies give $x^2+r^2=(2-r)^2$ and $(x-1)^2+r^2=(1+r)^2$. Subtracting, $x=-\\tfrac23$, and then $r=\\tfrac89$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-20": ["Split the unit square at $\\tfrac12$ in each direction. If $a,b\\lt\\tfrac12$, then $A+B=0$ and we need $c\\lt\\tfrac12$, a triangle of area $\\tfrac18$; if $a,b\\ge\\tfrac12$, we need $c\\ge\\tfrac32$, another $\\tfrac18$. In the two mixed quarters $A+B=1$ and $c$ always lies in $[\\tfrac12,\\tfrac32)$, area $\\tfrac12$. The probability is $\\tfrac18+\\tfrac18+\\tfrac12=\\tfrac34$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-21": ["The geometric series sums to $\\tfrac1{1-\\cos^2\\theta}=\\tfrac1{\\sin^2\\theta}=5$, so $\\sin^2\\theta=\\tfrac15$ and $\\cos2\\theta=1-2\\sin^2\\theta=\\tfrac35$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-22": ["The small centers are at height $1$ and form an equilateral triangle of side $2$, with circumradius $\\tfrac2{\\sqrt3}$. The big center is $3$ from each, so it is $\\sqrt{9-\\tfrac43}=\\tfrac{\\sqrt{69}}3$ above their plane. The top is at $1+\\tfrac{\\sqrt{69}}3+2=3+\\tfrac{\\sqrt{69}}3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-23": ["Since $z_1=0$ is a root, $c_0=0$. Real coefficients pair each non-real root with its conjugate, so $\\sum b_k=0$, hence $\\sum a_k=0$ and, by Vieta, $c_{2003}=-c_{2004}\\sum z_k=0$. The other $2003$ roots include an odd number of real ones (non-real roots come in pairs), so some $b_k=0$ and the product of the $b_k$ is $0$. That leaves $\\sum_{k=1}^{2004}c_k=P(1)-c_0=P(1)$, which can be nonzero: $P(x)=x(x^{2003}+1)$ meets every condition (its roots $0$ and the $2003$rd roots of $-1$ are distinct and sum to $0$) and has $P(1)=2$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-24": ["A disk of radius $1$ covers $\\overline{AB}$ exactly when its center lies in the lens $L$ where the unit disks about $A$ and $B$ overlap. So $S$ is the set of points within $1$ of $L$. The lens is convex, with two $120^\\circ$ arcs of radius $1$: its perimeter is $\\tfrac{4\\pi}3$ and its area $2(\\tfrac\\pi3-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}4)=\\tfrac{2\\pi}3-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$. By Steiner's formula the area of $S$ is $A+P\\cdot1+\\pi=\\tfrac{2\\pi}3-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2+\\tfrac{4\\pi}3+\\pi=3\\pi-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$, choice (C). (Piece by piece: a $120^\\circ$ sector of the ring between radii $1$ and $2$ over each arc, area $\\pi$ each, and a $60^\\circ$ unit sector at each corner.)","",[]],
"2004-amc-12a-25": ["In base $n$, $0.\\overline{133}_n=\\tfrac{n^2+3n+3}{n^3-1}$. With $g(m)=m^2+m+1$, the numerator is $g(n+1)$ and the denominator is $(n-1)g(n)$, so the product telescopes: $\\tfrac{g(100)}{g(4)}\\cdot\\tfrac1{3\\cdot4\\cdots98}=\\tfrac{10101}{21}\\cdot\\tfrac2{98!}=\\tfrac{962}{98!}$. Since $962=2\\cdot13\\cdot37$ is not a multiple of $98$, $n=98$ is smallest and $m=962$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-1": ["Rows $12$ through $22$ are $22-12+1=11$ rows, so $11\\cdot33=363$ seats, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-2": ["Count the two-digit numbers with no $7$: the tens digit has $8$ choices (not $0$ or $7$) and the units digit $9$, so $72$ of the $90$ have no $7$. That leaves $90-72=18$, choice (B).","Alternatively, by inclusion-exclusion: $10$ numbers have tens digit $7$ and $9$ have units digit $7$, with $77$ in both, so $10+9-1=18$.",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-3": ["Each practice doubles the one before, so going back from the fifth practice halves four times: $\\tfrac{48}{2^4}=3$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-4": ["The six faces multiply to $720=2^4\\cdot3^2\\cdot5$, so $P=\\tfrac{720}k$ with $k$ the hidden face. Take each prime's smallest exponent over the six cases: hiding $4$ leaves $2^2$, hiding $3$ or $6$ leaves $3^1$, and hiding $5$ leaves no $5$. So $2^2\\cdot3=12$ always divides $P$, and no more does, since $\\gcd(144,120)=24$ and $\\gcd(24,180)=12$. The answer is $12$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-5": ["Subtracting anything but $0$ only lowers the result, so take $d=0$ and make $c\\cdot a^b$ as large as possible from $1$, $2$, $3$: $1\\cdot3^2=9$ beats $1\\cdot2^3=8$ and $2\\cdot3^1=6$. The maximum is $9$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-6": ["Pull out the square of the smaller factorial: $99!\\cdot100!=(99!)^2\\cdot100=(10\\cdot99!)^2$, a perfect square, choice (C). The others leave $99$ ($98!\\cdot99!$), $100\\cdot99$, $100\\cdot101$ and $101$ beside a square, none of them a square.","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-7": ["She receives $\\tfrac{10}7d$ Canadian dollars, so $\\tfrac{10}7d-60=d$, giving $\\tfrac37d=60$ and $d=140$. Its digits add to $1+4+0=5$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-8": ["Southwest and southeast are at right angles, so the two downtowns and the airport form a right triangle with legs $8$ and $10$. The distance is $\\sqrt{64+100}=\\sqrt{164}$, between $12.8$ and $12.9$, closest to $13$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-9": ["The square's sides equal the radius, so a quarter of the circle lies inside the square. The union is the square plus the other three quarters of the circle: $100+\\tfrac34\\cdot100\\pi=100+75\\pi$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-10": ["The rows hold $1,3,5,\\ldots$ cans, and the first $n$ odd numbers add to $n^2$. So $n^2=100$ gives $10$ rows, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-11": ["$ab\\gt a+b$ is $(a-1)(b-1)\\gt1$. It fails when $a=1$ or $b=1$ ($8+8-1=15$ pairs) and when $a=b=2$ (one pair), so $16$ of the $64$ outcomes fail. The probability is $\\tfrac{48}{64}=\\tfrac34$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-12": ["The radius $OZ$ meets the tangent $XZ$ at a right angle, so in right triangle $OZX$, $b^2=c^2+a^2$. The annulus has area $\\pi b^2-\\pi c^2=\\pi a^2$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-13": ["Each coin is between $1.35$ and $1.95$ mm, so the number of coins $n$ satisfies $\\tfrac{14}{1.95}\\le n\\le\\tfrac{14}{1.35}$, that is $8\\le n\\le10$. Every thickness is $1.35$ plus a multiple of $0.2$, so $14-1.35n$ must be a multiple of $0.2$; in hundredths, $1400-135n$ divisible by $20$ needs $15n$ divisible by $20$, so $n$ is a multiple of $4$. Only $n=8$ works (eight quarters, $8\\cdot1.75=14$), choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-14": ["Say there are $b$ blue marbles. After the red ones are added the bag holds $3b$ marbles, and after the yellow ones $5b$. Doubling the blue adds $b$ more: $2b$ blue of $6b$, which is $\\tfrac13$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-15": ["With $n$ nickels and $d$ dimes, $n+d=20$, and swapping them gains $5(n-d)=70$ cents, so $n-d=14$. Then $n=17$, $d=3$, and the coins are worth $85+30=115$ cents, $\\$1.15$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-16": ["Join the three small centers: they form an equilateral triangle of side $2$, whose center is the big circle's center, at distance $\\tfrac2{\\sqrt3}$ from each vertex (the circumradius $\\tfrac{s}{\\sqrt3}$). Add the small radius out to the point of tangency: $R=1+\\tfrac{2}{\\sqrt3}=\\tfrac{3+2\\sqrt3}3$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-17": ["Write Jack's age as $10a+b$ and Bill's as $10b+a$. Then $10a+b+5=2(10b+a+5)$, so $8a=19b+5$. The digit $b=1$ gives $a=3$, and no other $b\\le9$ makes $19b+5$ a multiple of $8$ with $a\\le9$. Jack is $31$, Bill is $13$ (in five years $36=2\\cdot18$), and the difference is $18$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-18": ["The triangle is right-angled at $C$ ($12$-$16$-$20$), and $BDF$ is what is left after three corner triangles are cut off. Each corner triangle shares an angle with $ACE$, so its share of the area is the product of the two side fractions: at $A$, $\\tfrac3{12}\\cdot\\tfrac{15}{20}=\\tfrac3{16}$; at $C$, $\\tfrac9{12}\\cdot\\tfrac4{16}=\\tfrac3{16}$; at $E$, $\\tfrac{12}{16}\\cdot\\tfrac5{20}=\\tfrac3{16}$. So $BDF$ is $1-\\tfrac9{16}=\\tfrac7{16}$ of $ACE$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-19": ["The rule $a_n=a_{n-3}+a_{n-2}-a_{n-1}$ says $a_n-a_{n-2}=-(a_{n-1}-a_{n-3})$, so the steps two apart alternate between $+2$ and $-2$: $a_3-a_1=2$, $a_4-a_2=-2$, $a_5-a_3=2$, and so on. The terms run $2001,2002,2003,2000,2005,1998,\\ldots$: odd-numbered terms rise by $2$ and even-numbered terms fall by $2$, so $a_{2k}=2004-2k$. Then $a_{2004}=2004-2004=0$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-20": ["Give $A$ mass $1$ and let $B$, $C$ have masses $b$, $c$. On $AD$, $D$ carries $b+c$, and $\\tfrac{AT}{DT}=\\tfrac{b+c}1=3$. On $BE$, $E$ carries $1+c$, and $\\tfrac{BT}{ET}=\\tfrac{1+c}b=4$. So $c=3-b$ and $4-b=4b$, giving $b=\\tfrac45$ and $c=\\tfrac{11}5$. Then $\\tfrac{CD}{BD}=\\tfrac bc=\\tfrac4{11}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-21": ["The first progression is $1,4,\\ldots,6010$ (the numbers $\\equiv1\\pmod3$) and the second is $9,16,\\ldots,14030$ (the numbers $\\equiv2\\pmod7$, from $9$ on). A common term is $\\equiv16\\pmod{21}$, and must be at most $6010$: $16,37,\\ldots,5995$, which is $\\tfrac{5995-16}{21}+1=286$ terms. So $S$ has $2004+2004-286=3722$ numbers, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-22": ["Put the right angle at the origin with legs along the axes, $B=(12,0)$ and $A=(0,5)$. The inradius is $\\tfrac{5+12-13}2=2$, so the incenter is $(2,2)$, and the circumcenter is the hypotenuse's midpoint $(6,\\tfrac52)$. Their distance is $\\sqrt{4^2+(\\tfrac12)^2}=\\tfrac{\\sqrt{65}}2$, choice (D).","Alternatively, Euler's formula $OI^2=R(R-2r)$ with $R=\\tfrac{13}2$ and $r=2$ gives $OI^2=\\tfrac{13}2\\cdot\\tfrac52=\\tfrac{65}4$, the same $\\tfrac{\\sqrt{65}}2$.",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-23": ["Count the $64$ colorings that work, by the number of red faces. Six red: $1$. Five red: $6$, since the cube can rest on the blue face's axis and show four red sides. Four red: only when the two blue faces are opposite, $3$ ways; two adjacent blue faces leave no four-face belt of one color. By symmetry the same $10$ come with blue in the majority, so $20$ colorings work, and the probability is $\\tfrac{20}{64}=\\tfrac5{16}$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-24": ["$\\angle BAD=\\angle DAC$ are inscribed angles on chords $BD$ and $DC$, so $BD=CD$. Ptolemy's Theorem in $ABDC$ gives $AD\\cdot BC=AB\\cdot CD+AC\\cdot BD=CD(AB+AC)$. So $\\tfrac{AD}{CD}=\\tfrac{7+8}9=\\tfrac53$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-10b-25": ["Put the small circle's center at the origin with $A=(-1,0)$, $B=(1,0)$. The big circle tangent at $A$ has center $(1,0)$ and the one tangent at $B$ has center $(-1,0)$, both of radius $2$, and the small circle lies inside both. The big circles' centers are $2$ apart, so they meet at $(0,\\pm\\sqrt3)$ and each cuts off a segment with central angle $\\tfrac{2\\pi}3$: area $\\tfrac12\\cdot4\\left(\\tfrac{2\\pi}3-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2\\right)=\\tfrac{4\\pi}3-\\sqrt3$. The lens is twice that, $\\tfrac{8\\pi}3-2\\sqrt3$, and removing the small circle leaves $\\tfrac{5\\pi}3-2\\sqrt3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-1": ["Each practice doubles the one before, so going back from the fifth practice halves four times: $\\tfrac{48}{2^4}=3$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-2": ["Subtracting anything but $0$ only lowers the result, so take $d=0$ and make $c\\cdot a^b$ as large as possible from $1$, $2$, $3$: $1\\cdot3^2=9$ beats $1\\cdot2^3=8$ and $2\\cdot3^1=6$. The maximum is $9$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-3": ["$1296=6^4=2^43^4$, so $x+y=8$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-4": ["Ten numbers have tens digit $7$ and nine have units digit $7$, with $77$ in both, so $18$ of the $90$ numbers qualify. The probability is $\\tfrac15$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-5": ["She receives $\\tfrac{10}7d$ Canadian dollars, so $\\tfrac{10}7d-60=d$, giving $\\tfrac37d=60$ and $d=140$. Its digits add to $1+4+0=5$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-6": ["Southwest and southeast are at right angles, so the two downtowns and the airport form a right triangle with legs $8$ and $10$. The distance is $\\sqrt{64+100}=\\sqrt{164}$, between $12.8$ and $12.9$, closest to $13$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-7": ["The square's sides equal the radius, so a quarter of the circle lies inside the square. The union is the square plus the other three quarters of the circle: $100+\\tfrac34\\cdot100\\pi=100+75\\pi$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-8": ["The rows hold $1,3,5,\\ldots$ cans, and the first $n$ odd numbers add to $n^2$. So $n^2=100$ gives $10$ rows, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-9": ["A clockwise quarter turn sends $(x,y)$ to $(y,-x)$, so $B=(2,3)$. Reflecting in $y=x$ swaps the coordinates, giving $C=(3,2)$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-10": ["The radius $OZ$ meets the tangent $XZ$ at a right angle, so in right triangle $OZX$, $b^2=c^2+a^2$. The annulus has area $\\pi b^2-\\pi c^2=\\pi a^2$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-11": ["With $n$ students the total is $76n$, and it is at least $500+60(n-5)=60n+200$. So $16n\\ge200$ and $n\\ge13$. Thirteen works: five $100$s and eight scores of $61$ total $988=76\\cdot13$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-12": ["The rule $a_n=a_{n-3}+a_{n-2}-a_{n-1}$ says $a_n-a_{n-2}=-(a_{n-1}-a_{n-3})$, so the steps two apart alternate between $+2$ and $-2$: $a_3-a_1=2$, $a_4-a_2=-2$, $a_5-a_3=2$, and so on. The terms run $2001,2002,2003,2000,2005,1998,\\ldots$: odd-numbered terms rise by $2$ and even-numbered terms fall by $2$, so $a_{2k}=2004-2k$. Then $a_{2004}=2004-2004=0$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-13": ["$f(f^{-1}(x))=a(bx+a)+b=abx+a^2+b$ must equal $x$, so $ab=1$ and $b=-a^2$. Then $-a^3=1$, so $a=-1$, $b=-1$, and $a+b=-2$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-14": ["The right triangle $ABC$ has area $30$. The pentagon is $ABC$ less triangles $AMJ$ and $BNK$, which are similar to $ABC$ (right angles at $J$ and $K$) with hypotenuses $AM=1$ and $BN=8$. Their areas are $30\\cdot\\tfrac1{169}$ and $30\\cdot\\tfrac{64}{169}$, so the pentagon has area $30\\left(1-\\tfrac{65}{169}\\right)=\\tfrac{240}{13}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-15": ["Write Jack's age as $10a+b$ and Bill's as $10b+a$. Then $10a+b+5=2(10b+a+5)$, so $8a=19b+5$. The digit $b=1$ gives $a=3$, and no other $b\\le9$ makes $19b+5$ a multiple of $8$ with $a\\le9$. Jack is $31$, Bill is $13$ (in five years $36=2\\cdot18$), and the difference is $18$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-16": ["With $z=x+iy$, $i\\overline z=y+ix$, which equals $z$ exactly when $x=y$. The line $y=x$ meets the circle $|z|=5$ in $2$ points, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-17": ["The log sum says the product of the roots is $2^5=32$. By Vieta it is $-\\tfrac a8$, so $a=-256$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-18": ["Take $A=(t,4t^2+7t-1)$ and $B=(-t,4t^2-7t-1)$. The $y$-coordinates sum to $0$: $8t^2-2=0$, so $t=\\tfrac12$, $A=(\\tfrac12,\\tfrac72)$, $B=-A$. Then $AB=2|OA|=2\\sqrt{\\tfrac14+\\tfrac{49}4}=5\\sqrt2$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-19": ["The cross-section through the axis is an isosceles trapezoid with bases $36$ and $4$ and an inscribed circle. By Pitot's Theorem each leg is $\\tfrac{36+4}2=20$, so the height is $\\sqrt{20^2-16^2}=12$ and the radius is $6$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-20": ["Count the $64$ colorings that work, by the number of red faces. Six red: $1$. Five red: $6$, since the cube can rest on the blue face's axis and show four red sides. Four red: only when the two blue faces are opposite, $3$ ways; two adjacent blue faces leave no four-face belt of one color. By symmetry the same $10$ come with blue in the majority, so $20$ colorings work, and the probability is $\\tfrac{20}{64}=\\tfrac5{16}$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-21": ["The extreme values of $\\tfrac yx$ are the slopes $k$ of the lines $y=kx$ tangent to the ellipse. Substituting gives $(3k^2+k+2)x^2-(20k+11)x+40=0$, which has a double root when $(20k+11)^2=160(3k^2+k+2)$, that is $80k^2-280k+199=0$. By Vieta, $a+b=\\tfrac{280}{80}=\\tfrac72$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-22": ["Multiplying the middle row, middle column and both diagonals gives every entry once and the center three more times, so $P^4=P^3e^3$ and $P=e^3$. The main diagonal gives $100e=e^3$, so $e=10$ and $P=1000$. Then $cg=100$, $bc=20$ and $dg=20$, so $g\\mid20$ and $b=\\tfrac g5$ forces $5\\mid g$: $g\\in\\{5,10,20\\}$, each completing to a square of integers. The sum is $35$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-23": ["If the integer root is $r=a+b$, the roots sum to $2r=2004$, so $r=1002$ and the other two are roots of $x^2-1002x+q$ with $q=ab$. Then $m=1002^2+q$, so $q$ is an integer, and $0\\lt q\\lt501^2$ for distinct positive roots. The roots $501\\pm\\sqrt{501^2-q}$ are integers exactly when $501^2-q$ is a perfect square, which happens for $500$ of these $q$. So $251000-500=250500$ values of $n=-1002q$ remain, choice (C).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-24": ["Let $\\alpha=\\angle DBE$ and $\\beta=\\angle DBC=\\angle DBA$, with $a=\\tan\\alpha$ and $b=\\tan\\beta$. Then $\\angle CBE=\\alpha-\\beta$ and $\\angle ABE=\\alpha+\\beta$, and the geometric progression gives $\\tan(\\alpha-\\beta)\\tan(\\alpha+\\beta)=\\tfrac{a^2-b^2}{1-a^2b^2}=a^2$, which simplifies to $b^2(a^4-1)=0$. So $a=1$ and $\\alpha=45^\\circ$. The arithmetic progression gives $2\\cot(45^\\circ-\\beta)=1+\\tfrac1b$, that is $\\tfrac{2(1+b)}{1-b}=\\tfrac{b+1}b$, so $b=\\tfrac13$. Now $BD=10\\cos45^\\circ=5\\sqrt2$ and $DC=\\tfrac{BD}3$, so the area is $BD\\cdot DC=\\tfrac{50}3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2004-amc-12b-25": ["Within each number of digits the powers of $2$ start with $1$ and double, so there are three or four of them, with leading digits $1,2,4,8$ (or $9$) when there are four and $1$, then $2$ or $3$, then $5$ to $7$ when there are three. So a leading $4$ occurs exactly in the lengths holding four powers. The $2004$ powers $2^0,\\ldots,2^{2003}$ fill the lengths $1$ to $603$, and $2004-3\\cdot603=195$ of those lengths hold four, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-1": ["Mike's $\\$2$ is $10\\%$ of a $\\$20$ bill and Joe's is $20\\%$ of a $\\$10$ bill. The difference is $10$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-2": ["$1\\star2=\\tfrac3{-1}=-3$, and then $-3\\star3=\\tfrac0{-6}=0$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-3": ["The first equation gives $x=-2$. Then $-2b-10=-2$, so $b=-4$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-4": ["With width $w$ and length $2w$, $x^2=5w^2$. The area is $2w^2=\\tfrac25x^2$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-5": ["Alone, Dave pays for $6$ of his $7$ and Doug for $7$ of his $8$, $13$ in all. Together the $15$ windows bring $3$ free ones, so they pay for $12$. They save one window, $\\$100$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-6": ["The totals are $600$ and $600$, so the average is $\\tfrac{1200}{50}=24$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-7": ["If Mike rode at $r$ for time $t$, Josh rode at $\\tfrac45r$ for $2t$. Then $\\tfrac85rt+rt=13$, so Mike's distance $rt$ is $5$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-8": ["The four corner triangles are congruent right triangles with hypotenuse $\\sqrt{50}$. In triangle $ABH$ the short leg is $AH=BE=1$ and the long leg is $BH=1+s$, with $s$ the inner side. So $1+(1+s)^2=50$, $s=6$, and the area is $36$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-9": ["There are $\\binom52=10$ equally likely places for the two O's, and only one gives XOXOX. The probability is $\\tfrac1{10}$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-10": ["One solution means the discriminant of $4x^2+(a+8)x+9$ is zero: $(a+8)^2=144$. So $a=4$ or $a=-20$, with sum $-16$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-11": ["The red faces are the $6n^2$ unit faces on the surface, and all the unit cubes have $6n^3$ faces. Then $6n^2=\\tfrac14\\cdot6n^3$ gives $n=4$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-12": ["The base of length $2$ is two sides of the unit equilateral triangles. The trefoil splits into four of those triangles, each with a circular segment on one side, and each triangle with its segment is a $60^\\circ$ sector of radius $1$, area $\\tfrac\\pi6$. Four sectors give $\\tfrac{2\\pi}3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-13": ["Take $50$th roots: $130n\\gt n^2\\gt16$, so $4\\lt n\\lt130$. That is $n=5,\\ldots,129$, $125$ integers, choice (E).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-14": ["The first and last digits need the same parity, and then they fix the middle digit. With a first digit from $1$ to $9$, the last digit has $5$ choices of matching parity, giving $9\\cdot5=45$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-15": ["$3!\\cdot5!\\cdot7!=2^8\\cdot3^4\\cdot5^2\\cdot7$. A cube divisor uses $2^0,2^3,2^6$ and $3^0,3^3$, and no $5$ or $7$. That gives $3\\cdot2=6$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-16": ["For $\\overline{ab}$, the number minus its digit sum is $9a$, which ends in $6$ only for $a=4$. All $10$ numbers from $40$ to $49$ work, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-17": ["Each letter is an end of two of the five segments, so the five sums add to $2(3+5+6+7+9)=60$. Five terms of an arithmetic sequence average the middle one, so it is $12$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-18": ["Given B wins game $2$, list how A can still win. If B also won game $1$, A must win the next three: probability $\\tfrac18$. If A won game $1$, A needs $2$ of the remaining games before B does, probability $\\tfrac12$. So the chance B won game $1$ is $\\tfrac{1/8}{1/8+1/2}=\\tfrac15$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-19": ["The tilted square is a diamond with half-diagonal $\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2$, lowered into a gap of width $1$ until its lower sides touch the neighbors' top corners at $\\left(\\pm\\tfrac12,1\\right)$. A lower side has slope $1$, so the center is $\\tfrac12+\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2$ high, and the top point $B$ is another $\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2$ up: $\\sqrt2+\\tfrac12$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-20": ["Each slanted side of length $\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2$ runs $\\tfrac12$ across and $\\tfrac12$ up. So the octagon is a $2\\times2$ square with four corner triangles of legs $\\tfrac12$ cut off, area $4-4\\cdot\\tfrac18=\\tfrac72$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-21": ["$\\tfrac{6n}{n(n+1)/2}=\\tfrac{12}{n+1}$, so $n+1$ must divide $12$ and be at least $2$: $2,3,4,6,12$. That is $5$ values, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-22": ["The common elements are multiples of $12$ that are at most $4\\cdot2005=8020$, the smaller of the two largest elements. There are $\\left\\lfloor\\tfrac{8020}{12}\\right\\rfloor=668$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-23": ["With radius $r$, $AC=\\tfrac{2r}3$, so $OC=\\tfrac r3$. Since $O$ is the midpoint of $DE$, $[DCE]=2[DCO]=OC\\cdot DC=\\tfrac r3DC$, while $[ABD]=\\tfrac12\\cdot2r\\cdot DC=r\\,DC$. The ratio is $\\tfrac13$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-24": ["Both conditions force $n=p^2$ and $n+48=q^2$ with $p,q$ prime. Then $(q-p)(q+p)=48$ with both factors even: $2\\cdot24$ gives $p=11$, $q=13$; $4\\cdot12$ gives $p=4$; $6\\cdot8$ gives $p=1$. Only $n=121$ works, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10a-25": ["Triangles $ADE$ and $ABC$ share angle $A$, so $\\tfrac{[ADE]}{[ABC]}=\\tfrac{19\\cdot14}{25\\cdot42}=\\tfrac{19}{75}$. The quadrilateral is the other $\\tfrac{56}{75}$, so the ratio is $\\tfrac{19}{56}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-1": ["$2=0.1x$ gives $x=20$, and $2=0.2y$ gives $y=10$. So $x-y=10$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-2": ["The first equation gives $x=-2$. Then $-2b-10=-2$, so $b=-4$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-3": ["With width $w$ and length $2w$, $x^2=5w^2$. The area is $2w^2=\\tfrac25x^2$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-4": ["Alone, Dave pays for $6$ of his $7$ and Doug for $7$ of his $8$, $13$ in all. Together the $15$ windows bring $3$ free ones, so they pay for $12$. They save one window, $\\$100$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-5": ["The totals are $600$ and $600$, so the average is $\\tfrac{1200}{50}=24$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-6": ["If Mike rode at $r$ for time $t$, Josh rode at $\\tfrac45r$ for $2t$. Then $\\tfrac85rt+rt=13$, so Mike's distance $rt$ is $5$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-7": ["The four right triangles cut off by the extended sides are congruent, with hypotenuse $\\sqrt{50}$ and legs $p\\lt q$; the inner side is $q-p$. The leg along $\\overline{BH}$ from $B$ is the short one, so $p=BE=1$ and $q=\\sqrt{50-1}=7$. The inner side is $6$ and its area $36$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-8": ["$2005=5\\cdot401$ with $401$ prime. The three-digit factor must be $401$, and indeed $4+0+1=5$. So $A=4$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-9": ["One solution means the discriminant of $4x^2+(a+8)x+9$ is zero: $(a+8)^2=144$. So $a=4$ or $a=-20$, with sum $-16$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-10": ["The red faces are the $6n^2$ unit faces on the surface, and all the unit cubes have $6n^3$ faces. Then $6n^2=\\tfrac14\\cdot6n^3$ gives $n=4$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-11": ["The first and last digits need the same parity, and then they fix the middle digit. With a first digit from $1$ to $9$, the last digit has $5$ choices of matching parity, giving $9\\cdot5=45$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-12": ["The run is $(99,999)$ and $\\gcd(99,999)=9$, so the lattice points on the segment are $9$ equal steps apart, leaving $8$ strictly between $A$ and $B$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-13": ["Each letter is an end of two of the five segments, so the five sums add to $2(3+5+6+7+9)=60$. Five terms of an arithmetic sequence average the middle one, so it is $12$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-14": ["Each face comes up with probability $\\tfrac16$. A face with $k$ dots shows an odd number if $k$ is odd and loses no dot (probability $1-\\tfrac k{21}$), or if $k$ is even and loses one (probability $\\tfrac k{21}$). The total is $\\tfrac16\\left(3-\\tfrac{1+3+5}{21}+\\tfrac{2+4+6}{21}\\right)=\\tfrac16\\cdot\\tfrac{66}{21}=\\tfrac{11}{21}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-15": ["With radius $r$, $AC=\\tfrac{2r}3$, so $OC=\\tfrac r3$. Since $O$ is the midpoint of $DE$, $[DCE]=2[DCO]=OC\\cdot DC=\\tfrac r3DC$, while $[ABD]=\\tfrac12\\cdot2r\\cdot DC=r\\,DC$. The ratio is $\\tfrac13$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-16": ["The centers are $(s,s)$, $(3s,s)$, $(s,3s)$, and the big circle's is $(r,r)$. Tangency with the second circle puts the centers $r+s$ apart: $(r-3s)^2+(r-s)^2=(r+s)^2$, which simplifies to $r^2-10rs+9s^2=0$, that is $(r-s)(r-9s)=0$. As $r\\gt s$, $\\tfrac rs=9$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-17": ["The piece containing $W$ is a pyramid. Its base is the $\\tfrac12\\times\\tfrac12$ square at $W$ cut out of one face by the two sets of cuts, and its apex is the far end of the cube edge from $W$ perpendicular to that face, so its height is $1$. The volume is $\\tfrac13\\cdot\\tfrac14\\cdot1=\\tfrac1{12}$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-18": ["By inclusion-exclusion, the numbers from $1$ to $999$ divisible by none of $2,3,5$ number $999-(499+333+199)+(166+99+66)-33=266$. Remove $1$ and the $168-3=165$ primes other than $2,3,5$; the rest are prime-looking, $266-1-165=100$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-19": ["The odometer counts in base $9$ with the digits $0,1,2,3,5,\\ldots,9$ standing for $0$ to $8$. So $002005$ is $2004_9=2\\cdot729+4=1462$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-20": ["Each $y$ in $(0,1)$ has exactly two preimages under $f$, namely $\\tfrac y2$ and $1-\\tfrac y2$, and both are again in $(0,1)$. Starting from $\\tfrac12$ and going back $2005$ times doubles the count each time, giving $2^{2005}$ solutions, choice (E).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-21": ["The equation says $b=a^{c^{2005}}$. If $c=0$ then $b=1$ and $a=2004$; if $c=1$ then $b=a$ and $2a=2004$, so $a=1002$. If $c\\ge2$, then $b\\ge2^{2^{2005}}$, far too large. So there are $2$ triples, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-22": ["With edges $a,b,c$: $a+b+c=28$ and $ab+bc+ca=192$. So $a^2+b^2+c^2=28^2-2\\cdot192=400$, the space diagonal is $20$, and it is a diameter of the sphere: $r=10$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-23": ["With $a=2^i$ and $b=2^j$, $\\log_ab=\\tfrac ji$ is an integer when $i\\mid j$. For each $i$ there are $\\lfloor\\tfrac{25}i\\rfloor-1$ choices of $j\\ne i$, and $\\sum_{i=1}^{25}\\lfloor\\tfrac{25}i\\rfloor=87$, so $87-25=62$ of the $25\\cdot24=600$ ordered pairs work. The probability is $\\tfrac{62}{600}=\\tfrac{31}{300}$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-24": ["Comparing degrees, $Q$ has degree $2$. Setting $x=1,2,3$ makes the right side $0$, so $Q(1),Q(2),Q(3)$ are each roots of $P$, that is in $\\{1,2,3\\}$, and any such choice gives $P(Q(x))$ divisible by $P(x)$. The three values fix a polynomial of degree at most $2$, so there are $27$ candidates; remove the $5$ of lower degree, $(1,1,1)$, $(2,2,2)$, $(3,3,3)$, $(1,2,3)$, $(3,2,1)$. That leaves $22$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12a-25": ["Equilateral triangles in this grid have sides $\\sqrt2$, $\\sqrt6$, or $2\\sqrt2$. Side $\\sqrt2$: each of the $8$ unit cubes holds $8$, one cutting off each corner, $64$ in all. Side $2\\sqrt2$: the big cube holds $8$ the same way. Side $\\sqrt6$: the plane $x+y+z=3$ meets the grid in the center $(1,1,1)$ and the six permutations of $(0,1,2)$, a regular hexagon around it, whose alternate vertices give $2$ such triangles, such as $(0,1,2)$, $(1,2,0)$, $(2,0,1)$; the $4$ space diagonals give $8$. The total is $64+8+8=80$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-1": ["They pay $40$ cents a bar and sell at $50$ cents, so $1000$ bars make $10$ cents each, $\\$100$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-2": ["$\\tfrac x{100}\\cdot x=4$ gives $x^2=400$ and $x=20$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-3": ["Each day leaves $\\tfrac23$ of what there was, so after two days $\\tfrac49$ remains, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-4": ["Both inner values are $\\sqrt{25+144}=13$, so the result is $\\sqrt{13^2+13^2}=13\\sqrt2$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-5": ["A third of the CDs cost a fifth of her money, so all of them cost $\\tfrac35$, leaving $\\tfrac25$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-6": ["She needs $40$ A's and has $22$, so $18$ of the last $20$ must be A's. At most $2$ can be lower, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-7": ["Take the big square of side $2$. The circle in it has radius $1$, the square in that circle has side $\\sqrt2$, and the small circle has radius $\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2$, area $\\tfrac\\pi2$. The ratio is $\\tfrac{\\pi/2}4=\\tfrac\\pi8$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-8": ["The four quarter circles on a tile make one circle of radius $\\tfrac12$, area $\\tfrac\\pi4$. So each tile has $1-\\tfrac\\pi4$ shaded, and $80$ tiles give $80-20\\pi$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-9": ["The first die is odd with probability $\\tfrac23$ and the second is even with probability $\\tfrac23$. An odd sum needs one odd and one even: $\\tfrac23\\cdot\\tfrac23+\\tfrac13\\cdot\\tfrac13=\\tfrac59$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-10": ["Drop the altitude from $C$ to the midpoint $M$ of $AB$: $CM^2=49-1=48$. Then $MD=\\sqrt{64-48}=4$, so $BD=4-1=3$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-11": ["The terms go $2005$, $133$, $55$, $250$, $133$, and so on, repeating $133,55,250$ from the second term. Since $2005-2=2003\\equiv2\\pmod3$, the $2005$th term is $250$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-12": ["The product is prime exactly when eleven dice show $1$ and one shows $2$, $3$ or $5$. That is $12\\cdot3=36$ outcomes of $6^{12}$, so the probability is $\\tfrac{6^2}{6^{12}}=\\left(\\tfrac16\\right)^{10}$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-13": ["There are $668$ multiples of $3$, $501$ of $4$ and $167$ of $12$. The multiples of $3$ or $4$ number $668+501-167=1002$, and removing the multiples of $12$ leaves $835$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-14": ["$CD=CB=2$ and $CM=1$, and the angle $MCD$ is $180^\\circ-60^\\circ=120^\\circ$. The area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot2\\cdot1\\cdot\\sin120^\\circ=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-15": ["Of the $\\binom82=28$ pairs, a sum under $20$ uses no twenty and not both tens: $\\binom62-1=14$ pairs. So $14$ pairs reach $\\$20$, probability $\\tfrac12$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-16": ["If the second equation has roots $r,s$, then $p=-(r+s)$ and $m=rs$. The first has roots $2r,2s$, so $m=-2(r+s)=2p$ and $n=4rs=4m=8p$. So $\\tfrac np=8$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-17": ["Chain the powers: $4^{abcd}=5^{bcd}=6^{cd}=7^d=8$. So $2^{2abcd}=2^3$ and $abcd=\\tfrac32$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-18": ["The seven digits are distinct, increasing and from $2$ to $9$, so the number is fixed by which of those $8$ digits is left out. That gives $8$ numbers, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-19": ["The rest is $30\\%$ at $95$. The mean is $7+20+17+13.5+28.5=86$. The cumulative shares reach $35\\%$ at $80$ and $55\\%$ at $85$, so the median is $85$. The difference is $1$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-20": ["By symmetry each digit sits in each place in a fifth of the $120$ numbers, so each place averages $\\tfrac{1+3+5+7+8}5=4.8$. The mean is $4.8\\cdot11111=53332.8$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-21": ["For $p$, pick the number: $10$ ways, each using all four of its slips. For $q$, pick the two numbers, $\\binom{10}2=45$, and two slips of each, $\\binom42^2=36$. So $\\tfrac qp=\\tfrac{45\\cdot36}{10}=162$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-22": ["The sum is $\\tfrac{n(n+1)}2$, so $n!$ is divisible by it when $2(n-1)!$ is divisible by $n+1$. That fails exactly when $n+1$ is an odd prime: $n+1=3,5,7,11,13,17,19,23$, eight values. The other $24-8=16$ work, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-23": ["$EF$ is the midline, of length $\\tfrac{AB+DC}2$, and it splits the height in half. So the area ratio is $\\tfrac{AB+EF}{EF+DC}=2$, which gives $AB+\\tfrac{AB+DC}2=AB+DC+2DC$. Then $AB=5DC$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-24": ["With $x=\\overline{ab}$, $x^2-y^2=(x+y)(x-y)=11(a+b)\\cdot9(a-b)$. For a square, $11\\mid a+b$, so $a+b=11$, and then $a-b$ must be a square: $a-b=1$. So $x=65$, $y=56$, $m^2=121\\cdot9=33^2$, and $x+y+m=154$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2005-amc-10b-25": ["The numbers $1$ to $24$ have no partner up to $100$, so all can go in. The rest form $38$ pairs $\\{25,100\\},\\{26,99\\},\\ldots,\\{62,63\\}$, and $B$ takes at most one of each. The maximum is $24+38=62$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-1": ["They pay $40$ cents a bar and sell at $50$ cents, so $1000$ bars make $10$ cents each, $\\$100$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-2": ["$\\tfrac x{100}\\cdot x=4$ gives $x^2=400$ and $x=20$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-3": ["A third of the CDs cost a fifth of her money, so all of them cost $\\tfrac35$, leaving $\\tfrac25$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-4": ["She needs $40$ A's and has $22$, so $18$ of the last $20$ must be A's. At most $2$ can be lower, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-5": ["The four quarter circles on a tile make one circle of radius $\\tfrac12$, area $\\tfrac\\pi4$. So each tile has $1-\\tfrac\\pi4$ shaded, and $80$ tiles give $80-20\\pi$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-6": ["Drop the altitude from $C$ to the midpoint $M$ of $AB$: $CM^2=49-1=48$. Then $MD=\\sqrt{64-48}=4$, so $BD=4-1=3$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-7": ["The graph is the rhombus with vertices $(\\pm4,0)$ and $(0,\\pm3)$. Its diagonals are $8$ and $6$, so its area is $\\tfrac{8\\cdot6}2=24$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-8": ["The vertex is $(0,a^2)$, which lies on the line when $a^2=a$. So $a=0$ or $a=1$, $2$ values, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-9": ["The rest is $30\\%$ at $95$. The mean is $7+20+17+13.5+28.5=86$. The cumulative shares reach $35\\%$ at $80$ and $55\\%$ at $85$, so the median is $85$. The difference is $1$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-10": ["The terms go $2005$, $133$, $55$, $250$, $133$, and so on, repeating $133,55,250$ from the second term. Since $2005-2=2003\\equiv2\\pmod3$, the $2005$th term is $250$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-11": ["Of the $\\binom82=28$ pairs, a sum under $20$ uses no twenty and not both tens: $\\binom62-1=14$ pairs. So $14$ pairs reach $\\$20$, probability $\\tfrac12$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-12": ["If the second equation has roots $r,s$, then $p=-(r+s)$ and $m=rs$. The first has roots $2r,2s$, so $m=-2(r+s)=2p$ and $n=4rs=4m=8p$. So $\\tfrac np=8$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-13": ["By Change of Base, $x_k=\\tfrac{\\log(k+4)}{\\log(k+3)}$, so the product telescopes to $\\tfrac{\\log128}{\\log4}=\\log_4128=\\tfrac72$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-14": ["The radius is $k-6$ (the distance to $y=6$) and also $\\tfrac k{\\sqrt2}$ (the distance to $y=x$). So $k=\\tfrac{6\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt2-1}=12+6\\sqrt2$, and the radius is $6+6\\sqrt2$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-15": ["Let $T$ and $U$ be the sums of the tens and units digits, so $10T+U=221$. Four distinct nonzero digits give $10\\le U\\le30$ and $U\\equiv1\\pmod{10}$, so $(T,U)=(21,11)$ or $(20,21)$, and all eight digits sum to $32$ or $41$. They are $1,\\ldots,9$ less one digit, which sum to $45$ less that digit, so the missing digit is $13$ (impossible) or $4$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-16": ["The sphere in the first octant has center $(1,1,1)$, $\\sqrt3$ from the origin, and its farthest point is $1$ beyond that. By symmetry the same holds for all eight, so the radius is $1+\\sqrt3$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-17": ["The equation says $2^a3^b5^c7^d=10^{2005}$. Raising to the power of a common denominator of $a,b,c,d$ turns both sides into integers, and unique factorization then forces $a=c=2005$ and $b=d=0$. So there is $1$ tuple, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-18": ["The angles at $A$ and $B$ are acute when $C$ lies strictly between the lines through $A$ and $B$ perpendicular to $AB$, that is $4\\lt x+y\\lt14$. The angle at $C$ is acute when $C$ is outside the circle on diameter $AB$ (Thales). In the first quadrant the strip has area $\\tfrac{14^2}2-\\tfrac{4^2}2=90$, and the disk, of radius $\\tfrac{5\\sqrt2}2$ about $(4.5,4.5)$, lies inside it with area $12.5\\pi\\approx39.3$. So $R$ has area about $50.7$, closest to $51$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-19": ["With $x=\\overline{ab}$, $x^2-y^2=(x+y)(x-y)=11(a+b)\\cdot9(a-b)$. For a square, $11\\mid a+b$, so $a+b=11$, and then $a-b$ must be a square: $a-b=1$. So $x=65$, $y=56$, $m^2=121\\cdot9=33^2$, and $x+y+m=154$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-20": ["The eight numbers sum to $8$, so with $S=a+b+c+d$ the value is $S^2+(8-S)^2$, smallest when $S=4$. But the group holding $13$ would then need three others summing to $-9$, and no three of $-7,-5,-3,-2,2,4,6$ do. The next best is $S=5$ or $3$, giving $25+9=34$, which $13-7-3+2=5$ reaches, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-21": ["Write $n=7^km$ with $7\\nmid m$. Then $(k+1)d(m)=60$ and $(k+2)d(m)=80$, so $\\tfrac{k+2}{k+1}=\\tfrac43$ and $k=2$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-22": ["On the unit circle $\\overline{z}=\\tfrac1z$, so $z_{n+1}=iz_n^2$, and in polar form the angle obeys $\\theta_{n+1}=2\\theta_n+\\tfrac\\pi2$. The map $z\\mapsto iz^2$ is two-to-one on the unit circle, so going back from $z_{2005}=1$ doubles the number of possibilities at each of the $2005$ steps: $2^{2005}$ values, choice (E).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-23": ["Let $s=x+y=10^z$; then $x^2+y^2=10s$ and $xy=\\tfrac{s^2-10s}2$. So $x^3+y^3=s^3-3xy\\,s=s^3-\\tfrac32s(s^2-10s)=-\\tfrac12s^3+15s^2$. Thus $a=-\\tfrac12$, $b=15$, and $a+b=\\tfrac{29}2$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-24": ["The chord between $(p,p^2)$ and $(q,q^2)$ has slope $p+q$, so the three side slopes add to twice the sum $a+b+c$ of the $x$-coordinates. The sides make $60^\\circ$ angles, so with $\\tan\\theta=2$ the other slopes are $\\tan(\\theta\\pm60^\\circ)=\\tfrac{2\\pm\\sqrt3}{1\\mp2\\sqrt3}$, which add to $\\tfrac{16}{-11}$. So $2(a+b+c)=2-\\tfrac{16}{11}=\\tfrac6{11}$, $a+b+c=\\tfrac3{11}$, and $m+n=14$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2005-amc-12b-25": ["The two pole ants must both move to the equator, so the two poles are filled by two of the four equator ants: $4\\cdot3=12$ ordered choices. The other two equator ants then move along the equator square to different vertices, and the pole ants fill the two equator vertices left over in $2$ ways. If the two staying ants are adjacent, they have $4$ ways (each goes to the other or onward); this happens for $8$ of the $12$ choices. If they are opposite, they must go to the two other vertices, $2$ ways, for the other $4$ choices. That gives $(8\\cdot4+4\\cdot2)\\cdot2=80$ good moves out of $4^6=4096$, a probability of $\\tfrac5{256}$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-1": ["$5\\cdot3+8\\cdot2=31$ dollars, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-2": ["$h\\otimes h=h^3-h$, so $h\\otimes(h^3-h)=h^3-(h^3-h)=h$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-3": ["Mary is $\\tfrac35$ of $30$, that is $18$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-4": ["The hour digits add to at most $9$ (at $9$), and the minutes to at most $5+9=14$ (at $59$). So $9{:}59$ gives the most, $23$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-5": ["A plain slice costs $\\$1$ and an anchovy slice $\\tfrac{4+2}4=\\$1.50$. Dave pays $4\\cdot1.5+1=7$ and Doug $3$, so Dave pays $4$ more, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-6": ["Take seventh roots: $(7x)^2=14x$, so $49x^2=14x$ and $x=\\tfrac27$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-7": ["The square has the same area, $144$, so its side is $12$. To make it, one hexagon slides up by $4$, half the height, and along the step, which shrinks the width from $18$ to $12$. The step is that slide, $y=18-12=6$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-8": ["Equal $y$-values at $x=2$ and $x=4$ put the axis at $x=3$, so $b=-6$. Then $3=4-12+c$ gives $c=11$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-9": ["The runs are $7+8$, $4+5+6$ and $1+2+3+4+5$. A run of $k$ numbers from $a$ sums to $ka+\\tfrac{k(k-1)}2$, and no other $k\\ge2$ gives $15$ with $a\\ge1$. That is $3$ sets, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-10": ["The value is an integer $k$ when $\\sqrt x=120-k^2\\ge0$, which allows $k=0,1,\\ldots,10$. Each gives one $x$, so $11$ values, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-11": ["Expanding leaves $2xy=0$, so $x=0$ or $y=0$: the two axes, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-12": ["In I the rope is tied $8$ from each corner, so the dog roams a half circle of radius $8$, $32\\pi$. In II it is tied $4$ from a corner: the same half circle plus a quarter circle of radius $4$ around the corner, $32\\pi+4\\pi$. So II gives $4\\pi$ more, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-13": ["Winning needs an even first roll and a match, probability $\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac16=\\tfrac1{12}$. A fair prize $W$ has $\\tfrac W{12}=5$, so $W=60$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-14": ["The rings' insides stack up, each inner diameter $2$ less than the outer: $18,17,\\ldots,1$, adding to $171$. The top ring's thickness above and the bottom ring's below add $2$, for $173$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-15": ["In laps per minute, Odell goes $\\tfrac{250}{100\\pi}$ and Kershaw $\\tfrac{300}{120\\pi}$, both $\\tfrac5{2\\pi}$, and they run in opposite directions. Together they gain $\\tfrac5\\pi$ laps per minute, so in $30$ minutes they cover $\\tfrac{150}\\pi\\approx47.7$ laps and pass $47$ times, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-16": ["The centers lie on the axis from $A$, $3$ apart. Similar triangles from $A$ give $\\tfrac1d=\\tfrac2{d+3}$ for the small center's distance $d$, so $d=3$ and the big center is $6$ from $A$. Then $\\sin\\tfrac A2=\\tfrac26=\\tfrac13$, so $\\tan\\tfrac A2=\\tfrac1{2\\sqrt2}$. The height is $6+2=8$ and half the base is $\\tfrac8{2\\sqrt2}=2\\sqrt2$, so the area is $16\\sqrt2$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-17": ["Put $A=(0,0)$ and $E=(3,2)$, so $B,C=(1,0),(2,0)$ and $G,F=(1,2),(2,2)$. The four lines $AF$, $BE$, $HC$ and $GD$ have slopes $\\pm1$ and meet at $(1,1)$, $(1.5,1.5)$, $(2,1)$ and $(1.5,0.5)$. That is a square with diagonals $1$, area $\\tfrac12$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-18": ["The letter pair can start in $5$ places among the six spots. Then the digits fill the other $4$ spots in $10^4$ ways and the letters in $26^2$. The total is $5\\cdot10^4\\cdot26^2$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-19": ["Three angles in arithmetic progression average $60^\\circ$, so they are $60-d$, $60$ and $60+d$. They are positive and distinct for $d=1,\\ldots,59$, giving $59$ triangles, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-20": ["Six numbers fall into $5$ remainder classes mod $5$, so two share a class and their difference is a multiple of $5$. The probability is $1$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-21": ["Count the four-digit numbers with no $2$ or $3$: $7$ choices for the first digit and $8$ for each other, $7\\cdot8^3=3584$. Then $9000-3584=5416$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-22": ["Every resolvable debt is $300p+210g$ for integers $p,g$, so it is a multiple of $\\gcd(300,210)=30$. And $30$ works: $300\\cdot5-210\\cdot7=30$, five pigs paid with seven goats in change. Choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-23": ["The radii to $C$ and $D$ are perpendicular to the tangent, so triangles $ACE$ and $BDE$ are similar right triangles with ratio $3:8$. $AE=5$ and $AC=3$ give $CE=4$, so $DE=\\tfrac{32}3$. Then $CD=4+\\tfrac{32}3=\\tfrac{44}3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-24": ["The octahedron is two square pyramids. Their shared square joins four side-face centers, with side $\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2$ and area $\\tfrac12$, and each apex is $\\tfrac12$ away. The volume is $2\\cdot\\tfrac13\\cdot\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12=\\tfrac16$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10a-25": ["There are $3^7$ equally likely walks. Count the paths through all $8$ vertices: $3$ choices for the first move, $2$ for the second, and the walk then has exactly $3$ ways to finish. With the first two moves $000\\to100\\to110$, they continue $010,011,111,101,001$; or $010,011,001,101,111$; or $111,101,001,011,010$. That is $3\\cdot2\\cdot3=18$ paths, probability $\\tfrac{18}{2187}=\\tfrac2{243}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-1": ["$5\\cdot3+8\\cdot2=31$ dollars, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-2": ["$h\\otimes h=h^3-h$, so $h\\otimes(h^3-h)=h^3-(h^3-h)=h$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-3": ["Mary is $\\tfrac35$ of $30$, that is $18$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-4": ["The hour digits add to at most $9$ (at $9$), and the minutes to at most $5+9=14$ (at $59$). So $9{:}59$ gives the most, $23$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-5": ["A plain slice costs $\\$1$ and an anchovy slice $\\tfrac{4+2}4=\\$1.50$. Dave pays $4\\cdot1.5+1=7$ and Doug $3$, so Dave pays $4$ more, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-6": ["The square has the same area, $144$, so its side is $12$. To make it, one hexagon slides up by $4$, half the height, and along the step, which shrinks the width from $18$ to $12$. The step is that slide, $y=18-12=6$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-7": ["With Danielle's age $D$, Sally is $0.6D$ and Mary $1.2\\cdot0.6D=0.72D$. The sum is $2.32D=23.2$, so $D=10$ and Mary is $7.2$. Her next birthday is her $8$th, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-8": ["The runs are $7+8$, $4+5+6$ and $1+2+3+4+5$. A run of $k$ numbers from $a$ sums to $ka+\\tfrac{k(k-1)}2$, and no other $k\\ge2$ gives $15$ with $a\\ge1$. That is $3$ sets, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-9": ["From $13p+3e=100$, $13p\\equiv100\\pmod3$, that is $p\\equiv1\\pmod3$, and $p\\le7$. The options $p=1,4,7$ give $e=29,16,3$, and only $p=7$, $e=3$ has $p\\gt e$. So one of each costs $10$ cents, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-10": ["The value is an integer $k$ when $\\sqrt x=120-k^2\\ge0$, which allows $k=0,1,\\ldots,10$. Each gives one $x$, so $11$ values, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-11": ["Expanding leaves $2xy=0$, so $x=0$ or $y=0$: the two axes, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-12": ["The rings' insides stack up, each inner diameter $2$ less than the outer: $18,17,\\ldots,1$, adding to $171$. The top ring's thickness above and the bottom ring's below add $2$, for $173$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-13": ["Externally tangent circles have centers apart by the sum of their radii, so $r_1+r_2=3$, $r_2+r_3=4$, $r_1+r_3=5$. Adding, $r_1+r_2+r_3=6$, so the radii are $1,2,3$ and the areas sum to $(1+4+9)\\pi=14\\pi$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-14": ["Every resolvable debt is $300p+210g$ for integers $p,g$, so it is a multiple of $\\gcd(300,210)=30$. And $30$ works: $300\\cdot5-210\\cdot7=30$, five pigs paid with seven goats in change. Choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-15": ["$\\cos x=0$ means $x=\\tfrac\\pi2+k\\pi$. Taking $x=\\tfrac{3\\pi}2$, $\\cos(x+z)=\\sin z=\\tfrac12$, so $z=\\tfrac\\pi6$ works. No smaller positive $z$ does: with $x=\\tfrac\\pi2$ the condition is $-\\sin z=\\tfrac12$, which needs $z\\gt\\pi$. So the answer is $\\tfrac\\pi6$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-16": ["The radii to $C$ and $D$ are perpendicular to the tangent, so triangles $ACE$ and $BDE$ are similar right triangles with ratio $3:8$. $AE=5$ and $AC=3$ give $CE=4$, so $DE=\\tfrac{32}3$. Then $CD=4+\\tfrac{32}3=\\tfrac{44}3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-17": ["The radius $EF$ is perpendicular to the tangent $AF$, so $AF^2=AE^2-r^2$. In $\\triangle ADE$, $AD=s$, $DE=r$, and $\\angle ADE=180^\\circ-45^\\circ=135^\\circ$, so by the Law of Cosines $AE^2=s^2+r^2+\\sqrt2\\,sr$. Hence $AF^2=s^2+\\sqrt2\\,sr=9+5\\sqrt2$. As $s$ and $r$ are rational, $s^2=9$ and $sr=5$, so $s=3$, $r=\\tfrac53$, and $\\tfrac rs=\\tfrac59$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-18": ["Replacing $x$ by $\\tfrac1x$ gives $f(\\tfrac1x)+f(x)=\\tfrac1x$. The left sides agree, so $x=\\tfrac1x$ and $x=\\pm1$. On $\\{-1,1\\}$ the choice $f(x)=\\tfrac x2$ works, so the domain is $\\{-1,1\\}$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-19": ["The external tangents pass through the external center of similitude, which divides the centers externally in the ratio $4:9$: $\\tfrac{9(2,4)-4(14,9)}{9-4}=(-\\tfrac{38}5,0)$. So the tangent is $y=m(x+\\tfrac{38}5)$. Its distance from $(2,4)$ is $4$: $(\\tfrac{48}5m-4)^2=16(1+m^2)$, which reduces to $\\tfrac{1904}{25}m^2=\\tfrac{384}5m$. The root $m=0$ is the $x$-axis; the other is $m=\\tfrac{120}{119}$, and $b=\\tfrac{38}5m=\\tfrac{912}{119}$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-20": ["There are $3^7$ equally likely walks. Count the paths through all $8$ vertices: $3$ choices for the first move, $2$ for the second, and the walk then has exactly $3$ ways to finish. With the first two moves $000\\to100\\to110$, they continue $010,011,111,101,001$; or $010,011,001,101,111$; or $111,101,001,011,010$. That is $3\\cdot2\\cdot3=18$ paths, probability $\\tfrac{18}{2187}=\\tfrac2{243}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-21": ["$S_1$ is $1+x^2+y^2\\le10(x+y)$, that is $(x-5)^2+(y-5)^2\\le49$, a disk of area $49\\pi$. $S_2$ is $2+x^2+y^2\\le100(x+y)$, that is $(x-50)^2+(y-50)^2\\le4998$, of area $4998\\pi$. Both disks lie in $x+y\\gt0$ (their centers are $5\\sqrt2\\gt7$ and $50\\sqrt2\\gt\\sqrt{4998}$ from the line $x+y=0$), so the logarithms make sense. The ratio is $\\tfrac{4998}{49}=102$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-22": ["A side is fully visible from $P$ exactly when $P$ is beyond the line of that side. Three consecutive sides are visible when $P$ is beyond the lines of the outer two; those lines meet beyond the middle side, and the region past both is a $60^\\circ$ wedge pointing away from the hexagon. There are six such wedges, so each must cut off an arc of $\\tfrac{360^\\circ}{12}=30^\\circ$, with ends $15^\\circ$ either side of the wedge's axis. An end lies on the farther of the two lines, whose normal (toward that side's midpoint) makes $60^\\circ+15^\\circ=75^\\circ$ with the direction of that end, and that line is the apothem $\\sqrt3$ from the center. So $r\\cos75^\\circ=\\sqrt3$, and $r=\\tfrac{4\\sqrt3}{\\sqrt6-\\sqrt2}=3\\sqrt2+\\sqrt6$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-23": ["Each averaging step adds neighbours with weight $\\tfrac12$, so after $100$ steps the one entry is $\\tfrac1{2^{100}}\\sum\\binom{100}kx^k=\\left(\\tfrac{1+x}2\\right)^{100}$. Setting it equal to $2^{-50}$ gives $(1+x)^{100}=2^{50}$, so $1+x=\\sqrt2$ and $x=\\sqrt2-1$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12a-25": ["A set with $k$ elements is $k$ non-adjacent numbers from the $16-k$ numbers $k,\\ldots,15$, which can be done in $\\binom{17-2k}k$ ways. Summing over $k$: $15+78+165+126+21=405$ ($k\\ge6$ gives $0$), choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-1": ["The terms pair up as $(-1)+1=0$, and $2006$ terms make $1003$ pairs. The sum is $0$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-2": ["$x\\spadesuit y=x^2-y^2$. So $4\\spadesuit5=-9$ and $3\\spadesuit(-9)=9-81=-72$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-3": ["The Panthers scored $\\tfrac{34-14}2=10$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-4": ["The areas grow with the square of the diameter: the big circle is $9$ times the small one. So blue is $9-1=8$ times red, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-5": ["Since the rectangles do not overlap, a horizontal or vertical line separates them, so the square's side is at least the sum of their sides in that direction, at least $3+2=5$. Side by side, $3\\times4$ and $2\\times3$ fit in a $5\\times5$ square. So the area is $25$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-6": ["Each arc is a half circle of diameter $\\tfrac2\\pi$, with length $\\tfrac12\\pi\\cdot\\tfrac2\\pi=1$. The four arcs give $4$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-7": ["The denominator is $1-\\tfrac{x-1}x=\\tfrac1x$, so the inside is $x^2$ and the root is $|x|=-x$ for $x\\lt0$. Choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-8": ["The square's base is centered on the diameter, so a top corner is $s$ up and $\\tfrac s2$ across from the center. Then $r^2=s^2+\\tfrac{s^2}4=\\tfrac54\\cdot40=50$, and the semicircle has area $\\tfrac12\\cdot50\\pi=25\\pi$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-9": ["The $600$ grams hold $25+386=411$ calories, so $200$ grams hold a third of that, $137$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-10": ["With sides $a$, $3a$ and $15$, the triangle inequality needs $a+15\\gt3a$, so $a\\le7$, and $4a\\gt15$. The largest perimeter is $7+21+15=43$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-11": ["From $10!$ on every factorial ends in $00$. So only $7!+8!+9!=5040+40320+362880$ counts, ending in $40$. The tens digit is $4$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-12": ["Substitute $(1,2)$: $1=\\tfrac12+a$ and $2=\\tfrac14+b$. So $a=\\tfrac12$, $b=\\tfrac74$, and $a+b=\\tfrac94$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-13": ["Joe keeps all $2$ ounces of cream. JoAnn's $14$ ounces are $\\tfrac17$ cream, and drinking $2$ leaves $\\tfrac67$ of her cream, $\\tfrac{12}7$ ounces. The ratio is $2\\div\\tfrac{12}7=\\tfrac76$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-14": ["$ab=2$ by Vieta. Then $q=\\left(a+\\tfrac1b\\right)\\left(b+\\tfrac1a\\right)=ab+2+\\tfrac1{ab}=2+2+\\tfrac12=\\tfrac92$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-15": ["With $\\angle A=60^\\circ$, triangle $ABD$ is equilateral, so $BD$ equals the side $s$, while the long diagonal $AC$ is $s\\sqrt3$. In $BFDE$, $BD$ is the long diagonal, so the scale factor is $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ and the area is $\\tfrac{24}3=8$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-16": ["The $16$ years hold $4$ leap days, so they add $16\\cdot365+4=5844$ days, and $5844=7\\cdot834+6$. Six days after Sunday is Saturday, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-17": ["Bob's bag then has $6$ balls, two of the color Alice gave. The bags match only if Bob sends back that color, probability $\\tfrac26=\\tfrac13$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-18": ["The terms run $2,3,\\tfrac32,\\tfrac12,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac23,2,3,\\ldots$, repeating every $6$. Since $2006\\equiv2\\pmod6$, $a_{2006}=a_2=3$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-19": ["Put $O$ at the origin and $B=(1,1)$. Then $D=(1,\\sqrt3)$ and $E=(\\sqrt3,1)$ sit at $60^\\circ$ and $30^\\circ$, so sector $DOE$ has area $\\tfrac\\pi3$. Remove triangles $ODB$ and $OBE$, each $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3-1}2$: the region is $\\tfrac\\pi3+1-\\sqrt3$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-20": ["$\\overrightarrow{AB}=(2000,200)$ and $\\overrightarrow{AD}=(2,y+22)$ are perpendicular: $4000+200(y+22)=0$, so $\\overrightarrow{AD}=(2,-20)$. The area is $|AB|\\cdot|AD|=200\\sqrt{101}\\cdot2\\sqrt{101}=40{,}400$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-21": ["Each face $k$ has probability $\\tfrac k{21}$. A total of $7$ pairs $k$ with $7-k$: $\\tfrac{1\\cdot6+2\\cdot5+3\\cdot4+4\\cdot3+5\\cdot2+6\\cdot1}{441}=\\tfrac{56}{441}=\\tfrac8{63}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-22": ["In cents $N(4B+5J)=253=11\\cdot23$. Since $4B+5J\\ge9$ and $N\\gt1$, $N=11$ and $4B+5J=23$, so $B=2$, $J=3$. The jam costs $11\\cdot3\\cdot5=165$ cents, $\\$1.65$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-23": ["Split the quadrilateral by the segment from the top vertex to the crossing point, into $a$ next to the $3$ and $b$ next to the second $7$. Triangles on the same cevian give $\\tfrac37=\\tfrac a{b+7}$ and $\\tfrac77=\\tfrac{a+3}b$. So $b=a+3$ and $7a=3(a+10)$, giving $a=7.5$, $b=10.5$ and a total of $18$, choice (D).","Alternatively, use mass points and the bird's head. Call the top vertex $T$, the bottom vertices $L$ and $R$, the cevians $LP$ and $RQ$, and their crossing $X$. Triangles with apex $R$ give $LX=XP$, and triangles with apex $L$ give $QX:XR=3:7$. Masses $3$ at $R$ and $7$ at $Q$ balance $QR$; then $m_T+m_L=7$ and $m_L=m_T+3$ give $m_T=2$, $m_L=5$, so $TQ:TL=5:7$ and $TP:TR=3:5$. Triangle $LPR$ is $\\tfrac25$ of the whole, so the whole is $35$, and by the bird's head $[TQP]=\\tfrac57\\cdot\\tfrac35\\cdot35=15$. Since $LX=XP$, $[QXP]=[QXL]=3$, and the quadrilateral is $15+3=18$.",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-24": ["The radii $OA$ and $PD$ are perpendicular to $AD$, so $OADP$ is a right trapezoid with $OP=6$. Then $AD=\\sqrt{6^2-(4-2)^2}=4\\sqrt2$, and the trapezoid has area $\\tfrac{2+4}2\\cdot4\\sqrt2=12\\sqrt2$. The hexagon is two of them, $24\\sqrt2$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-10b-25": ["The ages are $9$ and seven of $1$ to $8$, so at least one of $2,4,6,8$ is an age and the number is even. If $5$ were an age too, the number would end in $0$; with two digits twice each and a digit sum divisible by $9$, it would be $9900$ or $9090$, and each misses at least two of the ages $4,7,8$. So $5$ is not an age, and $5544$ works for $1,2,3,4,6,7,8,9$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-1": ["The terms pair up as $(-1)+1=0$, and $2006$ terms make $1003$ pairs. The sum is $0$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-2": ["$x\\spadesuit y=x^2-y^2$. So $4\\spadesuit5=-9$ and $3\\spadesuit(-9)=9-81=-72$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-3": ["The Panthers scored $\\tfrac{34-14}2=10$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-4": ["The items cost $8+5+3+2+1-0.05=18.95$ dollars, so the change is $1.05$, that is $5.25\\%$ of $20$, closest to choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-5": ["Bob gains $5-3=2$ miles per hour, so closing the $1$-mile gap takes half an hour, $30$ minutes, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-6": ["The $600$ grams hold $25+386=411$ calories, so $200$ grams hold a third of that, $137$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-7": ["There are $2$ choices for the driver and then $3!=6$ ways to seat the other three, $12$ in all, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-8": ["Substitute $(1,2)$: $1=\\tfrac12+a$ and $2=\\tfrac14+b$. So $a=\\tfrac12$, $b=\\tfrac74$, and $a+b=\\tfrac94$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-9": ["The last digit $c$ is even, and the first two are chosen from $1,\\ldots,c-1$ in increasing order (a leading digit is never $0$), in $\\binom{c-1}2$ ways. For $c=4,6,8$ this gives $3+10+21=34$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-10": ["With sides $a$, $3a$ and $15$, the triangle inequality needs $a+15\\gt3a$, so $a\\le7$, and $4a\\gt15$. The largest perimeter is $7+21+15=43$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-11": ["Joe keeps all $2$ ounces of cream. JoAnn's $14$ ounces are $\\tfrac17$ cream, and drinking $2$ leaves $\\tfrac67$ of her cream, $\\tfrac{12}7$ ounces. The ratio is $2\\div\\tfrac{12}7=\\tfrac76$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-12": ["In vertex form $y=a(x-p)^2+p$, and $x=0$ gives $ap^2+p=-p$, so $a=-\\tfrac2p$. Expanding, $b=-2ap=4$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-13": ["With $\\angle A=60^\\circ$, triangle $ABD$ is equilateral, so $BD$ equals the side $s$, while the long diagonal $AC$ is $s\\sqrt3$. In $BFDE$, $BD$ is the long diagonal, so the scale factor is $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ and the area is $\\tfrac{24}3=8$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-14": ["In cents $N(4B+5J)=253=11\\cdot23$. Since $4B+5J\\ge9$ and $N\\gt1$, $N=11$ and $4B+5J=23$, so $B=2$, $J=3$. The jam costs $11\\cdot3\\cdot5=165$ cents, $\\$1.65$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-15": ["The radii $OA$ and $PD$ are perpendicular to $AD$, so $OADP$ is a right trapezoid with $OP=6$. Then $AD=\\sqrt{6^2-(4-2)^2}=4\\sqrt2$, and the trapezoid has area $\\tfrac{2+4}2\\cdot4\\sqrt2=12\\sqrt2$. The hexagon is two of them, $24\\sqrt2$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-16": ["$AC$ is a short diagonal, $\\sqrt3$ times the side, and $AC^2=50$, so the side squared is $\\tfrac{50}3$. The area is $\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}2\\cdot\\tfrac{50}3=25\\sqrt3$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-17": ["Each face $k$ has probability $\\tfrac k{21}$. A total of $7$ pairs $k$ with $7-k$: $\\tfrac{1\\cdot6+2\\cdot5+3\\cdot4+4\\cdot3+5\\cdot2+6\\cdot1}{441}=\\tfrac{56}{441}=\\tfrac8{63}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-18": ["Each step changes $x+y$ by $1$, so after ten steps $|x|+|y|$ is even and at most $10$, and every such point can be reached (go there, then waste the spare steps going back and forth). There are $4k$ points with $|x|+|y|=k\\ge1$, so the count is $1+4(2+4+6+8+10)=121$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-19": ["The ages are $9$ and seven of $1$ to $8$, so at least one of $2,4,6,8$ is an age and the number is even. If $5$ were an age too, the number would end in $0$; with two digits twice each and a digit sum divisible by $9$, it would be $9900$ or $9090$, and each misses at least two of the ages $4,7,8$. So $5$ is not an age, and $5544$ works for $1,2,3,4,6,7,8,9$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-20": ["On $[10^{-n},10^{-n+1})$ the two floors agree exactly when $4x\\lt10^{-n+1}$, that is $x\\lt2.5\\cdot10^{-n}$, an interval of length $1.5\\cdot10^{-n}$. Summing over $n\\ge1$ gives $1.5\\cdot\\tfrac19=\\tfrac16$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-21": ["Let the rectangle be $x$ by $y$, so $xy=2006$. The ellipse passes through $A$, so $2a=AB+AD=x+y$, and the foci are $BD=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ apart, so $2c=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$. Then $b^2=a^2-c^2=\\tfrac{(x+y)^2-(x^2+y^2)}4=\\tfrac{xy}2=1003$. The area gives $ab=2006$, so $a=2\\sqrt{1003}$, and the perimeter is $2(x+y)=4a=8\\sqrt{1003}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-22": ["There are more factors of $2$ than of $5$, so $n$ is the number of $5$s, $\\sum_i\\left(\\lfloor\\tfrac a{5^i}\\rfloor+\\lfloor\\tfrac b{5^i}\\rfloor+\\lfloor\\tfrac c{5^i}\\rfloor\\right)$ by Legendre's Formula. Each bracket is at least $\\lfloor\\tfrac{2006}{5^i}\\rfloor-2$, so $n\\ge(401-2)+(80-2)+(16-2)+(3-2)=492$. The choice $a=b=624$, $c=758$ reaches it: $152+152+188=492$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-23": ["Rotate $\\triangle CPB$ by $90^\\circ$ about $C$ so that $B$ goes to $A$ and $P$ to $P'$. Then $CP'=6$, $P'A=7$, and $\\triangle PCP'$ is a right isosceles triangle with $PP'=6\\sqrt2$. Since $PP'^2+P'A^2=72+49=121=PA^2$, the angle at $P'$ in $\\triangle PP'A$ is right, so $\\angle CP'A=45^\\circ+90^\\circ=135^\\circ$. By the Law of Cosines in $\\triangle CP'A$, $s^2=36+49+2\\cdot6\\cdot7\\cdot\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2=85+42\\sqrt2$, so $a+b=127$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-24": ["Let $u=\\cos(x+y)$ and $v=\\cos(x-y)$. Then $\\sin^2x+\\sin^2y=1-uv$ and $\\sin x\\sin y=\\tfrac{v-u}2$ by Product-to-Sum, so the condition reads $\\tfrac14-uv-\\tfrac v2+\\tfrac u2\\le0$, that is $(u+\\tfrac12)(v-\\tfrac12)\\ge0$. In the square both factors are nonnegative exactly when $x+y\\le\\tfrac{2\\pi}3$ and $|x-y|\\le\\tfrac\\pi3$ (both negative only on a line). This cuts off two corner triangles of area $\\tfrac{\\pi^2}{72}$ and one of area $\\tfrac{\\pi^2}{18}$, leaving $\\tfrac{\\pi^2}4-\\tfrac{\\pi^2}{12}=\\tfrac{\\pi^2}6$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2006-amc-12b-25": ["Like the Euclidean Algorithm, the rule keeps the gcd of consecutive terms, so $a_{2006}=1$ forces $\\gcd(999,a_2)=1$. Parity rules out even $a_2$: the parities then run odd, even, odd with period $3$, and $2006\\equiv2\\pmod3$ makes $a_{2006}$ even. Conversely, each odd $a_2$ coprime to $999$ works: the sequence settles into $1,1,0$ repeating well before term $2006$, as running all of them confirms. There are $\\varphi(999)=999\\cdot\\tfrac23\\cdot\\tfrac{36}{37}=648$ values coprime to $999$, and $a\\leftrightarrow999-a$ pairs odd with even, so $324$ are odd, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-1": ["Susan pays $4\\cdot20\\cdot0.75=60$ and Pam pays $5\\cdot20\\cdot0.7=70$, a difference of $10$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-2": ["$6@2=12-4=8$ and $6\\#2=6+2-24=-16$, so the ratio is $-\\tfrac12$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-3": ["The brick is fully under water and displaces $40\\cdot20\\cdot10=8000$ cubic cm. Spread over the $100\\cdot40=4000$ base, the water rises $2$ cm, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-4": ["With the smaller $n$, $n+2=3n$ gives $n=1$, so the integers are $1$ and $3$, with sum $4$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-5": ["In cents, $7p+8n=415$ and $5p+3n=177$. Eliminating gives $p=9$ and $n=44$, so $16p+10n=144+440=584$, that is $\\$5.84$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-6": ["The increases are $\\tfrac6{60}=10\\%$, $\\tfrac4{66}$, $\\tfrac6{70}$, $\\tfrac2{76}$ and $\\tfrac7{78}$, each under $10\\%$. The largest is from 2002 to 2003, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-7": ["Federal tax takes $0.2x$ and state tax $0.1\\cdot0.8x=0.08x$, so $0.28x=10{,}500$ and $x=37{,}500$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-8": ["In triangle $ABC$ the base angles are $\\tfrac{180^\\circ-40^\\circ}2=70^\\circ$, and in triangle $ADC$ they are $\\tfrac{180^\\circ-140^\\circ}2=20^\\circ$. So $\\angle BAD=70^\\circ-20^\\circ=50^\\circ$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-9": ["Write both in one base: $3^a=3^{4(b+2)}$ and $5^{3b}=5^{a-3}$. So $a=4b+8$ and $a=3b+3$, giving $b=-5$, $a=-12$ and $ab=60$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-10": ["Let $k$ be the number of people other than the father. The totals give $48+16k=20(k+1)$, so $k=7$: the mother and $6$ children, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-11": ["Each vertex is on $3$ of the $6$ faces, so the six face sums add to $3(1+2+\\cdots+8)=108$. Each face then sums to $18$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-12": ["Each tourist picks one of $2$ guides, $2^6=64$ ways, minus the $2$ where one guide gets everyone: $62$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-13": ["Let $h$ and $d$ be his distances from home and the stadium. Equal times give $d=h+\\tfrac{h+d}7$, so $7d=8h+d$ and $\\tfrac hd=\\tfrac68=\\tfrac34$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-14": ["A $3$-$4$-$5$ triangle is right, so its hypotenuse is the diameter $6$. The legs are $3.6$ and $4.8$, and the area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot3.6\\cdot4.8=8.64$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-15": ["Along a diagonal from the center: the big radius $2$, then $1$ to a small center, then $\\sqrt2$ to the corner, since that center is $1$ from both sides. Half the diagonal is $3+\\sqrt2$, so the side is $\\sqrt2(3+\\sqrt2)=3\\sqrt2+2$ and the area is $22+12\\sqrt2$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-16": ["Half the numbers are even, so a product $ad$ is odd with probability $\\tfrac14$ and even with $\\tfrac34$, and the same for $bc$. The difference is even when the two products have the same parity: $\\left(\\tfrac34\\right)^2+\\left(\\tfrac14\\right)^2=\\tfrac58$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-17": ["$75m=3\\cdot5^2\\cdot m$ must be a cube, so the least $m$ is $3^2\\cdot5=45$, giving $75m=3375=15^3$. Then $m+n=45+15=60$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-18": ["Put $H=(4,0)$ and $G=(8,0)$, so $A=(4,12)$, $B=(8,12)$ and $C=(8,8)$. Lines $AG$ and $CH$ meet at $M=\\left(\\tfrac{32}5,\\tfrac{24}5\\right)$. The shoelace formula on $A,B,C,M$ gives $\\tfrac{88}5$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-19": ["With side $s$ and brush width $w$, each strip covers $\\tfrac w{\\sqrt2}$ of a side next to each corner. The four unpainted parts are isosceles right triangles with hypotenuse $s-\\sqrt2w$ on a side, area $\\tfrac{(s-\\sqrt2w)^2}4$ each. They fill half the square: $(s-\\sqrt2w)^2=\\tfrac{s^2}2$, so $s-\\sqrt2w=\\tfrac s{\\sqrt2}$ and $\\tfrac sw=\\tfrac2{\\sqrt2-1}=2\\sqrt2+2$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-20": ["Square twice: $a^2+a^{-2}=4^2-2=14$, then $a^4+a^{-4}=14^2-2=194$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-21": ["The outer cube has side $2$, so the sphere has diameter $2$, which is the inner cube's space diagonal. The inner side is $\\tfrac2{\\sqrt3}$, and its surface area is $6\\cdot\\tfrac43=8$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-22": ["The digits run around in a cycle, so each digit shows up once as a hundreds digit, once as a tens digit and once as a units digit. So $S$ is $111=3\\cdot37$ times the sum of all the hundreds digits, and $37$ always divides it, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-23": ["$(m-n)(m+n)=96$ with both factors of the same parity, so both even: $2\\cdot48$, $4\\cdot24$, $6\\cdot16$, $8\\cdot12$. That gives $4$ pairs, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-24": ["The tangent $OC$ is perpendicular to $AC$ with $AC=2$ and $OA=2\\sqrt2$, so triangle $ACO$ is an isosceles right triangle, area $2$, and $\\angle CAE=45^\\circ$. The rectangle $ABFE$ has area $4\\sqrt2\\cdot2=8\\sqrt2$. Removing the two triangles and the two $45^\\circ$ sectors of area $\\tfrac\\pi2$ leaves $8\\sqrt2-4-\\pi$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10a-25": ["Mod $9$, $n$, $S(n)$ and $S(S(n))$ agree, so $3n\\equiv2007\\equiv0$ and $n\\equiv0\\pmod3$. Also $S(n)\\le28$ and $S(S(n))\\le10$, so $n\\ge1969$. Checking the multiples of $3$ from $1971$ to $2007$ leaves $1977$, $1980$, $1983$ and $2001$: $4$ values, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-1": ["Susan pays $4\\cdot20\\cdot0.75=60$ and Pam pays $5\\cdot20\\cdot0.7=70$, a difference of $10$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-2": ["The brick is fully under water and displaces $40\\cdot20\\cdot10=8000$ cubic cm. Spread over the $100\\cdot40=4000$ base, the water rises $2$ cm, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-3": ["With the smaller $n$, $n+2=3n$ gives $n=1$, so the integers are $1$ and $3$, with sum $4$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-4": ["She rode $16\\cdot\\tfrac12=8$ miles and walked $4\\cdot\\tfrac32=6$ miles, $14$ miles in $2$ hours. The average speed is $7$ mph, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-5": ["Federal tax takes $0.2x$ and state tax $0.1\\cdot0.8x=0.08x$, so $0.28x=10{,}500$ and $x=37{,}500$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-6": ["In triangle $ABC$ the base angles are $\\tfrac{180^\\circ-40^\\circ}2=70^\\circ$, and in triangle $ADC$ they are $\\tfrac{180^\\circ-140^\\circ}2=20^\\circ$. So $\\angle BAD=70^\\circ-20^\\circ=50^\\circ$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-7": ["Five consecutive terms of an arithmetic sequence average to the middle one, so $c=\\tfrac{30}5=6$; the others depend on the unknown difference. Only $c$ can be found, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-8": ["At the vertex at $5$, the two chords go to $12$ and to $10$. The angle between them is inscribed in the circle and cuts off the arc from $10$ to $12$, which is $2$ of the $12$ hours, $60^\\circ$. So the vertex angle is $30^\\circ$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-9": ["Let $h$ and $d$ be his distances from home and the stadium. Equal times give $d=h+\\tfrac{h+d}7$, so $7d=8h+d$ and $\\tfrac hd=\\tfrac68=\\tfrac34$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-10": ["A $3$-$4$-$5$ triangle is right, so its hypotenuse is the diameter $6$. The legs are $3.6$ and $4.8$, and the area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot3.6\\cdot4.8=8.64$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-11": ["The digits run around in a cycle, so each digit shows up once as a hundreds digit, once as a tens digit and once as a units digit. So $S$ is $111=3\\cdot37$ times the sum of all the hundreds digits, and $37$ always divides it, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-12": ["Half the numbers are even, so a product $ad$ is odd with probability $\\tfrac14$ and even with $\\tfrac34$, and the same for $bc$. The difference is even when the two products have the same parity: $\\left(\\tfrac34\\right)^2+\\left(\\tfrac14\\right)^2=\\tfrac58$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-13": ["The mouse is closest to the cheese at the foot of the perpendicular from $(12,10)$ to its line. Moving along the line from $(4,-2)$ by $t(1,-5)$, the vector to the cheese $(8-t,12+5t)$ is perpendicular to $(1,-5)$ when $8-t-60-25t=0$, that is $t=-2$. So the turning point is $(2,8)$ and $a+b=10$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-14": ["The five numbers $6-a,\\ldots,6-e$ are distinct integers with product $45=3^2\\cdot5$. Distinct factors force them to be $1,-1,3,-3,5$ (the $9$ cannot be used, since the remaining factors would repeat), whose sum is $5$. So $30-(a+b+c+d+e)=5$ and the sum is $25$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-15": ["The total is $28+n$, so the mean is $\\tfrac{28+n}5$. If $n\\le6$ the median is $6$, giving $n=2$; if $6\\lt n\\lt9$ the median is $n$, giving $28+n=5n$ and $n=7$; if $n\\ge9$ the median is $9$, giving $n=17$. Each fits its case, so the sum is $2+7+17=26$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-16": ["The digits form a three-term arithmetic progression with difference $d\\ge1$: there are $10-2d$ such sets for $d=1,2,3,4$, $20$ in all, of which $4$ contain $0$. A set without $0$ gives $3!=6$ numbers and a set with $0$ gives $4$ (the $0$ cannot lead). The total is $16\\cdot6+4\\cdot4=112$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-17": ["Square and add: $(\\sin a+\\sin b)^2+(\\cos a+\\cos b)^2=2+2(\\cos a\\cos b+\\sin a\\sin b)=2+2\\cos(a-b)$. The left side is $\\tfrac53+1=\\tfrac83$, so $\\cos(a-b)=\\tfrac13$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-18": ["Real coefficients bring the conjugates $-2i$ and $2-i$, so $f(x)=(x^2+4)(x^2-4x+5)$. Then $a+b+c+d=f(1)-1=5\\cdot2-1=9$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-19": ["Triangle $ABC$ has base $BC=223$ on the $x$-axis, so $A$ is at height $\\pm\\tfrac{2\\cdot2007}{223}=\\pm18$. Line $DE$ is $y=x-300$ with $DE=9\\sqrt2$, so $A$ is $\\tfrac{2\\cdot7002}{9\\sqrt2}=\\tfrac{1556}{\\sqrt2}$ from it: $\\lvert x-y-300\\rvert=1556$. The four solutions have $x=y+300\\pm1556$ with $y=\\pm18$, and their sum is $4\\cdot300=1200$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-20": ["Each face keeps a regular octagon of side $s$ whose corners are cut by isosceles right triangles with legs $\\tfrac s{\\sqrt2}$, so $1=s+\\sqrt2\\,s$ and $s=\\sqrt2-1$; the legs are $\\ell=1-\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}$. Each removed corner is a tetrahedron with three perpendicular edges $\\ell$, of volume $\\tfrac{\\ell^3}6$, so the total is $\\tfrac86\\ell^3=\\tfrac{(2-\\sqrt2)^3}6=\\tfrac{20-14\\sqrt2}6=\\tfrac{10-7\\sqrt2}3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-21": ["By Vieta the sum and product of the zeros are $-\\tfrac ba$ and $\\tfrac ca$; equal means $c=-b$. Then the sum of the coefficients is $a+b+c=a$, so the common value is $a$, the coefficient of $x^2$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-22": ["Mod $9$, $n$, $S(n)$ and $S(S(n))$ agree, so $3n\\equiv2007\\equiv0$ and $n\\equiv0\\pmod3$. Also $S(n)\\le28$ and $S(S(n))\\le10$, so $n\\ge1969$. Checking the multiples of $3$ from $1971$ to $2007$ leaves $1977$, $1980$, $1983$ and $2001$: $4$ values, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-23": ["Let $B=(t,2\\log_a t)$. Then $A$ has the same height, so $\\log_a x_A=2\\log_a t$ and $x_A=t^2$; the side $AB$ gives $t^2-t=6$, so $t=3$. The vertex $C$ lies directly above $B$ on $y=3\\log_a x$, so $BC=3\\log_a3-2\\log_a3=\\log_a3=6$. Hence $a^6=3$ and $a=\\sqrt[6]{3}$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-24": ["$\\sin x=\\sin nx$ means $nx=x+2k\\pi$ or $nx=\\pi-x+2k\\pi$, that is $x=\\tfrac{2k\\pi}{n-1}$ or $x=\\tfrac{(2k+1)\\pi}{n+1}$. In $[0,\\pi]$ the first family has $\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}2\\rfloor+1$ points and the second $\\lfloor\\tfrac n2\\rfloor+1$, together $n+1$. The two families share a point only at $x=\\tfrac\\pi2$, which lies in both exactly when $n\\equiv1\\pmod4$. So $F(n)=n+1$, less $1$ when $n\\equiv1\\pmod4$. The sum is $(3+4+\\cdots+2008)-501=2017033-501=2016532$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12a-25": ["Let $s_n$ count the spacy subsets of $\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$. Either $n$ is left out ($s_{n-1}$ ways) or $n$ is in and $n-1$, $n-2$ are out ($s_{n-3}$ ways), so $s_n=s_{n-1}+s_{n-3}$. From $s_1=2$, $s_2=3$, $s_3=4$: $6,9,13,19,28,41,60,88,129$. So $s_{12}=129$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-1": ["Each room has walls $2(12\\cdot8)+2(10\\cdot8)=352$ square feet, $292$ after the openings. Three rooms need $876$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-2": ["$3\\star5=8\\cdot5=40$ and $5\\star3=8\\cdot3=24$, so the difference is $16$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-3": ["The trips use $\\tfrac{120}{30}=4$ and $\\tfrac{120}{20}=6$ gallons, so $240$ miles take $10$ gallons, $24$ miles per gallon, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-4": ["The central angle $AOC$ is $360^\\circ-120^\\circ-140^\\circ=100^\\circ$, and the inscribed angle $ABC$ on the same arc is half of it, $50^\\circ$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-5": ["The facts chain together: Crups are Dramps, Dramps are Arogs, and Arogs are Brafs. So all Crups are Arogs and Brafs, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-6": ["The blanks give $4.5$ points, so she needs $6c\\ge95.5$, that is $c\\ge16$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-7": ["With right angles at $A$ and $B$ and equal sides, $ABCE$ is a square, so $CE$ equals the side and $CDE$ is equilateral. Then $\\angle E=90^\\circ+60^\\circ=150^\\circ$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-8": ["The digits are fixed by $a\\lt c$ with $a$ and $c$ of the same parity, $b=\\tfrac{a+c}2$. That is $\\binom52$ even pairs plus $\\binom52$ odd pairs, $20$ in all, and $b\\ge1$ so none starts with $0$. Choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-9": ["The message has $12$ letters s, so the last is shifted $1+2+\\cdots+12=78$ places. Since $78=3\\cdot26$, it comes back to s, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-10": ["Area $1$ with base $BC$ fixes the height at $\\tfrac2{BC}$, so $A$ is on one of the two lines parallel to $BC$ at that distance, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-11": ["The height to the base is $\\sqrt{9-1}=2\\sqrt2$, so the area is $2\\sqrt2$. Then $R=\\tfrac{abc}{4K}=\\tfrac{18}{8\\sqrt2}=\\tfrac9{4\\sqrt2}$, and the area is $\\pi R^2=\\tfrac{81}{32}\\pi$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-12": ["$N$ years ago the children's ages added to $T-3N$, so $T-N=2(T-3N)$. Then $T=5N$ and the ratio is $5$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-13": ["The circles meet at $(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$, and the common chord splits the lens into two halves. Each is a quarter circle of radius $2$ minus a right triangle with legs $2$: $\\pi-2$. The lens has area $2(\\pi-2)$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-14": ["The group size stays $n$. Then $0.4n-2=0.3n$, so $n=20$ and there were $8$ girls, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-15": ["The angles are $A$, $\\tfrac A2$, $\\tfrac A3$, $\\tfrac A4$, adding to $\\tfrac{25}{12}A=360^\\circ$. So $A=172.8^\\circ\\approx173^\\circ$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-16": ["$0.1j+0.9\\cdot83=84$ gives $0.1j=9.3$, so $j=93$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-17": ["By Viviani's Theorem the distances from any interior point of an equilateral triangle to its three sides add up to its height, so the height is $1+2+3=6$. The height of an equilateral triangle is $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$ times its side, so $AB=\\tfrac{2\\cdot6}{\\sqrt3}=4\\sqrt3$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-18": ["The outer centers form a square of side $2r$, each $1+r$ from the middle. Across a diagonal, $2(1+r)=2r\\sqrt2$, so $r=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2-1}=1+\\sqrt2$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-19": ["The wheel shows $1,2,3,6,7,9$. Mod $4$ they give columns $1,2,3$ twice each. Mod $5$ they give rows $1$ and $2$ twice each and rows $3$ and $4$ once. A square is shaded when row and column have the same parity, so the probability is $\\tfrac26\\cdot\\tfrac23+\\tfrac26\\cdot\\tfrac13+\\tfrac16\\cdot\\tfrac23+\\tfrac16\\cdot\\tfrac13=\\tfrac12$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-20": ["Pick the blocks in order: $25$, then $16$ off the first's row and column, then $9$. Ignoring order divides by $3!$: $\\tfrac{25\\cdot16\\cdot9}6=600$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-21": ["The altitude from $B$ to $AC$ is $\\tfrac{3\\cdot4}5=\\tfrac{12}5$. The square's top side $WZ$ cuts off a triangle similar to $ABC$, so $\\tfrac s5=\\tfrac{12/5-s}{12/5}$. Then $s\\left(\\tfrac15+\\tfrac5{12}\\right)=1$ and $s=\\tfrac{60}{37}$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-22": ["The number shows on exactly one die with probability $\\tfrac6{16}$, on both with $\\tfrac1{16}$, and on neither with $\\tfrac9{16}$. The expected return is $\\tfrac6{16}+\\tfrac2{16}-\\tfrac9{16}=-\\tfrac1{16}$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-23": ["Surface area scales with the square of length, so the top pyramid is $\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}$ the size, with height $\\tfrac h{\\sqrt2}$. Then $h-\\tfrac h{\\sqrt2}=2$, so $h=\\tfrac{2\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt2-1}=4+2\\sqrt2$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-24": ["Divisibility by $4$ forces the last two digits $44$, since $49$, $94$, $99$ fail. Divisibility by $9$ needs the number of $4$s to be a multiple of $9$. At least one $9$ is required, so the smallest has nine $4$s and one $9$, placed as far right as the ending allows: $4444444944$, ending in $4944$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-10b-25": ["The sum is $\\tfrac{9a^2+14b^2}{9ab}$. Since $\\gcd(a,b)=1$: $a\\mid14b^2$ gives $a\\mid14$, $b\\mid9a^2$ gives $b\\mid9$, and $9\\mid14b^2$ gives $3\\mid b$. If $b=9$, then $81\\mid9a^2$ puts $3$ in $a$, impossible; so $b=3$. Each $a\\in\\{1,2,7,14\\}$ then works, giving $4$ pairs, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-1": ["Each room has walls $2(12\\cdot8)+2(10\\cdot8)=352$ square feet, $292$ after the openings. Three rooms need $876$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-2": ["The trips use $\\tfrac{120}{30}=4$ and $\\tfrac{120}{20}=6$ gallons, so $240$ miles take $10$ gallons, $24$ miles per gallon, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-3": ["The central angle $AOC$ is $360^\\circ-120^\\circ-140^\\circ=100^\\circ$, and the inscribed angle $ABC$ on the same arc is half of it, $50^\\circ$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-4": ["$18$ bananas cost as much as $12$ apples, and $12$ apples as much as $8$ oranges, choice (B).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-5": ["The blanks give $4.5$ points, so she needs $6c\\ge95.5$, that is $c\\ge16$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-6": ["The perimeter is $18$, so each bug crawls $9$ before they meet. The bug going through $B$ covers $AB=5$ and then $4$ more, so $BD=4$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-7": ["With right angles at $A$ and $B$ and equal sides, $ABCE$ is a square, so $CE$ equals the side and $CDE$ is equilateral. Then $\\angle E=90^\\circ+60^\\circ=150^\\circ$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-8": ["$N$ years ago the children's ages added to $T-3N$, so $T-N=2(T-3N)$. Then $T=5N$ and the ratio is $5$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-9": ["Choose $x$ so that $3x-1=5$, that is $x=2$. Then $f(5)=4+2+1=7$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-10": ["The group size stays $n$. Then $0.4n-2=0.3n$, so $n=20$ and there were $8$ girls, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-11": ["The angles are $A$, $\\tfrac A2$, $\\tfrac A3$, $\\tfrac A4$, adding to $\\tfrac{25}{12}A=360^\\circ$. So $A=172.8^\\circ\\approx173^\\circ$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-12": ["$0.1j+0.9\\cdot83=84$ gives $0.1j=9.3$, so $j=93$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-13": ["The cycle lasts $63$ seconds and the color changes $3$ times in it. Leah sees a change exactly when her interval starts in the $3$ seconds before one of them, which is $9$ seconds of the $63$. The probability is $\\tfrac9{63}=\\tfrac17$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-15": ["The sum is $\\tfrac a{1-r}=7$, and the odd-power terms form a geometric series with sum $\\tfrac{ar}{1-r^2}=3$. Dividing, $\\tfrac r{1+r}=\\tfrac37$, so $r=\\tfrac34$ and $a=7(1-r)=\\tfrac74$. Then $a+r=\\tfrac52$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-16": ["The $12$ rotations are the identity, which fixes all $3^4=81$ colorings; $8$ turns of $120^\\circ$ about a vertex, each fixing $3^2=9$ (the opposite face, and the other three faces alike); and $3$ half-turns about edge midlines, each fixing $3^2=9$ (two pairs of faces swapped). By Burnside's Lemma the count is $\\tfrac{81+72+27}{12}=15$, choice (A).","Alternatively, with only three colors two faces always share a color, so a coloring is fixed by which colors appear how often: any two colorings with the same multiset of four colors can be rotated into each other. There are $\\binom{6}{4}=15$ multisets of size $4$ from $3$ colors, choice (A).",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-17": ["If $a\\gt0$, then $\\log_{10}b=ab^2\\gt0$, so $b\\gt1$; but $\\log_{10}b\\lt b\\lt ab^2$ there, which is impossible. So $a\\lt0$, hence $\\log_{10}b\\lt0$ and $0\\lt b\\lt1\\lt\\tfrac1b$. In order the set is $a,0,b,1,\\tfrac1b$, with median $b$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-18": ["Let the squares be $n^2$ and $(n+1)^2$, a gap of $2n+1$. Then $\\overline{acb}-\\overline{abc}=9(c-b)$ is one third of the gap, so $2n+1=27(c-b)$. Being odd, $2n+1=27$ and $n=13$. The numbers are $169+9=178$ and $169+18=187$, and $a+b+c=16$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-19": ["Taping $\\overline{AB}$ to $\\overline{DC}$ turns $\\overline{AD}$ and $\\overline{BC}$ into the rims, so the base circumference is $6$ and $r=\\tfrac3\\pi$. The height is the distance between $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{DC}$, $6\\sin\\angle ABC$. Then $\\pi\\cdot\\tfrac9{\\pi^2}\\cdot6\\sin\\angle ABC=6$ gives $\\sin\\angle ABC=\\tfrac\\pi9$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-20": ["The lines $y=ax+c$ and $y=ax+d$ cut every vertical line in a segment of length $|d-c|$, and the lines $y=bx+c$ and $y=bx+d$ meet the line $y=ax+c$ at points $\\tfrac{|d-c|}{|a-b|}$ apart horizontally. So the first area is $\\tfrac{(d-c)^2}{|a-b|}=18$ and likewise the second is $\\tfrac{(c+d)^2}{|a-b|}=72$. The ratio gives $c+d=2(d-c)$, so $d=3c$, and then $4c^2=18|a-b|$, that is $2c^2=9|a-b|$. So $3\\mid c$; the smallest choice $c=3$, $d=9$, $|a-b|=2$ with $\\{a,b\\}=\\{1,3\\}$ gives $a+b+c+d=16$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-21": ["In base $3$, $2007=2202100_3$. A palindrome with $k$ digits is fixed by its first $\\lceil k/2\\rceil$ digits, the first nonzero: so $2,2,6,6,18,18$ of them have $1$ to $6$ digits, $52$ in all. Of the seven-digit ones, $27$ start with $1$; those starting $20$ or $21$ number $2\\cdot9=18$; and those starting $22$ must start $220$, giving $2200022$, $2201022$, $2202022$, all below $2202100_3$. The total is $52+27+18+3=100$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-22": ["On each stretch where both particles stay on one side each, the midpoint moves along a segment, so $R$ is a triangle whose corners are the midpoint's positions at the start of each stretch. At the start the particles are at $A$ and the midpoint of $\\overline{BC}$; half a side later at the midpoint of $\\overline{AB}$ and at $C$; then at $B$ and the midpoint of $\\overline{CA}$. So the corners are the midpoints of the three medians. With the centroid $G$, the midpoint of the median from $A$ is $G+\\tfrac14(A-G)$, so $R$ is the image of $\\triangle ABC$ under a homothety of ratio $\\tfrac14$ about $G$, and the area ratio is $\\tfrac1{16}$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-23": ["With legs $a,b$ and hypotenuse $c$, the condition $\\tfrac{ab}2=3(a+b+c)$ gives $c=\\tfrac{ab}6-a-b$. Squaring and using $c^2=a^2+b^2$: $\\tfrac{a^2b^2}{36}-\\tfrac{ab(a+b)}3+2ab=0$, so $ab-12a-12b+72=0$ and $(a-12)(b-12)=72$. Negative factor pairs would need $a,b\\lt12$ with product $72$, that is $(-8,-9)$ or $(-6,-12)$, giving $c\\lt0$ or $b=0$. The $12$ positive divisors of $72$ give $6$ unordered pairs, the triangles $(13,84)$, $(14,48)$, $(15,36)$, $(16,30)$, $(18,24)$, $(20,21)$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-24": ["The sum is $\\tfrac{9a^2+14b^2}{9ab}$. Since $\\gcd(a,b)=1$: $a\\mid14b^2$ gives $a\\mid14$, $b\\mid9a^2$ gives $b\\mid9$, and $9\\mid14b^2$ gives $3\\mid b$. If $b=9$, then $81\\mid9a^2$ puts $3$ in $a$, impossible; so $b=3$. Each $a\\in\\{1,2,7,14\\}$ then works, giving $4$ pairs, choice (A).","",[]],
"2007-amc-12b-25": ["Put $B=(0,0,0)$, $A=(2,0,0)$, $C=(0,2,0)$. Since $\\overline{DE}$ is horizontal, write $\\vec{DE}=2u$ with $u$ a horizontal unit vector. Both $\\vec{CD}$ and $\\vec{EA}$ are perpendicular to $u$, so $\\vec{CA}\\cdot u=(\\vec{CD}+\\vec{DE}+\\vec{EA})\\cdot u=2$. As $|\\vec{CA}|=2\\sqrt2$, $u$ makes $45^\\circ$ with $\\vec{CA}$; take $u=(1,0,0)$ (the other choice, $(0,-1,0)$, is the mirror image). Then $D=(0,y,h)$ and $E=(2,y,h)$, and $CD=EA=2$ give $(y-2)^2+h^2=y^2+h^2=4$, so $y=1$ and $h^2=3$. Now $\\vec{BD}=(0,1,h)$ has length $2$ and is perpendicular to $\\vec{DE}=(2,0,0)$, so $\\triangle BDE$ is right-angled at $D$ with area $\\tfrac12\\cdot2\\cdot2=2$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-1": ["A third of the job took $2$ hours $40$ minutes, so the whole job takes $8$ hours. Starting at $8{:}30$ am, it ends at $4{:}30$ pm, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-2": ["With square side $s$, the rectangle is $2s$ wide and $4s$ long, area $8s^2$. The square is $\\tfrac18=12.5\\%$ of it, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-3": ["Since $\\langle6\\rangle=1+2+3=6$, a perfect number, every application gives $6$ again, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-4": ["$\\tfrac{20}3$ bananas are worth $8$ oranges, so one banana is worth $\\tfrac65$ of an orange. Then $\\tfrac52$ bananas are worth $\\tfrac52\\cdot\\tfrac65=3$ oranges, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-5": ["Each numerator cancels the next denominator, leaving $\\tfrac{2008}4=502$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-6": ["With each leg $d$ long, the time is $d\\left(\\tfrac13+\\tfrac1{20}+\\tfrac1{10}\\right)=\\tfrac{29d}{60}$ for distance $3d$. The average is $\\tfrac{180}{29}\\approx6.2$, closest to $6$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-7": ["Factor out the smallest powers: the top is $3^{4012}(3^4-1)$ and the bottom $3^{4010}(3^4-1)$. The ratio is $3^2=9$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-8": ["With sticker price $p$, store A charges $0.85p-90$ and store B $0.75p$, and A is $15$ cheaper: $0.85p-90=0.75p-15$. So $0.1p=75$ and $p=750$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-9": ["The expression is $\\tfrac{4x-x}6=\\tfrac x2$. It is an integer exactly when $x$ is even, and $x=2$ shows it need not be a multiple of $3$. Choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-10": ["Joining the midpoints of a square's sides gives a square of half the area. So the areas go $16$, $8$, $4$, and $S_3$ has area $4$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-11": ["At $4$ miles per hour the mile takes $15$ minutes. The water may grow by at most $30$ gallons in that time, a net $2$ gallons per minute, so LeRoy must bail at least $10-2=8$ gallons per minute, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-12": ["Blue is $\\tfrac r{1.25}=0.8r$ and green is $1.6r$, so the total is $0.8r+r+1.6r=3.4r$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-13": ["Together they paint $\\tfrac15+\\tfrac17$ of the room per hour, and they work for $t-1$ of the $t$ hours. So $\\left(\\tfrac15+\\tfrac17\\right)(t-1)=1$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-14": ["A $4:3$ screen with diagonal $27$ is a $3$-$4$-$5$ triangle scaled by $5.4$: $21.6$ wide and $16.2$ tall. The $2:1$ movie fills the width, so it is $10.8$ tall, and each strip is $\\tfrac{16.2-10.8}2=2.7$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-15": ["Say Ian drove $t$ hours at $s$. Han's extra distance is $(t+1)(s+5)-ts=5t+s+5=70$, so $s+5t=65$. Jan's extra is $(t+2)(s+10)-ts=10t+2s+20=2\\cdot65+20=150$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-16": ["The small circle's center is on the bisector, $30^\\circ$ from each side, so its distance from $O$ is twice its radius $r$. Internal tangency then needs $2r+r=R$, so $r=\\tfrac R3$ and the area ratio is $\\tfrac19$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-17": ["The region is a $6\\times3$ rectangle on each side plus a sector at each corner. The exterior angle at each corner is $360^\\circ-90^\\circ-90^\\circ-60^\\circ=120^\\circ$, so the three sectors make one full circle of radius $3$. The area is $3\\cdot18+9\\pi=54+9\\pi$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-18": ["With legs $a,b$ and hypotenuse $c$: $ab=40$ and $a+b=32-c$. Then $(32-c)^2=a^2+b^2+2ab=c^2+80$, so $1024-64c=80$ and $c=\\tfrac{59}4$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-19": ["In the first turn $P$ moves on a circle about $R$ of radius $PR=\\sqrt{2^2+6^2}=2\\sqrt{10}$, a quarter circle of length $\\sqrt{10}\\pi$. In the second it turns about the new $S$, at distance $PS=6$, a quarter circle of length $3\\pi$. The total is $(3+\\sqrt{10})\\pi$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-20": ["Triangles $ABK$ and $CDK$ are similar, so $AK:KC=BK:KD=9:12=3:4$. Triangles sharing a height split area in the ratio of bases: $[DKC]=\\tfrac43\\cdot24=32$, $[ABK]=\\tfrac34\\cdot24=18$, and $[BKC]=[AKD]=24$. The total is $98$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-21": ["All four sides are $\\sqrt{1+\\tfrac14}$, so $ABCD$ is a rhombus. Its diagonals are the space diagonal $AC=\\sqrt3$ and $BD=\\sqrt2$, a face diagonal's length, so the area is $\\tfrac12\\sqrt3\\sqrt2=\\tfrac{\\sqrt6}2$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-22": ["Follow the $8$ coin sequences from $6$ (H doubles and subtracts $1$, T halves and subtracts $1$). The second term is $11$ or $2$. From $11$ the third is $21$ or $\\tfrac92$, and the fourth is $41$, $\\tfrac{19}2$, $8$ or $\\tfrac54$. From $2$ the third is $3$ or $0$, and the fourth is $5$, $\\tfrac12$, $-1$ or $-1$. Five of the eight are integers, $\\tfrac58$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-23": ["Choose the two shared elements, $\\binom52=10$ ways. Each of the other three is in exactly one subset, $2^3=8$ ordered ways, which is $4$ unordered pairs. So $10\\cdot4=40$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-24": ["$2008^2$ ends in $4$, and $2^{2008}$ ends in $6$ since $4\\mid2008$. So $k$ ends in $0$ and is a multiple of $4$. Then $k^2$ ends in $0$ and $2^k$ ends in $6$, so the sum ends in $6$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10a-25": ["The touching inner corners form a regular hexagon with side $x$, so each mat's inner side is $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2x$ from the center and its outer side $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2x+1$. The outer corners are on the table: $\\left(\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2x+1\\right)^2+\\left(\\tfrac x2\\right)^2=16$, so $x^2+\\sqrt3x-15=0$ and $x=\\tfrac{3\\sqrt7-\\sqrt3}2$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-1": ["A third of the job took $2$ hours $40$ minutes, so the whole job takes $8$ hours. Starting at $8{:}30$ am, it ends at $4{:}30$ pm, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-2": ["The sum is $\\tfrac36+\\tfrac46=\\tfrac76$, whose reciprocal is $\\tfrac67$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-3": ["$\\tfrac{20}3$ bananas are worth $8$ oranges, so one banana is worth $\\tfrac65$ of an orange. Then $\\tfrac52$ bananas are worth $\\tfrac52\\cdot\\tfrac65=3$ oranges, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-4": ["Each numerator cancels the next denominator, leaving $\\tfrac{2008}4=502$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-5": ["The expression is $\\tfrac{4x-x}6=\\tfrac x2$. It is an integer exactly when $x$ is even, and $x=2$ shows it need not be a multiple of $3$. Choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-6": ["With sticker price $p$, store A charges $0.85p-90$ and store B $0.75p$, and A is $15$ cheaper: $0.85p-90=0.75p-15$. So $0.1p=75$ and $p=750$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-7": ["At $4$ miles per hour the mile takes $15$ minutes. The water may grow by at most $30$ gallons in that time, a net $2$ gallons per minute, so LeRoy must bail at least $10-2=8$ gallons per minute, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-8": ["The unit cube has surface area $6$, so the new cube has surface area $12$, faces of area $2$ and edge $\\sqrt2$. Its volume is $(\\sqrt2)^3=2\\sqrt2$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-9": ["A $4:3$ screen with diagonal $27$ is a $3$-$4$-$5$ triangle scaled by $5.4$: $21.6$ wide and $16.2$ tall. The $2:1$ movie fills the width, so it is $10.8$ tall, and each strip is $\\tfrac{16.2-10.8}2=2.7$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-10": ["Together they paint $\\tfrac15+\\tfrac17$ of the room per hour, and they work for $t-1$ of the $t$ hours. So $\\left(\\tfrac15+\\tfrac17\\right)(t-1)=1$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-11": ["Folding the net, the opposite faces are $1$ and $32$, $2$ and $16$, and $4$ and $8$; each cube's faces add to $63$. The two lower cubes show only four side faces, so hide the opposite pair with the smallest sum, $4+8$, leaving $51$ each. The top cube shows five faces, so hide the $1$, leaving $62$. The greatest sum is $51+51+62=164$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-12": ["$g$ is defined when $x+1$ is in $[0,2]$, that is on $[-1,1]$. As $f$ takes every value in $[0,1]$, $1-f$ takes every value in $[0,1]$ too. So the domain is $[-1,1]$ and the range $[0,1]$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-13": ["The small circle's center is on the bisector, $30^\\circ$ from each side, so its distance from $O$ is twice its radius $r$. Internal tangency then needs $2r+r=R$, so $r=\\tfrac R3$ and the area ratio is $\\tfrac19$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-14": ["Set $u=3x-18$ and $v=2y+7$. The region $\\lvert u\\rvert+\\lvert v\\rvert\\le3$ is a square with diagonals $6$, of area $18$. The change of variables stretches areas by $3\\cdot2=6$, so the original region has area $\\tfrac{18}6=3$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-15": ["$2008^2$ ends in $4$, and $2^{2008}$ ends in $6$ since $4\\mid2008$. So $k$ ends in $0$ and is a multiple of $4$. Then $k^2$ ends in $0$ and $2^k$ ends in $6$, so the sum ends in $6$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-16": ["With $A=\\log a$ and $B=\\log b$ the terms are $3A+7B$, $5A+12B$ and $8A+15B$. Equal differences give $2A+5B=3A+3B$, so $A=2B$, and the terms become $13B$, $22B$, $31B$ with difference $9B$. The $12$th term is $13B+11\\cdot9B=112B=\\log b^{112}$, so $n=112$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-17": ["If $a_1$ is even, $a_2=\\tfrac{a_1}2\\lt a_1$. If $a_1$ is odd, $a_2=3a_1+1$ and $a_3=\\tfrac{3a_1+1}2$ both exceed $a_1$; then $a_4$ is $\\tfrac{3a_1+1}4\\lt a_1$ when $a_3$ is even, and $3a_3+1\\gt a_1$ when $a_3$ is odd. So we need $\\tfrac{3a_1+1}2$ odd, that is $a_1\\equiv3\\pmod4$, which holds for $502$ numbers up to $2008$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-18": ["Let the vertices be $(x,0,0)$, $(0,y,0)$, $(0,0,z)$. The Pythagorean Theorem in the three coordinate planes gives $x^2+y^2=25$, $y^2+z^2=36$ and $z^2+x^2=49$; adding, the sum of squares is $55$, so $x^2=19$, $y^2=6$, $z^2=30$. The tetrahedron has three perpendicular edges at $O$, so its volume is $\\tfrac{xyz}6=\\tfrac{\\sqrt{19\\cdot6\\cdot30}}6=\\sqrt{95}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-19": ["The coefficient counts solutions of $a+b+c=28$ with $0\\le a\\le27$ and $0\\le b,c\\le14$. For each $b,c$ with $b+c\\ge1$ there is exactly one valid $a=28-b-c$, and $b+c=0$ would need $a=28$. So the count is $15\\cdot15-1=224$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-20": ["The bisector splits $AB$ in the ratio $3:4$: $AD=\\tfrac{15}7$, $DB=\\tfrac{20}7$, and its length is $CD=\\tfrac{2\\cdot3\\cdot4\\cos45^\\circ}{7}=\\tfrac{12\\sqrt2}7$. The two parts have areas $\\tfrac37\\cdot6=\\tfrac{18}7$ and $\\tfrac{24}7$, and $r=\\tfrac{\\text{area}}s$ gives $r_a=\\tfrac{36}{36+12\\sqrt2}=\\tfrac3{3+\\sqrt2}$ and $r_b=\\tfrac{48}{48+12\\sqrt2}=\\tfrac4{4+\\sqrt2}$. So $\\tfrac{r_a}{r_b}=\\tfrac{3(4+\\sqrt2)}{4(3+\\sqrt2)}=\\tfrac{3(10-\\sqrt2)}{28}$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-21": ["Swapping the first two entries with the last two turns heavy-tailed permutations into light-tailed ones, so the two kinds are equally many, and $120=2H+T$, where $T$ counts ties $a_1+a_2=a_4+a_5$. A tie needs the four outer numbers to split into two pairs with equal sums: with $3$ in the middle ($1+5=2+4$), $1$ ($2+5=3+4$) or $5$ ($1+4=2+3$), each giving $2\\cdot2\\cdot2=8$ orders, and impossible with $2$ or $4$ in the middle. So $T=24$ and $H=48$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-22": ["The touching inner corners form a regular hexagon with side $x$, so each mat's inner side is $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2x$ from the center and its outer side $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2x+1$. The outer corners are on the table: $\\left(\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2x+1\\right)^2+\\left(\\tfrac x2\\right)^2=16$, so $x^2+\\sqrt3x-15=0$ and $x=\\tfrac{3\\sqrt7-\\sqrt3}2$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-23": ["The left side is $(z+i)^4-1-i$ by the binomial theorem, so the equation is $(z+i)^4=1+i$. The solutions are $-i$ plus the four fourth roots of $1+i=\\sqrt2\\,e^{i\\pi/4}$, which form a square with circumradius $2^{1/8}$. Its area is $2R^2=2\\cdot2^{1/4}=2^{5/4}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-24": ["Put $C$ at the origin with $B=(4,0)$ and $D=(2,0)$; $A$ moves along the ray at $60^\\circ$. The angle $BAD$ is largest when the circle through $B$ and $D$ is tangent to that ray at $A$, and the power of $C$ gives $CA^2=CD\\cdot CB=8$, so $A=(\\sqrt2,\\sqrt6)$. With $\\vec{AB}=(4-\\sqrt2,-\\sqrt6)$ and $\\vec{AD}=(2-\\sqrt2,-\\sqrt6)$, $\\tan\\angle BAD=\\tfrac{\\lvert\\text{cross}\\rvert}{\\text{dot}}=\\tfrac{2\\sqrt6}{16-6\\sqrt2}=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{4\\sqrt2-3}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12a-25": ["With $z_n=a_n+b_ni$, the rule is $z_{n+1}=(\\sqrt3+i)z_n=2e^{i\\pi/6}z_n$. So $z_{100}=2^{99}e^{i\\cdot99\\pi/6}z_1$, and $\\tfrac{99\\pi}6=16\\pi+\\tfrac\\pi2$, so $z_{100}=2^{99}\\,i\\,z_1$. Then $z_1=\\tfrac{2+4i}{2^{99}i}=\\tfrac{4-2i}{2^{99}}$, and $a_1+b_1=\\tfrac2{2^{99}}=\\tfrac1{2^{98}}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-1": ["With $k$ three-pointers the total is $2\\cdot5+k=10+k$, and $k$ can be $0$ to $5$. That gives $6$ totals, choice (E).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-2": ["After the reversals the rows are $1,2,3,4$; $11,10,9,8$; $15,16,17,18$; $25,24,23,22$. The diagonals add to $1+10+17+22=50$ and $4+9+16+25=54$, a difference of $4$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-3": ["$x\\sqrt x=x^{3/2}$, and its cube root is $x^{1/2}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-4": ["Pay the other $20$ players the minimum, $20\\cdot15{,}000=300{,}000$. That leaves $400{,}000$ for one player, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-5": ["$(x-y)^2$ and $(y-x)^2$ are equal, so their difference is $0$ and its square is $0$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-6": ["$AB=\\tfrac45AD$ and $AC=\\tfrac9{10}AD$, so $BC=\\left(\\tfrac9{10}-\\tfrac45\\right)AD=\\tfrac1{10}AD$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-7": ["Scaling by $10$ multiplies area by $100$, so it takes $100$ small triangles, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-8": ["With $r$ roses, $3r+2c=50$ needs $r$ even, from $0$ to $16$. That is $9$ bouquets, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-9": ["By Vieta the solutions add to $\\tfrac{2a}a=2$, so their average is $1$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-10": ["The center is $\\sqrt{25-9}=4$ from the chord's midpoint $M$, so $C$ is $5-4=1$ beyond $M$. Then $AC=\\sqrt{3^2+1^2}=\\sqrt{10}$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-11": ["Let $u_4=x$. Then $u_5=2x+9$ and $u_6=2(2x+9)+x=5x+18=128$, so $x=22$ and $u_5=53$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-12": ["He walked $44\\cdot100{,}000+50{,}000=4{,}450{,}000$ steps, which is $\\tfrac{4{,}450{,}000}{1800}\\approx2472$ miles, closest to $2500$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-13": ["The first $n$ terms add to $n\\cdot n=n^2$, so the $n$th term is $n^2-(n-1)^2=2n-1$. The $2008$th is $4015$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-14": ["With the $30^\\circ$ angle at $O$, $AB=\\tfrac5{\\sqrt3}$, so $A=\\left(5,\\tfrac{5\\sqrt3}3\\right)$. A $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise turn sends $(x,y)$ to $(-y,x)$, giving $\\left(-\\tfrac53\\sqrt3,5\\right)$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-15": ["$a^2=(b+1)^2-b^2=2b+1$, so $a$ is odd and $b=\\tfrac{a^2-1}2\\lt100$ needs $a^2\\lt201$. Then $a=3,5,7,9,11,13$, which is $6$ triangles, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-16": ["With one head (probability $\\tfrac12$) the sum is odd half the time. With two heads (probability $\\tfrac14$) it is odd half the time too. With none the sum $0$ is even. So the probability is $\\tfrac14+\\tfrac18=\\tfrac38$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-17": ["Exactly one approval in three picks has probability $\\binom31(0.7)(0.3)^2=0.189$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-18": ["With $N$ bricks, they lay $\\tfrac N9+\\tfrac N{10}-10$ per hour together, and $5$ hours finish the job: $5\\left(\\tfrac{19N}{90}-10\\right)=N$. So $\\tfrac{5N}{90}=50$ and $N=900$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-19": ["The water's cross section is a segment of the radius-$4$ circle cut by a chord $2$ from the center. Then $\\cos\\theta=\\tfrac24$ gives a central angle of $120^\\circ$, and the segment is $\\tfrac{16\\pi}3-\\tfrac12\\cdot16\\sin120^\\circ=\\tfrac{16\\pi}3-4\\sqrt3$. Times the length $9$ it gives $48\\pi-36\\sqrt3$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-20": ["Each die is odd on half its faces, so the sum is odd in $18$ of the $36$ outcomes. The odd sums other than $5$, $7$, $9$ are $3=2+1$ and $11=3+8$, each in $2$ outcomes. That leaves $14$, probability $\\tfrac7{18}$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-21": ["The men take the odd or the even chairs, $2$ ways, and arrange themselves there in $5!=120$ ways. Each woman then has to avoid her husband's two neighbors and the chair across from him, all on the women's side, leaving $2$ chairs each. Those choices link up in a single cycle, so they fit together in $2$ ways. The total is $2\\cdot120\\cdot2=480$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-22": ["Of the $\\tfrac{6!}{3!\\,2!}=60$ orders, the reds need three separated places: $\\{1,3,5\\}$, $\\{2,4,6\\}$, $\\{1,3,6\\}$ or $\\{1,4,6\\}$. The white and blue beads then fill the rest with the whites apart in $3$, $3$, $2$ and $2$ ways. That is $10$ orders, probability $\\tfrac16$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-23": ["The painted part is $(a-2)(b-2)=\\tfrac{ab}2$, so $ab-4a-4b+8=0$ and $(a-4)(b-4)=8$. With $b\\gt a$ this gives $(5,12)$ and $(6,8)$, so $2$ pairs, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-24": ["Build equilateral triangle $BCE$ inside the quadrilateral. Then $BE=BA$ with $\\angle ABE=70^\\circ-60^\\circ=10^\\circ$, so $\\angle BAE=\\angle BEA=85^\\circ$. Also $CE=CD$ with $\\angle ECD=170^\\circ-60^\\circ=110^\\circ$, so $\\angle CED=35^\\circ$. At $E$, $85^\\circ+60^\\circ+35^\\circ=180^\\circ$, so $A$, $E$, $D$ are collinear and $\\angle BAD=85^\\circ$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-10b-25": ["Track how far the truck is ahead. Each $50$-second cycle it drives $20$ seconds, gaining $100$ feet, then waits $30$, losing $150$. At the starts of the cycles the gap is $200,150,100,50,0$, the first meeting. In the next cycle the gap goes up to $100$ and back down through $0$ (second meeting) to $-50$; then up through $0$ (third) to $50$ and down through $0$ (fourth) to $-100$; then up to $0$ (fifth) and down to $-150$. After that the truck never catches up again, so they meet $5$ times, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-1": ["With $k$ three-pointers the total is $2\\cdot5+k=10+k$, and $k$ can be $0$ to $5$. That gives $6$ totals, choice (E).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-2": ["After the reversals the rows are $1,2,3,4$; $11,10,9,8$; $15,16,17,18$; $25,24,23,22$. The diagonals add to $1+10+17+22=50$ and $4+9+16+25=54$, a difference of $4$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-3": ["Pay the other $20$ players the minimum, $20\\cdot15{,}000=300{,}000$. That leaves $400{,}000$ for one player, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-4": ["The angles along the diameter add to $180^\\circ$, so $\\angle COD=180^\\circ-30^\\circ-45^\\circ=105^\\circ$. The sector is $\\tfrac{105}{360}=\\tfrac7{24}$ of the circle, choice (D).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-5": ["With $r$ roses, $3r+2c=50$ needs $r$ even, from $0$ to $16$. That is $9$ bouquets, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-6": ["He walked $44\\cdot100{,}000+50{,}000=4{,}450{,}000$ steps, which is $\\tfrac{4{,}450{,}000}{1800}\\approx2472$ miles, closest to $2500$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-7": ["$(x-y)^2$ and $(y-x)^2$ are equal, so their difference is $0$ and its square is $0$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-8": ["$AB=\\tfrac45AD$ and $AC=\\tfrac9{10}AD$, so $BC=\\left(\\tfrac9{10}-\\tfrac45\\right)AD=\\tfrac1{10}AD$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-9": ["The center is $\\sqrt{25-9}=4$ from the chord's midpoint $M$, so $C$ is $5-4=1$ beyond $M$. Then $AC=\\sqrt{3^2+1^2}=\\sqrt{10}$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-10": ["With $N$ bricks, they lay $\\tfrac N9+\\tfrac N{10}-10$ per hour together, and $5$ hours finish the job: $5\\left(\\tfrac{19N}{90}-10\\right)=N$. So $\\tfrac{5N}{90}=50$ and $N=900$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-11": ["The part above water is a cone similar to the whole mountain with $\\tfrac18$ of its volume, so the scale factor is $\\sqrt[3]{1/8}=\\tfrac12$ and its height is $4000$ feet. The ocean is $8000-4000=4000$ feet deep, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-12": ["The first $n$ terms add to $n\\cdot n=n^2$, so the $n$th term is $n^2-(n-1)^2=2n-1$. The $2008$th is $4015$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-13": ["Put $A=(0,0)$, $B=(1,0)$, $D=(0,1)$, so the distance from $AD$ is $x$ and $E=\\left(\\tfrac12,\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2\\right)$. The strip $\\tfrac13\\le x\\le\\tfrac23$ has area $\\tfrac13$. The triangle's height over $x$ is $\\sqrt3x$ up to $\\tfrac12$ and symmetric after, so its part in the strip is two trapezoids of width $\\tfrac16$ with parallel sides $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}3$ and $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$, of area $2\\cdot\\tfrac16\\cdot\\tfrac12\\left(\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}3+\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2\\right)=\\tfrac{5\\sqrt3}{36}$. So $R$ has area $\\tfrac13-\\tfrac{5\\sqrt3}{36}=\\tfrac{12-5\\sqrt3}{36}$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-14": ["The circumference is $2\\pi$ times the radius: $2\\pi\\cdot2\\log_{10}a=4\\log_{10}b$. So $\\tfrac{\\log_{10}b}{\\log_{10}a}=\\pi$, which is $\\log_ab=\\pi$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-15": ["Around each corner of the square sit the square ($90^\\circ$), the two first-layer triangles ($60^\\circ$ each) and two second-layer triangles ($60^\\circ$ each), which leaves a $30^\\circ$ gap between two unit sides. Those four gaps are exactly what $S$ adds, since elsewhere the outline is already convex. Each gap is an isosceles triangle with area $\\tfrac12\\cdot1\\cdot1\\cdot\\sin30^\\circ=\\tfrac14$, so the total is $1$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-16": ["The painted part is $(a-2)(b-2)=\\tfrac{ab}2$, so $ab-4a-4b+8=0$ and $(a-4)(b-4)=8$. With $b\\gt a$ this gives $(5,12)$ and $(6,8)$, so $2$ pairs, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-17": ["Let $A=(-a,a^2)$, $B=(a,a^2)$ and $C=(c,c^2)$. The right angle must be at $C$ (at $A$ or $B$ it would force $C$ to coincide with them), so $\\vec{CA}\\cdot\\vec{CB}=(c^2-a^2)+(c^2-a^2)^2=0$, giving $c^2=a^2-1$. The height from $C$ to $AB$ is then $1$, so the area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot2a\\cdot1=a=2008$. So $C$ has $y$-coordinate $2008^2-1=4032063$, with digit sum $18$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-18": ["The base has side $14$, so the faces $ABE$ and $CDE$ have heights $\\tfrac{2\\cdot105}{14}=15$ and $\\tfrac{2\\cdot91}{14}=13$. Cut through $E$ perpendicular to $AB$: the section is a triangle with sides $15$, $13$ and $14$, a $13$-$14$-$15$ triangle of area $84$, so its height over the side $14$, the pyramid's height, is $12$. The volume is $\\tfrac13\\cdot196\\cdot12=784$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-19": ["Write $\\alpha=a+bi$ and $\\gamma=c+di$. Then $\\operatorname{Im}f(1)=1+b+d=0$ and $\\operatorname{Im}f(i)=-1+a+d=0$, so $a=1-d$ and $b=-1-d$. Now $\\lvert\\alpha\\rvert=\\sqrt{(1-d)^2+(1+d)^2}=\\sqrt{2+2d^2}\\ge\\sqrt2$ and $\\lvert\\gamma\\rvert\\ge\\lvert d\\rvert\\ge0$, both minimized at $d=0$, $c=0$. The minimum is $\\sqrt2$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-20": ["Track how far the truck is ahead. Each $50$-second cycle it drives $20$ seconds, gaining $100$ feet, then waits $30$, losing $150$. At the starts of the cycles the gap is $200,150,100,50,0$, the first meeting. In the next cycle the gap goes up to $100$ and back down through $0$ (second meeting) to $-50$; then up through $0$ (third) to $50$ and down through $0$ (fourth) to $-100$; then up to $0$ (fifth) and down to $-150$. After that the truck never catches up again, so they meet $5$ times, choice (B).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-21": ["With centers $(x,0)$ and $(y,1)$, the unit circles meet when the centers are at most $2$ apart: $(x-y)^2+1\\le4$, that is $\\lvert x-y\\rvert\\le\\sqrt3$. In the $2\\times2$ square of choices, the excluded corners are two triangles with legs $2-\\sqrt3$, of total area $(2-\\sqrt3)^2=7-4\\sqrt3$. The probability is $1-\\tfrac{7-4\\sqrt3}4=\\tfrac{4\\sqrt3-3}4$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-22": ["The $4$ empty spaces are a random $4$-subset of the $16$, and she cannot park exactly when no two of them are adjacent. Non-adjacent $4$-subsets of $16$ spaces number $\\binom{13}4=715$, out of $\\binom{16}4=1820$. So she can park with probability $1-\\tfrac{715}{1820}=1-\\tfrac{11}{28}=\\tfrac{17}{28}$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-23": ["$10^n$ has $(n+1)^2$ divisors, which pair up as $d$ and $\\tfrac{10^n}d$, so their product is $10^{n(n+1)^2/2}$ and the sum of the logarithms is $\\tfrac{n(n+1)^2}2=792$. Then $n(n+1)^2=1584=11\\cdot12^2$, so $n=11$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-24": ["Let triangle $n$ have side $s_n$ and $x_n=A_0A_n$. Its apex $B_n$ is at $\\left(x_{n-1}+\\tfrac{s_n}2,\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2s_n\\right)$ on $y=\\sqrt x$, so $\\tfrac34s_n^2=x_{n-1}+\\tfrac{s_n}2$. From $x_0=0$ this gives $s_1=\\tfrac23$, and inductively $s_n=\\tfrac{2n}3$ with $x_n=\\tfrac{n(n+1)}3$. So $A_0A_n\\ge100$ needs $n(n+1)\\ge300$, first true at $n=17$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2008-amc-12b-25": ["First the height: the horizontal pieces of the legs add to $19-11=8$, and $49-x^2=25-(8-x)^2$ gives $x=\\tfrac{11}2$, so $h=\\tfrac{5\\sqrt3}2$ and the trapezoid has area $15h$. The bisectors at $A$ and $D$ meet at $P$, which is equally far from $AB$, $AD$ and $CD$, hence $\\tfrac h2$ from $AD$; so triangle $APD$ has area $\\tfrac12\\cdot7\\cdot\\tfrac h2$, and likewise $BQC$ has $\\tfrac12\\cdot5\\cdot\\tfrac h2$. The hexagon is $15h-3h=12h=30\\sqrt3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-1": ["Since $10\\cdot12=120\\lt128\\le132=11\\cdot12$, it takes $11$ cans, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-2": ["A total of $15$ cannot use a quarter. With a dime, the other three coins would add to $5$, but three pennies and nickels give $3$, $7$, $11$ or $15$. Without one, $a$ nickels and $4-a$ pennies give $4a+4=15$, impossible. The others work: $25=10+5+5+5$, $35=10+10+10+5$, $45=25+10+5+5$, $55=25+10+10+10$. Choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-3": ["From the inside: $1+1=2$, then $1+\\tfrac12=\\tfrac32$, then $1+\\tfrac23=\\tfrac53$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-4": ["The swim takes $\\tfrac18$ hour and the run $\\tfrac12$ hour, leaving $2-\\tfrac58=\\tfrac{11}8$ hours for $15$ miles. He needs $15\\div\\tfrac{11}8=\\tfrac{120}{11}$ miles per hour, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-5": ["Squaring $111{,}111{,}111$ adds up shifted copies with no carries: $12345678987654321$. Its digits sum to $2(1+2+\\cdots+8)+9=81$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-6": ["The circle's diameter $4$ is the semicircle's radius, so the semicircle has area $\\tfrac12\\pi\\cdot16=8\\pi$ and the circle $4\\pi$. Half is shaded, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-7": ["Here $2\\%$ is $60\\%$ of the whole milk's fat, so whole milk has $\\tfrac2{0.6}=\\tfrac{10}3$ percent, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-8": ["A senior ticket is $75\\%$ of full price, so full price is $\\$8$ and a child's ticket $\\$4$. He pays $2(6+8+4)=\\$36$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-9": ["With ratio $r$, $ar^2=2009=7^2\\cdot41$. The only square factor above $1$ is $49$, so $r=7$ and $a=41$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-10": ["The altitude to the hypotenuse satisfies $BD^2=AD\\cdot DC=12$, so $BD=2\\sqrt3$. The area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot7\\cdot2\\sqrt3=7\\sqrt3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-11": ["For a cube of side $x$ the new solid has volume $(x+1)(x-1)x=x^3-x$. That is $5$ less when $x=5$, so the cube had volume $125$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-12": ["In triangle $BCD$, $BD\\gt17-5=12$. In triangle $ABD$, $BD\\lt5+9=14$. So $BD=13$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-13": ["Since $12^{mn}=2^{2mn}3^{mn}=(2^m)^{2n}(3^n)^m$, it equals $P^{2n}Q^m$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-14": ["With rectangle sides $x\\gt y$, the outer square has side $x+y$ and the inner one $x-y$. Areas in ratio $4$ mean sides in ratio $2$: $x+y=2(x-y)$, so $x=3y$. The ratio is $3$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-15": ["Going from $F_{n-1}$ to $F_n$ adds a square ring with $n$ diamonds on a side, $4(n-1)$ in all. So $F_n=1+4(1+2+\\cdots+(n-1))=2n^2-2n+1$, which gives $13$ for $n=3$. Then $F_{20}=800-40+1=761$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-16": ["$a-d$ is $\\pm2\\pm3\\pm4$, so $|a-d|$ can be $9$, $5$, $3$ or $1$. The sum is $18$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-17": ["$DB=5$. In right triangle $DBE$ the altitude from $B$ meets $DE$ at $A$, so $DB^2=DA\\cdot DE$ and $DE=\\tfrac{25}3$, which gives $BE=\\sqrt{\\tfrac{625}9-25}=\\tfrac{20}3$. In the same way $DF=\\tfrac{25}4$ and $BF=\\tfrac{15}4$. So $EF=\\tfrac{20}3+\\tfrac{15}4=\\tfrac{125}{12}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-18": ["Soccer players who swim are $0.4\\cdot60\\%=24\\%$ of the camp, so $36\\%$ play soccer and do not swim. The non-swimmers are $70\\%$, so the share is $\\tfrac{36}{70}\\approx51\\%$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-19": ["Rolling once around, circle $B$ travels $200\\pi$ along circle $A$. It ends on the same point of its own rim when that is a whole number of its circumferences $2\\pi r$, that is $r\\mid100$. Of the $9$ divisors of $100$, all but $100$ itself are below $100$, so there are $8$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-20": ["Their speeds add to $1$ km per minute with Andrea $3$ times as fast, so Lauren goes $\\tfrac14$ km per minute. After $5$ minutes $15$ km remain, which Lauren covers alone in $60$ minutes. The total is $65$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-21": ["Take the big radius $1$ and the small radius $r$. The small centers form a square of side $2r$ on a circle of radius $1-r$, so $2r=\\sqrt2(1-r)$ and $r=\\sqrt2-1$. The four small circles over the big one give $4r^2=4(3-2\\sqrt2)$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-22": ["The two top faces show two different tiles of the twelve, and every ordered pair of tiles is equally likely. Whatever the first shows, $v$, the sum is $7$ when the second shows $7-v\\ne v$, which $2$ of the other $11$ tiles do. The probability is $\\tfrac2{11}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-23": ["Adding triangle $ABE$ to both equal areas gives $[ABD]=[ABC]$, so $C$ and $D$ are equally far from $AB$ and $CD\\parallel AB$. Then triangles $ABE$ and $CDE$ are similar with ratio $9:12$, so $AE=\\tfrac37\\cdot14=6$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-24": ["Of the $\\binom83=56$ triples, a plane misses the inside only when it is a face plane. Each face holds $\\binom43=4$ triples, $24$ in all. So the probability is $1-\\tfrac{24}{56}=\\tfrac47$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10a-25": ["$I_k=10^{k+2}+64=2^{k+2}5^{k+2}+2^6$. For $k+2\\lt6$ the power of $2$ is $k+2\\le5$, and for $k+2\\gt6$ it is $6$. For $k=4$, $I_4=2^6(5^6+1)=2^6\\cdot15626$, and $15626=2\\cdot7813$ adds one more factor. The maximum is $7$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-1": ["From $10{:}34$ to $12{:}34$ is $2$ hours, and to $1{:}18$ is $44$ minutes more. So $h+m=2+44=46$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-2": ["From the inside: $1+1=2$, then $1+\\tfrac12=\\tfrac32$, then $1+\\tfrac23=\\tfrac53$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-3": ["The gap from $\\tfrac14$ to $\\tfrac34$ is $\\tfrac12$, and a third of it is $\\tfrac16$. So the number is $\\tfrac14+\\tfrac16=\\tfrac5{12}$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-4": ["A total of $15$ cannot use a quarter. With a dime, the other three coins would add to $5$, but three pennies and nickels give $3$, $7$, $11$ or $15$. Without one, $a$ nickels and $4-a$ pennies give $4a+4=15$, impossible. The others work: $25=10+5+5+5$, $35=10+10+10+5$, $45=25+10+5+5$, $55=25+10+10+10$. Choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-5": ["For a cube of side $x$ the new solid has volume $(x+1)(x-1)x=x^3-x$. That is $5$ less when $x=5$, so the cube had volume $125$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-6": ["Since $12^{mn}=2^{2mn}3^{mn}=(2^m)^{2n}(3^n)^m$, it equals $P^{2n}Q^m$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-7": ["Equal differences: $(5x-11)-(2x-3)=(3x+1)-(5x-11)$, so $3x-8=12-2x$ and $x=4$. The terms are $5,9,13,\\ldots$ with difference $4$, so $5+4(n-1)=2009$ gives $n=502$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-8": ["With rectangle sides $x\\gt y$, the outer square has side $x+y$ and the inner one $x-y$. Areas in ratio $4$ mean sides in ratio $2$: $x+y=2(x-y)$, so $x=3y$. The ratio is $3$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-9": ["The target $a+b+c$ is $f(1)$, and $1=x+3$ at $x=-2$. So $a+b+c=3\\cdot4-14+4=2$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-10": ["In triangle $BCD$, $BD\\gt17-5=12$. In triangle $ABD$, $BD\\lt5+9=14$. So $BD=13$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-11": ["Going from $F_{n-1}$ to $F_n$ adds a square ring with $n$ diamonds on a side, $4(n-1)$ in all. So $F_n=1+4(1+2+\\cdots+(n-1))=2n^2-2n+1$, which gives $13$ for $n=3$. Then $F_{20}=800-40+1=761$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-12": ["Write $n=100a+10b+c$; then $n=6(a+b+c)$ becomes $94a+4b=5c$. With $c\\le9$ the right side is at most $45$, so $a=0$ and $4b=5c$, giving $b=5$, $c=4$ (or $n=0$). So only $54$ works: $1$ number, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-13": ["If the ship turns through angle $\\theta$, the angle at $B$ in triangle $ABC$ is $180^\\circ-\\theta$, so the Law of Cosines gives $AC^2=100+400+400\\cos\\theta$. For $45^\\circ\\le\\theta\\le60^\\circ$, $\\cos\\theta$ lies between $\\tfrac12$ and $\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2$, so $AC^2$ lies between $700$ and about $783$, inside $[700,800]$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-14": ["The line passes through the vertex $(0,0)$, so it halves the area exactly when it passes through the midpoint of the opposite side, $\\left(\\tfrac{1+6m}2,\\tfrac12\\right)$. That gives $\\tfrac12=m\\cdot\\tfrac{1+6m}2$, that is $6m^2+m-1=0$, with roots $\\tfrac13$ and $-\\tfrac12$ (both give real triangles). By Vieta their sum is $-\\tfrac16$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-15": ["Group the terms in fours using $i^4=1$: terms $4j+1$ to $4j+4$ add to $(4j+1)i-(4j+2)-(4j+3)i+(4j+4)=2-2i$. After $96$ terms the sum is $24(2-2i)=48-48i$, and the next term, $97i^{97}=97i$, brings it to $48+49i$. So $n=97$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-16": ["A circle tangent to both positive axes has center $(r,r)$ and radius $r$. External tangency to the circle of radius $1$ at $(3,0)$ means the centers are $r+1$ apart: $(r-3)^2+r^2=(r+1)^2$, that is $r^2-8r+8=0$. Both roots are positive, and they add to $8$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-17": ["Each series sums to $\\tfrac a{1-r}$, so the condition is $\\tfrac a{1-r}=r$, that is $r^2-r+a=0$. The two different ratios are the two roots of this quadratic, and by Vieta $r_1+r_2=1$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-18": ["$I_k=10^{k+2}+64=2^{k+2}5^{k+2}+2^6$. For $k+2\\lt6$ the power of $2$ is $k+2\\le5$, and for $k+2\\gt6$ it is $6$. For $k=4$, $I_4=2^6(5^6+1)=2^6\\cdot15626$, and $15626=2\\cdot7813$ adds one more factor. The maximum is $7$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-20": ["Adding triangle $ABE$ to both equal areas gives $[ABD]=[ABC]$, so $C$ and $D$ are equally far from $AB$ and $CD\\parallel AB$. Then triangles $ABE$ and $CDE$ are similar with ratio $9:12$, so $AE=\\tfrac37\\cdot14=6$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-21": ["The polynomial is $p(x^4)$, so its zeros are the fourth roots of the zeros of $p$: $12$ in all. A positive real number has exactly $2$ real fourth roots, so $x^4=2009$ and $x^4=9002$ give $2$ real zeros each, and $x^4=2009+9002\\pi i$ gives none. So $12-4=8$ zeros are nonreal, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-22": ["By symmetry the plane passes through the center, halfway between the two parallel faces, and it cuts the six edges that join them at their midpoints. Consecutive midpoints are joined by midsegments of the faces, each half an edge long, so the section is a regular hexagon of side $\\tfrac12$. Its area is $\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}2\\cdot\\tfrac14=\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}8$, and $a+b+c=3+3+8=14$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-23": ["Write $f(x)=a(x-h)^2+k$; then $g(x)=-f(100-x)$ has vertex $(100-h,-k)$, and $g(h)=k$ gives $a(100-2h)^2=-2k$. With $d=\\lvert100-2h\\rvert$, the distance between the two axes, each parabola's roots lie $s=\\sqrt{-k/a}=\\tfrac d{\\sqrt2}$ on each side of its axis. Since $\\tfrac d2\\lt s\\lt d$, the roots interleave, and $x_3-x_2=2s-d=d(\\sqrt2-1)=150$, so $d=150(\\sqrt2+1)$. Then $x_4-x_1=d+2s=d(1+\\sqrt2)=150(3+2\\sqrt2)=450+300\\sqrt2$, and $m+n+p=752$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-24": ["Write $T_n=T(n)$, so $\\log_2T_n=T_{n-1}$. Then $\\log_2B=A\\,T_{2008}$, and $\\log_2\\log_2B=T_{2009}T_{2008}+T_{2007}$; one more log gives $T_{2008}+T_{2007}+\\varepsilon$ and the next gives $T_{2007}+\\varepsilon'$, with small positive extras. From there each $\\log_2$ steps down the tower, so the $2010$th log is $T_1=2$ plus a little, the $2011$th is $1$ plus a little, the $2012$th is a small positive number, and the $2013$th is a negative number, still defined; the $2014$th is not. So $k=2013$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12a-25": ["The recursion is the tangent addition formula: with $a_n=\\tan\\theta_n$, $\\theta_{n+2}=\\theta_n+\\theta_{n+1}$, starting from $\\theta_1=45^\\circ$ and $\\theta_2=30^\\circ$. Work modulo $180^\\circ$: the angles run $45,30,75,105,0,105,105,30,135,165,120,105,45,150,15,165,0,\\ldots$, and since they are $15^\\circ$ times Fibonacci combinations, the pattern repeats with the Fibonacci period $24$ modulo $12$. As $2009\\equiv17\\pmod{24}$, $\\theta_{2009}\\equiv\\theta_{17}=0^\\circ$, so $\\lvert a_{2009}\\rvert=0$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-1": ["With $b$ bagels the cost in cents is $50(5-b)+75b=250+25b$, a multiple of $100$ only when $b=2$ in the range $0$ to $5$. Choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-2": ["The top is $\\tfrac1{12}$ and the bottom $\\tfrac16$, so the value is $\\tfrac12$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-3": ["Three cans covered $30-25=5$ rooms, so $25$ rooms took $5\\cdot3=15$ cans, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-4": ["The two triangles take up the difference $25-15=10$ along the long side, $5$ each, and as isosceles right triangles they are $5$ tall, which is the yard's width. The yard is $25\\times5=125$, and the beds are $2\\cdot\\tfrac{25}2=25$, a fraction $\\tfrac15$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-5": ["Twenty percent less than $60$ is $48$, which is $\\tfrac43$ of the number. So the number is $36$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-6": ["The ages are $k\\cdot t^2=128=2^7$ with $k\\lt t$, all powers of $2$. The choice $t=8$, $k=2$ works ($t=4$ would need $k=8$, too old). The sum is $2+8+8=18$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-7": ["Try every grouping of $2\\times3+4\\times5$: the usual order gives $26$, $2\\times(3+4)\\times5=70$, $(2\\times3+4)\\times5=50$ and $2\\times(3+4\\times5)=46$. So $4$ values, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-8": ["After three months the price is multiplied by $1.2\\cdot0.8\\cdot1.25=1.2$. To return, April multiplies by $\\tfrac1{1.2}=\\tfrac56$, a fall of $16\\tfrac23\\%$, about $17$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-9": ["Triangle $ABC$ is isosceles with $AB=BC$, so $\\angle BCA=\\angle A=\\tfrac52\\angle B$, and $\\angle B+5\\angle B=180^\\circ$ gives $\\angle B=30^\\circ$ and $\\angle BCA=75^\\circ$. The vertical angle $DCE$ is also $75^\\circ$, and $CD=CE$ makes $\\angle D=\\tfrac{180^\\circ-75^\\circ}2=52.5^\\circ$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-10": ["The broken part, of length $5-x$, is the hypotenuse over legs $x$ and $1$: $(5-x)^2=x^2+1$. Then $25-10x=1$ and $x=2.4$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-11": ["The odd count of $5$s puts a $5$ in the middle, and the first three digits are $2,3,5$ in some order, which fixes the rest. There are $3!=6$ palindromes, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-12": ["Every triangle has two vertices on one line and the third on the other, so its height is the distance $h$ between the lines. The base is $1$, $2$ or $3$ on the first line and $1$ on the second, giving areas $\\tfrac h2$, $h$ and $\\tfrac{3h}2$. That is $3$ values, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-13": ["Each full roll lays down the perimeter $3+4+6+3+7=23$ along the axis, side by side in order $AB,BC,CD,DE,EA$. Since $2009=87\\cdot23+8$ and $AB$, $BC$ cover $0$ to $7$, the point lands in $CD$, which covers $7$ to $13$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-14": ["Track the millet just after each quart is added, with $\\tfrac34$ quart of other seed each time. Monday $0.25$, Tuesday $0.25\\cdot0.75+0.25=0.4375$, Wednesday $0.578$, Thursday $0.684$, Friday $0.763$. Friday is the first day the millet beats the $0.75$ of other seed, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-15": ["Subtracting, $\\tfrac16$ of the water weighs $a-b$, so a full bucket of water weighs $6(a-b)$ and the bucket $a-4(a-b)=4b-3a$. The total is $4b-3a+6a-6b=3a-2b$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-16": ["The tangent $BA$ is perpendicular to $OA$, and $BO$ bisects the $60^\\circ$ angle at $B$, so triangle $OAB$ is a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle with $BO=2\\cdot OA=2r$. Then $BD=BO-r=r$, and the ratio is $\\tfrac12$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-17": ["Each side gets area $\\tfrac52$. The part below the line is the triangle with vertices $(a,0)$, $(3,0)$, $(3,3)$ minus the missing unit square at the lower right, so $\\tfrac{3(3-a)}2-1=\\tfrac52$. Then $3-a=\\tfrac73$ and $a=\\tfrac23$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-18": ["Diagonal $AC=10$, so $AM=5$. Right triangle $AME$ shares angle $A$ with triangle $ABC$, so $\\tfrac{ME}{AM}=\\tfrac{BC}{AB}=\\tfrac34$ and $ME=\\tfrac{15}4$. The area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot5\\cdot\\tfrac{15}4=\\tfrac{75}8$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-19": ["The hour is right for $8$ of the $12$ hours (all but $1$, $10$, $11$, $12$), a fraction $\\tfrac23$. The minutes are right when neither digit is $1$: $5$ tens digits and $9$ units digits, $\\tfrac{45}{60}=\\tfrac34$. Together $\\tfrac23\\cdot\\tfrac34=\\tfrac12$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-20": ["The hypotenuse is $AC=\\sqrt5$. The bisector splits $BC=2$ in the ratio $AB:AC=1:\\sqrt5$, so $BD=\\tfrac2{1+\\sqrt5}=\\tfrac{\\sqrt5-1}2$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-21": ["Mod $8$ the powers of $3$ alternate $1,3,1,3,\\ldots$, so each pair adds to $4$. The $2010$ terms make $1005$ pairs, $1005\\cdot4\\equiv4\\pmod8$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-22": ["In the top view, $M=(0,1)$ and the cut runs to the corner $(2,2)$, with direction $(2,1)$. The perpendicular from $(2,0)$ meets it $\\tfrac2{\\sqrt5}$ from $(2,2)$ and has length $\\tfrac4{\\sqrt5}$, so triangle $B$ has area $\\tfrac45$. Then $c=2\\cdot\\tfrac45=\\tfrac85$, and the icing is the top $\\tfrac45$ plus the $2\\times2$ side, $s=\\tfrac{24}5$. So $c+s=\\tfrac{32}5$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-23": ["The picture shows the stretch within $\\tfrac18$ lap of the start. At $600$ seconds Rachel is $\\tfrac23$ lap along, so she is in view from $600+\\left(\\tfrac78-\\tfrac23\\right)90=618.75$ to $641.25$ seconds. Robert has done $7\\tfrac12$ laps, so he is in view from $630$ to $650$ seconds. Both are in view for $641.25-630=11.25$ of the $60$ seconds, probability $\\tfrac{3}{16}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-24": ["The legs of the trapezoids extend to meet at one center, and the $9$ trapezoids fill a $180^\\circ$ half turn, $20^\\circ$ each. Each trapezoid is cut from an isosceles triangle with apex $20^\\circ$, whose base angles are $80^\\circ$. The larger angle of the trapezoid is $180^\\circ-80^\\circ=100^\\circ$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-10b-25": ["An encircling stripe runs around one of the $3$ axes, on the $4$ faces around it. Each of those faces points its stripe the right way with probability $\\tfrac12$, so a given axis works with probability $\\tfrac1{16}$. Two axes cannot both work, since they share faces that would need different stripes. So the probability is $\\tfrac3{16}$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-1": ["With $b$ bagels the cost in cents is $50(5-b)+75b=250+25b$, a multiple of $100$ only when $b=2$ in the range $0$ to $5$. Choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-2": ["Three cans covered $30-25=5$ rooms, so $25$ rooms took $5\\cdot3=15$ cans, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-3": ["Twenty percent less than $60$ is $48$, which is $\\tfrac43$ of the number. So the number is $36$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-4": ["The two triangles take up the difference $25-15=10$ along the long side, $5$ each, and as isosceles right triangles they are $5$ tall, which is the yard's width. The yard is $25\\times5=125$, and the beds are $2\\cdot\\tfrac{25}2=25$, a fraction $\\tfrac15$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-5": ["The ages are $k\\cdot t^2=128=2^7$ with $k\\lt t$, all powers of $2$. The choice $t=8$, $k=2$ works ($t=4$ would need $k=8$, too old). The sum is $2+8+8=18$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-6": ["Try every grouping of $2\\times3+4\\times5$: the usual order gives $26$, $2\\times(3+4)\\times5=70$, $(2\\times3+4)\\times5=50$ and $2\\times(3+4\\times5)=46$. So $4$ values, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-7": ["After three months the price is multiplied by $1.2\\cdot0.8\\cdot1.25=1.2$. To return, April multiplies by $\\tfrac1{1.2}=\\tfrac56$, a fall of $16\\tfrac23\\%$, about $17$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-8": ["Subtracting, $\\tfrac16$ of the water weighs $a-b$, so a full bucket of water weighs $6(a-b)$ and the bucket $a-4(a-b)=4b-3a$. The total is $4b-3a+6a-6b=3a-2b$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-9": ["The line $AB$ is $x+y=3$, parallel to $x+y=7$, so every $C$ on that line is the same distance from $AB$: $\\tfrac{7-3}{\\sqrt2}=2\\sqrt2$. With $AB=3\\sqrt2$, the area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot3\\sqrt2\\cdot2\\sqrt2=6$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-10": ["The hour is right for $8$ of the $12$ hours (all but $1$, $10$, $11$, $12$), a fraction $\\tfrac23$. The minutes are right when neither digit is $1$: $5$ tens digits and $9$ units digits, $\\tfrac{45}{60}=\\tfrac34$. Together $\\tfrac23\\cdot\\tfrac34=\\tfrac12$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-11": ["Track the millet just after each quart is added, with $\\tfrac34$ quart of other seed each time. Monday $0.25$, Tuesday $0.25\\cdot0.75+0.25=0.4375$, Wednesday $0.578$, Thursday $0.684$, Friday $0.763$. Friday is the first day the millet beats the $0.75$ of other seed, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-12": ["Three steps multiply by $r^3=\\tfrac{8!}{7!}=8$, so $r=2$. The first term is four steps before the fifth: $\\tfrac{7!}{2^4}=\\tfrac{5040}{16}=315$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-13": ["Let $D$ be the foot of the altitude. The right triangles $ABD$ and $ACD$ are $5$-$12$-$13$ and $9$-$12$-$15$, so $BD=5$ and $CD=9$. If $D$ is between $B$ and $C$, $BC=14$; if not, $BC=9-5=4$. The sum is $18$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-14": ["Each side gets area $\\tfrac52$. The part below the line is the triangle with vertices $(a,0)$, $(3,0)$, $(3,3)$ minus the missing unit square at the lower right, so $\\tfrac{3(3-a)}2-1=\\tfrac52$. Then $3-a=\\tfrac73$ and $a=\\tfrac23$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-15": ["Each equation gives $x=\\tfrac{\\log(7/3)}{\\log(1+t)}$ for its $t$, so the largest $x$ has the smallest $t$. Since $0\\lt r\\lt3$, $\\tfrac r{10}$ is smaller than $r$, $2r$ and $\\sqrt r$ (as $r\\lt100$), and smaller than $\\tfrac1r$ (as $r^2\\lt10$). So the answer is choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-16": ["Extend $BA$ and $CD$ to meet at $E$. In triangle $BDE$, $\\angle DBE=23^\\circ$ and the exterior angle at $D$ is $46^\\circ$, so $\\angle BED=23^\\circ$ and $DE=BD=1$. Since $AD\\parallel BC$, triangles $EAD$ and $EBC$ are similar with ratio $5:9$, so $\\tfrac{ED}{EC}=\\tfrac59$ and $EC=\\tfrac95$. Then $CD=\\tfrac95-1=\\tfrac45$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-17": ["An encircling stripe runs around one of the $3$ axes, on the $4$ faces around it. Each of those faces points its stripe the right way with probability $\\tfrac12$, so a given axis works with probability $\\tfrac1{16}$. Two axes cannot both work, since they share faces that would need different stripes. So the probability is $\\tfrac3{16}$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-18": ["The picture shows the stretch within $\\tfrac18$ lap of the start. At $600$ seconds Rachel is $\\tfrac23$ lap along, so she is in view from $600+\\left(\\tfrac78-\\tfrac23\\right)90=618.75$ to $641.25$ seconds. Robert has done $7\\tfrac12$ laps, so he is in view from $630$ to $650$ seconds. Both are in view for $641.25-630=11.25$ of the $60$ seconds, probability $\\tfrac{3}{16}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-19": ["Complete a square to force a difference of squares: $n^4-360n^2+400=(n^2+20)^2-400n^2=(n^2-20n+20)(n^2+20n+20)$. For a prime the smaller factor must be $1$ (it cannot be $-1$ for an integer $n$), so $n^2-20n+19=0$, giving $n=1$ or $19$. Then $f(1)=41$ and $f(19)=761$, both prime, and the sum is $802$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-20": ["Each original edge loses both ends to pyramids but keeps its middle, giving $100$ edges of $R$. Each cut at $V_k$ adds a polygon with one side per edge at $V_k$, so the new edges number $\\sum\\deg V_k=2\\cdot100=200$ by the handshake lemma. In all $R$ has $300$ edges, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-21": ["Each woman stays put or swaps with a neighbour, so a reseating is a tiling of the row of $10$ seats by single seats and adjacent pairs. If $a_n$ counts these, the first seat is a single or starts a pair, so $a_n=a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}$ with $a_1=1$, $a_2=2$: the Fibonacci numbers, giving $a_{10}=89$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-22": ["Write $B=(b,b)$ and $D=(d,kd)$ with positive integers $b$, $d$; then $C=B+D$ is automatically in the first quadrant. The area is the determinant $\\lvert b\\cdot kd-b\\cdot d\\rvert=bd(k-1)=10^6$, so we count ordered triples $(b,d,k-1)$ of positive integers with product $2^6\\cdot5^6$. The exponents of $2$ split among three factors in $\\binom82=28$ ways, and likewise for $5$, so there are $28^2=784$ parallelograms, choice (C).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-23": ["Multiplying by $\\tfrac34(1+i)$ sends $x+iy$ to $\\tfrac34\\left((x-y)+i(x+y)\\right)$, which is in $S$ exactly when $\\lvert x-y\\rvert\\le\\tfrac43$ and $\\lvert x+y\\rvert\\le\\tfrac43$, that is $\\lvert x\\rvert+\\lvert y\\rvert\\le\\tfrac43$. Inside the square of area $4$ this removes four corner triangles with legs $\\tfrac23$, total area $\\tfrac89$. So the probability is $\\tfrac{4-8/9}4=\\tfrac79$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-24": ["On $[0,\\pi]$, $\\cos^{-1}(\\cos x)=x$, and $\\sin^{-1}(\\sin6x)$ is at most $\\tfrac\\pi2$, so $x\\le\\tfrac\\pi2$. Where $6x$ is within $\\tfrac\\pi2$ of $2\\pi k$, $\\sin^{-1}(\\sin6x)=6x-2\\pi k$, giving $x=\\tfrac{2\\pi k}5$: $x=0$ and $\\tfrac{2\\pi}5$. Where $6x$ is within $\\tfrac\\pi2$ of $\\pi+2\\pi k$, it equals $\\pi-6x+2\\pi k$, giving $x=\\tfrac{(2k+1)\\pi}7$: $\\tfrac\\pi7$ and $\\tfrac{3\\pi}7$. Each lies in the right range, so there are $4$ solutions, choice (B).","",[]],
"2009-amc-12b-25": ["$G$ is four $5\\times5$ blocks of lattice points, one in each quadrant, each only $4$ wide, so a square of side at least $6$ has one vertex in each block. Going counterclockwise from the vertex $A$ in the first block, let the side vector be $(-p,q)$; turning it by $90^\\circ$ gives the later sides $(-q,-p)$ and $(p,-q)$. Requiring all four vertices to stay in their blocks leaves a $k\\times k$ array of positions for $A$, where $k=5-\\lvert p-10\\rvert-\\lvert q\\rvert$. There is $1$ vector with $k=5$, $4$ with $k=4$, $8$ with $k=3$, $12$ with $k=2$ and $16$ with $k=1$, so the count is $25+4\\cdot16+8\\cdot9+12\\cdot4+16\\cdot1=225$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-1": ["$2001=3\\cdot23\\cdot29$. Distinct factors with the largest sum use $1$ and push everything else into one factor: $1\\cdot3\\cdot667$ gives $671$, beating $1\\cdot23\\cdot87$ ($111$), $1\\cdot29\\cdot69$ ($99$) and $3\\cdot23\\cdot29$ ($55$). The answer is $671$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-2": ["$2000\\cdot2000^{2000}=2000^{1+2000}=2000^{2001}$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-3": ["Each day leaves $80\\%$, so after two days $0.8^2=0.64$ of the original remains. The jar started with $\\tfrac{32}{0.64}=50$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-4": ["With fee $F$ and December connect charge $h$: $F+h=12.48$ and $F+2h=17.54$. Subtracting gives $h=5.06$, so $F=7.42$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-5": ["$MN$ is a midline, so it stays $\\tfrac12AB$. The height from $P$ to $AB$ is fixed, so the area of $PAB$ stays the same, and so does the trapezoid $ABNM$, which is $\\tfrac34$ of it. Only the perimeter changes, as $PA+PB$ grows when $P$ moves away. That is $1$ quantity, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-6": ["Only units digits matter, so add mod $10$: $1,1,2,3,5,8,3,1,4,5,9,4,3,7,0,7,7,4,1,5,6,\\ldots$ Every digit except $6$ shows up before the $21$st term, and $6$ arrives there, so it is last, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-7": ["The trisectors split the right angle at $D$ into $30^\\circ$ pieces, so $\\angle ADP=30^\\circ$ and $\\angle ADB=60^\\circ$. With $AD=1$: $AP=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}$, $DP=\\tfrac2{\\sqrt3}$, $AB=\\sqrt3$ and $DB=2$, so $PB=\\sqrt3-\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}=\\tfrac2{\\sqrt3}$. The perimeter is $2+\\tfrac4{\\sqrt3}=2+\\tfrac{4\\sqrt3}3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-8": ["$\\tfrac25F=\\tfrac45S$ gives $F=2S$: twice as many freshmen as sophomores, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-10": ["The triangle inequality allows $2\\lt x\\lt10$ and $2\\lt y\\lt10$, so $\\lvert x-y\\rvert$ takes every value from $0$ up to, but not including, $8$. The smallest positive value it cannot take is $8$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-11": ["$pq-p-q=(p-1)(q-1)-1$, so add $1$ to each choice and look for $(p-1)(q-1)$ with primes $5,7,11,13,17$. Only $120=10\\cdot12$ works, from $p=11$, $q=13$, so $119$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-12": ["Figure $n$ is two staircases of squares: rows of $1,3,\\ldots,2n+1,\\ldots,3,1$, which total $n^2+(n+1)^2$ (check: $1,5,13,25$). Figure $100$ has $100^2+101^2=20201$, choice (C).","Alternatively, checkerboard the figure with its four tip squares dark. The dark squares line up in a $(n+1)\\times(n+1)$ square array turned $45^\\circ$, and the light squares fill the gaps of that array in an $n\\times n$ array, so the count $n^2+(n+1)^2$ can be read off the picture with no sum at all.",[]],
"2000-amc-10-13": ["The board has rows of $1,2,3,4,5$ holes and columns of $5,4,3,2,1$. The five yellow pegs need five different rows and columns, so one per row and column; the one-hole top row forces yellow into the first column, the second row then into the second column, and so on down the long diagonal. The same forcing places red, then green, blue and orange, so there is exactly $1$ way, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-14": ["The five scores add to $400$, and the first four entered must add to a multiple of $4$, so the last score is $\\equiv400\\equiv0\\pmod4$: $76$ or $80$. If $76$ came last, the first four add to $324$, and the first three must add to a multiple of $3$, so the fourth entry is $\\equiv324\\equiv0\\pmod3$; none of $71,80,82,91$ is. With $80$ last, the order $76,82,91,71,80$ has running sums $76,158,249,320,400$, divisible by $1,2,3,4,5$. The last score is $80$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-15": ["$\\tfrac ab+\\tfrac ba=\\tfrac{a^2+b^2}{ab}=\\tfrac{(a-b)^2+2ab}{ab}$, and $a-b=ab$, so this is $\\tfrac{(ab)^2+2ab}{ab}=ab+2$. Subtracting $ab$ leaves $2$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-16": ["Put the bottom-left lattice point at the origin: $A=(0,3)$, $B=(6,0)$, $C=(4,2)$, $D=(2,0)$. Line $AB$ is $y=3-\\tfrac x2$ and line $CD$ is $y=x-2$; they meet at $E=(\\tfrac{10}3,\\tfrac43)$. Then $AE=\\sqrt{(\\tfrac{10}3)^2+(\\tfrac53)^2}=\\tfrac{5\\sqrt5}3$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-17": ["Trading a quarter for five nickels or a nickel for five pennies keeps the value; trading a penny for five quarters adds $124$ cents. So Boris always has $1+124k$ cents, a number $\\equiv1\\pmod{124}$. Of the choices only $745=1+6\\cdot124$ fits, $\\$7.45$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-18": ["Inside the square she sees a band $1$ km wide: $25-9=16$. Outside she sees four $5\\times1$ rectangles along the sides, $20$, and four quarter circles of radius $1$ at the corners, $\\pi$. The total is $36+\\pi\\approx39.1$, so $39$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-19": ["Take the square's side as $1$. The triangle with area $m$ has legs $1$ and $2m$. The two small triangles are similar to each other, with the square's sides matched crosswise, so the other one has legs $\\tfrac1{2m}$ and $1$ and area $\\tfrac1{4m}$. The ratio is $\\tfrac1{4m}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-20": ["Add $A+M+C+1=11$ to complete a product: the expression is $(A+1)(M+1)(C+1)-11$. The factors add to $13$, and their product is largest when they are as equal as possible, $4\\cdot4\\cdot5=80$. The maximum is $80-11=69$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-21": ["Some creepy crawlers are alligators, and every alligator is ferocious, so those creepy crawlers are ferocious creatures: II must be true. I and III each say something about the alligators outside the creepy crawlers, which the facts leave open. The answer is II only, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-22": ["With $m$ ounces of milk and $c$ of coffee in all, Angela drank $\\tfrac m4+\\tfrac c6=8$, so $3m+2c=96$, while $n$ people drank $m+c=8n$. Then $m=96-16n$ and $c=24n-96$, and both are positive only for $n=5$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-23": ["The mode is $2$ and the mean is $\\tfrac{25+x}7$. The median is $2$ for $x\\le2$, $x$ for $2\\lt x\\lt4$, and $4$ for $x\\ge4$. A median of $2$ equals the mode, so the progression would be constant. A median $x$ in $(2,4)$ needs $2x=2+\\tfrac{25+x}7$, so $x=3$ (the terms $2,3,4$). A median of $4$ needs the mean to be $6$, so $x=17$ (the terms $2,4,6$); a mean of $3$ or $0$ would put $x$ below $4$. The sum is $3+17=20$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-24": ["$f(3z)$ is the case $\\tfrac x3=3z$, that is $x=9z$, so $f(3z)=81z^2+9z+1=7$, or $81z^2+9z-6=0$. Vieta gives the sum of the roots as $-\\tfrac9{81}=-\\tfrac19$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-25": ["From day $300$ of year $N$ to day $200$ of year $N+1$ is $(L-300)+200$ days, with $L$ the length of year $N$; it must be a multiple of $7$, and $266=38\\cdot7$ works, so $L=366$. Then year $N-1$ has $365$ days, and from its day $100$ to day $300$ of year $N$ is $265+300=565\\equiv5\\pmod7$ days. Five days before a Tuesday is a Thursday, choice (A).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-1": ["The list is in order, so the median is the fifth term, $n+6=10$, and $n=4$. The offsets add to $0+3+4+5+6+8+10+12+15=63$, so the mean is $n+\\tfrac{63}9=4+7=11$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-2": ["The product of $x$'s reciprocal and its additive inverse is $\\tfrac1x\\cdot(-x)=-1$, so $x=-1+2=1$, which lies in $0\\lt x\\le2$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-3": ["With numbers $a$ and $b$, the final numbers are $2(a+3)$ and $2(b+3)$, and they add to $2(a+b)+12=2S+12$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-4": ["A line meets a circle in at most $2$ points, so each of the three sides gives at most $2$: at most $6$. A circle slightly larger than the incircle, with the same center, cuts every side twice, so $6$ is reached, choice (E).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-5": ["Check each piece for a mirror line. The straight I, the T, the plus-shaped X, the U, the V (symmetric about a diagonal) and the staircase W (also about a diagonal) have one; the L, Y, P, F, N and Z do not. That is $6$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-6": ["Write $N=10a+b$. Then $10a+b=ab+a+b$, so $9a=ab$, and since $a\\ne0$, $b=9$. The units digit is $9$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-7": ["Moving the point four places multiplies by $10^4$, so $10^4x=\\tfrac4x$, giving $x^2=4\\cdot10^{-4}$ and $x=0.02$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-8": ["They are together again after a number of days that is a multiple of $3$, $4$, $6$ and $7$, and the first such is $\\operatorname{lcm}(3,4,6,7)=84$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-9": ["Compared with paying $p\\%$ on everything, she pays an extra $2\\%$ of the income above $28000$, and this must equal an extra $0.25\\%$ of her whole income $I$: $0.02(I-28000)=0.0025I$. Then $0.0175I=560$ and $I=32000$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-10": ["Multiply the three equations: $(xyz)^2=24\\cdot48\\cdot72=288^2$, so $xyz=288$. Dividing by each product gives $z=12$, $y=6$, $x=4$, and $x+y+z=22$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-11": ["The square through the $k$th ring has side $2k+1$, and the one inside it has side $2k-1$. The ring holds $(2k+1)^2-(2k-1)^2=8k$ squares, which is $800$ for $k=100$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-12": ["Three consecutive integers include a multiple of $2$ and of $3$, so $6$ divides $n$, and with $7$ dividing $n$, so do $14$, $21$ and $42$. But $5\\cdot6\\cdot7=210$ is not divisible by $4$, so $28$ need not divide $n$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-13": ["The ten letters are ten different digits, so every digit is used once. $GHIJ$ is $9753$ or $7531$, and $DEF$ is $864$, $642$ or $420$; $ABC$ gets the three digits left, which must add to $9$. With $7531$, the spare odd digit is $9$, too big. With $9753$, the spare odd digit is $1$, and the spare even digits add to $8$ only for $DEF=642$, which leaves $0$ and $8$. So $ABC=810$ and $A=8$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-14": ["Say $f$ tickets sell at $p$ dollars and $140-f$ at $\\tfrac p2$. Then $fp+(140-f)\\tfrac p2=2001$, so $p(f+140)=4002=2\\cdot3\\cdot23\\cdot29$. The factor $f+140$ lies between $141$ and $279$, and the only divisor of $4002$ there is $174$. So $f=34$, $p=23$, and the full-price tickets raise $34\\cdot23=782$ dollars, choice (A).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-15": ["The crosswalk is a parallelogram. With the curb segment as base its area is $15\\cdot40=600$; with a stripe as base it is $50d$, where $d$ is the distance between the stripes. So $d=\\tfrac{600}{50}=12$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-16": ["Let the mean be $m$: the numbers are $m-10$, $5$ and $m+15$, and their sum is $3m$. So $2m+10=3m$, $m=10$, and the sum is $30$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-17": ["The radius $10$ becomes the slant height. The arc, $\\tfrac{252}{360}\\cdot20\\pi=14\\pi$, becomes the base circumference, so the base radius is $7$. The cone with base radius $7$ and slant side $10$ is choice (C).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-18": ["The tiling repeats in $3\\times3$ blocks of unit cells. In each block the square tiles cover $4$ cells' worth of area and the pentagons the other $5$, so the pentagons enclose $\\tfrac59\\approx55.6\\%$ of the plane, closest to $56$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-19": ["A selection is a count of each type adding to $4$, a solution of $g+c+p=4$ in nonnegative integers. Stars and bars gives $\\binom62=15$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-20": ["Cut triangles with legs $x$ from each corner. Each octagon side on the square is $2000-2x$, and each cut side is $x\\sqrt2$. Setting them equal gives $x=\\tfrac{2000}{2+\\sqrt2}$, and the side is $x\\sqrt2=\\tfrac{2000\\sqrt2}{2+\\sqrt2}=2000(\\sqrt2-1)$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-21": ["Cut through the common axis: the cone becomes a triangle with base $10$ and height $12$, and the cylinder a rectangle of width $2r$ and height $2r$. At height $2r$ the cone's half-width has shrunk by similar triangles to $5\\left(1-\\tfrac{2r}{12}\\right)$, and this must equal $r$. So $5-\\tfrac{5r}6=r$ and $r=\\tfrac{30}{11}$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-22": ["In a $3\\times3$ magic square the center $x$ is a third of the line sum, and two cells opposite each other through the center add to $2x$. So $w=2x-25$, $v=2x-21$, $y=2x-18$, $z=2x-24$. The top row gives $(2x-21)+24+(2x-25)=3x$, so $x=22$. Then $y=26$, $z=20$, and $y+z=46$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-23": ["Imagine drawing all five chips in a random order. The process stops when one color runs out, and the last chip drawn is white exactly when the whites run out first, that is, when the final chip of the whole order is red. That has probability $\\tfrac35$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-24": ["Let $AB=a$ and $CD=b$. Drop the perpendicular from $B$ to $CD$: it has length $7$ and cuts off $b-a$, so $BC^2=49+(b-a)^2$. With $BC=a+b$, $(a+b)^2-(b-a)^2=4ab=49$, so $AB\\cdot CD=12.25$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2001-amc-10-25": ["Multiples of $3$ or $4$ up to $2001$: $667+500-166=1001$. Those that are also multiples of $5$ are the multiples of $15$ or $20$: $133+100-33=200$. That leaves $1001-200=801$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-1": ["$2001=3\\cdot23\\cdot29$. Distinct factors with the largest sum use $1$ and push everything else into one factor: $1\\cdot3\\cdot667$ gives $671$, beating $1\\cdot23\\cdot87$ ($111$), $1\\cdot29\\cdot69$ ($99$) and $3\\cdot23\\cdot29$ ($55$). The answer is $671$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-2": ["$2000\\cdot2000^{2000}=2000^{1+2000}=2000^{2001}$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-3": ["Each day leaves $80\\%$, so after two days $0.8^2=0.64$ of the original remains. The jar started with $\\tfrac{32}{0.64}=50$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-4": ["Only units digits matter, so add mod $10$: $1,1,2,3,5,8,3,1,4,5,9,4,3,7,0,7,7,4,1,5,6,\\ldots$ Every digit except $6$ shows up before the $21$st term, and $6$ arrives there, so it is last, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-6": ["$pq-p-q=(p-1)(q-1)-1$, so add $1$ to each choice and look for $(p-1)(q-1)$ with primes $5,7,11,13,17$. Only $120=10\\cdot12$ works, from $p=11$, $q=13$, so $119$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-7": ["$729=3^6$, so $b=3^k$ with $k$ a divisor of $6$: $b=3,9,27,729$, which is $4$ values, choice (E).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-8": ["Figure $n$ is two staircases of squares: rows of $1,3,\\ldots,2n+1,\\ldots,3,1$, which total $n^2+(n+1)^2$ (check: $1,5,13,25$). Figure $100$ has $100^2+101^2=20201$, choice (C).","Alternatively, checkerboard the figure with its four tip squares dark. The dark squares line up in a $(n+1)\\times(n+1)$ square array turned $45^\\circ$, and the light squares fill the gaps of that array in an $n\\times n$ array, so the count $n^2+(n+1)^2$ can be read off the picture with no sum at all.",[]],
"2000-amc-12-9": ["The five scores add to $400$, and the first four entered must add to a multiple of $4$, so the last score is $\\equiv400\\equiv0\\pmod4$: $76$ or $80$. If $76$ came last, the first four add to $324$, and the first three must add to a multiple of $3$, so the fourth entry is $\\equiv324\\equiv0\\pmod3$; none of $71,80,82,91$ is. With $80$ last, the order $76,82,91,71,80$ has running sums $76,158,249,320,400$, divisible by $1,2,3,4,5$. The last score is $80$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-10": ["Reflecting in the $xy$-plane gives $Q=(1,2,-3)$; the half-turn about the $x$-axis negates $y$ and $z$, giving $R=(1,-2,3)$; the translation gives $S=(1,3,3)$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-11": ["$\\tfrac ab+\\tfrac ba=\\tfrac{a^2+b^2}{ab}=\\tfrac{(a-b)^2+2ab}{ab}$, and $a-b=ab$, so this is $\\tfrac{(ab)^2+2ab}{ab}=ab+2$. Subtracting $ab$ leaves $2$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-12": ["Add $A+M+C+1=13$ to complete a product: the expression is $(A+1)(M+1)(C+1)-13$. The factors add to $15$, and their product is largest when they are equal, $5\\cdot5\\cdot5=125$. The maximum is $125-13=112$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-13": ["With $m$ ounces of milk and $c$ of coffee in all, Angela drank $\\tfrac m4+\\tfrac c6=8$, so $3m+2c=96$, while $n$ people drank $m+c=8n$. Then $m=96-16n$ and $c=24n-96$, and both are positive only for $n=5$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-14": ["The mode is $2$ and the mean is $\\tfrac{25+x}7$. The median is $2$ for $x\\le2$, $x$ for $2\\lt x\\lt4$, and $4$ for $x\\ge4$. A median of $2$ equals the mode, so the progression would be constant. A median $x$ in $(2,4)$ needs $2x=2+\\tfrac{25+x}7$, so $x=3$ (the terms $2,3,4$). A median of $4$ needs the mean to be $6$, so $x=17$ (the terms $2,4,6$); a mean of $3$ or $0$ would put $x$ below $4$. The sum is $3+17=20$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-15": ["$f(3z)$ is the case $\\tfrac x3=3z$, that is $x=9z$, so $f(3z)=81z^2+9z+1=7$, or $81z^2+9z-6=0$. Vieta gives the sum of the roots as $-\\tfrac9{81}=-\\tfrac19$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-16": ["The square in row $r$ and column $c$ gets $17(r-1)+c$ and $13(c-1)+r$. These agree when $4r-3c=1$: $(r,c)=(1,1),(4,5),(7,9),(10,13),(13,17)$, numbered $1,56,111,166,221$. The sum is $555$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-17": ["Triangle $OAB$ is right-angled at $A$ with $OA=1$, $AB=\\tan\\theta$, $OB=\\sec\\theta$. By the Angle Bisector Theorem $\\tfrac{OC}{CA}=\\tfrac{OB}{AB}=\\tfrac1{\\sin\\theta}$, so $OC=\\tfrac1{1+\\sin\\theta}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-18": ["From day $300$ of year $N$ to day $200$ of year $N+1$ is $(L-300)+200$ days, with $L$ the length of year $N$; it must be a multiple of $7$, and $266=38\\cdot7$ works, so $L=366$. Then year $N-1$ has $365$ days, and from its day $100$ to day $300$ of year $N$ is $265+300=565\\equiv5\\pmod7$ days. Five days before a Tuesday is a Thursday, choice (A).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-19": ["By the Angle Bisector Theorem $BE=14\\cdot\\tfrac{13}{28}=6.5$, while $BD=7$, so $DE=\\tfrac12$. The $13$-$14$-$15$ triangle has area $84$, so its height to $BC$ is $12$, and $[ADE]=\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12\\cdot12=3$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-20": ["Multiply the three equations: $xyz+\\tfrac1{xyz}+\\left(x+y+z+\\tfrac1x+\\tfrac1y+\\tfrac1z\\right)=4\\cdot1\\cdot\\tfrac73=\\tfrac{28}3$. Adding them gives the bracket as $\\tfrac{22}3$, so $xyz+\\tfrac1{xyz}=2$ and $xyz=1$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-21": ["Take the square's side as $1$. The triangle with area $m$ has legs $1$ and $2m$. The two small triangles are similar to each other, with the square's sides matched crosswise, so the other one has legs $\\tfrac1{2m}$ and $1$ and area $\\tfrac1{4m}$. The ratio is $\\tfrac1{4m}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-22": ["From the graph, $P(-1)\\gt4$ and the sum of coefficients $P(1)\\gt2$. By Vieta the product of all four zeros is $d=P(0)\\approx5$. The real zeros lie near $2.3$ and $3.8$, so their sum is over $5$ and their product over $6$. Then the non-real pair has product $\\tfrac d{\\text{(real product)}}\\lt\\tfrac56$, the smallest of the five, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-23": ["The log sum is an integer exactly when the product is a power of $10$, so every number is of the form $2^a5^b$ ($1,2,4,5,8,10,16,20,25,32,40$) and the exponents of $2$ and $5$ balance. The $5$s come only from $5,10,20,25,40$, at most $6$ in all, and checking the choices finds exactly $4$ balanced sets of six: $\\{1,2,4,5,10,25\\}$, $\\{1,2,5,10,25,40\\}$, $\\{1,4,5,10,20,25\\}$, $\\{1,5,10,20,25,40\\}$. So the probability is $\\tfrac14$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-24": ["Let $AB=r$. Triangle $ABC$ is equilateral, so arc $BC$ is $\\tfrac{\\pi r}3=12$ and $r=\\tfrac{36}\\pi$. By symmetry the small circle's center is above the midpoint of $\\overline{AB}$ at height $s$, its radius, and internal tangency with the arc about $A$ gives $(\\tfrac r2)^2+s^2=(r-s)^2$, so $s=\\tfrac{3r}8$. The circumference is $2\\pi\\cdot\\tfrac{3r}8=\\tfrac{3\\pi}4\\cdot\\tfrac{36}\\pi=27$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-25": ["There are $8!$ ways to place the colors on a fixed octahedron. The octahedron has $24$ rotations, and since all colors differ, no rotation but the identity fixes a coloring, so each distinguishable octahedron is counted $24$ times. That gives $\\tfrac{8!}{24}=1680$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-1": ["With numbers $a$ and $b$, the final numbers are $2(a+3)$ and $2(b+3)$, and they add to $2(a+b)+12=2S+12$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-2": ["Write $N=10a+b$. Then $10a+b=ab+a+b$, so $9a=ab$, and since $a\\ne0$, $b=9$. The units digit is $9$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-3": ["Compared with paying $p\\%$ on everything, she pays an extra $2\\%$ of the income above $28000$, and this must equal an extra $0.25\\%$ of her whole income $I$: $0.02(I-28000)=0.0025I$. Then $0.0175I=560$ and $I=32000$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-4": ["Let the mean be $m$: the numbers are $m-10$, $5$ and $m+15$, and their sum is $3m$. So $2m+10=3m$, $m=10$, and the sum is $30$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-5": ["$10000!$ is the odd product times the even product $2\\cdot4\\cdots10000=2^{5000}\\cdot5000!$. So the odd product is $\\tfrac{10000!}{2^{5000}\\cdot5000!}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-6": ["The ten letters are ten different digits, so every digit is used once. $GHIJ$ is $9753$ or $7531$, and $DEF$ is $864$, $642$ or $420$; $ABC$ gets the three digits left, which must add to $9$. With $7531$, the spare odd digit is $9$, too big. With $9753$, the spare odd digit is $1$, and the spare even digits add to $8$ only for $DEF=642$, which leaves $0$ and $8$. So $ABC=810$ and $A=8$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-7": ["Say $f$ tickets sell at $p$ dollars and $140-f$ at $\\tfrac p2$. Then $fp+(140-f)\\tfrac p2=2001$, so $p(f+140)=4002=2\\cdot3\\cdot23\\cdot29$. The factor $f+140$ lies between $141$ and $279$, and the only divisor of $4002$ there is $174$. So $f=34$, $p=23$, and the full-price tickets raise $34\\cdot23=782$ dollars, choice (A).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-8": ["The radius $10$ becomes the slant height. The arc, $\\tfrac{252}{360}\\cdot20\\pi=14\\pi$, becomes the base circumference, so the base radius is $7$. The cone with base radius $7$ and slant side $10$ is choice (C).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-9": ["Take $x=500$ and $y=\\tfrac65$: $f(600)=\\tfrac{f(500)}{6/5}=\\tfrac{3\\cdot5}6=\\tfrac52$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-10": ["The tiling repeats in $3\\times3$ blocks of unit cells. In each block the square tiles cover $4$ cells' worth of area and the pentagons the other $5$, so the pentagons enclose $\\tfrac59\\approx55.6\\%$ of the plane, closest to $56$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-11": ["Imagine drawing all five chips in a random order. The process stops when one color runs out, and the last chip drawn is white exactly when the whites run out first, that is, when the final chip of the whole order is red. That has probability $\\tfrac35$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-12": ["Multiples of $3$ or $4$ up to $2001$: $667+500-166=1001$. Those that are also multiples of $5$ are the multiples of $15$ or $20$: $133+100-33=200$. That leaves $1001-200=801$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-13": ["Reflecting about $y=k$ gives $y=2k-(ax^2+bx+c)$. The two sums of coefficients are the values at $x=1$, which add to $(a+b+c)+(2k-a-b-c)=2k$, choice (E).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-14": ["Each of the $\\binom92=36$ pairs of vertices is a side of $2$ equilateral triangles, $72$ in all. The $3$ triangles $A_1A_4A_7$, $A_2A_5A_8$, $A_3A_6A_9$ have all three vertices in the set and were counted $3$ times each. So there are $72-3\\cdot2=66$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-15": ["A shortest path crosses the two faces that share an edge joining the two given edges. Unfolded, those faces form a rhombus of side $1$ made of two equilateral triangles, and the two midpoints are midpoints of opposite sides of it. The straight segment between them is parallel to the other two sides and has length $1$, choice (B).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-16": ["Order all $16$ items in $16!$ ways. For each leg only half of the orders put the sock first, and the legs are independent, so the count is $\\tfrac{16!}{2^8}$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-17": ["The angle $APB$ is obtuse exactly when $P$ is inside the circle on diameter $AB$ (Thales). Here $AB=\\sqrt{20}$, so the half of that disk inside the pentagon has area $\\tfrac12\\pi\\cdot5=\\tfrac{5\\pi}2$. The pentagon is the $(2\\pi+1)\\times4$ rectangle less the corner triangle of area $4$, that is $8\\pi$. The probability is $\\tfrac{5\\pi/2}{8\\pi}=\\tfrac5{16}$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-18": ["Two tangent circles of radii $r$ and $s$ resting on a line touch it at points $2\\sqrt{rs}$ apart. The big circles touch the line $2\\sqrt{1\\cdot4}=4$ apart, and the small circle of radius $r$ touches it between them, so $2\\sqrt r+2\\sqrt{4r}=4$. Then $6\\sqrt r=4$ and $r=\\tfrac49$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-19": ["The $y$-intercept gives $c=2$, so by Vieta the product of the zeros is $-2$. Their mean is $-\\tfrac a3=-2$, so $a=6$. The sum of the coefficients is $1+6+b+2=-2$, so $b=-11$, choice (A).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-20": ["By Varignon's Theorem the midpoint quadrilateral has sides parallel to the diagonals and half as long, so it is a square exactly when $AC$ and $BD$ are equal and perpendicular. Since $\\vec{AC}=(2,-6)$, $\\vec{BD}$ is $(6,2)$ or $(-6,-2)$, giving $D=(7,3)$ or $(-5,-1)$. In the first quadrant $D=(7,3)$ and $a+b=10$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-21": ["Add $1$ to each equation and factor: $(a+1)(b+1)=525$, $(b+1)(c+1)=147$, $(c+1)(d+1)=105$. Both $b+1$ and $c+1$ divide $21$, and their product is $147$, so they are $21,7$ or $7,21$. The first gives $(a,b,c,d)=(24,20,6,14)$ with product $40320=8!$; the second gives $(74,6,20,4)$ with product $35520$. So $a-d=10$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-22": ["Triangles $AHF$ and $CHE$ are similar, so $\\tfrac{AH}{HC}=\\tfrac{AF}{CE}=\\tfrac{1/3}{1/2}=\\tfrac23$ and $AH=\\tfrac25AC$. Likewise $\\tfrac{AJ}{JC}=\\tfrac{AG}{CE}=\\tfrac43$, so $AJ=\\tfrac47AC$ and $HJ=\\tfrac6{35}AC$. Triangle $EAC$ has base $EC$, half of $DC$, and the full height, so its area is $\\tfrac{70}4$. Then $[EHJ]=\\tfrac6{35}\\cdot\\tfrac{70}4=3$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-23": ["Dividing out the two integer roots leaves a monic quadratic with integer coefficients, whose non-real roots are a conjugate pair. For $\\tfrac{1+i\\sqrt{11}}2$ that quadratic is $x^2-x+3$, which works. The others give $x^2-x+\\tfrac12$, $x^2-x+\\tfrac54$, $x^2-2x+\\tfrac54$ and $x^2-x+\\tfrac72$, none with integer coefficients, choice (A).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-24": ["Let $BD=1$ and $CD=2$; the exterior angle gives $\\angle ADC=45^\\circ+15^\\circ=60^\\circ$. Drop the perpendicular $CE$ to line $AD$: then $DE=CD\\cos60^\\circ=1$ and $CE=\\sqrt3$. Triangle $BDE$ is isosceles with apex angle $120^\\circ$, so $\\angle DBE=30^\\circ$ and $BE=\\sqrt3$. Then $\\angle EBA=15^\\circ=\\angle EAB$, so $EA=EB=\\sqrt3=EC$, and the right triangle $AEC$ is isosceles: $\\angle ACE=45^\\circ$. Adding $\\angle ECD=30^\\circ$, $\\angle ACB=75^\\circ$, choice (D).","",[]],
"2001-amc-12-25": ["The rule says $a_{n+1}=\\tfrac{a_n+1}{a_{n-1}}$, and the terms run $x,\\ 2000,\\ \\tfrac{2001}x,\\ \\tfrac{x+2001}{2000x},\\ \\tfrac{x+1}{2000}$, then $x,2000$ again, so the sequence has period $5$. Setting each of the four terms other than $2000$ equal to $2001$ gives $x=2001$, $x=1$, $x=\\tfrac{2001}{4001999}$ and $x=4001999$, four different values, choice (D).","",[]],
"2000-amc-10-9": ["For $x\\lt2$, $\\lvert x-2\\rvert=2-x=p$, so $x=2-p$ and $x-p=2-2p$, choice (C).","",[]],
"2000-amc-12-5": ["For $x\\lt2$, $\\lvert x-2\\rvert=2-x=p$, so $x=2-p$ and $x-p=2-2p$, choice (C).","",[]]
});
